xh","createdAt":1794217443118,"updatedAt":1794217443118,"version":1},{"worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","factId":"DAIeWKMi3aNiILbk5_XHF","characterId":"PX9V9i5r7Q-xcIvjbyuck","eventId":"JLebbu3pA-RXZKvs35ANa","note":"","id":"BCvh-H_GDcxejr-4eBUxv","createdAt":1794213938330,"updatedAt":1794213938330,"version":1},{"worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","factId":"cl43gZBCqV0MqYES5eYjE","characterId":"PX9V9i5r7Q-xcIvjbyuck","eventId":"LQxthMqMx2ix2u3pYQu7A","note":"","id":"BSMASSmvcE59iartXyQrr","createdAt":1794213959855,"updatedAt":1794213959855,"version":1},{"worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","factId":"DAIeWKMi3aNiILbk5_XHF","characterId":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6","eventId":"6iI4LgbwDeFqR_Cruf7mE","note":"","id":"CEsA7gsdPcoOxIydSZTs7","createdAt":1794213799429,"updatedAt":1794213799429,"version":1},{"worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","factId":"z7neh0q0qeQk-mALg5Qn3","characterId":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6","eventId":"51sz_3kuWZsw6JR4Y0Ko2","note":"","id":"Dj9eKQyAnn83wJsafZ5ij","createdAt":1794213874934,"updatedAt":1794213874934,"version":1},{"worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","factId":"dakOw4S39Oa2eAj-qkIS7","characterId":"PX9V9i5r7Q-xcIvjbyuck","eventId":"LQxthMqMx2ix2u3pYQu7A","note":"","id":"MbYZYjZXB2WcwBnXDIwHr","createdAt":1794213970285,"updatedAt":1794213970285,"version":1},{"worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","factId":"cl43gZBCqV0MqYES5eYjE","characterId":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6","eventId":"FBthgk0AwfIy4svSwzmYI","note":"","id":"a1MWGCLDWQ-KakCqFcKgj","createdAt":1794213928509,"updatedAt":1794213928509,"version":1},{"worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","factId":"DAIeWKMi3aNiILbk5_XHF","characterId":"0BtClyv2numppMKl2y1if","eventId":"2i5J2Lc7W9L8liiqtAFCG","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"kLLiD0FebvDhNEbUUqcgq","createdAt":1794213808195,"updatedAt":1794213808195,"version":1},{"worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","factId":"cl43gZBCqV0MqYES5eYjE","characterId":"zn60qcdCoQc4XQ4KSnfky","eventId":"FBthgk0AwfIy4svSwzmYI","note":"","id":"nLyPHb0tQa-ErDdeok5v9","createdAt":1794214137310,"updatedAt":1794214137310,"version":1},{"worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","factId":"z7neh0q0qeQk-mALg5Qn3","characterId":"PX9V9i5r7Q-xcIvjbyuck","eventId":"2i5J2Lc7W9L8liiqtAFCG","note":"","beforeStory":true,"id":"oPysbZDb6uBUET9d4tp_c","createdAt":1794213874928,"updatedAt":1794213874928,"version":1},{"worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","factId":"qgGl9J9CfK_xIEa1idgMa","characterId":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6","eventId":"3z3uj6msTp-yJfszkLU-n","note":"","id":"oXXHDai5R-jWZMUZ_B2ZT","createdAt":1794213947620,"updatedAt":1794213947620,"version":1},{"worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","factId":"cl43gZBCqV0MqYES5eYjE","characterId":"-C6L6AyeDMMYuWemxYJ4J","eventId":"FBthgk0AwfIy4svSwzmYI","note":"","id":"qWxEKDACleZJYpRRZvOCl","createdAt":1794213928516,"updatedAt":1794213928516,"version":1},{"worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","factId":"DAIeWKMi3aNiILbk5_XHF","characterId":"-C6L6AyeDMMYuWemxYJ4J","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","note":"","id":"yJnm2RJ4It1mgUw2Qz1nz","createdAt":1794213808200,"updatedAt":1794213808200,"version":1},{"worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","factId":"xv_i-G3CxIZI-WRyGn84G","characterId":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6","eventId":"2i5J2Lc7W9L8liiqtAFCG","note":"","id":"zTYa4iGjskFoB1Cc9HeDw","createdAt":1794213760447,"updatedAt":1794213760447,"version":1}],"characterGoals":[],"sceneTexts":[{"id":"-zUvr40BGcdy9PBdfTSzt","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"2i5J2Lc7W9L8liiqtAFCG","text":"The Salt House kept its ledgers on the third floor, under the eaves, where the damp could not reach them and the clerks could not reach anything else. Ilse Varga had worked there for four years and had never once been warm.\n\nIt was the seventeenth of Brinemonth. She had already written two queries that morning in the margin of the Heron's book, one about a date and one about a sum, in her small slanting hand. Aurek called the queries her second job.\n\nShe sat at the long table with the Kestrel's book open in front of her and the Kestrel's tally-sticks laid out beside it in a row, like the bones of a small animal. Every sack that went over the side of a salt-ship at Varrow was notched on a stick by the pan-reeve and notched again by the ship's mate, and the stick was split down its length so that each man kept half. When the halves came back to the Salt House they were matched, and counted, and the count went into the book. Nobody could cut a notch out of a stick without the other half showing it. It was the oldest honest thing in the city.\n\nShe counted the sticks again. Thirty-four sticks, ten notches to a stick. Three hundred and forty measures.\n\nThe book said four hundred.\n\n\"You're frowning at it,\" said Aurek Stiles from the far end of the table. He was the senior of the two tallyclerks and had been for twenty years, and he said it without looking up from his own work. \"Frowning at a ledger is how the ink gets into your face.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel,\" Ilse said. \"Her book says four hundred measures out on the ninth. I've got three hundred and forty on the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then you've dropped six sticks behind the stove.\"\n\n\"I haven't.\"\n\n\"Then the mate's dropped them in the harbour. It happens.\" Aurek turned a page. \"Write it as received and put a query in the margin. The Magister reads the margins.\"\n\nShe did not write it as received. She sat with the pen over the page until the ink beaded on the nib and fell, a small black star on the blotter, and then she got up and went down the narrow stair to the Magister's room.\n\nMagister Oduya Fenn had run the Salt House since before Ilse was born. He was a tall man gone thin in the way of tall men who forget to eat, and his room was the only warm one in the building, because he had the only good stove. He glanced up when she knocked and smiled at her with real pleasure, which made what she had come to say harder.\n\n\"Ilse. Sit. You look like you've been out on the Pans.\"\n\n\"Only on the third floor, Magister.\" She did not sit. \"The Kestrel's book is sixty measures over her sticks.\"\n\nThe smile stayed exactly where it was. That was the first thing she would remember afterwards — not that it went, but that it stayed, a little too long, like a man holding a door that nobody was coming through.\n\n\"Sixty,\" he said.\n\n\"Six sticks. I've counted three times.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel came in through the Gullet in a rising sea. Her mate was sick over the side twice before they'd cleared the bar — your brother will tell you; he took her in.\" Fenn spread his long hands. \"Sticks go missing. The book is written from the manifest at the pans, and the manifest is right.\"\n\n\"Then the sticks should be with the manifest.\"\n\n\"Then they should.\" He said it kindly. \"Write the book as it stands, Ilse. I'll speak to the pan-reeve myself.\"\n\nShe went back up the stairs. Aurek did not ask her what had been said, and she did not tell him. She wrote four hundred in the column in her own clean hand, and in the margin she wrote nothing at all, because the Magister read the margins.\n\nWhen the bell went for the end of the day she put the thirty-four sticks into her satchel instead of into the bin. She did not decide to do it. Her hands did it while she was thinking about something else, and by the time she noticed, she was already on the stair.","wordCount":720,"createdAt":1794213585981,"updatedAt":1794230236198},{"id":"3nY0fUGljA0aPQn4Xytax","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Varga had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. There were forty crowns in his coat pocket. He had not come for them. He had come because the night before he had gone to Corin himself to ask what a man like that knew about six missing sticks, and Corin had been quiet a while and then said: more than your sister does. Come out on the ebb tomorrow and I'll show you.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe. And I'm three hundred crowns short of a berth on anything going south, which is the kind of thing that makes a man sell what he's holding.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter under his seal saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"Right on her night.\"\n\n\"You knew she'd be here.\"\n\n\"I carry the Reeve's post, Tomas. Letters, mostly, and I've a habit of reading them. I knew she'd be here because I carried the letter that said so, and then I carried it somewhere else.\" He sat up. \"The first load tonight. The rest on the spring tide, three nights on.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern: he had brought the Kestrel in through the Gullet on the ninth with that same boat towing astern.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.","wordCount":837,"createdAt":1794213785655,"updatedAt":1794230090495},{"id":"7EFhzSwLtEvxtgrTsUgUq","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"E8x7iKzVjIVHZtYoZ0wvQ","text":"Wren found her at the end of the day, as Mother Gannet had promised he would. He was sitting on the Salt House steps when she came out, with the porter glaring at him from the door, and he stood up as soon as he saw her and said, without any greeting at all, \"The Patience is up the Heron Creek. Tad Mossop saw her. She's on her side on the bar and there's nobody in her.\"\n\nIlse stood on the step with her satchel in her hand.\n\n\"Nobody,\" she said.\n\n\"Tad didn't go close. Tad said there was men about. Reeve's men, in a longboat, going up and down.\" Wren wiped his nose on his cut-down sleeve. \"Mother says you'll want to go and look, and Mother says don't, and Mother says if you're going anyway take me, because I know the way at low water and you don't.\"\n\n\"When's low water?\"\n\n\"Tonight. Middle of the night. It's near springs, so it'll go right out — you can walk to the Chapel nearly dry-shod if you know where the hard is.\" He looked at her with an expression that was much older than the rest of his face. \"Mother said you'd ask that. Mother said you were your mother's daughter.\"\n\nShe thought about going back up to the third floor and telling the auditor everything. The letter, the sticks, the Kestrel's book in her own hand. She thought about the auditor's kindly hen's face, and about the Reeve's men going up and down the channel in a longboat looking for a boat with her brother in it.\n\nIf she told the auditor tonight, the auditor would write to the capital tomorrow, and the capital would send someone in a week. A week was a long time to lie on a bar in the marsh with nobody in your boat.\n\n\"I'll need to go home first,\" she said. \"For boots.\"\n\n\"I'll come with you.\"\n\n\"You don't need to.\"\n\n\"Mother said not to let you out of my sight.\" Wren fell in beside her. \"Mother's never wrong. It's very annoying.\"\n\nThey went up Tallow Street together in the dusk, the tall woman in her clerk's grey and the small dirty boy in his enormous coat, and nobody looked at them twice, because that was the kind of street it was. At the cooper's Ilse left Wren on the step and climbed the stair through the middle of the house to her room.\n\nShe changed into her mother's old marsh boots, which were too big and which she had kept for ten years without once wearing. She took the letter out of her bodice — it had gone soft with her warmth, the seal tacky — and stood for a moment with it in her hand.\n\nIf she went into the marsh with it and they caught her, they would have it. If she left it here —\n\nShe put it in the hollow of the bolster, where the sticks had been. Then she blew out the lamp and went down to Wren.","wordCount":511,"createdAt":1794213918988,"updatedAt":1794217051510},{"id":"7dnGXekx9qghLiQKizjD4","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"FBthgk0AwfIy4svSwzmYI","text":"The marsh at low water was a different country. The channels that the Patience had sailed were trickles now, running between banks of mud as high as Ilse's head, and the mud shone in the moonlight like the skin of something sleeping. Wren went ahead of her along a path she could not see — the hard, he called it, a spine of shingle under the mud that the old salt-carriers had used before there were boats — and she followed him step for step and did not look down.\n\nIt took them two hours to reach the Chapel. The tower stood up out of the mud on its little island of stone, the arches of the old nave around its foot half-buried, the high windows empty. At high water the sea came in through those windows. Now, with the tide out, there were steps going down into the dark of the crypt, slick with weed, and a smell of salt and old candles.\n\n\"Here's where they load,\" Wren whispered. He pointed. Along the stone of the steps there were fresh scars, white in the moonlight, where something heavy had been dragged. \"Salt. Sacks of it. They bring it up the channel from the Pans at night and keep it up on the old gallery over the nave, above the tide-line, till the ship comes.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\nWren shrugged inside his coat. \"Everybody knows. Nobody says.\"\n\nThey found Tomas in the tower.\n\nHe had dragged himself up the first turn of the stair and wedged himself into a window-niche, and he was so still and so grey that Ilse thought for a moment that he was dead. Then his eyes opened and he said, quite clearly, \"You weren't supposed to come,\" and she knelt on the stair beside him and held his hand and could not say anything at all.\n\nHis leg was splinted with half a boat-hook. He had done it well. He had come across the marsh on his back, on the flood, with the tide still making, pushing with his good leg and the stump of the boat-hook, and twice the water had closed over his face. He had been sitting in the niche, he said, for a day and a night, while the Reeve's men came and went below him, carrying it down from the gallery to the crypt steps. He had heard everything. He had not been able to move.\n\n\"There was a voice in the longboat,\" he said. \"The first night. I thought I knew it. I didn't want to.\"\n\n\"Corin,\" Ilse said. \"Where's Corin?\"\n\n\"Went over the side. Took them off me.\" Tomas closed his eyes. \"Don't know if he got out. He said to tell you to keep the letter.\"\n\n\"I have it.\"\n\n\"Good. Ilse — listen. The ship's gone. The Black Mare. She went out on this morning's tide with the first load aboard, and she'll be back for the rest.\" His hand tightened on hers. \"And it's not just the Reeve. I heard them. They were talking about the Salt House. About the books. About someone who's going to fix the books before the auditor —\"\n\nThere was a sound below them. Wren, who had been keeping watch at the foot of the stair, came up it like a cat, silent, his eyes huge.\n\n\"Lantern,\" he breathed. \"Two men. On the hard.\"\n\nThey did not move. Ilse put her hand over Tomas's mouth, not because he would have made a sound but because she needed to hold something. Below them, in the ruined nave, a lantern came and went among the arches, and two men came with it, and stood at the head of the crypt steps where the white scars were.\n\nOne of them was big and wore a harbour officer's coat. She had seen the Reeve of Varrow at a hundred ceremonies on the quay; she knew his shoulders, his walk, the way he stood with his weight back on his heels.\n\nThe other was tall and thin, and held his lantern with a long hand that she had watched sign a thousand pages.\n\n\"She's asking for the flood year,\" the Magister was saying. His voice echoed in the stone. \"She's had it on the table for two days. She has copies of the Hesk ledgers, Halvard. Copies. She knew before she came.\"\n\n\"Then she knows nothing she can prove.\" The Reeve's voice was easy, almost bored. \"Hesk ledgers. A clerk in Hesk can't count. Everyone knows that.\"\n\n\"She'll ask for the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then give her the sticks. You told me the sticks were sorted.\"\n\nThe Magister was quiet. The lantern shook a little.\n\n\"One of my clerks,\" he said at last, \"found the Kestrel short. Before I could see to it.\"\n\n\"Which clerk?\"\n\nIlse felt Tomas's hand tighten on hers until the bones ground.\n\n\"It doesn't matter. I dealt with it. She wrote it in as I told her.\"\n\n\"Oduya.\" The Reeve sounded almost gentle. \"Everything matters. That's how we've lasted three years. Which clerk?\"\n\nThere was a long silence in the nave. Ilse could hear the water beginning to come back, very far off, a sound like breathing.\n\n\"The Varga girl,\" the Magister said.","wordCount":870,"createdAt":1794213918917,"updatedAt":1794230227918},{"id":"Dje9EwizLpXXUxhDeeTnI","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"W6kDqSJm2O5zS3kC2H7Of","text":"The storm came in at four, with the turn of the tide, as the old people on the quay had said it would. It came out of the south-west in one long grey wall, and the sea came with it.\n\nIlse watched from the top of the Gull Stair. She had run there from the Reeve's Hall with Corin behind her and most of the hall behind him, because Wren had come pelting up the hill shouting that the Reeve's longboat had gone out of the harbour with the Reeve in it, and that the Black Mare was lying off the Chapel again in the teeth of the weather, waiting for him.\n\n\"He's mad,\" Corin said, beside her. He had to shout it. \"He'll never get over the bar in this.\"\n\n\"He knows the bar.\"\n\n\"Nobody knows the bar in this.\"\n\nOut past the harbour mouth the sea had gone white. The salt-ships at their moorings were pitching and snatching at their chains, and the spray was coming over the quay wall in sheets, and down below them on the quay the pilots were out in their oilskins, shouting, hauling boats up the slip. Ilse searched for Tomas among them out of habit before she remembered that Tomas was in a bed at the Pans with his leg in a splint.\n\nThen she saw the longboat.\n\nIt was a small dark thing in the white water out by the Chapel, six oars rising and falling out of time, and it was making for the black ship. She could see the Black Mare now too, beyond it — two masts, no sail, her anchors down, plunging. Someone on her deck was waving a light.\n\n\"They'll make it,\" said Corin. \"Gods damn him, they'll —\"\n\nThe sea took the longboat on the Chapel bar.\n\nShe did not see it happen. One moment there were six oars and a dark hull and the next there was a wave, enormous, grey-green, standing up across the bar the way the sea stands up only where the bottom is very close, and when it went down there was nothing on the bar but white water. A long way off, the light on the Black Mare's deck stopped waving.\n\nNobody on the Gull Stair said anything at all.\n\nThe Black Mare did not wait. They saw her get her anchors up, or cut them — it was too far to tell — and get a scrap of sail on her, and claw her way round to the south, away from the bar and the Chapel and Varrow. Within half an hour she was gone into the rain.\n\nIlse found that she was holding the wet rail of the stair with both hands. She had won. She had stood in the long hall and counted the Reeve out in front of the whole town, and the sea had taken the count out of her hands. There would be no trial for Halvard Seyle now, no Exchequer, no question put to him in front of anyone. Every question would go to the Magister instead, who had stood up and answered, and to the men the Reeve had paid, who were still in Varrow and had all seen her face. She had thought it would feel like the end of something. It felt like a ledger with the last page torn out.\n\nThe Reeve's body came ashore two days later on the hard below the sea-wall at the Pans, the new sea-wall, which had held. One of the pan-men found it. Mother Gannet said afterwards that it was the only thing the Reeve ever built that did what it was meant to.","wordCount":612,"createdAt":1794213959581,"updatedAt":1794230068554},{"id":"GrscNQ8wIm4NI5dRvwnDG","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"51sz_3kuWZsw6JR4Y0Ko2","text":"The ledger for the year of the big tide was bound in green, because the binder had run out of brown that winter, and it stood out on the shelf like a wrong note. Lisbet Ormund had it open on the long table when Ilse came up the stair, and beside it the book for the year before, bound in brown, and beside that a sheet of her own paper covered in tiny, neat figures.\n\nAurek was not at his place. The auditor saw her looking.\n\n\"I've sent Master Stiles to the pan-reeve with a list of questions,\" she said. \"It'll take him most of the day, I'm afraid, because the pan-reeve won't know the answers and will have to send to the Reeve's office for them. Sit down, Miss Varga. Sit there, where I can see you.\"\n\nIlse sat.\n\n\"I want to show you something,\" said the auditor, \"and then I want to ask you something, and I'd like you to think before you answer the second thing, because I shall know if you don't.\" She turned the green ledger round. \"This is Thawmonth, the year of the tide. Here — the week of the flood. Nothing comes in at all, which is what you'd expect. Here, the month after. Very little. Here, the month after that. And here,\" she turned a page, \"is the first month that the books are short against the Hesk price.\"\n\nIlse looked. It was a column of numbers in Aurek's hand, tidy and upright, and beside each a ship's name and a date. There was nothing wrong with any of them.\n\n\"They're not short,\" she said.\n\n\"Not in the book. In the book they're very well. But I have here,\" the auditor tapped her own sheet, \"every cargo of Varrow salt that was sold at Hesk that month, from the Hesk customs ledgers, which I had copied before I left the capital. And the Hesk ledgers say that Varrow sent them a good deal less salt than Varrow's books say it sent. About one part in seven less.\" She sat back. \"One part in seven. Every month since. Three years.\"\n\nOne part in seven. Sixty measures out of four hundred was a little more than that. Ilse kept her face still and felt the paper crackle under her bodice when she breathed.\n\n\"Somebody,\" said Lisbet Ormund, \"is selling one-seventh of the salt of Varrow somewhere other than Hesk, and the books of the Salt House are written as though they weren't. Now. Here's my question.\" She leaned forward. \"I don't think you're a thief, Miss Varga. I knew of your mother. I don't think the Magister is a thief either, not in his heart, though I've been wrong about men's hearts before. But somebody in this building writes the numbers, and somebody tells them which numbers to write. Has anybody ever told you to write a number you knew was wrong?\"\n\nShe thought about the Kestrel's book. Four hundred in her own clean hand.\n\nShe thought about Tomas, who had not come home.\n\nShe thought: _if I say yes, she'll ask who. And if I say who, they'll know it was me. And Tomas is somewhere out in the marsh, and I don't know where, and I don't know who else knows._\n\n\"No, madam,\" she said.\n\nThe auditor looked at her for a long moment. Then she nodded, slowly, and turned the green book back around, and picked up her pen.\n\n\"I'll be here until the spring tide,\" she said, without looking up. \"After that I go back to the capital and write my report with whatever I have. If you think of anything before then, Miss Varga, I'd be very grateful to hear it. I'm at the Anchor, on Rope Walk. Anyone can tell you where it is.\"\n\nIlse went back to her own place and opened the Heron's book and could not see a single number in it.","wordCount":657,"createdAt":1794213829003,"updatedAt":1794216608782},{"id":"HLPVxR_FD6CONm9ekYEZI","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"QkH1pzVCb9awQuyhPF6LA","text":"They had been seen. Tomas never knew how — a turn of the Patience's sail against the sky, perhaps, or the creak of her blocks across still water, or simply that a man on the Chapel steps had looked up at the wrong moment. But the light at the Chapel went out all at once, and in the dark there was a shout, and then the sound of oars.\n\n\"Go,\" said Corin. \"Go, go —\"\n\nTomas went. He put the helm over and got the sail up in one long heave and the Patience came round, slow, so slow, broad-beamed old cow that she was, and the flood was against them now, pushing them back up towards the Chapel. Behind them the oars were coming on. Four oars at least. A longboat, light and fast, and the men in her knew the channel nearly as well as he did.\n\n\"They'll have us before the Heron Creek,\" Corin said.\n\n\"They won't.\"\n\n\"They will. I can count, Tomas, it's the only thing I'm good for besides lying.\" Corin was pulling off his boots. \"There's a cut on the left — the one with the dead tree. Where does it go?\"\n\n\"Nowhere. Mud. It's a dead end.\"\n\n\"Can a man stand in it?\"\n\n\"At this tide? To his chest, maybe. What are you —\"\n\n\"They want the boat. They'll follow the boat.\" Corin was on the gunwale now, crouched, his face white in the starlight. \"And they want me more than they want you, because I'm the one they've been paying. So when I go over, you keep going, and you make a great deal of noise, and I'll make rather less.\" He grinned suddenly. \"Tell your sister to keep the letter. Tell her it's worth more than I am.\"\n\n\"Corin —\"\n\nHe went over the side without a splash. Tomas never heard him hit the water. One moment there was a man on the gunwale and the next there was the dark and the dead tree going past on the left and nothing else.\n\nHe kept going. He did not know what else to do. He made, as instructed, a great deal of noise — he let the mainsheet run so the block screamed, and he banged the tiller against the thwart, and he shouted Corin's name once, loudly, towards the right-hand bank, as though looking for a man who had fallen in there. Behind him the oars checked, and a voice said something sharp, and the longboat split its attention for a moment between the boat and the bank.\n\nIt was enough to get him into the Heron Creek. It was not enough to get him out of it.\n\nThe Heron was narrow and shallow and he had taken it a thousand times at the top of the tide, but this was not the top of the tide and he was not looking where he was going. The Patience hit the bar at the creek's mouth with a long grinding shudder that went up through the keel and into his teeth, and stopped, and heeled, and he was thrown against the thwart so hard that he heard something in his leg go with a sound like a green stick breaking.\n\nHe lay in the bilge-water and did not scream, because the longboat was still out there.\n\nAfter a time — he did not know how long — he heard the oars go past the mouth of the creek, slow now, searching. A lantern swung. A man's voice said, \"Nothing. She's gone up the Heron and stuck, she'll be there in the morning, we'll have her then,\" and another voice, one he thought he knew, said, \"And the other one?\" and the first voice said, \"Drowned, if he's got any sense.\" Then the oars again, going away, towards the Chapel and the black ship and the salt.\n\nHe waited until he could not hear them. Then he dragged himself up onto the thwart, and looked at his leg, and looked away again quickly. The tide was still making. In an hour the Patience would float off the bar. In an hour he would have to be somewhere else.\n\nThe tower of the Drowned Chapel was a quarter mile off across the marsh, black against the first grey in the east. It was the only dry place for a mile. It was also where the men in the longboat were going.\n\nTomas studied it for a long time. Then he found the boat-hook, and broke the shaft across his good knee to make a splint, and began to bind his leg with the sail-ties, and tried to think of something else.","wordCount":778,"createdAt":1794213828903,"updatedAt":1794216610094},{"id":"LzHXEw2V7nin8dzd28aF-","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"DS5E6n6CNj-_T8xgA9nT-","text":"Varrow at low water smelt of mud and iron and the sweet rot of weed, and the whole of the lower town came down to the quay to look at the sky.\n\nThere was a storm coming. Ilse could see it from the top of the Gull Stair, a long bruise lying along the horizon to the south-west, with the light behind it gone the colour of a boiled sweet. The harbour had drained down to its channels and the salt-ships sat over on their bilges in the mud, masts leaning together like drunk men holding each other up. Below her the stair went down in its two hundred worn steps to the quay, and the quay was full.\n\nShe found Tomas where she always found him, sitting on an upturned basket outside the pilots' shed with a pipe he never lit, looking at the water as if it owed him money.\n\n\"You took the Kestrel in,\" she said.\n\n\"Hello, Ilse. Good day at the counting? Lovely to see you, Tomas.\" He moved over on the basket to make room for her. \"I took her in on the ninth. Why?\"\n\n\"Was it rough?\"\n\n\"It was the Gullet in a southerly. It's always rough.\" Her brother was three years older than her and had their mother's face, broad and patient, and the same habit of knowing what she was going to say before she said it and waiting for her to say it anyway. \"What's wrong with the Kestrel?\"\n\n\"Was her mate sick?\"\n\nTomas laughed. \"Jory Pell? Jory's been at sea since he could walk. He's never been sick in his life. He was singing.\" He stopped laughing. \"Why?\"\n\nShe told him. She kept her voice down, though nobody on the quay was listening to anything but the wind getting up. Tomas listened with the pipe in his teeth.\n\n\"Sixty measures,\" he said at last. \"That's — what is that, in silver?\"\n\n\"At the Hesk price, nine hundred crowns. Near enough.\"\n\nHe whistled, low and long. \"And the Magister said.\"\n\n\"He said sticks go missing.\"\n\n\"They do.\" But he was frowning now, the way she must have been frowning at the ledger. \"Not six of them off one ship, they don't. A man might lose one. Six is a decision.\" He took the pipe out of his mouth and turned it over. \"What are you going to do?\"\n\n\"I don't know. I wrote it in. Four hundred.\"\n\n\"Ilse.\"\n\n\"What was I supposed to write? He's the Magister. He got me the place.\" She pressed the satchel against her side and felt the sticks shift inside it. \"I took the sticks.\"\n\nTomas looked at the satchel and then at her. \"You took the — that's the Salt House's property.\"\n\n\"I know what it is.\"\n\n\"If anybody knows they were short, they'll know you took them.\"\n\n\"Nobody knows they were short. Aurek thinks I dropped them behind the stove.\"\n\nThe first of the rain came then, a few fat drops striking the stones between their feet, each one leaving a dark coin. All along the quay people began to move, hauling covers over baskets, shouting for children. Out past the harbour mouth the sea had gone a hard, flat grey.\n\n\"Go home,\" Tomas said. \"Put them somewhere dry. Don't do anything else tonight.\" He stood up and knocked the pipe against his palm out of habit, though there was nothing in it. \"I've got to see to the Patience before this comes in. I'll come by tomorrow.\"\n\n\"Tomas —\"\n\n\"Tomorrow.\" He put a hand on her head, the way he had when she was small, and then he was gone into the crowd, a broad back in an oilskin, moving against the people coming up.\n\nShe climbed the Gull Stair in the rain. By the time she reached the top the storm had come in over the bar and the whole harbour was white.","wordCount":651,"createdAt":1794213586083,"updatedAt":1794216601186},{"id":"MlpaXNr4YCl-xz5Iw7maA","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"kACrUDbXsjGSKBXJOWAnj","text":"Corin Ashe came back on the night before the spring tide, the way he had come the first time: a knock after midnight, a voice on the landing, a request not to be hit with whatever she was holding.\n\nThis time she opened the door all the way.\n\nHe looked terrible. He had a cut along his jaw that someone had stitched badly, and his clothes were not his own, and he was thinner than a man ought to get in two days. But he was smiling, and when he saw her face the smile went crooked and then went away altogether.\n\n\"Tomas,\" he said. \"Is he —\"\n\n\"He's alive. His leg's broken. He's at the Pans with a man called Hobb.\" She stood aside. \"Come in. You're letting the cold in.\"\n\nHe came in and stood in the middle of her small room and saw the bolster, which she had sewn up again, badly, and at the feathers that were still in the corners because she had not been able to bring herself to sweep them.\n\n\"I'm sorry about that,\" he said.\n\nShe had known since the Magister laughed. If the Reeve had never sent the letter to Fenn, then only one man in Varrow had known where it had gone, and he had pushed it under her door. But she said, \"You,\" anyway, because it needed saying.\n\n\"Me.\" He reached inside his borrowed coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilcloth and held it out to her, exactly as he had on the first night. \"Everybody hides things in the bolster, Ilse. I've been in forty rooms in this city. It's always the bolster or the chimney, and you don't have a chimney.\"\n\nShe did not take it. \"Why?\"\n\n\"Because Seyle's men were coming up Tallow Street with a key the cooper's wife had been paid for, and I got there first. Because I knew where they'd look.\" He was still holding it out. \"And because I thought — when I came up out of that creek, half drowned, with the Reeve's men going past me close enough to touch — I thought, I could sell this. I could sell this back to Halvard Seyle for a great deal of money and take a berth south to Hesk and never come back. And I had it in my hand on the Hesk quay for a whole day, with the berth paid for, before I turned round.\"\n\n\"Why did you turn round?\"\n\nHe thought about it. She watched him think about it, and was surprised to find that she believed him when he answered.\n\n\"Because you didn't hit me with the poker,\" he said. \"The first night. You could have. You had it right there behind the door, I saw the end of it. And you opened the door a hand's width instead, and took a stranger's letter, and kept it.\" He shrugged. \"Nobody's ever done that. Usually they hit me.\"\n\nShe took the letter. The seal was still pressed down where she had warmed it with the knife. He had not opened it again.\n\n\"The auditor's at the Anchor,\" she said. \"On Rope Walk.\"\n\n\"I know where the Anchor is.\"\n\n\"Will you come with me?\"\n\nCorin Ashe was quiet for a long moment, the long clever face in the lamplight, the scar through his eyebrow.\n\n\"I'm a smuggler,\" he said. \"And a liar. Nobody will believe a word I say.\"\n\n\"They'll believe the sticks,\" said Ilse. \"Aurek has the sticks. And they'll believe the letter. And they'll believe me, because I'm going to tell them I lied, and nobody says that unless it's true.\" She put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, and reached for her coat. \"You only have to stand there.\"","wordCount":628,"createdAt":1794213937749,"updatedAt":1794230232064},{"id":"NVyBIK8aw7sOcfT43X-IL","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"r9SBgyYpFJBk2b1o5XkXt","text":"They brought Tomas out ahead of the flood, the two of them, Ilse under one arm and Wren under the other, and it took four hours, and Ilse did not afterwards remember much of it except the cold and the weight and her brother's breath hissing in her ear at every step. Wren found them the hard every time.\n\nAt the edge of the marsh, where the path came up onto the sea-wall by the Pans, they left Tomas with a pan-man Wren knew, who asked no questions and sent his own son running for a bone-setter, and then Ilse walked home alone through the waking streets with the sun coming up behind her.\n\nThe Magister had said her name. She kept hearing it. Not _the clerk_, not _one of my girls_. _The Varga girl_, in that tired voice she had loved for four years, like a man putting down something heavy.\n\nShe climbed the stair through the middle of the cooper's house. The step that creaked, creaked. The cooper's wife was not singing.\n\nHer door was open.\n\nNot broken. Open, the way you leave a door when you have a key and are in a hurry. Inside, the room looked almost as she had left it — the table, the chair, the lamp-hook — except that everything had been moved a little and put back a little wrong, the way a stranger puts things back. The chair faced the wrong wall. Her mother's comb was on the left of the shelf instead of the right.\n\nShe went back down the stair. The cooper's wife was in the kitchen with the baby on her hip and the door half shut, and when Ilse knocked on it she opened it no wider.\n\n\"Was anybody up?\" Ilse said. \"In the night. To my room.\"\n\n\"I was asleep.\" The cooper's wife did not look at her. She looked at the baby, who was chewing on a wooden spoon. \"We were all asleep. The cooper's been at the Pans all week, you know that. There's nobody here but me.\"\n\n\"My door was open.\"\n\n\"Then you left it open. You were out all night, weren't you? Out in the marsh, by the look of your boots.\" The woman's eyes went to the boots and came away quickly. \"I don't ask where my lodgers go, Miss Varga. I'd thank you not to ask me where I sleep.\"\n\n\"Somebody had a key.\"\n\n\"Then somebody had a key.\" The cooper's wife began to close the door. \"There's three keys to that room and one of them's been lost since Midwinter. Ask the cooper. When he's home.\"\n\nThe door shut. Behind it the baby began to cry, and the woman began to sing to it, the same three lines over and over, too loud.\n\nIlse stood in the passage for a while. Then she went back up.\n\nHer room was cold. Someone had lit her lamp and let it burn down to nothing and the wick had charred, and there was a smell in the air that was not hers — wet wool, and tar, and something sharper, like cloves.\n\nShe stood in the middle of the floor with her hands at her sides and tried to think the way her mother would have thought. Who had known she was going into the marsh. Who had known she would not be home. Wren had known. Mother Gannet had known. Anyone who had seen her go down Tallow Street at dusk in her mother's boots had known, and half the lower town had.\n\nIt did not narrow anything. It only made the room feel larger, and full of people.\n\nThe bolster lay on the bed with its seam cut open from end to end.\n\nShe did not need to look. She looked anyway. She put her hand into the hollow and felt the feathers and the ticking and nothing else, and then she sat down on the bed with the feathers settling around her like snow, and for the first time since the Kestrel's sticks she let herself cry.\n\nShe had not told anyone where she kept it. Not Tomas. Not Wren. Not the auditor, who she had lied to.\n\nShe had told no one, and someone had known.","wordCount":709,"createdAt":1794213918801,"updatedAt":1794217053561},{"id":"Usw4wE1YbL4pbi8KHJD9t","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"Jwi40uq_PG4-AT77dhdd6","text":"The auditor came that same afternoon, two days early.\n\nIlse heard her before she saw her — a carriage on the cobbles of Tallow Street, which was a thing that did not happen; nobody drove a carriage into the lower town because the streets were too narrow and the carters too rude. Then voices in the yard, and the porter's voice going up high the way it did when he was frightened, and then a long silence, and then feet on the stair.\n\nLisbet Ormund was not what Ilse had expected. She had pictured someone like the Magister, tall and grey and sorrowful, or someone like the Reeve, red-faced and loud. The woman who came into the third-floor room was small and round and perhaps fifty, in a brown travelling coat with the mud of the coast road still on its hem, and she had a face like a kindly hen. She stood in the doorway and took in the shelves of ledgers, all forty years of them, with an expression of pure delight.\n\n\"Oh,\" she said. \"Oh, look at that. You keep them up here? Under the roof?\"\n\n\"It's the driest room, madam,\" said Aurek, who had got to his feet so fast his stool had gone over.\n\n\"It's the hottest room in summer,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Paper doesn't like heat any more than it likes damp. Never mind. Never mind.\" She came in and took off her gloves. \"I'm the Exchequer's auditor. I expect you've been told I'd be along. I expect you were told the twentieth.\" She smiled at both of them. \"I find it saves a great deal of everyone's time to come on the eighteenth.\"\n\nIlse found that she had stood up too, and that her hand had gone flat against the front of her bodice, where the letter was. She took it away.\n\n\"Magister Fenn —\" Aurek began.\n\n\"Is downstairs, and has given me the run of the place, and very gracious he was about it. He's sending up tea. You'll be Master Stiles. And you are —?\"\n\n\"Ilse Varga, madam. Tallyclerk.\"\n\n\"Varga.\" The auditor considered her a moment longer than was comfortable. \"There was a Varga at the Hesk customs house, years ago. A woman. Very good with figures.\"\n\n\"My mother, madam.\"\n\n\"Was she? Well.\" Lisbet Ormund turned back to the shelves. \"Then you'll know the difference between a book that's right and a book that's tidy, Miss Varga. Most people don't.\" She ran a finger along the spines, the newest first. \"I'll want this year's, and last year's, and — let me see — the year of the big tide. When was that? Three years back?\"\n\n\"Four, madam,\" said Aurek.\n\n\"Three,\" said Ilse, at the same moment.\n\nIlse and Aurek looked at each other. The auditor regarded them both, pleasantly.\n\n\"The spring tide that took the Pans,\" Ilse said. \"It was three years in Thawmonth. I'd been here a year.\"\n\n\"Then I'll have that one,\" said Lisbet Ormund, \"and the one before it, so I can see the difference. And I'll have the tally-sticks for whatever's come in this month. All of them.\" She sat down at the end of the long table, in the place where nobody ever sat because the draught came under the door there, and folded her small hands. \"I'm told sticks go missing,\" she said. \"I'm told it happens all the time.\"\n\nNobody said anything. Down in the yard the porter was shouting for someone to bring up the tea.","wordCount":583,"createdAt":1794213785710,"updatedAt":1794217037110},{"id":"_cWgE1WXNcxTSXXa2eZ2N","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"LQxthMqMx2ix2u3pYQu7A","text":"The hearing was called for the hour after noon on the day of the spring tide, in the long hall at the top of the town where the Reeve of Varrow had sat in judgement on fishing disputes and drunk sailors for as long as there had been a Reeve. Lisbet Ormund had called it. She had the right; she had shown the clerk of the Hall a paper with the Exchequer's seal on it, and the clerk had gone white and sent for the Reeve, and the Reeve had come, and smiled, and said that of course the auditor must have whatever she needed.\n\nHe sat now in his own chair at the head of the hall, in the harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs. The Magister sat below him on the bench where witnesses sat, very straight, his long hands on his knees. Half the lower town had come up the hill to stand at the back. Ilse could see Mother Gannet's milky eye in the crowd, and Wren's dirty face beside her, and a great many people she had passed on the Gull Stair every day of her life and never spoken to. The old people on the quay had been saying since dawn that there was weather coming in with the tide.\n\nThe auditor stood at a small table in the middle of the floor with her papers in front of her.\n\n\"I'll be brief,\" she said, \"because the tide turns at four and I'm told half of you have boats to see to. I have audited the books of the Salt House of Varrow for the last four years. I find that for the last three of them, one-seventh part of the salt shipped from the Pans has not reached Hesk, and that the books have been written to hide it. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. Miss Varga.\"\n\nIlse stood up.\n\nShe had thought her legs would shake. They did not. She walked out to the table in the middle of the floor in her clerk's grey with her hair pinned and laid the things down on it one by one, the way she would have laid out a ship's sticks to be counted.\n\nThe Kestrel's book, open at the ninth.\n\nThirty-four tally-sticks, which Aurek Stiles had brought up the hill in his coat.\n\nA letter with a red seal pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\n\"On the ninth,\" she said, \"the Kestrel came in through the Gullet with three hundred and forty measures of salt on her sticks. I wrote four hundred in her book, because I was told to. That was a lie, and it was mine.\" She heard the crowd at the back shift and murmur. She did not look at them. \"Two days after I wrote it, the auditor asked me if I'd ever been told to write a number I knew was wrong, and I said no. That was a lie too.\"\n\n\"Miss Varga,\" said the Reeve, pleasantly, from his chair. \"You'll understand that a clerk who admits to two lies in one breath is not —\"\n\n\"And this,\" said Ilse, \"is a letter under your seal, Reeve, to the Magister, telling him where the sixty measures from the Kestrel were to go, and when, and on what ship. It never reached him. A man called Corin Ashe took it out of your post-bag, and gave it to me, and I've kept it.\" She looked up at him. \"You can read it, if you like. Or the auditor can read it aloud. It's quite short.\"\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\nHalvard Seyle looked at the letter on the table, and at the auditor, and at the Magister on the witnesses' bench, who had not moved. Then he looked past them all to the back of the hall, where Corin Ashe was standing against the wall with his arms folded, in a clean coat, and smiled.\n\n\"A smuggler,\" the Reeve said. \"A smuggler steals a letter, and a clerk who lies admits she lies, and an old man —\"\n\n\"I'll speak to the letter,\" said Oduya Fenn.\n\nHe stood up from the bench. He was not looking at the Reeve; he was looking at Ilse, and his face was quite calm.\n\n\"I never received it,\" he said. \"But I know what it says, because I know what every letter Halvard Seyle has written me for three years has said. I'll tell the auditor about every one of them. I'll tell her about the wall. I have kept a book of my own, Madam Auditor — a true one. I kept it because I am a clerk, and I could not bear not to. It is in my room at the Salt House, behind the loose firebrick in the chimney-breast, where the stove's heat never reaches, and it has every measure in it that I ever wrote false.\"\n\nSomebody in the crowd said, loudly, \"Gods,\" and somebody else laughed, high and shocked.\n\nThe Reeve was on his feet.\n\n\"This hearing is closed,\" he said. \"I am the Reeve of Varrow and this is my hall, and I'll not have —\"\n\n\"It isn't closed, Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund mildly. \"It's the Exchequer's hearing. It's only being held in your hall.\" She picked up the letter. \"Sit down, please. I'm going to read this aloud.\"\n\nHe did not sit down. Something went out of his big red face all at once, like air out of a bladder. Then he turned and walked out through the door behind his chair, the one that only the Reeve used.\n\n\"Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund sharply, and then, to the clerk of the Hall: \"Stop that man.\" The clerk of the Hall looked from the Reeve's back to the auditor and did not move. Nobody in Varrow had ever stopped the Reeve doing anything.","wordCount":984,"createdAt":1794213959655,"updatedAt":1794230097839},{"id":"cIVKajXy41E1kBfi48ORI","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"dAT2R-4HoTYOS7lortcKb","text":"Tomas did not come by the day after the storm, as he had said he would.\n\nThe next morning Ilse went down the Gull Stair before the Salt House bell and found Mother Gannet already at her place at the foot of it, gutting eels into a bucket with a short black knife. The old woman had sold eels at the bottom of the stair for as long as there had been a stair, by her own account, and possibly longer. She had one eye milky and one eye sharp, and it was generally understood in the lower town that the milky one saw more.\n\n\"He's not back,\" Mother Gannet said, before Ilse had opened her mouth. \"The Patience isn't on her mooring. I've had the boy up and down the quay since first light asking.\"\n\n\"Which boy?\"\n\n\"Mine.\" The old woman jerked her chin. Sitting on the bottom step, eating a cold potato with great concentration, was a child of nine or ten in a man's coat cut down at the sleeves. He had a face so dirty it was hard to say whether he was a boy at all. \"Wren. He runs for me. He's not mine, but he runs for me.\"\n\n\"Nobody's seen her,\" Wren said, through potato. \"Not in the harbour. Not at the Pans. Tad Mossop says he saw a sail in the channels last night past the Heron Creek, but Tad Mossop says a lot of things.\"\n\nIlse sat down on the step beside him because her legs had decided it for her.\n\n\"He's a pilot,\" Mother Gannet said, not unkindly. \"He knows every inch of that marsh. If he's out there he's out there because he means to be.\" She dropped an eel into the bucket. \"Question is who he's out there with.\"\n\n\"Corin Ashe.\"\n\n\"Ah.\" The old woman's sharp eye went to her. \"Then the question's what he's out there for.\"\n\nIlse looked at the bucket of eels, at the grey light on the mud, at the salt-ships still lying over on their sides from the ebb. She thought about the letter against her skin and about the auditor at the end of the long table in the draught, asking for the year of the big tide.\n\n\"Mother,\" she said. \"You remember the tide that took the Pans.\"\n\n\"I remember every tide since the old king died.\"\n\n\"What happened after? To the Pans?\"\n\nMother Gannet wiped the black knife on her apron and was quiet for long enough that Wren stopped chewing.\n\n\"Sea came over the wall at the spring tide in Thawmonth,\" she said at last. \"Took the wall, took the pans behind it, took the pan-reeve's house and the pan-reeve's wife with it. Half the salt-works in Varrow, gone under in one night. The Salt House wrote to the capital for money to build it back, and the capital wrote back and said it was a matter for the harbour.\" She spat, neatly, into the mud. \"So it was the Reeve's to mend, and the Reeve said there was no money for it, and for a whole year the pan-men sat on the quay with their hands in their laps. And then one day there was money. And the wall went up, and the pans went back, and everyone said what a fine man the Reeve was.\"\n\n\"Where did the money come from?\"\n\n\"Nobody asked. That's the thing about money that comes when you need it. Nobody asks.\" The old woman picked up another eel. \"Your mother would've asked.\"\n\n\"My mother's dead.\"\n\n\"So she is. And so's the pan-reeve's wife, and the Salt House is still standing, and the Reeve's still got his fine house on the hill, and his fine new wall, which, I'll tell you for nothing, is built of the worst stone I ever saw and won't see out another ten years of tides.\" The knife went in. \"You asked.\"\n\nIlse stood up. \"If Tomas comes back —\"\n\n\"The boy'll find you.\" Mother Gannet did not look up. \"Go to your counting, girl. Count something.\"","wordCount":678,"createdAt":1794213829104,"updatedAt":1794230091419},{"id":"e2mMdH9PXVbEF1dEavL_G","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"g2q6xv5SNSaq9VyyZ2uux","text":"Her room was over the cooper's, at the top of a house so narrow that the stair went up through the middle of it like the stone in a plum. She lit the lamp and put the sticks on the table and sat with them for a while, and then she put them inside the hollow of the bolster, which was the only hiding place she had, and felt foolish.\n\nThe rain beat on the slates. Somewhere below her the cooper's wife was singing to the baby, the same three lines over and over.\n\nIt was close to midnight when someone knocked.\n\nShe did not open the door. She stood behind it with the lamp in one hand and the poker in the other and said, \"Who is it?\"\n\n\"A friend of your brother's,\" said a man's voice. It was light and pleasant and not from Varrow — there was something of the capital in the vowels, or something trying to sound like it. \"Though I doubt he'd put it that way. Please don't hit me with whatever you're holding.\"\n\n\"What do you want?\"\n\n\"To give you something. You can open the door a hand's width and I'll put it through, and then I'll go away, and you'll never have to look at me.\" A pause. \"Though I'm told I'm not bad to look at.\"\n\nShe opened the door a hand's width. The man on the landing was perhaps thirty, soaked through, his dark hair plastered to his skull. He had a long, clever face and a scar through one eyebrow and he was smiling as if this were all a great joke that she would be let in on presently.\n\nHe held out a packet wrapped in oilcloth.\n\n\"What is it?\"\n\n\"A letter. Not to you. I think you'd better have it anyway.\" He kept holding it out. \"My name's Corin Ashe. Your brother knows me. He'll tell you I'm a liar and a smuggler, and he'll be right about the second one.\"\n\n\"Why would you give me a letter that isn't to me?\"\n\n\"Because I can read, Miss Varga, and having read it I'd rather it were in the hands of someone who counts sticks than in mine.\" He pushed the packet through the gap. She took it, mostly to stop him dropping it. \"There's a seal on it you'll recognise. Don't break it here. Don't break it at all, if you've any sense. Just — keep it somewhere.\"\n\n\"How do you know about the sticks?\"\n\n\"Your brother came to find me, an hour after the rain started.\" He was already turning away. \"He's a better brother than he lets on. Bolt your door.\" She heard him going down the stair, light and quick, missing the step that creaked as if he knew it was there. The street door opened and shut.\n\nShe closed her own door and bolted it and set the lamp down and turned the packet over in her hands. Under the oilcloth was good paper, folded small, and a seal of red wax pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\nIt was the seal of the Harbour Reeve.\n\nIlse sat on the bed with the letter in her lap and the sticks in the bolster under her and listened to the rain until the lamp burned down.","wordCount":553,"createdAt":1794213586005,"updatedAt":1794217030963},{"id":"mj6iwx7iLYJHc79ukJbEt","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"6iI4LgbwDeFqR_Cruf7mE","text":"She broke the seal in the morning, of course. Corin Ashe had known she would; that was why he had told her not to.\n\nThe storm had blown itself out in the night and left the sky scoured and pale. She sat at her table with a heel of yesterday's bread and slid the blade of her knife under the wax, carefully, so that it lifted in one piece and she could have pressed it back if she had been a different sort of person. Inside was a single sheet, folded twice, in a secretary's hand. At the bottom, two initials she had seen on a hundred harbour warrants, and the seal under them. H.S. — Halvard Seyle, Reeve of Varrow.\n\nThe letter was to Fenn.\n\n_Magister —_\n\n_The sixty from the Kestrel will go with the rest. The Black Mare lies off the Chapel on the night of the eighteenth and will take the first of it then, and the rest on the spring tide; there are no other nights. Ormund leaves the capital on the twelfth and will be with you within the week, so see that the books are clean before she comes. I need not tell you what a clean book means. The levy is paid in full this year, as every year. You have my word on the Pans._\n\n_— H.S._\n\nShe read it four times. The fourth time she read it aloud, quietly, to hear whether it said something else when it was spoken. It did not.\n\n_The sixty from the Kestrel._ So it was not six sticks dropped in the harbour. _With the rest._ So it was not only the Kestrel. She thought of all the books on the third floor, the forty years of them on their shelves under the eaves, and of how many times she had written a number into a column without counting the sticks, because the sticks had been counted by somebody else, because the Magister read the margins.\n\n_You have my word on the Pans._ That one she did not understand at all.\n\nShe folded the letter again, and then she did a thing she was not proud of: she warmed the knife over the lamp and pressed the wax back down, so that from a little way off it looked whole. Then she put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, where it crackled when she breathed, and she went to work.\n\nAurek Stiles was already at the long table. When she came in he said, \"You look like a woman who's slept on a bag of nails,\" and she said, \"I found the sticks,\" and he said, \"Behind the stove?\" and she said, \"Behind the stove,\" and took them out of her satchel and put them in the bin, all thirty-four.\n\nIt was the first lie she had told at the Salt House. It went down easier than she had expected, and that frightened her more than the letter did.\n\nAt midday she went down to the Magister's room on an errand about the Heron's book and stood in his doorway while he signed it. His hand did not shake. He asked after Tomas, and whether the Patience had come through the storm, and he told her she was the best clerk he had had in thirty years, which he had told her before. She watched him sign and thought: _see that the books are clean._\n\nShe had always believed that she could tell when someone was lying to her. She went back up the stairs knowing that she had been wrong about that, and did not know for how long.","wordCount":607,"createdAt":1794213785792,"updatedAt":1794230089009},{"id":"nbNVKCOb83T4NmhuilNvk","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"GIXhkOMgrF_FKcO5VyMzd","text":"The auditor left Varrow on the twelfth day of Seedmonth, in the same carriage that had come down Tallow Street and frightened the porter, and the whole of Tallow Street came out to see her go.\n\nShe had stayed three weeks longer than she meant to. She had sat in the Magister's room with the firebrick out of the chimney-breast and the Magister's true book open on the desk — a small black book, black with soot at the corners, with three years of real numbers in it in a long thin careful hand — and she had copied every page. Then she had sat in the third-floor room with Aurek and Ilse for ten days more and put the real numbers into the real books, page by page, in the margins, in red.\n\n\"The Exchequer will want the Magister in the capital,\" she told Ilse, the last evening, standing at the door of the Salt House with her gloves in her hand. \"There'll be a trial. I'll speak for him. I'll say he kept a true book when he could have kept none, and gave it up when he could have burned it, and that's not nothing.\" She pulled on one glove. \"I don't know what they'll do. Something less than they might have, I hope.\"\n\n\"And the Salt House?\"\n\n\"Will need a Magister.\" Lisbet Ormund pulled on the other glove. \"I've written to say so. I've written to say who.\" She looked up at Ilse with her kindly hen's face. \"Master Stiles has been here twenty years and is a very good clerk, and he told me himself that he would rather go into the sea after the Reeve than have the stove. So I've written your name.\"\n\nIlse opened her mouth and shut it again.\n\n\"You'll say you're too young,\" said the auditor. \"And that you lied to me. You did. You also told me you'd lied, in front of the whole town, which I have never once in thirty years of this work seen anyone do. Your mother would have.\" She held out her small gloved hand, and Ilse took it. \"Count everything, Miss Varga. Write in the margins. Good night.\"\n\nThe carriage went down Tallow Street in the morning with the carters cursing it, and Ilse watched it from the third-floor window until it was gone.\n\nTomas came down the Gull Stair that afternoon on two crutches, which took him nearly an hour and which he would not let anyone help him with, and sat on the bench outside the pilots' shed at the bottom with his splinted leg stuck out in front of him and his unlit pipe in his teeth. Corin Ashe sat beside him. Neither of them said much. The men the Reeve had paid had gone, most of them, on the first ship out after his burying; the two who had not drank at the Anchor now, and studied the floor when Corin came in. Corin had a pilot's oilskin on, which was too big for him, and a pilot's book in his pocket, which Tomas had made him buy, and he was learning the Gullet from the bench, channel by channel, with Tomas drawing it in the dust with the end of a crutch.\n\n\"He'll make a pilot,\" Tomas said to Ilse, when she came down to them at the end of the day. \"Gods know how. He gets everything wrong and then argues with the sea about it.\"\n\n\"I'm very persuasive,\" said Corin.\n\n\"The sea isn't.\"\n\nWren was sitting on the bottom step of the Gull Stair eating a cold potato. Mother Gannet was gutting eels. The salt-ships lay over in the mud on the ebb, and the evening light came down the harbour the colour of honey, and somewhere up on the hill a bell was ringing for the end of the day.\n\nIlse climbed back up to the Salt House as the light went. The third floor was empty; Aurek had gone home. She lit the lamp and sat down at the long table, at her own place, and opened the Kestrel's book at the ninth.\n\nFour hundred, in her own clean hand. And beside it, in the margin, in red, in the auditor's tiny writing: _340. Sticks counted by I.V. L.O._\n\nShe dipped her pen, and turned to a clean page, and began to count.","wordCount":729,"createdAt":1794213959510,"updatedAt":1794230238265},{"id":"qjE8HIXuXQiStxROVNd-7","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"3z3uj6msTp-yJfszkLU-n","text":"She went to work. She did not know what else to do with herself, and it seemed important, in a way she could not have explained, that she should be at her place at the long table when the bell went, in her clerk's grey, with her hair pinned, as if nothing had happened. As if she were still the best clerk the Magister had had in thirty years.\n\nAurek glanced up when she came in and opened his mouth to say something about bags of nails, and then saw her face and shut it again.\n\nThe auditor was not there. Her sheet of tiny figures lay on the end of the table under a glass paperweight, and the green ledger was gone from beside it.\n\n\"She's downstairs,\" Aurek said quietly. \"With him. Since first bell.\" He hesitated. \"Ilse. I took the sticks out of the bin.\"\n\nShe turned.\n\n\"The Kestrel's. The day you put them in. I'm not a fool, girl, whatever you think. Nobody finds six sticks behind a stove and then finds them gone again.\" He did not look at her; he looked at his own ledger, at his own tidy upright hand. \"I've been here twenty years. I've written what I was told for three of them. I didn't count. I didn't ask. I told myself the Magister read the margins.\" He turned a page that did not need turning. \"They're in my coat. In the press. If you want them.\"\n\nShe stood very still.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\n\"Because you didn't write anything in the margin,\" said Aurek Stiles. \"You always write in the margin. You've written in the margin every day for four years. And that day you didn't, and I thought: she's frightened. And I thought: I've been frightened for three years and I never once noticed it was the same thing.\"\n\nAt midday the auditor came up the stair and took her sheet of figures from under the paperweight and went down again without a word to either of them. A little after that, the porter came up to say that the Magister wanted Miss Varga in his room.\n\nThe stove was lit. It was always lit. Oduya Fenn sat behind his desk with the green ledger closed in front of him and his long hands folded on top of it, and he did not smile when she came in. That was how she knew he had not slept either.\n\n\"Sit down, Ilse.\"\n\nShe did not sit down. She had decided on the stair that she would tell him, all of it. If he was going to give her name to the Reeve a second time, she wanted to watch him do it.\n\n\"I was at the Chapel last night,\" she said. \"In the tower. With Tomas, who has a broken leg, and has been lying in a window since the night before last while you loaded salt underneath him.\"\n\nThe Magister closed his eyes.\n\n\"I heard you tell the Reeve my name.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"I imagine you did.\" He opened his eyes again and looked at her, and his face was older than she had ever seen it. \"Will you let me tell you how it happened? Not to excuse it. I don't think it can be excused. But I'd like someone to know, and I think you've earned it more than anyone.\"\n\nShe sat down.\n\nHe told her. What Mother Gannet had told her, and what Mother Gannet could not have known: the price of salt going up at Hesk, and the Salt House half empty, and the Exchequer writing every quarter to ask why the revenue was down. And then Halvard Seyle in this room, in that chair, with a bottle of something good, saying: _I can build you a wall, Oduya. All it needs is a little salt that nobody counts._\n\n\"One part in seven,\" the Magister said. \"For one year. To pay for the wall. That was all it was going to be. I told myself it was the Exchequer's own salt paying for the Exchequer's own wall, and if the Exchequer had done its duty there'd have been no need. I told myself a great many things.\" He looked down at his hands. \"The wall was built in a year. And at the end of the year I went to Halvard and said, that's enough, and he said — he said, Oduya, you've been writing false books for a year. Do you want to explain that to the Exchequer, or shall I? And then he smiled, and gave me his word on the Pans. He has written it at the bottom of every letter since. It means the wall stands as long as I keep writing.\"\n\n\"So you kept writing.\"\n\n\"So I kept writing. And the wall is bad stone, and Halvard has a new house, and the Black Mare comes in on the spring tides and takes one-seventh of the salt of Varrow to wherever it goes.\" He lifted his hands, let them fall. \"And you counted the Kestrel's sticks. I knew you would, one day. You're the only one of them who ever counted.\"\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Did you take it?\"\n\nHis face did not change. \"What letter?\"\n\n\"The Reeve's letter to you. About the Kestrel. I had it.\"\n\nFor a moment the Magister of the Salt House looked at her with an expression she could not read at all, and then, slowly, he began to laugh. It was not a happy sound.\n\n\"He never sent it,\" he said. \"I never had it. I wondered — I wondered why he was so certain I'd been told about the Black Mare taking a first load on the eighteenth, when I hadn't. He said, _I wrote to you._ And I said, _you didn't._ And we each thought the other was lying.\" He wiped his eyes. \"Oh, Ilse. Somebody stole the Reeve's post. And now somebody has stolen it from you.\"","wordCount":995,"createdAt":1794213937949,"updatedAt":1794230093903},{"id":"qyCgyTw499A7cpR62_YCE","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"JLebbu3pA-RXZKvs35ANa","text":"Lisbet Ormund had taken the best room at the Anchor, which was not saying a great deal, and had spent the first evening moving the furniture so that the table caught the light from the window and the bed did not catch the draught from the door. She was at the table now, with her shoes off and her stockinged feet on a hot brick wrapped in flannel, writing her report.\n\nShe had written it three times. Each time it said the same thing — _one part in seven, three years, the Salt House books written to conceal it_ — and each time it ended in the same place, which was _I have no proof of who._\n\nWhen the landlord knocked to say the Reeve was below and wished to call on her, she said, \"Yes, of course, send him up,\" and put her shoes back on, and turned her report face down on the table, and put the hot brick under the bed.\n\nHalvard Seyle filled the doorway. He was a big man and knew it, and he had come in his harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs, and he bowed to her with great courtesy and said he hoped she was comfortable, and that Varrow was not too rough for a lady from the capital, and that if there was anything at all she needed she had only to send to the Reeve's Hall.\n\n\"How kind,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Do sit down.\"\n\nHe sat. The chair complained.\n\n\"I hear you've been at the Salt House books,\" he said.\n\n\"I have.\"\n\n\"Poor Fenn. He's not the man he was. The flood hit him very hard — he took it personally, you know, as though the sea had done it to spite him. I've had to carry a good deal of the work since.\" He smiled. \"If you've found anything amiss, I'd hope you'd come to me first. Before the capital. It would be a shame to ruin an old man's name over a few clerical errors that the harbour could put right quietly.\" He paused, as if a thing had only just occurred to him. \"I'd wager there's a clerk of his, a Varga girl, who has something of mine that came out of my post. I'd hate her to come to harm over a piece of paper. Paper's cheap. Girls who can count are not.\"\n\n\"A few clerical errors,\" the auditor repeated.\n\n\"Sticks go missing,\" said the Reeve. \"It happens all the time.\"\n\nLisbet Ormund regarded him for a long moment. Then she turned her report over on the table and pushed it across to him.\n\n\"One part in seven,\" she said. \"Every month for three years. That is not a few clerical errors, Reeve. That is a hundred and forty thousand crowns of the Exchequer's revenue, near enough, and it is going somewhere, and it is not going to Hesk.\" She folded her hands. \"I'll tell you what I have, since you've been good enough to call. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. I have a tallyclerk who lied to my face and was frightened to death doing it, and a Magister who hasn't slept in two nights. What I don't have is a name.\" She smiled at him. \"I don't suppose you'd care to give me one?\"\n\nThe Reeve did not smile back.\n\n\"You've been in Varrow three days, madam,\" he said. \"You don't know the tides. People who don't know the tides get into trouble out on the marsh. Every year we lose one or two. Visitors, mostly.\" He stood up. \"I'd be careful.\"\n\n\"I always am,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Good night, Reeve.\"\n\nWhen he had gone she sat for a while without moving. Then she got the hot brick back out from under the bed — it had gone lukewarm — and put her feet on it, and turned her report face down again, and wrote at the bottom of a fresh sheet, in her tiny neat hand: _Seyle. Certain. No proof._","wordCount":675,"createdAt":1794213937856,"updatedAt":1794230229982}],"plotThreads":[],"motifs":[],"proseNotes":[{"id":"-2Q9xrEfjH8hm7A9UlOR6","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"6iI4LgbwDeFqR_Cruf7mE","quote":"if she had been a different sort of person","prefix":" she could have pressed it back ","suffix":". Inside was a single sheet, fol","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"And a page later she is that sort of person. Nicely done.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794216341194,"updatedAt":1794217261435,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794217261435},{"id":"-smbJehV__npMut549dA1","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"QkH1pzVCb9awQuyhPF6LA","quote":"one he thought he knew","prefix":"e her then,\" and another voice, ","suffix":", said, \"And the other one?\" and","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Whose? If it's Fenn, Tomas should tell Ilse in the tower, before she sees Fenn for herself. As it stands it's a dropped thread.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"xh1OpGS_KqhM93w3WQAD6","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Yes, Fenn. Tomas now says in the tower that he thought he knew a voice in the longboat and didn't want to. Ilse hears Fenn a page later.","createdAt":1794217262417}],"createdAt":1794216341219,"updatedAt":1794217262935,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217262935},{"id":"0QJjlUczuIz3n8kY2WrIy","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"DS5E6n6CNj-_T8xgA9nT-","quote":"I'll come by tomorrow.","prefix":" Patience before this comes in. ","suffix":"\"\n\n\"Tomas —\"\n\n\"Tomorrow.\" He put","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"The first of the book's \"tomorrows\". He doesn't come, and I've tried to pin down which day he goes out with Corin instead. See the letter, under \"The clock\".","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"k8M9OneExB2JgcHPofGin","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Clock fixed as you suggested: hearing on Day 5. Tomas means Day 2, and goes out with Corin that night instead.","createdAt":1794217263883}],"createdAt":1794216341187,"updatedAt":1794217264401,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217264401},{"id":"0ZaAqMFnUOXQJlplfPWkz","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"QkH1pzVCb9awQuyhPF6LA","quote":"because I'm the one they've been paying","prefix":"ant me more than they want you, ","suffix":". So when I go over, you keep go","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"This is a big reveal to throw away in a subordinate clause: Corin has been on the Reeve's payroll? If so, it changes how we read his taking the letter, and nobody picks it up again, not Ilse, not the hearing. Either make something of it or cut it.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"wq0aIJ_XaVZnIPxIBA0SJ","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Built in: Corin carries the Reeve's post (he says so to Tomas in ch. 2), which is how he came to be holding the letter.","createdAt":1794217265333}],"createdAt":1794216341218,"updatedAt":1794217265851,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217265851},{"id":"33AVTqH_CenzJF5vFoCzT","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"Jwi40uq_PG4-AT77dhdd6","quote":"It was three years in Thawmonth. I'd been here a year.","prefix":"hat took the Pans,\" Ilse said. \"","suffix":"\"\n\n\"Then I'll have that one,\" sa","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"We never learn what month it is now. Since the coda is dated (Seedmonth), I'd tell the reader early, here or in ch. 1, so the dates have something to count from.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"aLNzIoO1w9cOBPZ03TuI3","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"It's Brinemonth. Ch. 1 now opens the Kestrel scene on the seventeenth, so the auditor's eighteenth has something to count from.","createdAt":1794217266782}],"createdAt":1794216341204,"updatedAt":1794217267302,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217267302},{"id":"7iplrTwefFD7-Rcgh5GU9","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"3z3uj6msTp-yJfszkLU-n","quote":"I've been frightened for three years and I never once noticed it was the same thing.","prefix":"he's frightened. And I thought: ","suffix":"\"\n\nAt midday the auditor came up","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"One of the best things in the book.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794216341228,"updatedAt":1794217267867,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794217267867},{"id":"AkI04BOmfoVzXFZZuK1qE","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"6iI4LgbwDeFqR_Cruf7mE","quote":"Ormund leaves the capital on the twelfth and will be with you within the week","prefix":" then; there is no other night. ","suffix":", so see that the books are clea","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"This is the one fixed date in the book, so I'd hang the clock on it. She leaves the capital on the 12th, is expected on the 20th, arrives on the 18th. Which day is Ilse reading this: the 17th or the 18th? Everything else follows from that.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"5UVugaK-L152zMkZLGDgd","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Hung the clock on it. She arrives the afternoon of the eighteenth, Day 2.","createdAt":1794217268799}],"createdAt":1794216341198,"updatedAt":1794217269320,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217269320},{"id":"B5cFShi8QHW0kZfvoOQlL","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"g2q6xv5SNSaq9VyyZ2uux","quote":"\"Your brother came to find me, an hour after the rain started.\"","prefix":"do you know about the sticks?\"\n\n","suffix":" He was already turning away. \"H","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"This answers the question, and it tells us something about Tomas before he does anything. Good.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794230018747,"updatedAt":1794230282180,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794230282180},{"id":"C_-gcoO0TQIfeQ4_m0Nuc","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"2i5J2Lc7W9L8liiqtAFCG","quote":"the two tallyclerks","prefix":"the table. He was the senior of ","suffix":" and had been for twenty years, ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Three? We only ever meet two, Aurek and Ilse. Either give the third a name and a desk (and somewhere to be on the day the auditor arrives), or make it two.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"OcX1Mb1Z3rEP2nuiVt4Hi","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Made it two.","createdAt":1794217270273}],"createdAt":1794216341184,"updatedAt":1794217367198,"version":4,"resolvedAt":1794217367198},{"id":"CsKgCEJSjgjyNxwG2SNBY","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"kACrUDbXsjGSKBXJOWAnj","quote":"he said.\n\nShe had known since the Magister laughed.","prefix":" them.\n\n\"I'm sorry about that,\" ","suffix":" If the Reeve had never sent the","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"How does the laugh tell her? It tells her the Reeve never got the letter to Fenn, which she knew. I think what she has realised is that only Corin knew the letter existed; say so, and it's earned.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"uaoTH3pXNk7ywKKjt6vER","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Rewritten: she reasons that only one man knew the letter existed.","createdAt":1794217314625}],"createdAt":1794216341234,"updatedAt":1794217315132,"version":4,"resolvedAt":1794217315132},{"id":"FKTZPmKZtyG3WXoM4O5_A","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"W6kDqSJm2O5zS3kC2H7Of","quote":"Ilse found that she was holding the wet rail of the stair with both hands.","prefix":"ur she was gone into the rain.\n\n","suffix":" She had won. She had stood in t","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"You asked whether this gives Ilse enough without slowing the storm. Enough, yes; \"a ledger with the last page torn out\" is exactly right. To keep the storm moving I've put the Black Mare's escape before it, so the action finishes before she starts to think. One query: \"the men the Reeve had paid … had all seen her face\" is a real threat, and the coda never answers it. One line in the coda would do it.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794230018770,"updatedAt":1794230288063,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794230288063},{"id":"GeIrZTT2xr5zMW8zRKekw","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"QkH1pzVCb9awQuyhPF6LA","quote":"They had been seen.","prefix":"","suffix":" Tomas never knew how — a turn o","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"This is the scene you asked about. My view is in the letter: I'd move it to open ch. 3, straight after the longboat, so it's no longer a flashback.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"rYCbMMhAy-I_VflDulZ8U","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Moved. It opens ch. 3 now, straight after the longboat, and is no longer a flashback.","createdAt":1794217316082}],"createdAt":1794216341215,"updatedAt":1794217316599,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217316599},{"id":"HXUxsv2sXKM9vBoIWvnBU","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","quote":"Tomas Brenn","prefix":"o side like a fat man laughing. ","suffix":" had sailed her for eleven years","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Brenn? Ilse is a Varga, and they share a mother who was a Varga. Half-siblings, or a slip? If they're half-siblings, one line somewhere would cover it.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"VXYMXLUwgVWyg03jcjrSR","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"A slip. He's Tomas Varga throughout now.","createdAt":1794217317547}],"createdAt":1794216341208,"updatedAt":1794217318065,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217318065},{"id":"IjR5W6nabMc3w7LeauArU","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"GIXhkOMgrF_FKcO5VyMzd","quote":"She had stayed three weeks longer than she meant to.","prefix":"Street came out to see her go.\n\n","suffix":" She had sat in the Magister's r","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Clock: she meant to leave after the spring tide. If the hearing is on (say) the 21st and she leaves on the 12th of Seedmonth, that's about three weeks if Seedmonth follows directly, but \"a fortnight\" here plus \"ten days more\" below makes nearer twenty-four days. Pick one.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"ykJKbqfzSMimLDa6zYIZv","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Three weeks.","createdAt":1794217319006}],"createdAt":1794216341240,"updatedAt":1794217319516,"version":4,"resolvedAt":1794217319516},{"id":"J0WX0XQs0EZ25oshcdlWL","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"r9SBgyYpFJBk2b1o5XkXt","quote":"Someone had lit her lamp and let it burn down to nothing and the wick had charred","prefix":"nt back up.\n\nHer room was cold. ","suffix":", and there was a smell in the a","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"She took the lamp with her when she went down to Wren in the last scene. Either she leaves it, or this is a second lamp, or whoever came in brought their own.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"Y3Fx3jWxE8w6Fr24o6fv7","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"She blows it out before she leaves. Whoever came in lit it.","createdAt":1794217320455}],"createdAt":1794216341226,"updatedAt":1794217320965,"version":4,"resolvedAt":1794217320965},{"id":"LEE8tZ-8Bb-q7j7BZIR4v","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"kACrUDbXsjGSKBXJOWAnj","quote":"and I got there first","prefix":"ooper's wife had been paid for, ","suffix":". Because I knew where they'd lo","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"So who moved the chair and the comb, Corin or the Reeve's men after him? And if Seyle's men came and found nothing, does the Reeve now know Ilse had the letter? That matters a great deal at the hearing.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"615EWwXMWabDV28FOPyxK","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Corin. He searched before he found it; the chair and the comb are him. The Reeve's men found the room as he left it. I've left that for the reader, but tell me if it needs saying.","createdAt":1794217321914}],"createdAt":1794216341235,"updatedAt":1794217322432,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217322432},{"id":"OufmFm7xt1Sn3ajBfWcSm","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"FBthgk0AwfIy4svSwzmYI","quote":"\"The Varga girl,\" the Magister said.","prefix":"r off, a sound like breathing.\n\n","suffix":"","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Excellent chapter turn.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794216341224,"updatedAt":1794217322999,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794217322999},{"id":"RIYPTUClNGrCD7AFAuUmT","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"g2q6xv5SNSaq9VyyZ2uux","quote":"which was the only hiding place she had, and felt foolish","prefix":"side the hollow of the bolster, ","suffix":".\n\nThe rain beat on the slates. ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Good plant: it pays off with Corin's \"always the bolster or the chimney\" in ch. 5.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794216341189,"updatedAt":1794217323565,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794217323565},{"id":"T9s6OZL_BxbYvjzQexmbS","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"2i5J2Lc7W9L8liiqtAFCG","quote":"like the bones of a small animal","prefix":"ks laid out beside it in a row, ","suffix":". Every sack that went over the ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Lovely. And it's the book's whole method in one image: things that ought to match.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794216341179,"updatedAt":1794217324133,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794217324133},{"id":"TH-8-DsJSHbrbAeb9J7uj","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"FBthgk0AwfIy4svSwzmYI","quote":"I didn't want to.","prefix":"rst night. I thought I knew it. ","suffix":"\"\n\n\"Corin,\" Ilse said. \"Where's ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"\"I didn't want to.\" Lovely. And it means the reader gets there a page before Ilse does, when the tall man with the long hand comes into the nave.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794230018757,"updatedAt":1794230284330,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794230284330},{"id":"U0EDPJoOe8dO-E5fpZkVb","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"LQxthMqMx2ix2u3pYQu7A","quote":". Nobody stopped him","prefix":"at the auditor, and did not move","suffix":", because nobody in Varrow had e","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"The Exchequer's auditor, in her own hearing, lets the man she has just named walk out? I believe nobody in Varrow stops him, but I'd like Lisbet at least to try.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"G7zxdDkPGux76rZDg6io4","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Lisbet now tells the clerk of the Hall to stop him, and he doesn't move.","createdAt":1794217325075}],"createdAt":1794216341238,"updatedAt":1794217325582,"version":4,"resolvedAt":1794217325582},{"id":"U_BWEpuW1R7ZVDmgJi_RT","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"6iI4LgbwDeFqR_Cruf7mE","quote":"Halvard Seyle, Reeve of Varrow.","prefix":" on a hundred harbour warrants: ","suffix":"\n\nThe letter was to Fenn.\n\n_Magi","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"The letter we're shown is signed \"— H.S.\", not with his name. Initials or full signature? The hearing turns on whose letter this is, so it's worth deciding.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"gdQJ9lZ9YKmemPFATHKgh","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Initials, under the seal. Ilse says 'under your seal' at the hearing (accepted).","createdAt":1794217326530}],"createdAt":1794216341196,"updatedAt":1794217327064,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217327064},{"id":"V5wmmkYgowPRIK0P3Grc1","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"LQxthMqMx2ix2u3pYQu7A","quote":"\"Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund sharply","prefix":" one that only the Reeve used.\n\n","suffix":", and then, to the clerk of the ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Good. She tries, and that makes the town's not moving mean something.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794230018769,"updatedAt":1794230287530,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794230287530},{"id":"W_yRUbz4numBpz_O5Mkac","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"Jwi40uq_PG4-AT77dhdd6","quote":"The auditor came two days early.","prefix":"","suffix":"\n\nIlse heard her before she saw ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Same day as the letter, or the next? It matters, because Tomas goes out with Corin \"that night\". If it's the same day, \"That afternoon the auditor came, two days early\" or similar would tell us.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"JEVsF0X18ybeWpBJhrH70","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Same day: 'that same afternoon, two days early'.","createdAt":1794217328015}],"createdAt":1794216341201,"updatedAt":1794217328532,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217328532},{"id":"YvpnvDiJlcgnHIY-hUskj","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"GIXhkOMgrF_FKcO5VyMzd","quote":"I.V.","prefix":" writing: _340. Sticks counted. ","suffix":"_\n\nShe dipped her pen, and turne","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"\"I.V.\" are Ilse's initials, in the auditor's hand. Deliberate (she's crediting Ilse) or a slip for L.O.? If deliberate, it's lovely, but I missed it the first time, so a reader may too.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"Fa2jK5sKU1YRYsVdIq55W","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Deliberate. She's crediting Ilse. I've added her own initials after it (I.V. L.O.) so it reads as a signed note about Ilse rather than a slip.","createdAt":1794217329463}],"createdAt":1794216341241,"updatedAt":1794217329982,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217329982},{"id":"Z1UEePQ1VUBokQylpvOSU","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"Jwi40uq_PG4-AT77dhdd6","quote":"I find it saves a great deal of everyone's time to come on the eighteenth.","prefix":".\" She smiled at both of them. \"","suffix":"\"\n\nIlse found that she had stood","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"She's a delight from her first line.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794216341203,"updatedAt":1794217330548,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794217330548},{"id":"Z53xRqrEOXyG0stRwCskb","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"QkH1pzVCb9awQuyhPF6LA","quote":"They had been seen.","prefix":"","suffix":" Tomas never knew how — a turn o","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"You asked whether the chase works as the opening of ch. 3. It does. Straight on from the longboat, and everything Ilse doesn't know in the next two scenes is now ours to worry about.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794230018751,"updatedAt":1794230283266,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794230283266},{"id":"_AwatIldDIVmQG5zkzWp0","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"3z3uj6msTp-yJfszkLU-n","quote":"\"I was at the Chapel last night,\" she said.","prefix":" Ilse.\"\n\nShe did not sit down.\n\n","suffix":" \"In the tower. With Tomas, who ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"She tells one of the conspirators everything she knows, before she's told the auditor. It's the bravest thing she does, and the riskiest. I'd like a beat of her deciding to, or of her knowing what it might cost Tomas.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"heHfzg8XoePzu8N4qg3j8","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Added a beat: she decides on the stair to tell him, so she can watch him do it if he gives her name again.","createdAt":1794217331479}],"createdAt":1794216341230,"updatedAt":1794217331999,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217331999},{"id":"_yEAtVOKqZhBamU4Etz9q","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"GIXhkOMgrF_FKcO5VyMzd","quote":"with the firebrick","prefix":" had sat in the Magister's room ","suffix":" out of the chimney-breast and t","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"The stove is \"always lit\" (ch. 5). Three years behind a firebrick in a lit stove and the book would be ash. \"Scorched at the corners\" in the coda suggests you know! A cold flue, or under the hearthstone?","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"vNNZShgrICE2JIuWtAthj","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Moved it: a loose firebrick in the chimney-breast, where the stove's heat doesn't reach. 'Scorched at the corners' stays.","createdAt":1794217332972}],"createdAt":1794216341237,"updatedAt":1794217333484,"version":4,"resolvedAt":1794217333484},{"id":"aGzwNMRRn0DBDO5XvxR-Q","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"3z3uj6msTp-yJfszkLU-n","quote":"And then he smiled, and gave me his word on the Pans.","prefix":"t to the Exchequer, or shall I? ","suffix":" He has written it at the bottom","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"The letter's last line finally lands. Good.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794230018761,"updatedAt":1794230285397,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794230285397},{"id":"cN5VPtH1uyHwtVtGhPQIr","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"LQxthMqMx2ix2u3pYQu7A","quote":"the tide turns at four","prefix":"l be brief,\" she said, \"because ","suffix":" and I'm told half of you have b","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Clock: working forward from the ebb at dusk on the 18th, I get high water on the 21st in the late afternoon. So \"the tide turns at four\" holds. So do the ships pitching at their moorings and a longboat able to cross the Chapel bar.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794230018767,"updatedAt":1794230286997,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794230286997},{"id":"d3RK8uUO3GnyhB0FPWp5T","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"W6kDqSJm2O5zS3kC2H7Of","quote":"The sea took the longboat on the Chapel bar.","prefix":"in. \"Gods damn him, they'll —\"\n\n","suffix":"\n\nOne moment there were six oars","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Yes.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794216341239,"updatedAt":1794217334048,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794217334048},{"id":"fY5HMjrDppYRLC3ved9_U","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"Jwi40uq_PG4-AT77dhdd6","quote":"Down in the yard the porter was shouting for someone to bring up the tea.","prefix":"e time.\"\n\nNobody said anything. ","suffix":"","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"I've cut the paragraph that followed. It has Ilse learn from Mother Gannet that Tomas went out with Corin, which ch. 3 then has her learn again, from the same woman, as news, and Mother Gannet introduced twice. This line makes a good scene ending on its own. If you'd rather keep the paragraph, then ch. 3's first scene needs rewriting so that Ilse already knows.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"g8olqDZM6JXCkFNrCO69l","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Accepted the cut. Gannet is introduced once now.","createdAt":1794217334980}],"createdAt":1794216341206,"updatedAt":1794217335515,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217335515},{"id":"hRKvxUQaCxDba80Rvu966","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","quote":"I carry the Reeve's post, Tomas.","prefix":".\"\n\n\"You knew she'd be here.\"\n\n\"","suffix":" Letters, mostly, and I've a hab","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"This settles \"the one they've been paying\" cleanly, and \"Right on her night\" now agrees with the letter. Clock checked: the 18th, first load; spring tide \"three nights on\", the 21st.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794230018750,"updatedAt":1794230282713,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794230282713},{"id":"iPYR1a1juLm8uIF3YmYGG","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"6iI4LgbwDeFqR_Cruf7mE","quote":"That one she did not understand at all.","prefix":"_You have my word on the Pans._ ","suffix":"\n\nShe folded the letter again, a","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Does this line ever get explained? I took it to mean the wall, which Fenn tells her about in ch. 5, but the payoff isn't pointed. One line in ch. 5 would do it.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"wAgpkenRDP_H-4JHr90Z1","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Fenn now explains it in ch. 5: the Reeve's word on the Pans means the wall stands as long as Fenn keeps writing.","createdAt":1794217336446}],"createdAt":1794216341200,"updatedAt":1794217336966,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217336966},{"id":"jloSeZIpwnJGfgYpyXE6S","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"kACrUDbXsjGSKBXJOWAnj","quote":"to Hesk and never come back","prefix":"of money and take a berth south ","suffix":". And I had it in my hand on the","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Ch. 2 now gives him \"a berth on anything going south\". Is Hesk south, and by sea? If so, \"and go south\" here, and the two plants meet. If Hesk is the road, he's choosing between a ship and a road, and we need to know which he turned his back on.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"e-zW9Pr7vME1_j7k4bPmF","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Hesk is south by sea. Ch. 5 now has \"take a berth south to Hesk\" and a day \"on the Hesk quay, with the berth paid for\".","createdAt":1794230280036}],"createdAt":1794230018765,"updatedAt":1794230286462,"version":4,"resolvedAt":1794230286462},{"id":"k53VW2NzZOsqCAmIkb2PI","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"2i5J2Lc7W9L8liiqtAFCG","quote":"It was the seventeenth of Brinemonth.","prefix":" and had never once been warm.\n\n","suffix":" She had already written two que","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"This does exactly what it needs to: a date, and Ilse at work in the margin before the day she stops. One small snag: \"the small slanting hand that Aurek called her second job\". Is it the hand he calls her second job, or the queries? I'd make it the queries.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"w04GMX9mJLQ31S-a2rOTj","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Reworded: \"in her small slanting hand. Aurek called the queries her second job.\"","createdAt":1794230276010}],"createdAt":1794230018744,"updatedAt":1794230281649,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794230281649},{"id":"kbpZlrLpoY7eYlCOsppt0","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"g2q6xv5SNSaq9VyyZ2uux","quote":"\"How do you know about the sticks?\"","prefix":"se. Just — keep it somewhere.\"\n\n","suffix":"\n\nBut he had already turned. She","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Good question, and I'd like an answer. At this point only Ilse, Fenn and Tomas know the sticks are short (Aurek thinks they're behind the stove). Did Tomas go to Corin between the quay and midnight? If so, that's an important move in the who-knows-what chain, and it should be shown or said.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"qQ65lLJ9nROdoHu0LmGv8","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Tomas went to Corin an hour after the rain. Corin says so now, on his way out.","createdAt":1794217337897}],"createdAt":1794216341191,"updatedAt":1794217338416,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217338416},{"id":"keloSpH3DFPMkx0usUHOn","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"6iI4LgbwDeFqR_Cruf7mE","quote":"She broke the seal in the morning, of course.","prefix":"","suffix":" Corin Ashe had known she would;","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Great chapter opening.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794216341193,"updatedAt":1794217338981,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794217338981},{"id":"kfDTHdr7cIbVgHcbvlgVn","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"JLebbu3pA-RXZKvs35ANa","quote":"twenty-two thousand crowns","prefix":"clerical errors, Reeve. That is ","suffix":" of the Exchequer's revenue, nea","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Ilse is a counter and some readers will be too. 60 measures is 900 crowns (ch. 1), so 15 a measure; 22,000 crowns over 36 months is about 40 measures a month going astray, which is one-seventh of about 285 measures a month, less than the Kestrel's single cargo of 400. I'd make this figure much bigger, or say how many ships.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"6BdPo7xfCWV5EEYYG4jRv","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Made it a hundred and forty thousand, which works at your fifteen a measure for a port shipping about four Kestrels a month.","createdAt":1794217339913}],"createdAt":1794216341234,"updatedAt":1794217340436,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217340436},{"id":"knqX_9H1lDrr-380x8UQL","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"FBthgk0AwfIy4svSwzmYI","quote":"keep it here dry","prefix":"nnel from the Pans at night and ","suffix":" till the ship comes.\"\n\n\"How do ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"\"Dry\"? A page ago the sea comes in through the high windows at high water. A crypt that floods twice a day is the last place to keep salt. Somewhere in the tower above the tide line?","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"ySFG8oUDdpM-wipwoAiQl","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Kept up in the tower, above the tide-line.","createdAt":1794217341363}],"createdAt":1794216341221,"updatedAt":1794217341881,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217341881},{"id":"owiowR6YPOD4a3iitog6a","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"FBthgk0AwfIy4svSwzmYI","quote":"keep it up in the tower, above the tide-line","prefix":"nnel from the Pans at night and ","suffix":", till the ship comes.\"\n\n\"How do","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"The fix makes a new problem. If the salt is up in the tower, the Reeve's men carry it up and down the tower stair, and Tomas is wedged in a niche on \"the first turn of the stair\". And a paragraph later he watches them \"loading the black ship from the crypt\". Either the salt is stored higher up and he's hidden higher still, or they keep it somewhere else above the tide (the nave arches?) and the tower is his alone.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"bEFb_cLXKTKHm1y1Fvtm0","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"The salt is on the old gallery over the nave now, and the men carry it down to the crypt steps. Tomas stays on the first turn of the stair.","createdAt":1794230277010}],"createdAt":1794230018753,"updatedAt":1794230289131,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794230289131},{"id":"pLSWd3R-TzqrdMYyC86GR","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"GIXhkOMgrF_FKcO5VyMzd","quote":"I.V. L.O.","prefix":"riting: _340. Sticks counted by ","suffix":"_\n\nShe dipped her pen, and turne","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Reads as deliberate now. Lovely.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794230018773,"updatedAt":1794230288598,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794230288598},{"id":"pf_suO56erepzb9tzYHbz","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","quote":"That was what the forty crowns in his coat pocket were for.","prefix":"at was what Corin had told him. ","suffix":"\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" T","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Why does Tomas, who told Ilse the night before \"don't do anything else tonight\", take forty crowns from a man he calls a liar? Money, the Patience, Ilse? We need one line of motive here, because everything after this costs him a leg.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"PhXo3iBPlBb6oBEnKXgQV","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Added: he went to Corin himself the night before, for Ilse, and Corin said 'more than your sister does'. It's not for the money.","createdAt":1794217342829}],"createdAt":1794216341210,"updatedAt":1794217343348,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217343348},{"id":"qLmq73ExS1WXV3xFzUftS","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"2i5J2Lc7W9L8liiqtAFCG","quote":"like a man holding a door that nobody was coming through","prefix":"t it stayed, a little too long, ","suffix":".\n\n\"Sixty,\" he said.\n\n\"Six stick","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"This is where I knew I'd be happy for twelve thousand words.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794216341186,"updatedAt":1794217343914,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794217343914},{"id":"qZReRpNSTg8EC9cl7XPFH","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"FBthgk0AwfIy4svSwzmYI","quote":"She went out on this morning's tide with all of it aboard, and she'll be back for the rest.","prefix":"he ship's gone. The Black Mare. ","suffix":"\" His hand tightened on hers. \"A","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"The letter said she'd take \"all of it\" on the spring tide, \"there is no other night\". Now she's gone and is coming back. Fine if the plan changed (Corin: \"She's early\"), but someone should say why.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"rfX1FXb7MmVOGH1UxIFSo","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"The letter now says the first load on the eighteenth and the rest on the spring tide.","createdAt":1794217344845}],"createdAt":1794216341223,"updatedAt":1794217345364,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217345364},{"id":"qaTAizJt4dLvugTkttoJ4","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"kACrUDbXsjGSKBXJOWAnj","quote":"\"Because you didn't hit me with the poker,\" he said.","prefix":"believed him when he answered.\n\n","suffix":" \"The first night. You could hav","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"This is the best answer he could give, and for me it just earns the turn. See the letter.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"45N5uuBZ726tSMUtJqy_D","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Planted his want in ch. 2: three hundred crowns short of a berth south.","createdAt":1794217346295}],"createdAt":1794216341236,"updatedAt":1794217346816,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217346816},{"id":"rwKr0p8HRuSgCMxDCZ7HQ","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"GIXhkOMgrF_FKcO5VyMzd","quote":"scorched at the corners","prefix":" the desk — a small black book, ","suffix":", with three years of real numbe","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Now that it's in the chimney-breast \"where the stove's heat never reaches\", why is it scorched? Cut \"scorched at the corners\" or give it a reason (it was nearer the flue than he thought?).","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"7oMapO1cT07GOVJwkptGg","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Soot, not heat: \"black with soot at the corners\".","createdAt":1794230281027}],"createdAt":1794230018771,"updatedAt":1794230289662,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794230289662},{"id":"s0C-2JZHutD0Z5p_heF-g","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"g2q6xv5SNSaq9VyyZ2uux","quote":"It was heavier than a letter ought to be.","prefix":"mostly to stop him dropping it. ","suffix":" \"There's a seal on it you'll re","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Why is it heavy? In ch. 2 it's \"a single sheet, folded twice\". If something else is in the packet, we need to see it; if not, cut this.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"uTSBWdIxrPZdPAW5a_w5Q","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Cut.","createdAt":1794217347745}],"createdAt":1794216341190,"updatedAt":1794217348265,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217348265},{"id":"tDqThDg4dij4lbyiM9WZA","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","quote":"That's tomorrow.","prefix":"he letter said the spring tide. ","suffix":"\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light m","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Clock: spring tide is \"tomorrow\" here, \"tomorrow\" again in ch. 4 (Wren), and the day of the hearing in ch. 6, which by my count is two days apart from this. See the letter for a timeline.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"-oBrB3LZLOvaCPrxmc5Bt","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Fixed: 'the rest on the spring tide, three nights on'.","createdAt":1794217349195}],"createdAt":1794216341212,"updatedAt":1794217349714,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217349714},{"id":"tRUaGiH-gv7NCo4Nj8HnF","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"3z3uj6msTp-yJfszkLU-n","quote":"She had decided on the stair that she would tell him, all of it.","prefix":", Ilse.\"\n\nShe did not sit down. ","suffix":" If he was going to give her nam","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Yes. That's the beat, and the reason is very her.","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794230018759,"updatedAt":1794230284865,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794230284865},{"id":"uLy_qQtkqSwj8oaFWAbxX","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"Jwi40uq_PG4-AT77dhdd6","quote":"\"I'm told it happens all the time.\"","prefix":"d sticks go missing,\" she said. ","suffix":"\n\nNobody said anything. Down in ","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"I love that this phrase is the conspiracy's script: Aurek, Fenn, now the auditor quoting it back, and the Reeve in ch. 5. Make sure we can hear where she got it (from Fenn downstairs, presumably).","status":"resolved","replies":[],"createdAt":1794216341205,"updatedAt":1794217350298,"version":2,"resolvedAt":1794217350298},{"id":"uodCRzfX6QxyK39j7RB4z","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"3z3uj6msTp-yJfszkLU-n","quote":"why he was so certain I'd been told about the Black Mare taking a","prefix":"had it. I wondered — I wondered ","suffix":" first load on the eighteenth, w","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"But the letter doesn't say she's coming early; it says the spring tide. Either the letter should say early, or Fenn means a different message.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"VNPWfAvpwzPwEZu-9ewC6","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"The letter now says the eighteenth, and Fenn refers to 'a first load on the eighteenth'.","createdAt":1794217351237}],"createdAt":1794216341232,"updatedAt":1794217351747,"version":4,"resolvedAt":1794217351747},{"id":"vG7abkOWECq8ZoAJA_r2u","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"FBthgk0AwfIy4svSwzmYI","quote":"for a day and a night","prefix":" sitting in the niche, he said, ","suffix":", while the Reeve's men came and","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Clock: \"a day and a night\" here, \"two days\" when Ilse tells Fenn in ch. 5. Your note says you suspected this.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"7wwwawU0pQP3OnqIq0Nv7","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Ch. 5 now says 'since the night before last'.","createdAt":1794217352677}],"createdAt":1794216341222,"updatedAt":1794217353197,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794217353197},{"id":"wY0icoRVzNHDyJkZWT8o0","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"JLebbu3pA-RXZKvs35ANa","quote":"a Varga girl, who has something of mine","prefix":"o him. \"There's a clerk of his, ","suffix":" that came out of my post. I'd h","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"Who told him? He knows the letter went missing (Fenn: \"I wrote to you.\" \"You didn't.\"), and Fenn gave him the name at the Chapel, but only about the Kestrel. If Fenn told him this morning, after Ilse asked \"Did you take it?\", that's Fenn giving her name a second time, the very thing she went in to watch for, and she should find out. If it's only the Reeve's guess, a word would say so (\"I'm told\", or \"I'd wager\").","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"ibstM2O4O3UXRM1s6_Tof","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"A guess: \"I'd wager\". Fenn named her at the Chapel over the Kestrel, and the Reeve has put it together.","createdAt":1794230279031}],"createdAt":1794230018763,"updatedAt":1794230285929,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794230285929},{"id":"xExBAVPWzmwOLieB_K5Mr","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"FBthgk0AwfIy4svSwzmYI","quote":"His leg was splinted with half a boat-hook.","prefix":"could not say anything at all.\n\n","suffix":" He had done it well. He had com","kind":"note","author":{"kind":"editor","name":"Margit Hale"},"body":"I've moved this sentence up so that it comes in order. A query too: when he set off (end of the ch. 3 chase) \"the tide was still making\", so he crossed on the flood, not ahead of it. Which is worse, and better for him to have survived.","status":"resolved","replies":[{"id":"ODzJ7yeGpX9e1Txb9eCFR","author":{"kind":"me"},"body":"Accepted the move. He crosses \"on the flood, with the tide still making\" now. Worse for him, as you say.","createdAt":1794230278026}],"createdAt":1794230018755,"updatedAt":1794230283795,"version":3,"resolvedAt":1794230283795}],"revisionTasks":[{"id":"0tXMy02q9cgbw3sZLOjwl","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"The true book. Now that it's in the chimney-breast \"where the stove's heat never reaches\", it shouldn't be \"scorched at the corners\" in the coda. Cut the scorching, or give it a reason.","group":"NEW THINGS THE FIXES HAVE MADE","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale, second read"},"done":true,"order":50,"createdAt":1794230180030,"updatedAt":1794230269746,"version":2,"doneAt":1794230269746},{"id":"1WJSwYDN6JcfDr_L2D4bl","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"The letter is \"heavier than a letter ought to be\" (ch. 1), but it's a single sheet folded twice (ch. 2).","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"0ewOUoVuYW7HMdAEbdfQI"},{"kind":"chapter","id":"jlmIkSHDh9AA9762jhrTA"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":30,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217189887,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217189887},{"id":"6IEMqjd4MgWh3JNcxSFG8","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Two more things would make the clock easy to keep. First, say what month it is. We're told the flood was \"three years in Thawmonth\" and the coda is in Seedmonth, but never when the main story happens. One phrase in ch. 1 would give every later date something to count from. Second, make the auditor's arrival on the 18th explicitly the same day as the letter (I've put a comment there).","group":"WHAT'S WORKING","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"0ewOUoVuYW7HMdAEbdfQI"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":6,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217179387,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217179387},{"id":"6RA0RwYn2NmLPk7CSvGqO","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"The coda is lovely. Corin learning the Gullet from a bench, the margin note in red, the final count: keep all of it. One query is in the margin, though. The note in the auditor's hand ends \"I.V.\", which are Ilse's initials, not hers. If she's crediting Ilse, it's a beautiful touch, but I missed it the first time and so will others. One more word would make it clear.","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"0BtClyv2numppMKl2y1if"},{"kind":"character","id":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":21,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217185987,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217185987},{"id":"6Skbs27Qc8LlGzDi_W9MO","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Ilse takes the lamp with her to the marsh (end of ch. 4, first scene), and in ch. 4's last scene \"someone had let the lamp burn down\".","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"gaZvmW29IkkOvZThd6GM2"},{"kind":"character","id":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":31,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217190321,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217190321},{"id":"72oTzTKmKQztfmhsdfMuR","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"The Reeve's \"Varga girl\". At the Anchor he knows a Varga girl has \"something of mine that came out of my post\". Who told him? If it was Fenn, after Ilse's \"Did you take it?\" that morning, then Fenn has given her name a second time. That's exactly what she went in to watch for, and she ought to find out, perhaps at the hearing. If it's only the Reeve's guess, a word (\"I'm told\", \"I'd wager\") would say so.","group":"NEW THINGS THE FIXES HAVE MADE","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"ImEgyEleKDTXxPtYHnyiw"},{"kind":"character","id":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale, second read"},"done":true,"order":48,"createdAt":1794230180030,"updatedAt":1794230268881,"version":2,"doneAt":1794230268881},{"id":"7OuSX0JkCSruzQLhHrjdw","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Plant what he wants. If the book once showed us that Corin wants something Seyle's money could buy, a passage out, a debt cleared, we'd feel what he gives up by turning back.","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"0BtClyv2numppMKl2y1if"},{"kind":"character","id":"acnw-9DtWg8L1spLSEAuf"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":22,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217186419,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217186419},{"id":"8NWSPUiL5Xesns-CAmsxU","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Corin's two plans. Ch. 2 gives him \"a berth on anything going south\"; ch. 5 has him thinking of selling the letter \"and go to Hesk\", \"on the Hesk road\". If Hesk is south and by sea, make it \"go south\" in ch. 5 and the two plants meet. If Hesk is the road, then we need to know which escape he turned his back on.","group":"NEW THINGS THE FIXES HAVE MADE","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"jlmIkSHDh9AA9762jhrTA"},{"kind":"chapter","id":"fJwp9CZ9bVPBwlRrx5BGo"},{"kind":"character","id":"0BtClyv2numppMKl2y1if"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale, second read"},"done":true,"order":49,"createdAt":1794230180030,"updatedAt":1794230269313,"version":2,"doneAt":1794230269313},{"id":"AjNTG6OFhAjMt2Ro6dHln","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"I'd move it to open ch. 3, straight after the longboat at the end of ch. 2. Three reasons. First, the ch. 2 cliffhanger then gets its answer at once instead of a chapter later, and the chapter break still does the work of a held breath. Second, the reader then knows Tomas is lying hurt in the tower while Ilse sits with Mother Gannet and lies to the auditor. That turns two quiet scenes into scenes of dread, which is better than the mild surprise the flashback gets you now. Third, it's the only place in the book where time runs backwards, and it's in the chapter where the clock is already shakiest.","group":"WHAT'S WORKING","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"tAVx_Yjtxb6sBMvYpfDe2"},{"kind":"chapter","id":"jlmIkSHDh9AA9762jhrTA"},{"kind":"character","id":"-C6L6AyeDMMYuWemxYJ4J"},{"kind":"character","id":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6"},{"kind":"character","id":"u1DgTeIdVBBaq2-4yBy6H"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":17,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217184254,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217184254},{"id":"EON-wh2WvIwaVeGBN3DAe","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"How does Tomas cross a quarter mile of marsh to the tower on a broken leg, at the flood? I believe he can, but I'd like a sentence of it.","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"-C6L6AyeDMMYuWemxYJ4J"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":34,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217191620,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217191620},{"id":"EgS443kHVoPKJWMJOWEeU","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Fenn's true book has been in the stove \"behind the firebrick\" for three years, and the stove is \"always lit\". It would be ash.","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"ImEgyEleKDTXxPtYHnyiw"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":33,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217191187,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217191187},{"id":"GdzEMj8FQt-KHIKZG-CbN","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Fenn and \"coming early\" (ch. 5). Fenn says the Reeve was sure he'd been told \"about the Black Mare coming early\". The letter doesn't say she's coming early. If you take my clock suggestion, it will.","group":"WHAT'S WORKING","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"fJwp9CZ9bVPBwlRrx5BGo"},{"kind":"character","id":"ImEgyEleKDTXxPtYHnyiw"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":12,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217182021,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217182021},{"id":"H1kC5vpsa17w236M3MTN3","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"In the wreck scene Ilse is only a spectator. That's fine for a page, which is about what it is now, but the sea does the justice in this book, not anyone in it. I don't think that's wrong for this story (the wall that \"held\" is a fine irony), but I'd want Ilse to feel something about it besides awe. Fenn's fate depends on that death; the Reeve's men are still in Varrow. One paragraph of her thinking about what the Reeve's death means for the hearing she has just won would do it.","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6"},{"kind":"character","id":"ImEgyEleKDTXxPtYHnyiw"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":20,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217185553,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217185553},{"id":"HNibIsJQYoJKbOXneCMWG","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Tomas crawls to the tower \"ahead of the flood\", but when he sets off at the end of the chase \"the tide was still making\". So he crossed on the flood. That's worse for him, and a better story. Your call.","group":"The fixes left four small slips behind. I've corrected three in the file and queried the fourth","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"-C6L6AyeDMMYuWemxYJ4J"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale, second read"},"done":true,"order":45,"createdAt":1794230180030,"updatedAt":1794230267548,"version":2,"doneAt":1794230267548},{"id":"HxFKqwnEpPOnk3Bwq-UFa","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"\"I'm the one they've been paying\" (Corin, ch. 3). If Corin has been on the Reeve's payroll, that's a large fact: it changes why he stole the letter, and how the hearing should treat him. Nobody picks it up. Either build it in (he was the Reeve's courier, which is how he could get at the post-bag) or cut it.","group":"WHAT'S WORKING","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"tAVx_Yjtxb6sBMvYpfDe2"},{"kind":"character","id":"0BtClyv2numppMKl2y1if"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":9,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217180706,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217180706},{"id":"IAru9vxboV2J4EMR-NzNY","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"\"Another voice, one he thought he knew\" (ch. 3). If it's Fenn, Tomas should tell Ilse in the tower before she sees Fenn for herself, or it should be someone we haven't suspected. At the moment it's a thread left hanging.","group":"WHAT'S WORKING","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"tAVx_Yjtxb6sBMvYpfDe2"},{"kind":"character","id":"ImEgyEleKDTXxPtYHnyiw"},{"kind":"character","id":"-C6L6AyeDMMYuWemxYJ4J"},{"kind":"character","id":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":10,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217181137,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217181137},{"id":"JlNjcxMWQHhMNXh6dKQLe","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"The salt in the tower. Moving it \"up in the tower, above the tide-line\" solves the damp, but the tower is where Tomas is hiding, \"on the first turn of the stair\". The Reeve's men would carry sacks up and down past him all day and night. And a paragraph later he watches them load \"from the crypt\". Either store it somewhere else above the tide (the nave arches?), or put it higher in the tower and Tomas higher still. The second gives you a good scene, but then he'd have climbed a stair on a broken leg.","group":"NEW THINGS THE FIXES HAVE MADE","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"-C6L6AyeDMMYuWemxYJ4J"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale, second read"},"done":true,"order":47,"createdAt":1794230180030,"updatedAt":1794230268429,"version":2,"doneAt":1794230268429},{"id":"Ms9kiz8iycN6mZB_Z473G","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"The letter now says the Mare takes \"the first of it\" on the 18th, but Tomas in the tower still says she went out \"with all of it aboard\". I've made it \"the first load\".","group":"The fixes left four small slips behind. I've corrected three in the file and queried the fourth","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"-C6L6AyeDMMYuWemxYJ4J"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale, second read"},"done":true,"order":42,"createdAt":1794230180030,"updatedAt":1794230266203,"version":2,"doneAt":1794230266203},{"id":"Pt1CEKk-fJ0KjQJizgnU-","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"\"I knew your mother\" (Ormund, ch. 3). In ch. 2 she only knew of \"a Varga at the Hesk customs house\". I've made it \"I knew of your mother\".","group":"WHAT'S WORKING","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"tAVx_Yjtxb6sBMvYpfDe2"},{"kind":"chapter","id":"jlmIkSHDh9AA9762jhrTA"},{"kind":"character","id":"PX9V9i5r7Q-xcIvjbyuck"},{"kind":"character","id":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":13,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217182471,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217182471},{"id":"Qtct84475ZfCFfDwvFtsQ","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Mother Gannet tells Ilse twice. At the end of ch. 2's second scene she tells Ilse the Patience went out with \"a long clever one with a scar in his eyebrow\". In ch. 3 she's introduced again as though for the first time, and the two of them work out together, as news, that Tomas is out there with Corin. I've cut the ch. 2 paragraph (it's marked; reject the cut if you'd rather rewrite ch. 3 instead). The second scene of ch. 2 ends well without it, on the porter shouting for the tea.","group":"WHAT'S WORKING","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"jlmIkSHDh9AA9762jhrTA"},{"kind":"chapter","id":"tAVx_Yjtxb6sBMvYpfDe2"},{"kind":"character","id":"u1DgTeIdVBBaq2-4yBy6H"},{"kind":"character","id":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6"},{"kind":"character","id":"-C6L6AyeDMMYuWemxYJ4J"},{"kind":"character","id":"0BtClyv2numppMKl2y1if"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":8,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217180253,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217180253},{"id":"Rh1nXjuSlu5jXZRzSVuVy","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Ch. 5: \"two days\" becomes \"a day and a night\" (or just \"since the night before last\"). The Reeve's \"four days\" becomes three. Corin's \"four days\" becomes \"three\".","group":"WHAT'S WORKING","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"fJwp9CZ9bVPBwlRrx5BGo"},{"kind":"character","id":"0BtClyv2numppMKl2y1if"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":3,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217178053,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217178053},{"id":"SJZu1pcSUL3sjlsll1zKe","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Housekeeping, for later rather than now. Ship names are usually italicised (Kestrel, Patience, Black Mare, Heron); I haven't done it in the file, because it would light up every page with changes. Do it in one pass when the text has settled. The quotes are straight rather than curly, which a typesetter will fix. On the title page your name appears twice, and the word count says \"about 12,000\"; I make it 12,285, so I've changed it to 12,300.","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":false,"order":41,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794216780601,"version":1},{"id":"T_MaFkxPg1qWhVs6bWz5N","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Ilse. Strong and consistent. My one wish is for a moment early on in which she's good at her job in front of us. We're told she writes in the margin every day (Aurek tells us in ch. 5, beautifully), but we never see her do it before the day she doesn't. A single marginal note in ch. 1, before the Kestrel, would make Aurek's speech land twice as hard.","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"fJwp9CZ9bVPBwlRrx5BGo"},{"kind":"chapter","id":"0ewOUoVuYW7HMdAEbdfQI"},{"kind":"character","id":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6"},{"kind":"character","id":"ioIm-IYJ5oHevQIEf1FcP"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":24,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217187286,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217187286},{"id":"W97YcswP5XsaZJ5_y60cF","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Lisbet Ormund. A delight. In her own scene (ch. 5, at the Anchor) she can't see her own \"kindly hen's face\", so I've taken it out there. Elsewhere that phrase comes four times; I've kept it for her arrival and her farewell.","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"fJwp9CZ9bVPBwlRrx5BGo"},{"kind":"character","id":"PX9V9i5r7Q-xcIvjbyuck"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":28,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217189020,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217189020},{"id":"WRhPw3sFnOuZADUIdjKAo","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Ilse tells Fenn everything (ch. 5: \"I was at the Chapel last night… I heard you tell the Reeve my name\"). She's telling one of the two conspirators what she knows before she's told the auditor, while her brother is lying at the Pans with a broken leg. It's the bravest thing she does in the book and the riskiest. I believe she'd do it, but I'd like one beat of her choosing to.","group":"WHAT'S WORKING","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"fJwp9CZ9bVPBwlRrx5BGo"},{"kind":"character","id":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6"},{"kind":"character","id":"ImEgyEleKDTXxPtYHnyiw"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":16,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217183805,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217183805},{"id":"WS1H8K6iAe7p4PurVzKuS","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"\"Looked\". You knew. There are 39 \"looked\" in about 12,300 words, plus 13 \"look\" and 10 \"looking\". I've changed about twenty where they hurt, mostly \"looked up when she came in\", \"looked at her for a long moment\" and \"looked at X and then at Y\". The ones I've left alone are doing something (\"She did not need to look. She looked anyway.\").","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":38,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217193353,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217193353},{"id":"WmVqtRpLXp-nr2Fkda_Cb","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"The paragraph also raises something the book doesn't yet answer. \"The men the Reeve had paid … were still in Varrow and had all seen her face\" is a real threat, and the coda never mentions them. One line would do it: Lisbet's arrangements, Corin keeping an eye out, the men melting away.","group":"The fixes left four small slips behind. I've corrected three in the file and queried the fourth","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"PX9V9i5r7Q-xcIvjbyuck"},{"kind":"character","id":"0BtClyv2numppMKl2y1if"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale, second read"},"done":true,"order":46,"createdAt":1794230180030,"updatedAt":1794230267997,"version":2,"doneAt":1794230267997},{"id":"_HXVJgke6H8EWw9KTd8HE","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Italics for ship names and curly quotes are still for the final pass, as we agreed.","group":"SMALL THINGS","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale, second read"},"done":false,"order":55,"createdAt":1794230180030,"updatedAt":1794230180030,"version":1},{"id":"_sBpbNJGCaYvAyS3I_vbG","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"The salt is kept \"dry\" in the Chapel crypt, which floods twice a day.","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":32,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217190752,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217190752},{"id":"bI3TgIxF_9tjRfVBNXTV2","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Ch. 3's second scene now opens \"Tomas did not come by that day\". Coming straight after a night scene, that reads as the day the chase ended. I've made it \"the day after the storm\".","group":"The fixes left four small slips behind. I've corrected three in the file and queried the fourth","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"tAVx_Yjtxb6sBMvYpfDe2"},{"kind":"character","id":"-C6L6AyeDMMYuWemxYJ4J"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale, second read"},"done":true,"order":44,"createdAt":1794230180030,"updatedAt":1794230267099,"version":2,"doneAt":1794230267099},{"id":"cKqatNReUiQHo9WdB-bGB","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Lisbet says Fenn \"hasn't slept in three nights\". She arrived on the 18th and this is the evening of the 20th, so I've made it two.","group":"The fixes left four small slips behind. I've corrected three in the file and queried the fourth","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"PX9V9i5r7Q-xcIvjbyuck"},{"kind":"character","id":"ImEgyEleKDTXxPtYHnyiw"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale, second read"},"done":true,"order":43,"createdAt":1794230180030,"updatedAt":1794230266649,"version":2,"doneAt":1794230266649},{"id":"geIABGE5Ti6YrRMesykCS","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Corin knows about the sticks (ch. 1: \"How do you know about the sticks?\"). At midnight only Ilse, Fenn and Tomas know the count is short. The obvious answer is that Tomas went to Corin after leaving Ilse on the quay, which would also explain why Tomas is in Corin's boat the next night. If so, show it or have someone say it. It's a link in the chain and at the moment it's missing.","group":"WHAT'S WORKING","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"0ewOUoVuYW7HMdAEbdfQI"},{"kind":"character","id":"0BtClyv2numppMKl2y1if"},{"kind":"character","id":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6"},{"kind":"character","id":"ImEgyEleKDTXxPtYHnyiw"},{"kind":"character","id":"-C6L6AyeDMMYuWemxYJ4J"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":7,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217179819,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217179819},{"id":"hH4Qut97w_7XtyooMfwMW","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Tomas. The weakest motive in the book. On Day 1 he tells Ilse \"Don't do anything else tonight\". On Day 2 he takes forty crowns from a man he calls a liar and sails him into the marsh. Is it for Ilse? For money for the Patience? Because Corin told him something? One line will do, but it needs to be there, because it costs him a leg. He's also \"Tomas Brenn\" once (ch. 2), while his sister is a Varga and their mother was a Varga. Half-siblings, or a slip?","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"jlmIkSHDh9AA9762jhrTA"},{"kind":"character","id":"-C6L6AyeDMMYuWemxYJ4J"},{"kind":"character","id":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6"},{"kind":"character","id":"0BtClyv2numppMKl2y1if"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":25,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217187719,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217187719},{"id":"hcVpDtomiKy2270r1naov","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Ch. 3 opening: \"did not come by the next day, as he had said he would, and he did not come the day after. On the second morning…\" doesn't quite work, because the second morning is the day after. Something like \"He did not come that day either\" would do.","group":"WHAT'S WORKING","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"tAVx_Yjtxb6sBMvYpfDe2"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":5,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217178954,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217178954},{"id":"iasg0Nja4kTkfIS2HLx_N","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"The one pattern I'd keep an eye on when you do your own \"looked\" pass is \"looked at X, and at Y, and at Z\". The Reeve does it at the hearing, and the clerk of the Hall did it again in your new paragraph eight paragraphs later. I've changed the clerk's.","group":"THE LINES YOU DIDN'T TAKE","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale, second read"},"done":false,"order":52,"createdAt":1794230180030,"updatedAt":1794230180030,"version":1},{"id":"icwH4H3_dYdWe2sSffFBU","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Wren in ch. 4: not \"springs tomorrow\" but something like \"near springs, so it'll go right out\", which is all his argument needs.","group":"WHAT'S WORKING","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"gaZvmW29IkkOvZThd6GM2"},{"kind":"character","id":"zn60qcdCoQc4XQ4KSnfky"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":2,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217177621,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217177621},{"id":"j90iXQmn7msfTqRANr49B","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Coda: \"three weeks longer\", or move the date.","group":"WHAT'S WORKING","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":4,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217178505,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217178505},{"id":"jFKQiZ7RYb6YwM5P5u6eN","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"How Ilse knows it was Corin who took the letter from the bolster (\"She had known since the Magister laughed\"). The laugh tells her the Reeve never sent the letter. It's the realisation that only Corin knew the letter existed that should tell her. Say that, and the moment is earned.","group":"WHAT'S WORKING","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6"},{"kind":"character","id":"0BtClyv2numppMKl2y1if"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":14,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217182921,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217182921},{"id":"kDZNQKmhRyP5n-vZhL_s5","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"\"Afterward\" and \"toward\" (three of each) are the only American spellings in an otherwise British book. I've made them \"afterwards\" and \"towards\". \"God knows\" in the coda becomes \"Gods know\", since everyone else in Varrow swears by \"Gods\".","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":40,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217193787,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217193787},{"id":"kF7SeEH_NcU8IuOcadlEN","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"The Reeve simply walks out of the Exchequer's hearing and nobody, including the auditor, tries to stop him. \"Nobody in Varrow had ever stopped the Reeve doing anything\" is a good line, but Lisbet isn't from Varrow. Give her one failed attempt and the line becomes stronger.","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"PX9V9i5r7Q-xcIvjbyuck"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":19,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217185120,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217185120},{"id":"kL7kOQXpP2beylaIwdMo8","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"The storm isn't planted on the day. Ch. 6 says it came in \"as the old people on the quay had said it would\", but nobody had said so. I've inserted a sentence in the hearing scene to show the shape of the fix; better still, have Lisbet's \"the tide turns at four\" carry a warning about the weather.","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"K8aajj_pAmXJ6pXZqiX7A"},{"kind":"character","id":"PX9V9i5r7Q-xcIvjbyuck"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":18,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217184686,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217184686},{"id":"kbQKRNwlyGQjZr6COMPDi","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Tomas \"came up the Gull Stair\" in the coda and sat \"at the bottom\". I've made it \"down\".","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"-C6L6AyeDMMYuWemxYJ4J"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":36,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217192486,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217192486},{"id":"kuRcI_6rrpSoCBzmogLao","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Aurek. His turn in ch. 5 is one of the best things in the book. Ch. 1 calls him \"the senior of the three tallyclerks\", and we only ever meet two.","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"fJwp9CZ9bVPBwlRrx5BGo"},{"kind":"chapter","id":"0ewOUoVuYW7HMdAEbdfQI"},{"kind":"character","id":"ioIm-IYJ5oHevQIEf1FcP"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":27,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217188586,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217188586},{"id":"lC_FDG2BPmIgKfCFu4Ehf","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Repeated phrases. Some repetition is the book's music and should stay: the margins, \"her own clean hand\", \"sticks go missing\", \"it happens all the time\". Others are just repeats: \"half the lower town\" (4 times), \"kindly hen\" (4), \"clever face\" (5), \"for a long moment\" (4), and \"very\" (38). I've thinned each a little.","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":false,"order":39,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794216780601,"version":1},{"id":"mLvpJ2624nf4gCY_6cmh2","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"In ch. 1, \"the small slanting hand that Aurek called her second job\": I think Aurek means the queries rather than the handwriting. A small reword.","group":"SMALL THINGS","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"0ewOUoVuYW7HMdAEbdfQI"},{"kind":"character","id":"ioIm-IYJ5oHevQIEf1FcP"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale, second read"},"done":true,"order":53,"createdAt":1794230180030,"updatedAt":1794230270614,"version":2,"doneAt":1794230270614},{"id":"mv7MRkIcdF8U6Pp8aSvmT","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"\"You have my word on the Pans\" (the letter, ch. 2): Ilse doesn't understand it, and the book never quite explains it. One line in Fenn's confession (the wall) would do it.","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"jlmIkSHDh9AA9762jhrTA"},{"kind":"character","id":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6"},{"kind":"character","id":"ImEgyEleKDTXxPtYHnyiw"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":37,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217192921,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217192921},{"id":"mvopMDf6p9lHSSkzXra4p","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"The arithmetic. 60 measures is 900 crowns, so 15 crowns a measure. At that price, 22,000 crowns over three years is about 40 measures a month going astray, which is one-seventh of about 285 measures a month. That's less than the Kestrel's single cargo of 400. Make the total much bigger, or tell us how few ships there are. Your heroine counts for a living, and some of your readers will check the sums.","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":35,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217192053,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217192053},{"id":"opKrlKiGHOcrtkpZuZZ5C","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"The Reeve's letter. In ch. 2 it's in \"a secretary's hand\", signed; the text we're shown is signed \"— H.S.\"; at the hearing Ilse calls it \"a letter in your hand\". I've changed that to \"under your seal\" in both places it comes up, but decide what the signature is, because the hearing turns on it.","group":"WHAT'S WORKING","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"jlmIkSHDh9AA9762jhrTA"},{"kind":"character","id":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":11,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217181587,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217181587},{"id":"podhupMlvmMe7kgk8fejN","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"On the word count, you asked. I make the revised text 12,724 words, so \"about 12,700\" is the honest figure, and I'd put that on the title page rather than \"about 13,000\".","group":"SMALL THINGS","links":[],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale, second read"},"done":false,"order":54,"createdAt":1794230180030,"updatedAt":1794230180030,"version":1},{"id":"puPq-EuLja0hcxtlYXLdJ","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Fenn. The best-drawn character, as I said. His confession in ch. 5 is long, and much of it retells what Mother Gannet told us in ch. 3. I've cut the retold part so it goes straight to what only he could know. You may want to go further.","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"fJwp9CZ9bVPBwlRrx5BGo"},{"kind":"chapter","id":"tAVx_Yjtxb6sBMvYpfDe2"},{"kind":"character","id":"ImEgyEleKDTXxPtYHnyiw"},{"kind":"character","id":"u1DgTeIdVBBaq2-4yBy6H"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":26,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217188153,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217188153},{"id":"tdifyCLYsx641xYHdMo8C","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Halvard Seyle. The only character who stays at one note. He's big, loud, red and easy. I'd like one moment where he's frightening because he's reasonable. The \"visitors, mostly\" threat at the Anchor is close; let him be right about something.","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"acnw-9DtWg8L1spLSEAuf"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":29,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217189454,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217189454},{"id":"uET5sXHC4hU9FxqQO_AeB","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Corin in ch. 2: the Black Mare isn't \"a night\" early, she's three nights early, and what she's taking is the first load. Tomas says in ch. 4 that she's \"coming back for the rest\", so you already have the idea; it just needs to be in the plan from the start, and probably in the letter too (\"she will take the first of it on the 18th and the rest on the spring tide\").","group":"WHAT'S WORKING","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"jlmIkSHDh9AA9762jhrTA"},{"kind":"chapter","id":"gaZvmW29IkkOvZThd6GM2"},{"kind":"character","id":"0BtClyv2numppMKl2y1if"},{"kind":"character","id":"-C6L6AyeDMMYuWemxYJ4J"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":1,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217177170,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217177170},{"id":"uYqdkk-CPQYcfby9Lumdu","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"Decide about \"the one they've been paying\" (see above). If he worked for Seyle, his return is a betrayal of an employer as well as a sale he didn't make, and that's a bigger turn and a better one.","group":"The storm is where it feels rushed, for three reasons, and none of them needs much length","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"acnw-9DtWg8L1spLSEAuf"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":23,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217186852,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217186852},{"id":"uZsJYNQ-CKwyYN6600qB_","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"And two lines I've tidied in the file because the revision made them contradict themselves. In ch. 2, \"That was what the forty crowns… were for\" is followed at once by \"He had not come for the forty crowns\". In ch. 5, \"only one man in Varrow had known it existed\" isn't true, because the Reeve and his secretary knew it existed. Only Corin knew where it had gone.","group":"NEW THINGS THE FIXES HAVE MADE","links":[{"kind":"chapter","id":"jlmIkSHDh9AA9762jhrTA"},{"kind":"chapter","id":"fJwp9CZ9bVPBwlRrx5BGo"},{"kind":"character","id":"0BtClyv2numppMKl2y1if"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale, second read"},"done":true,"order":51,"createdAt":1794230180030,"updatedAt":1794230270180,"version":2,"doneAt":1794230270180},{"id":"wdMF4W3Ac3SwZEfQtRDlc","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"After the search. If Seyle's men came with the cooper's wife's key and found the bolster already empty, the Reeve now knows Ilse had something. He should behave like a man who knows that, either in his visit to the auditor or at the hearing. At the moment he's surprised by nothing.","group":"WHAT'S WORKING","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"acnw-9DtWg8L1spLSEAuf"},{"kind":"character","id":"tQ_N01gqaxmxd8YGrh3e6"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":15,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217183360,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217183360},{"id":"x1K7EwQpN-Jrb8k3gUN2F","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","text":"My suggestion is to keep the hearing on Day 5, because the plot needs those days: the search, Fenn's confession, Corin's day on the Hesk road. Then fix everything else to match:","group":"WHAT'S WORKING","links":[{"kind":"character","id":"ImEgyEleKDTXxPtYHnyiw"},{"kind":"character","id":"0BtClyv2numppMKl2y1if"}],"source":{"kind":"letter","letter":"Margit Hale"},"done":true,"order":0,"createdAt":1794216780601,"updatedAt":1794217176726,"version":2,"doneAt":1794217176726}],"continuitySuppressions":[],"writingLogs":[{"id":"wklC85e6r0gW6bn6w1jPn","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","date":"2026-11-09","words":12690,"createdAt":1794213585981,"updatedAt":1794230097839}],"sceneRevisions":[{"id":"-05ug-q4efqgkaFuMaSJ7","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"LQxthMqMx2ix2u3pYQu7A","text":"The hearing was called for the hour after noon on the day of the spring tide, in the long hall at the top of the town where the Reeve of Varrow had sat in judgement on fishing disputes and drunk sailors for as long as there had been a Reeve. Lisbet Ormund had called it. She had the right; she had shown the clerk of the Hall a paper with the Exchequer's seal on it, and the clerk had gone white and sent for the Reeve, and the Reeve had come, and smiled, and said that of course the auditor must have whatever she needed.\n\nHe sat now in his own chair at the head of the hall, in the harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs. The Magister sat below him on the bench where witnesses sat, very straight, his long hands on his knees. Half the lower town had come up the hill to stand at the back. Ilse could see Mother Gannet's milky eye in the crowd, and Wren's dirty face beside her, and a great many people she had passed on the Gull Stair every day of her life and never spoken to.\n\nThe auditor stood at a small table in the middle of the floor with her papers in front of her.\n\n\"I'll be brief,\" she said, \"because the tide turns at four and I'm told half of you have boats to see to. I have audited the books of the Salt House of Varrow for the last four years. I find that for the last three of them, one-seventh part of the salt shipped from the Pans has not reached Hesk, and that the books have been written to hide it. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. Miss Varga.\"\n\nIlse stood up.\n\nShe had thought her legs would shake. They did not. She walked out to the table in the middle of the floor in her clerk's grey with her hair pinned and laid the things down on it one by one, the way she would have laid out a ship's sticks to be counted.\n\nThe Kestrel's book, open at the ninth.\n\nThirty-four tally-sticks, which Aurek Stiles had brought up the hill in his coat.\n\nA letter with a red seal pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\n\"On the ninth,\" she said, \"the Kestrel came in through the Gullet with three hundred and forty measures of salt on her sticks. I wrote four hundred in her book, because I was told to. That was a lie, and it was mine.\" She heard the crowd at the back shift and murmur. She did not look at them. \"Two days later the auditor asked me if I'd ever been told to write a number I knew was wrong, and I said no. That was a lie too.\"\n\n\"Miss Varga,\" said the Reeve, pleasantly, from his chair. \"You'll understand that a clerk who admits to two lies in one breath is not —\"\n\n\"And this,\" said Ilse, \"is a letter in your hand, Reeve, to the Magister, telling him where the sixty measures from the Kestrel were to go, and when, and on what ship. It never reached him. A man called Corin Ashe took it out of your post-bag, and gave it to me, and I've kept it.\" She looked up at him. \"You can read it, if you like. Or the auditor can read it aloud. It's quite short.\"\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\nHalvard Seyle looked at the letter on the table, and at the auditor, and at the Magister on the witnesses' bench, who had not moved. Then he looked past them all to the back of the hall, where Corin Ashe was standing against the wall with his arms folded, in a clean coat, and smiled.\n\n\"A smuggler,\" the Reeve said. \"A smuggler steals a letter, and a clerk who lies admits she lies, and an old man —\"\n\n\"I'll speak to the letter,\" said Oduya Fenn.\n\nHe stood up from the bench. He was not looking at the Reeve; he was looking at Ilse, and his face was quite calm.\n\n\"I never received it,\" he said. \"But I know what it says, because I know what every letter Halvard Seyle has written me for three years has said. I'll tell the auditor about every one of them. I'll tell her about the wall. I have kept a book of my own, Madam Auditor — a true one. I kept it because I am a clerk, and I could not bear not to. It is in the stove in my room at the Salt House, behind the firebrick, and it has every measure in it that I ever wrote false.\"\n\nSomebody in the crowd said, very loudly, \"Gods,\" and somebody else laughed, high and shocked.\n\nThe Reeve was on his feet.\n\n\"This hearing is closed,\" he said. \"I am the Reeve of Varrow and this is my hall, and I'll not have —\"\n\n\"It isn't closed, Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund mildly. \"It's the Exchequer's hearing. It's only being held in your hall.\" She picked up the letter. \"Sit down, please. I'm going to read this aloud.\"\n\nHe did not sit down. He looked at the auditor, and at the Magister, and at the crowd filling the back of the hall, and something went out of his big red face all at once, like air out of a bladder. Then he turned and walked out through the door behind his chair, the one that only the Reeve used, and nobody stopped him, because nobody in Varrow had ever stopped the Reeve doing anything.","wordCount":947,"createdAt":1794216654071},{"id":"-Da6rF2tsJXotO5BWi1LY","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"kACrUDbXsjGSKBXJOWAnj","text":"Corin Ashe came back on the night before the spring tide, the way he had come the first time: a knock after midnight, a voice on the landing, a request not to be hit with whatever she was holding.\n\nThis time she opened the door all the way.\n\nHe looked terrible. He had a cut along his jaw that someone had stitched badly, and his clothes were not his own, and he was thinner than a man ought to get in two days. But he was smiling, and when he saw her face the smile went crooked and then went away altogether.\n\n\"Tomas,\" he said. \"Is he —\"\n\n\"He's alive. His leg's broken. He's at the Pans with a man called Hobb.\" She stood aside. \"Come in. You're letting the cold in.\"\n\nHe came in and stood in the middle of her small room and saw the bolster, which she had sewn up again, badly, and at the feathers that were still in the corners because she had not been able to bring herself to sweep them.\n\n\"I'm sorry about that,\" he said.\n\nShe had known. She had known since the Magister laughed. But she said, \"You,\" anyway, because it needed saying.\n\n\"Me.\" He reached inside his borrowed coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilcloth and held it out to her, exactly as he had on the first night. \"Everybody hides things in the bolster, Ilse. I've been in forty rooms in this city. It's always the bolster or the chimney, and you don't have a chimney.\"\n\nShe did not take it. \"Why?\"\n\n\"Because Seyle's men were coming up Tallow Street with a key the cooper's wife had been paid for, and I got there first. Because I knew where they'd look.\" He was still holding it out. \"And because I thought — when I came up out of that creek, half drowned, with the Reeve's men going past me close enough to touch — I thought, I could sell this. I could sell this back to Halvard Seyle for a great deal of money and go to Hesk and never come back. And I had it in my hand on the Hesk road for a whole day before I turned round.\"\n\n\"Why did you turn round?\"\n\nHe thought about it. She watched him think about it, and was surprised to find that she believed him when he answered.\n\n\"Because you didn't hit me with the poker,\" he said. \"The first night. You could have. You had it right there behind the door, I saw the end of it. And you opened the door a hand's width instead, and took a stranger's letter, and kept it.\" He shrugged. \"Nobody's ever done that. Usually they hit me.\"\n\nShe took the letter. The seal was still pressed down where she had warmed it with the knife. He had not opened it again.\n\n\"The auditor's at the Anchor,\" she said. \"On Rope Walk.\"\n\n\"I know where the Anchor is.\"\n\n\"Will you come with me?\"\n\nCorin Ashe was quiet for a long moment, the long clever face in the lamplight, the scar through his eyebrow.\n\n\"I'm a smuggler,\" he said. \"And a liar. Nobody will believe a word I say.\"\n\n\"They'll believe the sticks,\" said Ilse. \"Aurek has the sticks. And they'll believe the letter. And they'll believe me, because I'm going to tell them I lied, and nobody says that unless it's true.\" She put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, and reached for her coat. \"You only have to stand there.\"","wordCount":593,"createdAt":1794217072141},{"id":"-JK-xlktVTMf8BQJFTqTs","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"JLebbu3pA-RXZKvs35ANa","text":"Lisbet Ormund had taken the best room at the Anchor, which was not saying a great deal, and had spent the first evening moving the furniture so that the table caught the light from the window and the bed did not catch the draught from the door. She was at the table now, with her shoes off and her stockinged feet on a hot brick wrapped in flannel, writing her report.\n\nShe had written it three times. Each time it said the same thing — _one part in seven, three years, the Salt House books written to conceal it_ — and each time it ended in the same place, which was _I have no proof of who._\n\nWhen the landlord knocked to say the Reeve was below and wished to call on her, she said, \"Yes, of course, send him up,\" and put her shoes back on, and turned her report face down on the table, and put the hot brick under the bed.\n\nHalvard Seyle filled the doorway. He was a big man and knew it, and he had come in his harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs, and he bowed to her with great courtesy and said he hoped she was comfortable, and that Varrow was not too rough for a lady from the capital, and that if there was anything at all she needed she had only to send to the Reeve's Hall.\n\n\"How kind,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Do sit down.\"\n\nHe sat. The chair complained.\n\n\"I hear you've been at the Salt House books,\" he said.\n\n\"I have.\"\n\n\"Poor Fenn. He's not the man he was. The flood hit him very hard — he took it personally, you know, as though the sea had done it to spite him. I've had to carry a good deal of the work since.\" He smiled. \"If you've found anything amiss, I'd hope you'd come to me first. Before the capital. It would be a shame to ruin an old man's name over a few clerical errors that the harbour could put right quietly.\"\n\n\"A few clerical errors,\" the auditor repeated.\n\n\"Sticks go missing,\" said the Reeve. \"It happens all the time.\"\n\nLisbet Ormund regarded him for a long moment. Then she turned her report over on the table and pushed it across to him.\n\n\"One part in seven,\" she said. \"Every month for three years. That is not a few clerical errors, Reeve. That is twenty-two thousand crowns of the Exchequer's revenue, near enough, and it is going somewhere, and it is not going to Hesk.\" She folded her hands. \"I'll tell you what I have, since you've been good enough to call. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. I have a tallyclerk who lied to my face and was frightened to death doing it, and a Magister who hasn't slept in three nights. What I don't have is a name.\" She smiled at him. \"I don't suppose you'd care to give me one?\"\n\nThe Reeve did not smile back.\n\n\"You've been in Varrow four days, madam,\" he said. \"You don't know the tides. People who don't know the tides get into trouble out on the marsh. Every year we lose one or two. Visitors, mostly.\" He stood up. \"I'd be careful.\"\n\n\"I always am,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Good night, Reeve.\"\n\nWhen he had gone she sat for a while without moving. Then she got the hot brick back out from under the bed — it had gone lukewarm — and put her feet on it, and turned her report face down again, and wrote at the bottom of a fresh sheet, in her tiny neat hand: _Seyle. Certain. No proof._","wordCount":619,"createdAt":1794217063875},{"id":"-N-4TDwJ2LeAqxiZBwk9Y","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"QkH1pzVCb9awQuyhPF6LA","text":"They had been seen. Tomas never knew how — a turn of the Patience's sail against the sky, perhaps, or the creak of her blocks across still water, or simply that a man on the Chapel steps had looked up at the wrong moment. But the light at the Chapel went out all at once, and in the dark there was a shout, and then the sound of oars.\n\n\"Go,\" said Corin. \"Go, go —\"\n\nTomas went. He put the helm over and got the sail up in one long heave and the Patience came round, slow, so slow, broad-beamed old cow that she was, and the flood was against them now, pushing them back up towards the Chapel. Behind them the oars were coming on. Four oars at least. A longboat, light and fast, and the men in her knew the channel nearly as well as he did.\n\n\"They'll have us before the Heron Creek,\" Corin said.\n\n\"They won't.\"\n\n\"They will. I can count, Tomas, it's the only thing I'm good for besides lying.\" Corin was pulling off his boots. \"There's a cut on the left — the one with the dead tree. Where does it go?\"\n\n\"Nowhere. Mud. It's a dead end.\"\n\n\"Can a man stand in it?\"\n\n\"At this tide? To his chest, maybe. What are you —\"\n\n\"They want the boat. They'll follow the boat.\" Corin was on the gunwale now, crouched, his face white in the starlight. \"And they want me more than they want you, because I'm the one they've been paying. So when I go over, you keep going, and you make a great deal of noise, and I'll make rather less.\" He grinned suddenly. \"Tell your sister to keep the letter. Tell her it's worth more than I am.\"\n\n\"Corin —\"\n\nHe went over the side without a splash. Tomas never heard him hit the water. One moment there was a man on the gunwale and the next there was the dark and the dead tree going past on the left and nothing else.\n\nHe kept going. He did not know what else to do. He made, as instructed, a great deal of noise — he let the mainsheet run so the block screamed, and he banged the tiller against the thwart, and he shouted Corin's name once, loudly, toward the right-hand bank, as though looking for a man who had fallen in there. Behind him the oars checked, and a voice said something sharp, and the longboat split its attention for a moment between the boat and the bank.\n\nIt was enough to get him into the Heron Creek. It was not enough to get him out of it.\n\nThe Heron was narrow and shallow and he had taken it a thousand times at the top of the tide, but this was not the top of the tide and he was not looking where he was going. The Patience hit the bar at the creek's mouth with a long grinding shudder that went up through the keel and into his teeth, and stopped, and heeled, and he was thrown against the thwart so hard that he heard something in his leg go with a sound like a green stick breaking.\n\nHe lay in the bilge-water and did not scream, because the longboat was still out there.\n\nAfter a time — he did not know how long — he heard the oars go past the mouth of the creek, slow now, searching. A lantern swung. A man's voice said, \"Nothing. She's gone up the Heron and stuck, she'll be there in the morning, we'll have her then,\" and another voice, one he thought he knew, said, \"And the other one?\" and the first voice said, \"Drowned, if he's got any sense.\" Then the oars again, going away, toward the Chapel and the black ship and the salt.\n\nHe waited until he could not hear them. Then he dragged himself up onto the thwart, and looked at his leg, and looked away again quickly. The tide was still making. In an hour the Patience would float off the bar. In an hour he would have to be somewhere else.\n\nThe tower of the Drowned Chapel was a quarter mile off across the marsh, black against the first grey in the east. It was the only dry place for a mile. It was also where the men in the longboat were going.\n\nTomas looked at it for a long time. Then he found the boat-hook, and broke the shaft across his knee to make a splint, and began to bind his leg with the sail-ties, and tried to think of something else.","wordCount":778,"createdAt":1794216609993},{"id":"-PMTuNdMcsG-WL6BUfyZR","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"6iI4LgbwDeFqR_Cruf7mE","text":"She broke the seal in the morning, of course. Corin Ashe had known she would; that was why he had told her not to.\n\nThe storm had blown itself out in the night and left the sky scoured and pale. She sat at her table with a heel of yesterday's bread and slid the blade of her knife under the wax, carefully, so that it lifted in one piece and she could have pressed it back if she had been a different sort of person. Inside was a single sheet, folded twice, in a secretary's hand. At the bottom, a signature she had seen on a hundred harbour warrants. Halvard Seyle, Reeve of Varrow.\n\nThe letter was to Fenn.\n\n_Magister —_\n\n_The sixty from the Kestrel will go with the rest. The Black Mare lies off the Chapel on the spring tide and will take all of it then; there is no other night. Ormund leaves the capital on the twelfth and will be with you within the week, so see that the books are clean before she comes. I need not tell you what a clean book means. The levy is paid in full this year, as every year. You have my word on the Pans._\n\n_— H.S._\n\nShe read it four times. The fourth time she read it aloud, very quietly, to hear whether it said something else when it was spoken. It did not.\n\n_The sixty from the Kestrel._ So it was not six sticks dropped in the harbour. _With the rest._ So it was not only the Kestrel. She thought of all the books on the third floor, the forty years of them on their shelves under the eaves, and of how many times she had written a number into a column without counting the sticks, because the sticks had been counted by somebody else, because the Magister read the margins.\n\n_You have my word on the Pans._ That one she did not understand at all.\n\nShe folded the letter again and looked at the seal, and then she did a thing she was not proud of: she warmed the knife over the lamp and pressed the wax back down, so that from a little way off it looked whole. Then she put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, where it crackled when she breathed, and she went to work.\n\nAurek Stiles was already at the long table. He looked up when she came in and said, \"You look like a woman who's slept on a bag of nails,\" and she said, \"I found the sticks,\" and he said, \"Behind the stove?\" and she said, \"Behind the stove,\" and took them out of her satchel and put them in the bin, all thirty-four.\n\nIt was the first lie she had told at the Salt House. It went down easier than she had expected, and that frightened her more than the letter did.\n\nAt midday she went down to the Magister's room on an errand about the Heron's book and stood in his doorway while he signed it. His hand did not shake. He asked after Tomas, and whether the Patience had come through the storm, and he told her she was the best clerk he had had in thirty years, which he had told her before. She watched him sign and thought: _see that the books are clean._\n\nShe had always believed that she could tell when someone was lying to her. She went back up the stairs knowing that she had been wrong about that, and did not know for how long.","wordCount":599,"createdAt":1794216603674},{"id":"-smzDJyCDhAhhHFoSzCRH","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"3z3uj6msTp-yJfszkLU-n","text":"She went to work. She did not know what else to do with herself, and it seemed important, in a way she could not have explained, that she should be at her place at the long table when the bell went, in her clerk's grey, with her hair pinned, as if nothing had happened. As if she were still the best clerk the Magister had had in thirty years.\n\nAurek glanced up when she came in and opened his mouth to say something about bags of nails, and then saw her face and shut it again.\n\nThe auditor was not there. Her sheet of tiny figures lay on the end of the table under a glass paperweight, and the green ledger was gone from beside it.\n\n\"She's downstairs,\" Aurek said quietly. \"With him. Since first bell.\" He hesitated. \"Ilse. I took the sticks out of the bin.\"\n\nShe turned.\n\n\"The Kestrel's. The day you put them in. I'm not a fool, girl, whatever you think. Nobody finds six sticks behind a stove and then finds them gone again.\" He did not look at her; he looked at his own ledger, at his own tidy upright hand. \"I've been here twenty years. I've written what I was told for three of them. I didn't count. I didn't ask. I told myself the Magister read the margins.\" He turned a page that did not need turning. \"They're in my coat. In the press. If you want them.\"\n\nShe stood very still.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\n\"Because you didn't write anything in the margin,\" said Aurek Stiles. \"You always write in the margin. You've written in the margin every day for four years. And that day you didn't, and I thought: she's frightened. And I thought: I've been frightened for three years and I never once noticed it was the same thing.\"\n\nAt midday the auditor came up the stair and took her sheet of figures from under the paperweight and went down again without a word to either of them. A little after that, the porter came up to say that the Magister wanted Miss Varga in his room.\n\nThe stove was lit. It was always lit. Oduya Fenn sat behind his desk with the green ledger closed in front of him and his long hands folded on top of it, and he did not smile when she came in. That was how she knew he had not slept either.\n\n\"Sit down, Ilse.\"\n\nShe did not sit down. She had decided on the stair that she would tell him, all of it. If he was going to give her name to the Reeve a second time, she wanted to watch him do it.\n\n\"I was at the Chapel last night,\" she said. \"In the tower. With Tomas, who has a broken leg, and has been lying in a window since the night before last while you loaded salt underneath him.\"\n\nThe Magister closed his eyes.\n\n\"I heard you tell the Reeve my name.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"I imagine you did.\" He opened his eyes again and looked at her, and his face was older than she had ever seen it. \"Will you let me tell you how it happened? Not to excuse it. I don't think it can be excused. But I'd like someone to know, and I think you've earned it more than anyone.\"\n\nShe sat down.\n\nHe told her. What Mother Gannet had told her, and what Mother Gannet could not have known: the price of salt going up at Hesk, and the Salt House half empty, and the Exchequer writing every quarter to ask why the revenue was down. And then Halvard Seyle in this room, in that chair, with a bottle of something good, saying: _I can build you a wall, Oduya. All it needs is a little salt that nobody counts._\n\n\"One part in seven,\" the Magister said. \"For one year. To pay for the wall. That was all it was going to be. I told myself it was the Exchequer's own salt paying for the Exchequer's own wall, and if the Exchequer had done its duty there'd have been no need. I told myself a great many things.\" He looked down at his hands. \"The wall was built in a year. And at the end of the year I went to Halvard and said, that's enough, and he said — he said, Oduya, you've been writing false books for a year. Do you want to explain that to the Exchequer, or shall I?\"\n\n\"So you kept writing.\"\n\n\"So I kept writing. And the wall is bad stone, and Halvard has a new house, and the Black Mare comes in on the spring tides and takes one-seventh of the salt of Varrow to wherever it goes.\" He lifted his hands, let them fall. \"And you counted the Kestrel's sticks. I knew you would, one day. You're the only one of them who ever counted.\"\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Did you take it?\"\n\nHis face did not change. \"What letter?\"\n\n\"The Reeve's letter to you. About the Kestrel. I had it.\"\n\nFor a moment the Magister of the Salt House looked at her with an expression she could not read at all, and then, very slowly, he began to laugh. It was not a happy sound.\n\n\"He never sent it,\" he said. \"I never had it. I wondered — I wondered why he was so certain I'd been told about the Black Mare taking a first load on the eighteenth, when I hadn't. He said, _I wrote to you._ And I said, _you didn't._ And we each thought the other was lying.\" He wiped his eyes. \"Oh, Ilse. Somebody stole the Reeve's post. And now somebody has stolen it from you.\"","wordCount":962,"createdAt":1794217061810},{"id":"-ycx5ANhb4efG-22hgJ2V","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"3z3uj6msTp-yJfszkLU-n","text":"She went to work. She did not know what else to do with herself, and it seemed important, in a way she could not have explained, that she should be at her place at the long table when the bell went, in her clerk's grey, with her hair pinned, as if nothing had happened. As if she were still the best clerk the Magister had had in thirty years.\n\nAurek glanced up when she came in and opened his mouth to say something about bags of nails, and then saw her face and shut it again.\n\nThe auditor was not there. Her sheet of tiny figures lay on the end of the table under a glass paperweight, and the green ledger was gone from beside it.\n\n\"She's downstairs,\" Aurek said quietly. \"With him. Since first bell.\" He hesitated. \"Ilse. I took the sticks out of the bin.\"\n\nShe turned.\n\n\"The Kestrel's. The day you put them in. I'm not a fool, girl, whatever you think. Nobody finds six sticks behind a stove and then finds them gone again.\" He did not look at her; he looked at his own ledger, at his own tidy upright hand. \"I've been here twenty years. I've written what I was told for three of them. I didn't count. I didn't ask. I told myself the Magister read the margins.\" He turned a page that did not need turning. \"They're in my coat. In the press. If you want them.\"\n\nShe stood very still.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\n\"Because you didn't write anything in the margin,\" said Aurek Stiles. \"You always write in the margin. You've written in the margin every day for four years. And that day you didn't, and I thought: she's frightened. And I thought: I've been frightened for three years and I never once noticed it was the same thing.\"\n\nAt midday the auditor came up the stair and took her sheet of figures from under the paperweight and went down again without a word to either of them. A little after that, the porter came up to say that the Magister wanted Miss Varga in his room.\n\nThe stove was lit. It was always lit. Oduya Fenn sat behind his desk with the green ledger closed in front of him and his long hands folded on top of it, and he did not smile when she came in. That was how she knew he had not slept either.\n\n\"Sit down, Ilse.\"\n\nShe did not sit down. She had decided on the stair that she would tell him, all of it. If he was going to give her name to the Reeve a second time, she wanted to watch him do it.\n\n\"I was at the Chapel last night,\" she said. \"In the tower. With Tomas, who has a broken leg, and has been lying in a window since the night before last while you loaded salt underneath him.\"\n\nThe Magister closed his eyes.\n\n\"I heard you tell the Reeve my name.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"I imagine you did.\" He opened his eyes again and looked at her, and his face was older than she had ever seen it. \"Will you let me tell you how it happened? Not to excuse it. I don't think it can be excused. But I'd like someone to know, and I think you've earned it more than anyone.\"\n\nShe sat down.\n\nHe told her. What Mother Gannet had told her, and what Mother Gannet could not have known: the price of salt going up at Hesk, and the Salt House half empty, and the Exchequer writing every quarter to ask why the revenue was down. And then Halvard Seyle in this room, in that chair, with a bottle of something good, saying: _I can build you a wall, Oduya. All it needs is a little salt that nobody counts._\n\n\"One part in seven,\" the Magister said. \"For one year. To pay for the wall. That was all it was going to be. I told myself it was the Exchequer's own salt paying for the Exchequer's own wall, and if the Exchequer had done its duty there'd have been no need. I told myself a great many things.\" He looked down at his hands. \"The wall was built in a year. And at the end of the year I went to Halvard and said, that's enough, and he said — he said, Oduya, you've been writing false books for a year. Do you want to explain that to the Exchequer, or shall I?\"\n\n\"So you kept writing.\"\n\n\"So I kept writing. And the wall is bad stone, and Halvard has a new house, and the Black Mare comes in on the spring tides and takes one-seventh of the salt of Varrow to wherever it goes.\" He lifted his hands, let them fall. \"And you counted the Kestrel's sticks. I knew you would, one day. You're the only one of them who ever counted.\"\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Did you take it?\"\n\nHis face did not change. \"What letter?\"\n\n\"The Reeve's letter to you. About the Kestrel. I had it.\"\n\nFor a moment the Magister of the Salt House looked at her with an expression she could not read at all, and then, very slowly, he began to laugh. It was not a happy sound.\n\n\"He never sent it,\" he said. \"I never had it. I wondered — I wondered why he was so certain I'd been told about the Black Mare coming early, when I hadn't. He said, _I wrote to you._ And I said, _you didn't._ And we each thought the other was lying.\" He wiped his eyes. \"Oh, Ilse. Somebody stole the Reeve's post. And now somebody has stolen it from you.\"","wordCount":957,"createdAt":1794217059759},{"id":"03TGhG8k3NeTovUqOZkLN","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"2i5J2Lc7W9L8liiqtAFCG","text":"The Salt House kept its ledgers on the third floor, under the eaves, where the damp could not reach them and the clerks could not reach anything else. Ilse Varga had worked there for four years and had never once been warm.\n\nShe sat at the long table with the Kestrel's book open in front of her and the Kestrel's tally-sticks laid out beside it in a row, like the bones of a small animal. Every sack that went over the side of a salt-ship at Varrow was notched on a stick by the pan-reeve and notched again by the ship's mate, and the stick was split down its length so that each man kept half. When the halves came back to the Salt House they were matched, and counted, and the count went into the book. It was the oldest honest thing in the city. Nobody could cut a notch out of a stick without the other half showing it.\n\nShe counted the sticks again. Thirty-four sticks, ten notches to a stick. Three hundred and forty measures.\n\nThe book said four hundred.\n\n\"You're frowning at it,\" said Aurek Stiles from the far end of the table. He was the senior of the three tallyclerks and had been for twenty years, and he said it without looking up from his own work. \"Frowning at a ledger is how the ink gets into your face.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel,\" Ilse said. \"Her book says four hundred measures out on the ninth. I've got three hundred and forty on the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then you've dropped six sticks behind the stove.\"\n\n\"I haven't.\"\n\n\"Then the mate's dropped them in the harbour. It happens.\" Aurek turned a page. \"Write it as received and put a query in the margin. The Magister reads the margins.\"\n\nShe did not write it as received. She sat with the pen over the page until the ink beaded on the nib and fell, a small black star on the blotter, and then she got up and went down the narrow stair to the Magister's room.\n\nMagister Oduya Fenn had run the Salt House since before Ilse was born. He was a tall man gone thin in the way of tall men who forget to eat, and his room was the only warm one in the building, because he had the only good stove. He looked up when she knocked and smiled at her with real pleasure, which made what she had come to say harder.\n\n\"Ilse. Sit. You look like you've been out on the Pans.\"\n\n\"Only on the third floor, Magister.\" She did not sit. \"The Kestrel's book is sixty measures over her sticks.\"\n\nThe smile stayed exactly where it was. That was the first thing she would remember afterward — not that it went, but that it stayed, a little too long, like a man holding a door that nobody was coming through.\n\n\"Sixty,\" he said.\n\n\"Six sticks. I've counted three times.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel came in through the Gullet in a rising sea. Her mate was sick over the side twice before they'd cleared the bar — your brother will tell you, he took her in.\" Fenn spread his long hands. \"Sticks go missing. The book is written from the manifest at the pans, and the manifest is right.\"\n\n\"Then the sticks should be with the manifest.\"\n\n\"Then they should.\" He said it kindly. \"Write the book as it stands, Ilse. I'll speak to the pan-reeve myself.\"\n\nShe went back up the stairs. Aurek did not ask her what had been said, and she did not tell him. She wrote four hundred in the column in her own clean hand, and in the margin she wrote nothing at all, because the Magister read the margins.\n\nWhen the bell went for the end of the day she put the thirty-four sticks into her satchel instead of into the bin. She did not decide to do it. Her hands did it while she was thinking about something else, and by the time she noticed, she was already on the stair.\n\n## The Quay at Low Water\n[\n  Place: #The Quay\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Tomas Brenn\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?The Kestrel's book is sixty measures short:@Tomas Brenn\n]\n\nVarrow at low water smelt of mud and iron and the sweet rot of weed, and the whole of the lower town came down to the quay to look at the sky.\n\nThere was a storm coming. Ilse could see it from the top of the Gull Stair, a long bruise lying along the horizon to the south-west, with the light behind it gone the colour of a boiled sweet. The harbour had drained down to its channels and the salt-ships sat over on their bilges in the mud, masts leaning together like drunk men holding each other up. Below her the stair went down in its two hundred worn steps to the quay, and the quay was full.\n\nShe found Tomas where she always found him, sitting on an upturned basket outside the pilots' shed with a pipe he never lit, looking at the water as if it owed him money.\n\n\"You took the Kestrel in,\" she said.\n\n\"Hello, Ilse. Good day at the counting? Lovely to see you, Tomas.\" He moved over on the basket to make room for her. \"I took her in on the ninth. Why?\"\n\n\"Was it rough?\"\n\n\"It was the Gullet in a southerly. It's always rough.\" He looked at her properly. Her brother was three years older than her and had their mother's face, broad and patient, and the same habit of knowing what she was going to say before she said it and waiting for her to say it anyway. \"What's wrong with the Kestrel?\"\n\n\"Was her mate sick?\"\n\nTomas laughed. \"Jory Pell? Jory's been at sea since he could walk. He's never been sick in his life. He was singing.\" He stopped laughing. \"Why?\"\n\nShe told him. She kept her voice down, though nobody on the quay was listening to anything but the wind getting up. Tomas listened with the pipe in his teeth.\n\n\"Sixty measures,\" he said at last. \"That's — what is that, in silver?\"\n\n\"At the Hesk price, nine hundred crowns. Near enough.\"\n\nHe whistled, low. \"And the Magister said.\"\n\n\"He said sticks go missing.\"\n\n\"They do.\" But he was frowning now, the way she must have been frowning at the ledger. \"Not six of them off one ship, they don't. A man might lose one. Six is a decision.\" He took the pipe out of his mouth and turned it over. \"What are you going to do?\"\n\n\"I don't know. I wrote it in. Four hundred.\"\n\n\"Ilse.\"\n\n\"What was I supposed to write? He's the Magister. He got me the place.\" She pressed the satchel against her side and felt the sticks shift inside it. \"I took the sticks.\"\n\nTomas looked at the satchel and then at her. \"You took the — that's the Salt House's property.\"\n\n\"I know what it is.\"\n\n\"If anybody knew they were short, they'll know you took them.\"\n\n\"Nobody knows they were short. Aurek thinks I dropped them behind the stove.\"\n\nThe first of the rain came then, a few fat drops striking the stones between their feet, each one leaving a dark coin. All along the quay people began to move, hauling covers over baskets, shouting for children. Out past the harbour mouth the sea had gone a hard, flat grey.\n\n\"Go home,\" Tomas said. \"Put them somewhere dry. Don't do anything else tonight.\" He stood up and knocked the pipe against his palm out of habit, though there was nothing in it. \"I've got to see to the Patience before this comes in. I'll come by tomorrow.\"\n\n\"Tomas —\"\n\n\"Tomorrow.\" He put a hand on her head, the way he had when she was small, and then he was gone into the crowd, a broad back in an oilskin, moving against the people coming up.\n\nShe climbed the Gull Stair in the rain. By the time she reached the top the storm had come in over the bar and the whole harbour was white.\n\n[\n  Place: #Ilse's Room\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Corin Ashe\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The Reeve's letter:@Ilse Varga ^The Kestrel's tally-sticks:#Ilse's Room\n]\n\nHer room was over the cooper's, at the top of a house so narrow that the stair went up through the middle of it like the stone in a plum. She lit the lamp and put the sticks on the table and looked at them for a while, and then she put them inside the hollow of the bolster, which was the only hiding place she had, and felt foolish.\n\nThe rain beat on the slates. Somewhere below her the cooper's wife was singing to the baby, the same three lines over and over.\n\nIt was close to midnight when someone knocked.\n\nShe did not open the door. She stood behind it with the lamp in one hand and the poker in the other and said, \"Who is it?\"\n\n\"A friend of your brother's,\" said a man's voice. It was light and pleasant and not from Varrow — there was something of the capital in the vowels, or something trying to sound like it. \"Though I doubt he'd put it that way. Please don't hit me with whatever you're holding.\"\n\n\"What do you want?\"\n\n\"To give you something. You can open the door a hand's width and I'll put it through, and then I'll go away, and you'll never have to look at me.\" A pause. \"Though I'm told I'm not bad to look at.\"\n\nShe opened the door a hand's width. The man on the landing was perhaps thirty, soaked through, his dark hair plastered to his skull. He had a long, clever face and a scar through one eyebrow and he was smiling as if this were all a great joke that she would be let in on presently.\n\nHe held out a packet wrapped in oilcloth.\n\n\"What is it?\"\n\n\"A letter. Not to you. I think you'd better have it anyway.\" He kept holding it out. \"My name's Corin Ashe. Your brother knows me. He'll tell you I'm a liar and a smuggler, and he'll be right about the second one.\"\n\n\"Why would you give me a letter that isn't to me?\"\n\n\"Because I can read, Miss Varga, and having read it I'd rather it were in the hands of someone who counts sticks than in mine.\" He pushed the packet through the gap. She took it, mostly to stop him dropping it. It was heavier than a letter ought to be. \"There's a seal on it you'll recognise. Don't break it here. Don't break it at all, if you've any sense. Just — keep it somewhere.\"\n\n\"How do you know about the sticks?\"\n\nBut he had already turned. She heard him going down the stair, light and quick, missing the step that creaked as if he knew it was there. The street door opened and shut.\n\nShe closed her own door and bolted it and set the lamp down and turned the packet over in her hands. Under the oilcloth was good paper, folded small, and a seal of red wax pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\nIt was the seal of the Harbour Reeve.\n\nIlse sat on the bed with the letter in her lap and the sticks in the bolster under her and listened to the rain until the lamp burned down.","wordCount":1921,"createdAt":1794213586001},{"id":"0YshHdxOsSZpPOxuK5lMj","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"g2q6xv5SNSaq9VyyZ2uux","text":"Her room was over the cooper's, at the top of a house so narrow that the stair went up through the middle of it like the stone in a plum. She lit the lamp and put the sticks on the table and looked at them for a while, and then she put them inside the hollow of the bolster, which was the only hiding place she had, and felt foolish.\n\nThe rain beat on the slates. Somewhere below her the cooper's wife was singing to the baby, the same three lines over and over.\n\nIt was close to midnight when someone knocked.\n\nShe did not open the door. She stood behind it with the lamp in one hand and the poker in the other and said, \"Who is it?\"\n\n\"A friend of your brother's,\" said a man's voice. It was light and pleasant and not from Varrow — there was something of the capital in the vowels, or something trying to sound like it. \"Though I doubt he'd put it that way. Please don't hit me with whatever you're holding.\"\n\n\"What do you want?\"\n\n\"To give you something. You can open the door a hand's width and I'll put it through, and then I'll go away, and you'll never have to look at me.\" A pause. \"Though I'm told I'm not bad to look at.\"\n\nShe opened the door a hand's width. The man on the landing was perhaps thirty, soaked through, his dark hair plastered to his skull. He had a long, clever face and a scar through one eyebrow and he was smiling as if this were all a great joke that she would be let in on presently.\n\nHe held out a packet wrapped in oilcloth.\n\n\"What is it?\"\n\n\"A letter. Not to you. I think you'd better have it anyway.\" He kept holding it out. \"My name's Corin Ashe. Your brother knows me. He'll tell you I'm a liar and a smuggler, and he'll be right about the second one.\"\n\n\"Why would you give me a letter that isn't to me?\"\n\n\"Because I can read, Miss Varga, and having read it I'd rather it were in the hands of someone who counts sticks than in mine.\" He pushed the packet through the gap. She took it, mostly to stop him dropping it. It was heavier than a letter ought to be. \"There's a seal on it you'll recognise. Don't break it here. Don't break it at all, if you've any sense. Just — keep it somewhere.\"\n\n\"How do you know about the sticks?\"\n\nBut he had already turned. She heard him going down the stair, light and quick, missing the step that creaked as if he knew it was there. The street door opened and shut.\n\nShe closed her own door and bolted it and set the lamp down and turned the packet over in her hands. Under the oilcloth was good paper, folded small, and a seal of red wax pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\nIt was the seal of the Harbour Reeve.\n\nIlse sat on the bed with the letter in her lap and the sticks in the bolster under her and listened to the rain until the lamp burned down.","wordCount":539,"createdAt":1794213785636},{"id":"1-JUe19xwfZL-aURQL1EX","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"GIXhkOMgrF_FKcO5VyMzd","text":"The auditor left Varrow on the twelfth day of Seedmonth, in the same carriage that had come down Tallow Street and frightened the porter, and the whole of Tallow Street came out to see her go.\n\nShe had stayed three weeks longer than she meant to. She had sat in the Magister's room with the firebrick out of the chimney-breast and the Magister's true book open on the desk — a small black book, scorched at the corners, with three years of real numbers in it in a long thin careful hand — and she had copied every page. Then she had sat in the third-floor room with Aurek and Ilse for ten days more and put the real numbers into the real books, page by page, in the margins, in red.\n\n\"The Exchequer will want the Magister in the capital,\" she told Ilse, the last evening, standing at the door of the Salt House with her gloves in her hand. \"There'll be a trial. I'll speak for him. I'll say he kept a true book when he could have kept none, and gave it up when he could have burned it, and that's not nothing.\" She pulled on one glove. \"I don't know what they'll do. Something less than they might have, I hope.\"\n\n\"And the Salt House?\"\n\n\"Will need a Magister.\" Lisbet Ormund pulled on the other glove. \"I've written to say so. I've written to say who.\" She looked up at Ilse with her kindly hen's face. \"Master Stiles has been here twenty years and is a very good clerk, and he told me himself that he would rather go into the sea after the Reeve than have the stove. So I've written your name.\"\n\nIlse opened her mouth and shut it again.\n\n\"You'll say you're too young,\" said the auditor. \"And that you lied to me. You did. You also told me you'd lied, in front of the whole town, which I have never once in thirty years of this work seen anyone do. Your mother would have.\" She held out her small gloved hand, and Ilse took it. \"Count everything, Miss Varga. Write in the margins. Good night.\"\n\nThe carriage went down Tallow Street in the morning with the carters cursing it, and Ilse watched it from the third-floor window until it was gone.\n\nTomas came down the Gull Stair that afternoon on two crutches, which took him nearly an hour and which he would not let anyone help him with, and sat on the bench outside the pilots' shed at the bottom with his splinted leg stuck out in front of him and his unlit pipe in his teeth. Corin Ashe sat beside him. Neither of them said much. Corin had a pilot's oilskin on, which was too big for him, and a pilot's book in his pocket, which Tomas had made him buy, and he was learning the Gullet from the bench, channel by channel, with Tomas drawing it in the dust with the end of a crutch.\n\n\"He'll make a pilot,\" Tomas said to Ilse, when she came down to them at the end of the day. \"Gods know how. He gets everything wrong and then argues with the sea about it.\"\n\n\"I'm very persuasive,\" said Corin.\n\n\"The sea isn't.\"\n\nWren was sitting on the bottom step of the Gull Stair eating a cold potato. Mother Gannet was gutting eels. The salt-ships lay over in the mud on the ebb, and the evening light came down the harbour the colour of honey, and somewhere up on the hill a bell was ringing for the end of the day.\n\nIlse climbed back up to the Salt House as the light went. The third floor was empty; Aurek had gone home. She lit the lamp and sat down at the long table, at her own place, and opened the Kestrel's book at the ninth.\n\nFour hundred, in her own clean hand. And beside it, in the margin, in red, in the auditor's tiny writing: _340. Sticks counted by I.V. L.O._\n\nShe dipped her pen, and turned to a clean page, and began to count.","wordCount":690,"createdAt":1794230234132},{"id":"1SS9BS6zve73RHrGzB06r","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"51sz_3kuWZsw6JR4Y0Ko2","text":"The ledger for the year of the big tide was bound in green, because the binder had run out of brown that winter, and it stood out on the shelf like a wrong note. Lisbet Ormund had it open on the long table when Ilse came up the stair, and beside it the book for the year before, bound in brown, and beside that a sheet of her own paper covered in tiny, very neat figures.\n\nAurek was not at his place. The auditor saw her looking.\n\n\"I've sent Master Stiles to the pan-reeve with a list of questions,\" she said. \"It'll take him most of the day, I'm afraid, because the pan-reeve won't know the answers and will have to send to the Reeve's office for them. Sit down, Miss Varga. Sit there, where I can see you.\"\n\nIlse sat.\n\n\"I want to show you something,\" said the auditor, \"and then I want to ask you something, and I'd like you to think before you answer the second thing, because I shall know if you don't.\" She turned the green ledger round. \"This is Thawmonth, the year of the tide. Here — the week of the flood. Nothing comes in at all, which is what you'd expect. Here, the month after. Very little. Here, the month after that. And here,\" she turned a page, \"is the first month that the books are short against the Hesk price.\"\n\nIlse looked. It was a column of numbers in Aurek's hand, tidy and upright, and beside each a ship's name and a date. There was nothing wrong with any of them.\n\n\"They're not short,\" she said.\n\n\"Not in the book. In the book they're very well. But I have here,\" the auditor tapped her own sheet, \"every cargo of Varrow salt that was sold at Hesk that month, from the Hesk customs ledgers, which I had copied before I left the capital. And the Hesk ledgers say that Varrow sent them a good deal less salt than Varrow's books say it sent. About one part in seven less.\" She sat back. \"One part in seven. Every month since. Three years.\"\n\nOne part in seven. Sixty measures out of four hundred was a little more than that. Ilse kept her face very still and felt the paper crackle under her bodice when she breathed.\n\n\"Somebody,\" said Lisbet Ormund, \"is selling one-seventh of the salt of Varrow somewhere other than Hesk, and the books of the Salt House are written as though they weren't. Now. Here's my question.\" She leaned forward. \"I don't think you're a thief, Miss Varga. I knew your mother. I don't think the Magister is a thief either, not in his heart, though I've been wrong about men's hearts before. But somebody in this building writes the numbers, and somebody tells them which numbers to write. Has anybody ever told you to write a number you knew was wrong?\"\n\nShe thought about the Kestrel's book. Four hundred in her own clean hand.\n\nShe thought about Tomas, who had not come home.\n\nShe thought: _if I say yes, she'll ask who. And if I say who, they'll know it was me. And Tomas is somewhere out in the marsh, and I don't know where, and I don't know who else knows._\n\n\"No, madam,\" she said.\n\nThe auditor looked at her for a long moment. Then she nodded, slowly, and turned the green book back around, and picked up her pen.\n\n\"I'll be here until the spring tide,\" she said, without looking up. \"After that I go back to the capital and write my report with whatever I have. If you think of anything before then, Miss Varga, I'd be very grateful to hear it. I'm at the Anchor, on Rope Walk. Anyone can tell you where it is.\"\n\nIlse went back to her own place and opened the Heron's book and could not see a single number in it.","wordCount":658,"createdAt":1794216608690},{"id":"1_T8cKIkeZ3-EOWg5TjGN","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"LQxthMqMx2ix2u3pYQu7A","text":"The hearing was called for the hour after noon on the day of the spring tide, in the long hall at the top of the town where the Reeve of Varrow had sat in judgement on fishing disputes and drunk sailors for as long as there had been a Reeve. Lisbet Ormund had called it. She had the right; she had shown the clerk of the Hall a paper with the Exchequer's seal on it, and the clerk had gone white and sent for the Reeve, and the Reeve had come, and smiled, and said that of course the auditor must have whatever she needed.\n\nHe sat now in his own chair at the head of the hall, in the harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs. The Magister sat below him on the bench where witnesses sat, very straight, his long hands on his knees. Half the lower town had come up the hill to stand at the back. Ilse could see Mother Gannet's milky eye in the crowd, and Wren's dirty face beside her, and a great many people she had passed on the Gull Stair every day of her life and never spoken to. The old people on the quay had been saying since dawn that there was weather coming in with the tide.\n\nThe auditor stood at a small table in the middle of the floor with her papers in front of her.\n\n\"I'll be brief,\" she said, \"because the tide turns at four and I'm told half of you have boats to see to. I have audited the books of the Salt House of Varrow for the last four years. I find that for the last three of them, one-seventh part of the salt shipped from the Pans has not reached Hesk, and that the books have been written to hide it. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. Miss Varga.\"\n\nIlse stood up.\n\nShe had thought her legs would shake. They did not. She walked out to the table in the middle of the floor in her clerk's grey with her hair pinned and laid the things down on it one by one, the way she would have laid out a ship's sticks to be counted.\n\nThe Kestrel's book, open at the ninth.\n\nThirty-four tally-sticks, which Aurek Stiles had brought up the hill in his coat.\n\nA letter with a red seal pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\n\"On the ninth,\" she said, \"the Kestrel came in through the Gullet with three hundred and forty measures of salt on her sticks. I wrote four hundred in her book, because I was told to. That was a lie, and it was mine.\" She heard the crowd at the back shift and murmur. She did not look at them. \"Two days after I wrote it, the auditor asked me if I'd ever been told to write a number I knew was wrong, and I said no. That was a lie too.\"\n\n\"Miss Varga,\" said the Reeve, pleasantly, from his chair. \"You'll understand that a clerk who admits to two lies in one breath is not —\"\n\n\"And this,\" said Ilse, \"is a letter under your seal, Reeve, to the Magister, telling him where the sixty measures from the Kestrel were to go, and when, and on what ship. It never reached him. A man called Corin Ashe took it out of your post-bag, and gave it to me, and I've kept it.\" She looked up at him. \"You can read it, if you like. Or the auditor can read it aloud. It's quite short.\"\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\nHalvard Seyle looked at the letter on the table, and at the auditor, and at the Magister on the witnesses' bench, who had not moved. Then he looked past them all to the back of the hall, where Corin Ashe was standing against the wall with his arms folded, in a clean coat, and smiled.\n\n\"A smuggler,\" the Reeve said. \"A smuggler steals a letter, and a clerk who lies admits she lies, and an old man —\"\n\n\"I'll speak to the letter,\" said Oduya Fenn.\n\nHe stood up from the bench. He was not looking at the Reeve; he was looking at Ilse, and his face was quite calm.\n\n\"I never received it,\" he said. \"But I know what it says, because I know what every letter Halvard Seyle has written me for three years has said. I'll tell the auditor about every one of them. I'll tell her about the wall. I have kept a book of my own, Madam Auditor — a true one. I kept it because I am a clerk, and I could not bear not to. It is in my room at the Salt House, behind the loose firebrick in the chimney-breast, where the stove's heat never reaches, and it has every measure in it that I ever wrote false.\"\n\nSomebody in the crowd said, loudly, \"Gods,\" and somebody else laughed, high and shocked.\n\nThe Reeve was on his feet.\n\n\"This hearing is closed,\" he said. \"I am the Reeve of Varrow and this is my hall, and I'll not have —\"\n\n\"It isn't closed, Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund mildly. \"It's the Exchequer's hearing. It's only being held in your hall.\" She picked up the letter. \"Sit down, please. I'm going to read this aloud.\"\n\nHe did not sit down. Something went out of his big red face all at once, like air out of a bladder. Then he turned and walked out through the door behind his chair, the one that only the Reeve used.\n\n\"Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund sharply, and then, to the clerk of the Hall: \"Stop that man.\" The clerk of the Hall looked from the Reeve's back to the auditor, and did not move. Nobody stopped him, because nobody in Varrow had ever stopped the Reeve doing anything.","wordCount":988,"createdAt":1794230097791},{"id":"1sX5q_3Ar-ifu1yB06ZqR","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Brenn had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. That was what the forty crowns in his coat pocket were for. He had not come for the forty crowns. He had come because he had gone to find Corin himself, the night before, to ask what a man like that knew about six missing sticks, and Corin had been quiet a while and then said: more than your sister does. Come out on the ebb tomorrow and I'll show you.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter under his seal saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"Right on her night.\"\n\n\"You knew she'd be here.\"\n\n\"I carry the Reeve's post, Tomas. Letters, mostly, and I've a habit of reading them. I knew she'd be here because I carried the letter that said so, and then I carried it somewhere else.\" He sat up. \"The first load tonight. The rest on the spring tide, three nights on.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern: he had brought the Kestrel in through the Gullet on the ninth with that same boat towing astern.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.","wordCount":817,"createdAt":1794217043293},{"id":"23rN8JAGxQFCsuckhkGCw","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Brenn had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. That was what the forty crowns in his coat pocket were for.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter under his seal saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"She's early.\"\n\n\"How early?\"\n\n\"A night. A whole night.\" He sat up. \"The letter said the spring tide. That's tomorrow.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern: he had taken her in through the Gullet on the ninth.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.","wordCount":711,"createdAt":1794216606299},{"id":"2N48Tb_xLnv23cyd8RFrm","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"3z3uj6msTp-yJfszkLU-n","text":"She went to work. She did not know what else to do with herself, and it seemed important, in a way she could not have explained, that she should be at her place at the long table when the bell went, in her clerk's grey, with her hair pinned, as if nothing had happened. As if she were still the best clerk the Magister had had in thirty years.\n\nAurek looked up when she came in and opened his mouth to say something about bags of nails, and then looked at her face properly and shut it again.\n\nThe auditor was not there. Her sheet of tiny figures lay on the end of the table under a glass paperweight, and the green ledger was gone from beside it.\n\n\"She's downstairs,\" Aurek said quietly. \"With him. Since first bell.\" He hesitated. \"Ilse. I took the sticks out of the bin.\"\n\nShe turned.\n\n\"The Kestrel's. The night you put them in. I'm not a fool, girl, whatever you think. Nobody finds six sticks behind a stove and then finds them gone again.\" He did not look at her; he looked at his own ledger, at his own tidy upright hand. \"I've been here twenty years. I've written what I was told for three of them. I didn't count. I didn't ask. I told myself the Magister read the margins.\" He turned a page that did not need turning. \"They're in my coat. In the press. If you want them.\"\n\nShe stood very still.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\n\"Because you didn't write anything in the margin,\" said Aurek Stiles. \"You always write in the margin. You've written in the margin every day for four years. And that day you didn't, and I thought: she's frightened. And I thought: I've been frightened for three years and I never once noticed it was the same thing.\"\n\nAt midday the auditor came up the stair and took her sheet of figures from under the paperweight and went down again without a word to either of them. A little after that, the porter came up to say that the Magister wanted Miss Varga in his room.\n\nThe stove was lit. It was always lit. Oduya Fenn sat behind his desk with the green ledger closed in front of him and his long hands folded on top of it, and he did not smile when she came in. That was how she knew he had not slept either.\n\n\"Sit down, Ilse.\"\n\nShe did not sit down.\n\n\"I was at the Chapel last night,\" she said. \"In the tower. With Tomas, who has a broken leg, and has been lying in a window for two days while you loaded salt underneath him.\"\n\nThe Magister closed his eyes.\n\n\"I heard you tell the Reeve my name.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"I imagine you did.\" He opened his eyes again and looked at her, and his face was older than she had ever seen it. \"Will you let me tell you how it happened? Not to excuse it. I don't think it can be excused. But I'd like someone to know, and I think you've earned it more than anyone.\"\n\nShe sat down.\n\nHe told her. The tide in Thawmonth that took the wall and the pans and the pan-reeve's wife. The letter to the capital, and the letter back, _a matter for the harbour._ A whole year of the pan-men sitting on the quay, and the price of salt going up at Hesk, and the Salt House half empty, and the Exchequer writing every quarter to ask why the revenue was down. And then Halvard Seyle in this room, in that chair, with a bottle of something good, saying: _I can build you a wall, Oduya. All it needs is a little salt that nobody counts._\n\n\"One part in seven,\" the Magister said. \"For one year. To pay for the wall. That was all it was going to be. I told myself it was the Exchequer's own salt paying for the Exchequer's own wall, and if the Exchequer had done its duty there'd have been no need. I told myself a great many things.\" He looked down at his hands. \"The wall was built in a year. And at the end of the year I went to Halvard and said, that's enough, and he said — he said, Oduya, you've been writing false books for a year. Do you want to explain that to the Exchequer, or shall I?\"\n\n\"So you kept writing.\"\n\n\"So I kept writing. And the wall is bad stone, and Halvard has a new house, and the Black Mare comes in on the spring tides and takes one-seventh of the salt of Varrow to wherever it goes.\" He lifted his hands, let them fall. \"And you counted the Kestrel's sticks. I knew you would, one day. You're the only one of them who ever counted.\"\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Did you take it?\"\n\nHis face did not change. \"What letter?\"\n\n\"The Reeve's letter to you. About the Kestrel. I had it.\"\n\nFor a moment the Magister of the Salt House looked at her with an expression she could not read at all, and then, very slowly, he began to laugh. It was not a happy sound.\n\n\"He never sent it,\" he said. \"I never had it. I wondered — I wondered why he was so certain I'd been told about the Black Mare coming early, when I hadn't. He said, _I wrote to you._ And I said, _you didn't._ And we each thought the other was lying.\" He wiped his eyes. \"Oh, Ilse. Somebody stole the Reeve's post. And now somebody has stolen it from you.\"","wordCount":948,"createdAt":1794216650325},{"id":"2drD73jPc1SLrBJmG6giy","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"r9SBgyYpFJBk2b1o5XkXt","text":"They brought Tomas out ahead of the flood, the two of them, Ilse under one arm and Wren under the other, and it took four hours, and Ilse did not afterwards remember much of it except the cold and the weight and her brother's breath hissing in her ear at every step. Wren found them the hard every time.\n\nAt the edge of the marsh, where the path came up onto the sea-wall by the Pans, they left Tomas with a pan-man Wren knew, who asked no questions and sent his own son running for a bone-setter, and then Ilse walked home alone through the waking streets with the sun coming up behind her.\n\nThe Magister had said her name. She kept hearing it. Not _the clerk_, not _one of my girls_. _The Varga girl_, in that tired voice she had loved for four years, like a man putting down something heavy.\n\nShe climbed the stair through the middle of the cooper's house. The step that creaked, creaked. The cooper's wife was not singing.\n\nHer door was open.\n\nNot broken. Open, the way you leave a door when you have a key and are in a hurry. Inside, the room looked almost as she had left it — the table, the chair, the lamp-hook — except that everything had been moved a little and put back a little wrong, the way a stranger puts things back. The chair faced the wrong wall. Her mother's comb was on the left of the shelf instead of the right.\n\nShe went back down the stair. The cooper's wife was in the kitchen with the baby on her hip and the door half shut, and when Ilse knocked on it she opened it no wider.\n\n\"Was anybody up?\" Ilse said. \"In the night. To my room.\"\n\n\"I was asleep.\" The cooper's wife did not look at her. She looked at the baby, who was chewing on a wooden spoon. \"We were all asleep. The cooper's been at the Pans all week, you know that. There's nobody here but me.\"\n\n\"My door was open.\"\n\n\"Then you left it open. You were out all night, weren't you? Out in the marsh, by the look of your boots.\" The woman's eyes went to the boots and came away quickly. \"I don't ask where my lodgers go, Miss Varga. I'd thank you not to ask me where I sleep.\"\n\n\"Somebody had a key.\"\n\n\"Then somebody had a key.\" The cooper's wife began to close the door. \"There's three keys to that room and one of them's been lost since Midwinter. Ask the cooper. When he's home.\"\n\nThe door shut. Behind it the baby began to cry, and the woman began to sing to it, the same three lines over and over, too loud.\n\nIlse stood in the passage for a while. Then she went back up.\n\nHer room was cold. Someone had let the lamp burn down to nothing and the wick had charred, and there was a smell in the air that was not hers — wet wool, and tar, and something sharper, like cloves.\n\nShe stood in the middle of the floor with her hands at her sides and tried to think the way her mother would have thought. Who had known she was going into the marsh. Who had known she would not be home. Wren had known. Mother Gannet had known. Anyone who had seen her go down Tallow Street at dusk in her mother's boots had known, and half the lower town had.\n\nIt did not narrow anything. It only made the room feel larger, and full of people.\n\nThe bolster lay on the bed with its seam cut open from end to end.\n\nShe did not need to look. She looked anyway. She put her hand into the hollow and felt the feathers and the ticking and nothing else, and then she sat down on the bed with the feathers settling around her like snow, and for the first time since the Kestrel's sticks she let herself cry.\n\nShe had not told anyone where she kept it. Not Tomas. Not Wren. Not the auditor, who she had lied to.\n\nShe had told no one, and someone had known.","wordCount":706,"createdAt":1794217053561},{"id":"3OOPy2H4wv5jH63MlusEj","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"kACrUDbXsjGSKBXJOWAnj","text":"Corin Ashe came back on the night before the spring tide, the way he had come the first time: a knock after midnight, a voice on the landing, a request not to be hit with whatever she was holding.\n\nThis time she opened the door all the way.\n\nHe looked terrible. He had a cut along his jaw that someone had stitched badly, and his clothes were not his own, and he was thinner than a man ought to get in four days. But he was smiling, and when he saw her face the smile went crooked and then went away altogether.\n\n\"Tomas,\" he said. \"Is he —\"\n\n\"He's alive. His leg's broken. He's at the Pans with a man called Hobb.\" She stood aside. \"Come in. You're letting the cold in.\"\n\nHe came in and stood in the middle of her small room and looked at the bolster, which she had sewn up again, badly, and at the feathers that were still in the corners because she had not been able to bring herself to sweep them.\n\n\"I'm sorry about that,\" he said.\n\nShe had known. She had known since the Magister laughed. But she said, \"You,\" anyway, because it needed saying.\n\n\"Me.\" He reached inside his borrowed coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilcloth and held it out to her, exactly as he had on the first night. \"Everybody hides things in the bolster, Ilse. I've been in forty rooms in this city. It's always the bolster or the chimney, and you don't have a chimney.\"\n\nShe did not take it. \"Why?\"\n\n\"Because Seyle's men were coming up Tallow Street with a key the cooper's wife had been paid for, and I got there first. Because I knew where they'd look.\" He was still holding it out. \"And because I thought — when I came up out of that creek, half drowned, with the Reeve's men going past me close enough to touch — I thought, I could sell this. I could sell this back to Halvard Seyle for a great deal of money and go to Hesk and never come back. And I had it in my hand on the Hesk road for a whole day before I turned round.\"\n\n\"Why did you turn round?\"\n\nHe thought about it. She watched him think about it, and was surprised to find that she believed him when he answered.\n\n\"Because you didn't hit me with the poker,\" he said. \"The first night. You could have. You had it right there behind the door, I saw the end of it. And you opened the door a hand's width instead, and took a stranger's letter, and kept it.\" He shrugged. \"Nobody's ever done that. Usually they hit me.\"\n\nShe took the letter. The seal was still pressed down where she had warmed it with the knife. He had not opened it again.\n\n\"The auditor's at the Anchor,\" she said. \"On Rope Walk.\"\n\n\"I know where the Anchor is.\"\n\n\"Will you come with me?\"\n\nCorin Ashe looked at her for a long moment, the long clever face in the lamplight, the scar through his eyebrow.\n\n\"I'm a smuggler,\" he said. \"And a liar. Nobody will believe a word I say.\"\n\n\"They'll believe the sticks,\" said Ilse. \"Aurek has the sticks. And they'll believe the letter. And they'll believe me, because I'm going to tell them I lied, and nobody says that unless it's true.\" She put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, and reached for her coat. \"You only have to stand there.\"\n# The Last Tally\n\n[\n  Place: #The Reeve's Hall\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Lisbet Ormund @@Halvard Seyle @@Oduya Fenn @@Aurek Stiles @@Corin Ashe @@Mother Gannet @@Wren\n  When: +1 day\n  Items: ^The Reeve's letter:@Lisbet Ormund\n  Facts: ?Fenn is in it with the Reeve:@Lisbet Ormund ?Fenn kept a true book:@Lisbet Ormund ?Fenn kept a true book:reader\n]\n\nThe hearing was called for the hour after noon on the day of the spring tide, in the long hall at the top of the town where the Reeve of Varrow had sat in judgement on fishing disputes and drunk sailors for as long as there had been a Reeve. Lisbet Ormund had called it. She had the right; she had shown the clerk of the Hall a paper with the Exchequer's seal on it, and the clerk had gone white and sent for the Reeve, and the Reeve had come, and smiled, and said that of course the auditor must have whatever she needed.\n\nHe sat now in his own chair at the head of the hall, in the harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs. The Magister sat below him on the bench where witnesses sat, very straight, his long hands on his knees. Half the lower town had come up the hill to stand at the back. Ilse could see Mother Gannet's milky eye in the crowd, and Wren's dirty face beside her, and a great many people she had passed on the Gull Stair every day of her life and never spoken to.\n\nThe auditor stood at a small table in the middle of the floor with her papers in front of her.\n\n\"I'll be brief,\" she said, \"because the tide turns at four and I'm told half of you have boats to see to. I have audited the books of the Salt House of Varrow for the last four years. I find that for the last three of them, one-seventh part of the salt shipped from the Pans has not reached Hesk, and that the books have been written to hide it. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. Miss Varga.\"\n\nIlse stood up.\n\nShe had thought her legs would shake. They did not. She walked out to the table in the middle of the floor in her clerk's grey with her hair pinned and laid the things down on it one by one, the way she would have laid out a ship's sticks to be counted.\n\nThe Kestrel's book, open at the ninth.\n\nThirty-four tally-sticks, which Aurek Stiles had brought up the hill in his coat.\n\nA letter with a red seal pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\n\"On the ninth,\" she said, \"the Kestrel came in through the Gullet with three hundred and forty measures of salt on her sticks. I wrote four hundred in her book, because I was told to. That was a lie, and it was mine.\" She heard the crowd at the back shift and murmur. She did not look at them. \"Two days later the auditor asked me if I'd ever been told to write a number I knew was wrong, and I said no. That was a lie too.\"\n\n\"Miss Varga,\" said the Reeve, pleasantly, from his chair. \"You'll understand that a clerk who admits to two lies in one breath is not —\"\n\n\"And this,\" said Ilse, \"is a letter in your hand, Reeve, to the Magister, telling him where the sixty measures from the Kestrel were to go, and when, and on what ship. It never reached him. A man called Corin Ashe took it out of your post-bag, and gave it to me, and I've kept it.\" She looked up at him. \"You can read it, if you like. Or the auditor can read it aloud. It's quite short.\"\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\nHalvard Seyle looked at the letter on the table, and at the auditor, and at the Magister on the witnesses' bench, who had not moved. Then he looked past them all to the back of the hall, where Corin Ashe was standing against the wall with his arms folded, in a clean coat, and smiled.\n\n\"A smuggler,\" the Reeve said. \"A smuggler steals a letter, and a clerk who lies admits she lies, and an old man —\"\n\n\"I'll speak to the letter,\" said Oduya Fenn.\n\nHe stood up from the bench. He was not looking at the Reeve; he was looking at Ilse, and his face was quite calm.\n\n\"I never received it,\" he said. \"But I know what it says, because I know what every letter Halvard Seyle has written me for three years has said. I'll tell the auditor about every one of them. I'll tell her about the wall. I have kept a book of my own, Madam Auditor — a true one. I kept it because I am a clerk, and I could not bear not to. It is in the stove in my room at the Salt House, behind the firebrick, and it has every measure in it that I ever wrote false.\"\n\nSomebody in the crowd said, very loudly, \"Gods,\" and somebody else laughed, high and shocked.\n\nThe Reeve was on his feet.\n\n\"This hearing is closed,\" he said. \"I am the Reeve of Varrow and this is my hall, and I'll not have —\"\n\n\"It isn't closed, Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund mildly. \"It's the Exchequer's hearing. It's only being held in your hall.\" She picked up the letter. \"Sit down, please. I'm going to read this aloud.\"\n\nHe did not sit down. He looked at the auditor, and at the Magister, and at the crowd filling the back of the hall, and something went out of his big red face all at once, like air out of a bladder. Then he turned and walked out through the door behind his chair, the one that only the Reeve used, and nobody stopped him, because nobody in Varrow had ever stopped the Reeve doing anything.","wordCount":1596,"createdAt":1794213959653},{"id":"3lRP6Bn6gaah-7p8UX2a0","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"kACrUDbXsjGSKBXJOWAnj","text":"Corin Ashe came back on the night before the spring tide, the way he had come the first time: a knock after midnight, a voice on the landing, a request not to be hit with whatever she was holding.\n\nThis time she opened the door all the way.\n\nHe looked terrible. He had a cut along his jaw that someone had stitched badly, and his clothes were not his own, and he was thinner than a man ought to get in four days. But he was smiling, and when he saw her face the smile went crooked and then went away altogether.\n\n\"Tomas,\" he said. \"Is he —\"\n\n\"He's alive. His leg's broken. He's at the Pans with a man called Hobb.\" She stood aside. \"Come in. You're letting the cold in.\"\n\nHe came in and stood in the middle of her small room and looked at the bolster, which she had sewn up again, badly, and at the feathers that were still in the corners because she had not been able to bring herself to sweep them.\n\n\"I'm sorry about that,\" he said.\n\nShe had known. She had known since the Magister laughed. But she said, \"You,\" anyway, because it needed saying.\n\n\"Me.\" He reached inside his borrowed coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilcloth and held it out to her, exactly as he had on the first night. \"Everybody hides things in the bolster, Ilse. I've been in forty rooms in this city. It's always the bolster or the chimney, and you don't have a chimney.\"\n\nShe did not take it. \"Why?\"\n\n\"Because Seyle's men were coming up Tallow Street with a key the cooper's wife had been paid for, and I got there first. Because I knew where they'd look.\" He was still holding it out. \"And because I thought — when I came up out of that creek, half drowned, with the Reeve's men going past me close enough to touch — I thought, I could sell this. I could sell this back to Halvard Seyle for a great deal of money and go to Hesk and never come back. And I had it in my hand on the Hesk road for a whole day before I turned round.\"\n\n\"Why did you turn round?\"\n\nHe thought about it. She watched him think about it, and was surprised to find that she believed him when he answered.\n\n\"Because you didn't hit me with the poker,\" he said. \"The first night. You could have. You had it right there behind the door, I saw the end of it. And you opened the door a hand's width instead, and took a stranger's letter, and kept it.\" He shrugged. \"Nobody's ever done that. Usually they hit me.\"\n\nShe took the letter. The seal was still pressed down where she had warmed it with the knife. He had not opened it again.\n\n\"The auditor's at the Anchor,\" she said. \"On Rope Walk.\"\n\n\"I know where the Anchor is.\"\n\n\"Will you come with me?\"\n\nCorin Ashe looked at her for a long moment, the long clever face in the lamplight, the scar through his eyebrow.\n\n\"I'm a smuggler,\" he said. \"And a liar. Nobody will believe a word I say.\"\n\n\"They'll believe the sticks,\" said Ilse. \"Aurek has the sticks. And they'll believe the letter. And they'll believe me, because I'm going to tell them I lied, and nobody says that unless it's true.\" She put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, and reached for her coat. \"You only have to stand there.\"","wordCount":595,"createdAt":1794216652821},{"id":"4er3Y14acw9XjaLqulNvm","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"LQxthMqMx2ix2u3pYQu7A","text":"The hearing was called for the hour after noon on the day of the spring tide, in the long hall at the top of the town where the Reeve of Varrow had sat in judgement on fishing disputes and drunk sailors for as long as there had been a Reeve. Lisbet Ormund had called it. She had the right; she had shown the clerk of the Hall a paper with the Exchequer's seal on it, and the clerk had gone white and sent for the Reeve, and the Reeve had come, and smiled, and said that of course the auditor must have whatever she needed.\n\nHe sat now in his own chair at the head of the hall, in the harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs. The Magister sat below him on the bench where witnesses sat, very straight, his long hands on his knees. Half the lower town had come up the hill to stand at the back. Ilse could see Mother Gannet's milky eye in the crowd, and Wren's dirty face beside her, and a great many people she had passed on the Gull Stair every day of her life and never spoken to. The old people on the quay had been saying since dawn that there was weather coming in with the tide.\n\nThe auditor stood at a small table in the middle of the floor with her papers in front of her.\n\n\"I'll be brief,\" she said, \"because the tide turns at four and I'm told half of you have boats to see to. I have audited the books of the Salt House of Varrow for the last four years. I find that for the last three of them, one-seventh part of the salt shipped from the Pans has not reached Hesk, and that the books have been written to hide it. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. Miss Varga.\"\n\nIlse stood up.\n\nShe had thought her legs would shake. They did not. She walked out to the table in the middle of the floor in her clerk's grey with her hair pinned and laid the things down on it one by one, the way she would have laid out a ship's sticks to be counted.\n\nThe Kestrel's book, open at the ninth.\n\nThirty-four tally-sticks, which Aurek Stiles had brought up the hill in his coat.\n\nA letter with a red seal pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\n\"On the ninth,\" she said, \"the Kestrel came in through the Gullet with three hundred and forty measures of salt on her sticks. I wrote four hundred in her book, because I was told to. That was a lie, and it was mine.\" She heard the crowd at the back shift and murmur. She did not look at them. \"Two days after I wrote it, the auditor asked me if I'd ever been told to write a number I knew was wrong, and I said no. That was a lie too.\"\n\n\"Miss Varga,\" said the Reeve, pleasantly, from his chair. \"You'll understand that a clerk who admits to two lies in one breath is not —\"\n\n\"And this,\" said Ilse, \"is a letter under your seal, Reeve, to the Magister, telling him where the sixty measures from the Kestrel were to go, and when, and on what ship. It never reached him. A man called Corin Ashe took it out of your post-bag, and gave it to me, and I've kept it.\" She looked up at him. \"You can read it, if you like. Or the auditor can read it aloud. It's quite short.\"\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\nHalvard Seyle looked at the letter on the table, and at the auditor, and at the Magister on the witnesses' bench, who had not moved. Then he looked past them all to the back of the hall, where Corin Ashe was standing against the wall with his arms folded, in a clean coat, and smiled.\n\n\"A smuggler,\" the Reeve said. \"A smuggler steals a letter, and a clerk who lies admits she lies, and an old man —\"\n\n\"I'll speak to the letter,\" said Oduya Fenn.\n\nHe stood up from the bench. He was not looking at the Reeve; he was looking at Ilse, and his face was quite calm.\n\n\"I never received it,\" he said. \"But I know what it says, because I know what every letter Halvard Seyle has written me for three years has said. I'll tell the auditor about every one of them. I'll tell her about the wall. I have kept a book of my own, Madam Auditor — a true one. I kept it because I am a clerk, and I could not bear not to. It is in my room at the Salt House, behind the loose firebrick in the chimney-breast, where the stove's heat never reaches, and it has every measure in it that I ever wrote false.\"\n\nSomebody in the crowd said, loudly, \"Gods,\" and somebody else laughed, high and shocked.\n\nThe Reeve was on his feet.\n\n\"This hearing is closed,\" he said. \"I am the Reeve of Varrow and this is my hall, and I'll not have —\"\n\n\"It isn't closed, Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund mildly. \"It's the Exchequer's hearing. It's only being held in your hall.\" She picked up the letter. \"Sit down, please. I'm going to read this aloud.\"\n\nHe did not sit down. Something went out of his big red face all at once, like air out of a bladder. Then he turned and walked out through the door behind his chair, the one that only the Reeve used.\n\n\"Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund sharply, and then, to the clerk of the Hall: \"Stop that man.\" The clerk of the Hall looked from the Reeve's back, and at the auditor, and did not move. Nobody stopped him, because nobody in Varrow had ever stopped the Reeve doing anything.","wordCount":989,"createdAt":1794230097734},{"id":"4h0xufqkG4vLxCtT6Lvm7","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"g2q6xv5SNSaq9VyyZ2uux","text":"Her room was over the cooper's, at the top of a house so narrow that the stair went up through the middle of it like the stone in a plum. She lit the lamp and put the sticks on the table and sat with them for a while, and then she put them inside the hollow of the bolster, which was the only hiding place she had, and felt foolish.\n\nThe rain beat on the slates. Somewhere below her the cooper's wife was singing to the baby, the same three lines over and over.\n\nIt was close to midnight when someone knocked.\n\nShe did not open the door. She stood behind it with the lamp in one hand and the poker in the other and said, \"Who is it?\"\n\n\"A friend of your brother's,\" said a man's voice. It was light and pleasant and not from Varrow — there was something of the capital in the vowels, or something trying to sound like it. \"Though I doubt he'd put it that way. Please don't hit me with whatever you're holding.\"\n\n\"What do you want?\"\n\n\"To give you something. You can open the door a hand's width and I'll put it through, and then I'll go away, and you'll never have to look at me.\" A pause. \"Though I'm told I'm not bad to look at.\"\n\nShe opened the door a hand's width. The man on the landing was perhaps thirty, soaked through, his dark hair plastered to his skull. He had a long, clever face and a scar through one eyebrow and he was smiling as if this were all a great joke that she would be let in on presently.\n\nHe held out a packet wrapped in oilcloth.\n\n\"What is it?\"\n\n\"A letter. Not to you. I think you'd better have it anyway.\" He kept holding it out. \"My name's Corin Ashe. Your brother knows me. He'll tell you I'm a liar and a smuggler, and he'll be right about the second one.\"\n\n\"Why would you give me a letter that isn't to me?\"\n\n\"Because I can read, Miss Varga, and having read it I'd rather it were in the hands of someone who counts sticks than in mine.\" He pushed the packet through the gap. She took it, mostly to stop him dropping it. \"There's a seal on it you'll recognise. Don't break it here. Don't break it at all, if you've any sense. Just — keep it somewhere.\"\n\n\"How do you know about the sticks?\"\n\nBut he had already turned. She heard him going down the stair, light and quick, missing the step that creaked as if he knew it was there. The street door opened and shut.\n\nShe closed her own door and bolted it and set the lamp down and turned the packet over in her hands. Under the oilcloth was good paper, folded small, and a seal of red wax pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\nIt was the seal of the Harbour Reeve.\n\nIlse sat on the bed with the letter in her lap and the sticks in the bolster under her and listened to the rain until the lamp burned down.","wordCount":530,"createdAt":1794217030963},{"id":"5ShbwCTkQ6kCB9DHTveZK","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"r9SBgyYpFJBk2b1o5XkXt","text":"They brought Tomas out on the last of the ebb, the two of them, Ilse under one arm and Wren under the other, and it took four hours, and Ilse did not afterward remember much of it except the cold and the weight and her brother's breath hissing in her ear at every step. Wren found them the hard every time. At the edge of the marsh, where the path came up onto the sea-wall by the Pans, they left Tomas with a pan-man Wren knew, who asked no questions and sent his own son running for a bone-setter, and then Ilse walked home alone through the waking streets with the sun coming up behind her.\n\nThe Magister had said her name. She kept hearing it. Not *the clerk*, not *one of my girls*. *The Varga girl*, in that tired voice she had loved for four years, like a man putting down something heavy.\n\nShe climbed the stair through the middle of the cooper's house. The step that creaked, creaked. The cooper's wife was not singing.\n\nHer door was open.\n\nNot broken. Open, the way you leave a door when you have a key and are in a hurry. Inside, the room looked almost as she had left it — the table, the chair, the lamp-hook — except that everything had been moved a little and put back a little wrong, the way a stranger puts things back. The chair faced the wrong wall. Her mother's comb was on the left of the shelf instead of the right.\n\nThe bolster lay on the bed with its seam cut open from end to end.\n\nShe did not need to look. She looked anyway. She put her hand into the hollow and felt the feathers and the ticking and nothing else, and then she sat down on the bed with the feathers settling around her like snow, and for the first time since the Kestrel's sticks she let herself cry.\n\nShe had not told anyone where she kept it. Not Tomas. Not Wren. Not the auditor, who she had lied to.\n\nShe had told no one, and someone had known.\n# Weights and Measures\n## The Magister's Stove\n[\n  Place: #The Salt House\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Aurek Stiles @@Oduya Fenn @@Lisbet Ormund\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The Kestrel's tally-sticks:@Aurek Stiles\n  Facts: ?The Reeve's letter never reached Fenn:@Ilse Varga\n]\n\nShe went to work. She did not know what else to do with herself, and it seemed important, in a way she could not have explained, that she should be at her place at the long table when the bell went, in her clerk's grey, with her hair pinned, as if nothing had happened. As if she were still the best clerk the Magister had had in thirty years.\n\nAurek looked up when she came in and opened his mouth to say something about bags of nails, and then looked at her face properly and shut it again.\n\nThe auditor was not there. Her sheet of tiny figures lay on the end of the table under a glass paperweight, and the green ledger was gone from beside it.\n\n\"She's downstairs,\" Aurek said quietly. \"With him. Since first bell.\" He hesitated. \"Ilse. I took the sticks out of the bin.\"\n\nShe turned.\n\n\"The Kestrel's. The night you put them in. I'm not a fool, girl, whatever you think. Nobody finds six sticks behind a stove and then finds them gone again.\" He did not look at her; he looked at his own ledger, at his own tidy upright hand. \"I've been here twenty years. I've written what I was told for three of them. I didn't count. I didn't ask. I told myself the Magister read the margins.\" He turned a page that did not need turning. \"They're in my coat. In the press. If you want them.\"\n\nShe stood very still.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\n\"Because you didn't write anything in the margin,\" said Aurek Stiles. \"You always write in the margin. You've written in the margin every day for four years. And that day you didn't, and I thought: she's frightened. And I thought: I've been frightened for three years and I never once noticed it was the same thing.\"\n\nAt midday the auditor came up the stair and took her sheet of figures from under the paperweight and went down again without a word to either of them. A little after that, the porter came up to say that the Magister wanted Miss Varga in his room.\n\nThe stove was lit. It was always lit. Oduya Fenn sat behind his desk with the green ledger closed in front of him and his long hands folded on top of it, and he did not smile when she came in. That was how she knew he had not slept either.\n\n\"Sit down, Ilse.\"\n\nShe did not sit down.\n\n\"I was at the Chapel last night,\" she said. \"In the tower. With Tomas, who has a broken leg, and has been lying in a window for two days while you loaded salt underneath him.\"\n\nThe Magister closed his eyes.\n\n\"I heard you tell the Reeve my name.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"I imagine you did.\" He opened his eyes again and looked at her, and his face was older than she had ever seen it. \"Will you let me tell you how it happened? Not to excuse it. I don't think it can be excused. But I'd like someone to know, and I think you've earned it more than anyone.\"\n\nShe sat down.\n\nHe told her. The tide in Thawmonth that took the wall and the pans and the pan-reeve's wife. The letter to the capital, and the letter back, *a matter for the harbour.* A whole year of the pan-men sitting on the quay, and the price of salt going up at Hesk, and the Salt House half empty, and the Exchequer writing every quarter to ask why the revenue was down. And then Halvard Seyle in this room, in that chair, with a bottle of something good, saying: *I can build you a wall, Oduya. All it needs is a little salt that nobody counts.*\n\n\"One part in seven,\" the Magister said. \"For one year. To pay for the wall. That was all it was going to be. I told myself it was the Exchequer's own salt paying for the Exchequer's own wall, and if the Exchequer had done its duty there'd have been no need. I told myself a great many things.\" He looked down at his hands. \"The wall was built in a year. And at the end of the year I went to Halvard and said, that's enough, and he said — he said, Oduya, you've been writing false books for a year. Do you want to explain that to the Exchequer, or shall I?\"\n\n\"So you kept writing.\"\n\n\"So I kept writing. And the wall is bad stone, and Halvard has a new house, and the Black Mare comes in on the spring tides and takes one-seventh of the salt of Varrow to wherever it goes.\" He lifted his hands, let them fall. \"And you counted the Kestrel's sticks. I knew you would, one day. You're the only one of them who ever counted.\"\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Did you take it?\"\n\nHis face did not change. \"What letter?\"\n\n\"The Reeve's letter to you. About the Kestrel. I had it.\"\n\nFor a moment the Magister of the Salt House looked at her with an expression she could not read at all, and then, very slowly, he began to laugh. It was not a happy sound.\n\n\"He never sent it,\" he said. \"I never had it. I wondered — I wondered why he was so certain I'd been told about the Black Mare coming early, when I hadn't. He said, *I wrote to you.* And I said, *you didn't.* And we each thought the other was lying.\" He wiped his eyes. \"Oh, Ilse. Somebody stole the Reeve's post. And now somebody has stolen it from you.\"\n\n## The Anchor on Rope Walk\n[\n  Place: #The Anchor\n  Characters: @@Lisbet Ormund @@Halvard Seyle\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?The Reeve is skimming the salt:@Lisbet Ormund\n]\n\nLisbet Ormund had taken the best room at the Anchor, which was not saying a great deal, and had spent the first evening moving the furniture so that the table caught the light from the window and the bed did not catch the draught from the door. She was at the table now, with her shoes off and her stockinged feet on a hot brick wrapped in flannel, writing her report.\n\nShe had written it three times. Each time it said the same thing — *one part in seven, three years, the Salt House books written to conceal it* — and each time it ended in the same place, which was *I have no proof of who.*\n\nWhen the landlord knocked to say the Reeve was below and wished to call on her, she said, \"Yes, of course, send him up,\" and put her shoes back on, and turned her report face down on the table, and put the hot brick under the bed.\n\nHalvard Seyle filled the doorway. He was a big man and knew it, and he had come in his harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs, and he bowed to her with great courtesy and said he hoped she was comfortable, and that Varrow was not too rough for a lady from the capital, and that if there was anything at all she needed she had only to send to the Reeve's Hall.\n\n\"How kind,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Do sit down.\"\n\nHe sat. The chair complained.\n\n\"I hear you've been at the Salt House books,\" he said.\n\n\"I have.\"\n\n\"Poor Fenn. He's not the man he was. The flood hit him very hard — he took it personally, you know, as though the sea had done it to spite him. I've had to carry a good deal of the work since.\" He smiled. \"If you've found anything amiss, I'd hope you'd come to me first. Before the capital. It would be a shame to ruin an old man's name over a few clerical errors that the harbour could put right quietly.\"\n\n\"A few clerical errors,\" the auditor repeated.\n\n\"Sticks go missing,\" said the Reeve. \"It happens all the time.\"\n\nLisbet Ormund looked at him for a long moment with her kindly hen's face. Then she turned her report over on the table and pushed it across to him.\n\n\"One part in seven,\" she said. \"Every month for three years. That is not a few clerical errors, Reeve. That is twenty-two thousand crowns of the Exchequer's revenue, near enough, and it is going somewhere, and it is not going to Hesk.\" She folded her hands. \"I'll tell you what I have, since you've been good enough to call. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. I have a tallyclerk who lied to my face and was frightened to death doing it, and a Magister who hasn't slept in three nights. What I don't have is a name.\" She smiled at him. \"I don't suppose you'd care to give me one?\"\n\nThe Reeve did not smile back.\n\n\"You've been in Varrow four days, madam,\" he said. \"You don't know the tides. People who don't know the tides get into trouble out on the marsh. Every year we lose one or two. Visitors, mostly.\" He stood up. \"I'd be careful.\"\n\n\"I always am,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Good night, Reeve.\"\n\nWhen he had gone she sat for a while without moving. Then she got the hot brick back out from under the bed, which had gone lukewarm, and put her feet on it, and turned her report face down again, and wrote at the bottom of a fresh sheet, in her tiny neat hand: *Seyle. Certain. No proof.*\n\n[\n  Place: #Ilse's Room\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Corin Ashe\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The Reeve's letter:@Ilse Varga ^The Reeve's letter:found\n]\n\nCorin Ashe came back on the night before the spring tide, the way he had come the first time: a knock after midnight, a voice on the landing, a request not to be hit with whatever she was holding.\n\nThis time she opened the door all the way.\n\nHe looked terrible. He had a cut along his jaw that someone had stitched badly, and his clothes were not his own, and he was thinner than a man ought to get in four days. But he was smiling, and when he saw her face the smile went crooked and then went away altogether.\n\n\"Tomas,\" he said. \"Is he —\"\n\n\"He's alive. His leg's broken. He's at the Pans with a man called Hobb.\" She stood aside. \"Come in. You're letting the cold in.\"\n\nHe came in and stood in the middle of her small room and looked at the bolster, which she had sewn up again, badly, and at the feathers that were still in the corners because she had not been able to bring herself to sweep them.\n\n\"I'm sorry about that,\" he said.\n\nShe had known. She had known since the Magister laughed. But she said, \"You,\" anyway, because it needed saying.\n\n\"Me.\" He reached inside his borrowed coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilcloth and held it out to her, exactly as he had on the first night. \"Everybody hides things in the bolster, Ilse. I've been in forty rooms in this city. It's always the bolster or the chimney, and you don't have a chimney.\"\n\nShe did not take it. \"Why?\"\n\n\"Because Seyle's men were coming up Tallow Street with a key the cooper's wife had been paid for, and I got there first. Because I knew where they'd look.\" He was still holding it out. \"And because I thought — when I came up out of that creek, half drowned, with the Reeve's men going past me close enough to touch — I thought, I could sell this. I could sell this back to Halvard Seyle for a great deal of money and go to Hesk and never come back. And I had it in my hand on the Hesk road for a whole day before I turned round.\"\n\n\"Why did you turn round?\"\n\nHe thought about it. She watched him think about it, and was surprised to find that she believed him when he answered.\n\n\"Because you didn't hit me with the poker,\" he said. \"The first night. You could have. You had it right there behind the door, I saw the end of it. And you opened the door a hand's width instead, and took a stranger's letter, and kept it.\" He shrugged. \"Nobody's ever done that. Usually they hit me.\"\n\nShe took the letter. The seal was still pressed down where she had warmed it with the knife. He had not opened it again.\n\n\"The auditor's at the Anchor,\" she said. \"On Rope Walk.\"\n\n\"I know where the Anchor is.\"\n\n\"Will you come with me?\"\n\nCorin Ashe looked at her for a long moment, the long clever face in the lamplight, the scar through his eyebrow.\n\n\"I'm a smuggler,\" he said. \"And a liar. Nobody will believe a word I say.\"\n\n\"They'll believe the sticks,\" said Ilse. \"Aurek has the sticks. And they'll believe the letter. And they'll believe me, because I'm going to tell them I lied, and nobody says that unless it's true.\" She put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, and reached for her coat. \"You only have to stand there.\"","wordCount":2611,"createdAt":1794213937746},{"id":"5_oL5Zk9_jmD3pcQrx5Fd","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Brenn had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. That was what the forty crowns in his coat pocket were for.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter under his seal saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"She's early.\"\n\n\"How early?\"\n\n\"A night. A whole night.\" He sat up. \"The letter said the spring tide. That's tomorrow.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern, because he had taken her in through the Gullet on the ninth.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.","wordCount":712,"createdAt":1794216606233},{"id":"69F7LLn-waAa2B1jLFs_Z","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"LQxthMqMx2ix2u3pYQu7A","text":"The hearing was called for the hour after noon on the day of the spring tide, in the long hall at the top of the town where the Reeve of Varrow had sat in judgement on fishing disputes and drunk sailors for as long as there had been a Reeve. Lisbet Ormund had called it. She had the right; she had shown the clerk of the Hall a paper with the Exchequer's seal on it, and the clerk had gone white and sent for the Reeve, and the Reeve had come, and smiled, and said that of course the auditor must have whatever she needed.\n\nHe sat now in his own chair at the head of the hall, in the harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs. The Magister sat below him on the bench where witnesses sat, very straight, his long hands on his knees. Half the lower town had come up the hill to stand at the back. Ilse could see Mother Gannet's milky eye in the crowd, and Wren's dirty face beside her, and a great many people she had passed on the Gull Stair every day of her life and never spoken to. The old people on the quay had been saying since dawn that there was weather coming in with the tide.\n\nThe auditor stood at a small table in the middle of the floor with her papers in front of her.\n\n\"I'll be brief,\" she said, \"because the tide turns at four and I'm told half of you have boats to see to. I have audited the books of the Salt House of Varrow for the last four years. I find that for the last three of them, one-seventh part of the salt shipped from the Pans has not reached Hesk, and that the books have been written to hide it. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. Miss Varga.\"\n\nIlse stood up.\n\nShe had thought her legs would shake. They did not. She walked out to the table in the middle of the floor in her clerk's grey with her hair pinned and laid the things down on it one by one, the way she would have laid out a ship's sticks to be counted.\n\nThe Kestrel's book, open at the ninth.\n\nThirty-four tally-sticks, which Aurek Stiles had brought up the hill in his coat.\n\nA letter with a red seal pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\n\"On the ninth,\" she said, \"the Kestrel came in through the Gullet with three hundred and forty measures of salt on her sticks. I wrote four hundred in her book, because I was told to. That was a lie, and it was mine.\" She heard the crowd at the back shift and murmur. She did not look at them. \"Two days after I wrote it, the auditor asked me if I'd ever been told to write a number I knew was wrong, and I said no. That was a lie too.\"\n\n\"Miss Varga,\" said the Reeve, pleasantly, from his chair. \"You'll understand that a clerk who admits to two lies in one breath is not —\"\n\n\"And this,\" said Ilse, \"is a letter under your seal, Reeve, to the Magister, telling him where the sixty measures from the Kestrel were to go, and when, and on what ship. It never reached him. A man called Corin Ashe took it out of your post-bag, and gave it to me, and I've kept it.\" She looked up at him. \"You can read it, if you like. Or the auditor can read it aloud. It's quite short.\"\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\nHalvard Seyle looked at the letter on the table, and at the auditor, and at the Magister on the witnesses' bench, who had not moved. Then he looked past them all to the back of the hall, where Corin Ashe was standing against the wall with his arms folded, in a clean coat, and smiled.\n\n\"A smuggler,\" the Reeve said. \"A smuggler steals a letter, and a clerk who lies admits she lies, and an old man —\"\n\n\"I'll speak to the letter,\" said Oduya Fenn.\n\nHe stood up from the bench. He was not looking at the Reeve; he was looking at Ilse, and his face was quite calm.\n\n\"I never received it,\" he said. \"But I know what it says, because I know what every letter Halvard Seyle has written me for three years has said. I'll tell the auditor about every one of them. I'll tell her about the wall. I have kept a book of my own, Madam Auditor — a true one. I kept it because I am a clerk, and I could not bear not to. It is in my room at the Salt House, behind the loose firebrick in the chimney-breast, where the stove's heat never reaches, and it has every measure in it that I ever wrote false.\"\n\nSomebody in the crowd said, very loudly, \"Gods,\" and somebody else laughed, high and shocked.\n\nThe Reeve was on his feet.\n\n\"This hearing is closed,\" he said. \"I am the Reeve of Varrow and this is my hall, and I'll not have —\"\n\n\"It isn't closed, Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund mildly. \"It's the Exchequer's hearing. It's only being held in your hall.\" She picked up the letter. \"Sit down, please. I'm going to read this aloud.\"\n\nHe did not sit down. Something went out of his big red face all at once, like air out of a bladder. Then he turned and walked out through the door behind his chair, the one that only the Reeve used.\n\n\"Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund sharply, and then, to the clerk of the Hall: \"Stop that man.\" The clerk of the Hall looked at the Reeve's back, and at the auditor, and did not move. Nobody stopped him, because nobody in Varrow had ever stopped the Reeve doing anything.","wordCount":990,"createdAt":1794230097616},{"id":"77mqEqqcrRQJ5uaYXDrba","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"Jwi40uq_PG4-AT77dhdd6","text":"The auditor came two days early.\n\nIlse heard her before she saw her — a carriage on the cobbles of Tallow Street, which was a thing that did not happen; nobody drove a carriage into the lower town because the streets were too narrow and the carters too rude. Then voices in the yard, and the porter's voice going up high the way it did when he was frightened, and then a long silence, and then feet on the stair.\n\nLisbet Ormund was not what Ilse had expected. She had pictured someone like the Magister, tall and grey and sorrowful, or someone like the Reeve, red-faced and loud. The woman who came into the third-floor room was small and round and perhaps fifty, in a brown travelling coat with the mud of the coast road still on its hem, and she had a face like a kindly hen. She stood in the doorway and looked at the shelves of ledgers, all forty years of them, with an expression of pure delight.\n\n\"Oh,\" she said. \"Oh, look at that. You keep them up here? Under the roof?\"\n\n\"It's the driest room, madam,\" said Aurek, who had got to his feet so fast his stool had gone over.\n\n\"It's the hottest room in summer,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Paper doesn't like heat any more than it likes damp. Never mind. Never mind.\" She came in and took off her gloves. \"I'm the Exchequer's auditor. I expect you've been told I'd be along. I expect you were told the twentieth.\" She smiled at both of them. \"I find it saves a great deal of everyone's time to come on the eighteenth.\"\n\nIlse found that she had stood up too, and that her hand had gone flat against the front of her bodice, where the letter was. She took it away.\n\n\"Magister Fenn —\" Aurek began.\n\n\"Is downstairs, and has given me the run of the place, and very gracious he was about it. He's sending up tea. You'll be Master Stiles. And you are —?\"\n\n\"Ilse Varga, madam. Tallyclerk.\"\n\n\"Varga.\" The auditor looked at her a moment longer than was comfortable. \"There was a Varga at the Hesk customs house, years ago. A woman. Very good with figures.\"\n\n\"My mother, madam.\"\n\n\"Was she? Well.\" Lisbet Ormund turned back to the shelves. \"Then you'll know the difference between a book that's right and a book that's tidy, Miss Varga. Most people don't.\" She ran a finger along the spines, the newest first. \"I'll want this year's, and last year's, and — let me see — the year of the big tide. When was that? Three years back?\"\n\n\"Four, madam,\" said Aurek.\n\n\"Three,\" said Ilse, at the same moment.\n\nThey looked at each other. The auditor looked at both of them, pleasantly.\n\n\"The spring tide that took the Pans,\" Ilse said. \"It was three years in Thawmonth. I'd been here a year.\"\n\n\"Then I'll have that one,\" said Lisbet Ormund, \"and the one before it, so I can see the difference. And I'll have the tally-sticks for whatever's come in this month. All of them.\" She sat down at the end of the long table, in the place where nobody ever sat because the draught came under the door there, and folded her small hands. \"I'm told sticks go missing,\" she said. \"I'm told it happens all the time.\"\n\nNobody said anything. Down in the yard the porter was shouting for someone to bring up the tea.\n\nThat night Ilse did not go home over the cooper's. She went down the Gull Stair to the quay, and she did not find Tomas on his basket outside the pilots' shed, and when she asked old Mother Gannet, who sold eels at the foot of the stair and saw everything, the old woman only said that the Patience had gone out on the evening ebb, and that there'd been two men in her, and that one of them was a long clever one with a scar in his eyebrow.","wordCount":671,"createdAt":1794216604940},{"id":"7Q9tGqHCk9WQagPzaMCY8","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"3z3uj6msTp-yJfszkLU-n","text":"She went to work. She did not know what else to do with herself, and it seemed important, in a way she could not have explained, that she should be at her place at the long table when the bell went, in her clerk's grey, with her hair pinned, as if nothing had happened. As if she were still the best clerk the Magister had had in thirty years.\n\nAurek glanced up when she came in and opened his mouth to say something about bags of nails, and then saw her face and shut it again.\n\nThe auditor was not there. Her sheet of tiny figures lay on the end of the table under a glass paperweight, and the green ledger was gone from beside it.\n\n\"She's downstairs,\" Aurek said quietly. \"With him. Since first bell.\" He hesitated. \"Ilse. I took the sticks out of the bin.\"\n\nShe turned.\n\n\"The Kestrel's. The day you put them in. I'm not a fool, girl, whatever you think. Nobody finds six sticks behind a stove and then finds them gone again.\" He did not look at her; he looked at his own ledger, at his own tidy upright hand. \"I've been here twenty years. I've written what I was told for three of them. I didn't count. I didn't ask. I told myself the Magister read the margins.\" He turned a page that did not need turning. \"They're in my coat. In the press. If you want them.\"\n\nShe stood very still.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\n\"Because you didn't write anything in the margin,\" said Aurek Stiles. \"You always write in the margin. You've written in the margin every day for four years. And that day you didn't, and I thought: she's frightened. And I thought: I've been frightened for three years and I never once noticed it was the same thing.\"\n\nAt midday the auditor came up the stair and took her sheet of figures from under the paperweight and went down again without a word to either of them. A little after that, the porter came up to say that the Magister wanted Miss Varga in his room.\n\nThe stove was lit. It was always lit. Oduya Fenn sat behind his desk with the green ledger closed in front of him and his long hands folded on top of it, and he did not smile when she came in. That was how she knew he had not slept either.\n\n\"Sit down, Ilse.\"\n\nShe did not sit down.\n\n\"I was at the Chapel last night,\" she said. \"In the tower. With Tomas, who has a broken leg, and has been lying in a window for two days while you loaded salt underneath him.\"\n\nThe Magister closed his eyes.\n\n\"I heard you tell the Reeve my name.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"I imagine you did.\" He opened his eyes again and looked at her, and his face was older than she had ever seen it. \"Will you let me tell you how it happened? Not to excuse it. I don't think it can be excused. But I'd like someone to know, and I think you've earned it more than anyone.\"\n\nShe sat down.\n\nHe told her. The tide in Thawmonth that took the wall and the pans and the pan-reeve's wife. The letter to the capital, and the letter back, _a matter for the harbour._ A whole year of the pan-men sitting on the quay, and the price of salt going up at Hesk, and the Salt House half empty, and the Exchequer writing every quarter to ask why the revenue was down. And then Halvard Seyle in this room, in that chair, with a bottle of something good, saying: _I can build you a wall, Oduya. All it needs is a little salt that nobody counts._\n\n\"One part in seven,\" the Magister said. \"For one year. To pay for the wall. That was all it was going to be. I told myself it was the Exchequer's own salt paying for the Exchequer's own wall, and if the Exchequer had done its duty there'd have been no need. I told myself a great many things.\" He looked down at his hands. \"The wall was built in a year. And at the end of the year I went to Halvard and said, that's enough, and he said — he said, Oduya, you've been writing false books for a year. Do you want to explain that to the Exchequer, or shall I?\"\n\n\"So you kept writing.\"\n\n\"So I kept writing. And the wall is bad stone, and Halvard has a new house, and the Black Mare comes in on the spring tides and takes one-seventh of the salt of Varrow to wherever it goes.\" He lifted his hands, let them fall. \"And you counted the Kestrel's sticks. I knew you would, one day. You're the only one of them who ever counted.\"\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Did you take it?\"\n\nHis face did not change. \"What letter?\"\n\n\"The Reeve's letter to you. About the Kestrel. I had it.\"\n\nFor a moment the Magister of the Salt House looked at her with an expression she could not read at all, and then, very slowly, he began to laugh. It was not a happy sound.\n\n\"He never sent it,\" he said. \"I never had it. I wondered — I wondered why he was so certain I'd been told about the Black Mare coming early, when I hadn't. He said, _I wrote to you._ And I said, _you didn't._ And we each thought the other was lying.\" He wiped his eyes. \"Oh, Ilse. Somebody stole the Reeve's post. And now somebody has stolen it from you.\"","wordCount":946,"createdAt":1794216650506},{"id":"7_-FcfesmDx14d6Gag1lE","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"r9SBgyYpFJBk2b1o5XkXt","text":"They brought Tomas out on the last of the ebb, the two of them, Ilse under one arm and Wren under the other, and it took four hours, and Ilse did not afterward remember much of it except the cold and the weight and her brother's breath hissing in her ear at every step. Wren found them the hard every time.At the edge of the marsh, where the path came up onto the sea-wall by the Pans, they left Tomas with a pan-man Wren knew, who asked no questions and sent his own son running for a bone-setter, and then Ilse walked home alone through the waking streets with the sun coming up behind her.\n\nThe Magister had said her name. She kept hearing it. Not _the clerk_, not _one of my girls_. _The Varga girl_, in that tired voice she had loved for four years, like a man putting down something heavy.\n\nShe climbed the stair through the middle of the cooper's house. The step that creaked, creaked. The cooper's wife was not singing.\n\nHer door was open.\n\nNot broken. Open, the way you leave a door when you have a key and are in a hurry. Inside, the room looked almost as she had left it — the table, the chair, the lamp-hook — except that everything had been moved a little and put back a little wrong, the way a stranger puts things back. The chair faced the wrong wall. Her mother's comb was on the left of the shelf instead of the right.\n\nShe went back down the stair. The cooper's wife was in the kitchen with the baby on her hip and the door half shut, and when Ilse knocked on it she opened it no wider.\n\n\"Was anybody up?\" Ilse said. \"In the night. To my room.\"\n\n\"I was asleep.\" The cooper's wife did not look at her. She looked at the baby, who was chewing on a wooden spoon. \"We were all asleep. The cooper's been at the Pans all week, you know that, there's nobody here but me.\"\n\n\"My door was open.\"\n\n\"Then you left it open. You were out all night, weren't you? Out in the marsh, by the look of your boots.\" The woman's eyes went to the boots and came away quickly. \"I don't ask where my lodgers go, Miss Varga. I'd thank you not to ask me where I sleep.\"\n\n\"Somebody had a key.\"\n\n\"Then somebody had a key.\" The cooper's wife began to close the door. \"There's three keys to that room and one of them's been lost since Midwinter. Ask the cooper. When he's home.\"\n\nThe door shut. Behind it the baby began to cry, and the woman began to sing to it, the same three lines over and over, too loud.\n\nIlse stood in the passage for a while. Then she went back up.\n\nHer room was cold. Someone had let the lamp burn down to nothing and the wick had charred, and there was a smell in the air that was not hers — wet wool, and tar, and something sharper, like cloves.\n\nShe stood in the middle of the floor with her hands at her sides and tried to think the way her mother would have thought. Who had known she was going into the marsh. Who had known she would not be home. Wren had known. Mother Gannet had known. Anyone who had seen her go down Tallow Street at dusk in her mother's boots had known, and half the lower town had.\n\nIt did not narrow anything. It only made the room feel larger, and full of people.\n\nThe bolster lay on the bed with its seam cut open from end to end.\n\nShe did not need to look. She looked anyway. She put her hand into the hollow and felt the feathers and the ticking and nothing else, and then she sat down on the bed with the feathers settling around her like snow, and for the first time since the Kestrel's sticks she let herself cry.\n\nShe had not told anyone where she kept it. Not Tomas. Not Wren. Not the auditor, who she had lied to.\n\nShe had told no one, and someone had known.","wordCount":707,"createdAt":1794216612457},{"id":"7odlWcVqVtxKtsSrN7wQF","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"kACrUDbXsjGSKBXJOWAnj","text":"Corin Ashe came back on the night before the spring tide, the way he had come the first time: a knock after midnight, a voice on the landing, a request not to be hit with whatever she was holding.\n\nThis time she opened the door all the way.\n\nHe looked terrible. He had a cut along his jaw that someone had stitched badly, and his clothes were not his own, and he was thinner than a man ought to get in four days. But he was smiling, and when he saw her face the smile went crooked and then went away altogether.\n\n\"Tomas,\" he said. \"Is he —\"\n\n\"He's alive. His leg's broken. He's at the Pans with a man called Hobb.\" She stood aside. \"Come in. You're letting the cold in.\"\n\nHe came in and stood in the middle of her small room and looked at the bolster, which she had sewn up again, badly, and at the feathers that were still in the corners because she had not been able to bring herself to sweep them.\n\n\"I'm sorry about that,\" he said.\n\nShe had known. She had known since the Magister laughed. But she said, \"You,\" anyway, because it needed saying.\n\n\"Me.\" He reached inside his borrowed coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilcloth and held it out to her, exactly as he had on the first night. \"Everybody hides things in the bolster, Ilse. I've been in forty rooms in this city. It's always the bolster or the chimney, and you don't have a chimney.\"\n\nShe did not take it. \"Why?\"\n\n\"Because Seyle's men were coming up Tallow Street with a key the cooper's wife had been paid for, and I got there first. Because I knew where they'd look.\" He was still holding it out. \"And because I thought — when I came up out of that creek, half drowned, with the Reeve's men going past me close enough to touch — I thought, I could sell this. I could sell this back to Halvard Seyle for a great deal of money and go to Hesk and never come back. And I had it in my hand on the Hesk road for a whole day before I turned round.\"\n\n\"Why did you turn round?\"\n\nHe thought about it. She watched him think about it, and was surprised to find that she believed him when he answered.\n\n\"Because you didn't hit me with the poker,\" he said. \"The first night. You could have. You had it right there behind the door, I saw the end of it. And you opened the door a hand's width instead, and took a stranger's letter, and kept it.\" He shrugged. \"Nobody's ever done that. Usually they hit me.\"\n\nShe took the letter. The seal was still pressed down where she had warmed it with the knife. He had not opened it again.\n\n\"The auditor's at the Anchor,\" she said. \"On Rope Walk.\"\n\n\"I know where the Anchor is.\"\n\n\"Will you come with me?\"\n\nCorin Ashe looked at her for a long moment, the long clever face in the lamplight, the scar through his eyebrow.\n\n\"I'm a smuggler,\" he said. \"And a liar. Nobody will believe a word I say.\"\n\n\"They'll believe the sticks,\" said Ilse. \"Aurek has the sticks. And they'll believe the letter. And they'll believe me, because I'm going to tell them I lied, and nobody says that unless it's true.\" She put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, and reached for her coat. \"You only have to stand there.\"\n# The Last Tally\n## The Reeve's Hall\n[\n  Place: #The Reeve's Hall\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Lisbet Ormund @@Halvard Seyle @@Oduya Fenn @@Aurek Stiles @@Corin Ashe @@Mother Gannet @@Wren\n  When: +1 day\n  Items: ^The Reeve's letter:@Lisbet Ormund\n  Facts: ?Fenn is in it with the Reeve:@Lisbet Ormund ?Fenn kept a true book:@Lisbet Ormund ?Fenn kept a true book:reader\n]\n\nThe hearing was called for the hour after noon on the day of the spring tide, in the long hall at the top of the town where the Reeve of Varrow had sat in judgement on fishing disputes and drunk sailors for as long as there had been a Reeve. Lisbet Ormund had called it. She had the right; she had shown the clerk of the Hall a paper with the Exchequer's seal on it, and the clerk had gone white and sent for the Reeve, and the Reeve had come, and smiled, and said that of course the auditor must have whatever she needed.\n\nHe sat now in his own chair at the head of the hall, in the harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs. The Magister sat below him on the bench where witnesses sat, very straight, his long hands on his knees. Half the lower town had come up the hill to stand at the back. Ilse could see Mother Gannet's milky eye in the crowd, and Wren's dirty face beside her, and a great many people she had passed on the Gull Stair every day of her life and never spoken to.\n\nThe auditor stood at a small table in the middle of the floor with her papers in front of her.\n\n\"I'll be brief,\" she said, \"because the tide turns at four and I'm told half of you have boats to see to. I have audited the books of the Salt House of Varrow for the last four years. I find that for the last three of them, one-seventh part of the salt shipped from the Pans has not reached Hesk, and that the books have been written to hide it. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. Miss Varga.\"\n\nIlse stood up.\n\nShe had thought her legs would shake. They did not. She walked out to the table in the middle of the floor in her clerk's grey with her hair pinned and laid the things down on it one by one, the way she would have laid out a ship's sticks to be counted.\n\nThe Kestrel's book, open at the ninth.\n\nThirty-four tally-sticks, which Aurek Stiles had brought up the hill in his coat.\n\nA letter with a red seal pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\n\"On the ninth,\" she said, \"the Kestrel came in through the Gullet with three hundred and forty measures of salt on her sticks. I wrote four hundred in her book, because I was told to. That was a lie, and it was mine.\" She heard the crowd at the back shift and murmur. She did not look at them. \"Two days later the auditor asked me if I'd ever been told to write a number I knew was wrong, and I said no. That was a lie too.\"\n\n\"Miss Varga,\" said the Reeve, pleasantly, from his chair. \"You'll understand that a clerk who admits to two lies in one breath is not —\"\n\n\"And this,\" said Ilse, \"is a letter in your hand, Reeve, to the Magister, telling him where the sixty measures from the Kestrel were to go, and when, and on what ship. It never reached him. A man called Corin Ashe took it out of your post-bag, and gave it to me, and I've kept it.\" She looked up at him. \"You can read it, if you like. Or the auditor can read it aloud. It's quite short.\"\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\nHalvard Seyle looked at the letter on the table, and at the auditor, and at the Magister on the witnesses' bench, who had not moved. Then he looked past them all to the back of the hall, where Corin Ashe was standing against the wall with his arms folded, in a clean coat, and smiled.\n\n\"A smuggler,\" the Reeve said. \"A smuggler steals a letter, and a clerk who lies admits she lies, and an old man —\"\n\n\"I'll speak to the letter,\" said Oduya Fenn.\n\nHe stood up from the bench. He was not looking at the Reeve; he was looking at Ilse, and his face was quite calm.\n\n\"I never received it,\" he said. \"But I know what it says, because I know what every letter Halvard Seyle has written me for three years has said. I'll tell the auditor about every one of them. I'll tell her about the wall. I have kept a book of my own, Madam Auditor — a true one. I kept it because I am a clerk, and I could not bear not to. It is in the stove in my room at the Salt House, behind the firebrick, and it has every measure in it that I ever wrote false.\"\n\nSomebody in the crowd said, very loudly, \"Gods,\" and somebody else laughed, high and shocked.\n\nThe Reeve was on his feet.\n\n\"This hearing is closed,\" he said. \"I am the Reeve of Varrow and this is my hall, and I'll not have —\"\n\n\"It isn't closed, Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund mildly. \"It's the Exchequer's hearing. It's only being held in your hall.\" She picked up the letter. \"Sit down, please. I'm going to read this aloud.\"\n\nHe did not sit down. He looked at the auditor, and at the Magister, and at the crowd filling the back of the hall, and something went out of his big red face all at once, like air out of a bladder. Then he turned and walked out through the door behind his chair, the one that only the Reeve used, and nobody stopped him, because nobody in Varrow had ever stopped the Reeve doing anything.\n\n## Spring Tide\n[\n  Place: #The Gull Stair\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Corin Ashe @@Wren @@Halvard Seyle:dead elsewhere\n  When: same day\n]\n\nThe storm came in at four, with the turn of the tide, as the old people on the quay had said it would. It came out of the south-west in one long grey wall, and the sea came with it.\n\nIlse watched from the top of the Gull Stair. She had run there from the Reeve's Hall with Corin behind her and half the lower town behind him, because Wren had come pelting up the hill shouting that the Reeve's longboat had gone out of the harbour with the Reeve in it, and that the Black Mare was lying off the Chapel again in the teeth of the weather, waiting for him.\n\n\"He's mad,\" Corin said, beside her. He had to shout it. \"He'll never get over the bar in this.\"\n\n\"He knows the bar.\"\n\n\"Nobody knows the bar in this.\"\n\nOut past the harbour mouth the sea had gone white. The salt-ships at their moorings were pitching and snatching at their chains, and the spray was coming over the quay wall in sheets, and down below them on the quay the pilots were out in their oilskins, shouting, hauling boats up the slip. Ilse looked for Tomas among them out of habit before she remembered that Tomas was in a bed at the Pans with his leg in a splint.\n\nThen she saw the longboat.\n\nIt was a small dark thing in the white water out by the Chapel, six oars rising and falling out of time, and it was making for the black ship. She could see the Black Mare now too, beyond it — two masts, no sail, her anchors down, plunging. Someone on her deck was waving a light.\n\n\"They'll make it,\" said Corin. \"Gods damn him, they'll —\"\n\nThe sea took the longboat on the Chapel bar.\n\nShe did not see it happen. One moment there were six oars and a dark hull and the next there was a wave, enormous, grey-green, standing up across the bar the way the sea stands up only where the bottom is very close, and when it went down there was nothing on the bar but white water. A long way off, the light on the Black Mare's deck stopped waving.\n\nNobody on the Gull Stair said anything at all.\n\nThe Black Mare did not wait. They saw her get her anchors up, or cut them — it was too far to tell — and get a scrap of sail on her, and claw her way round to the south, away from the bar and the Chapel and Varrow. Within half an hour she was gone into the rain.\n\nThe Reeve's body came ashore two days later on the hard below the sea-wall at the Pans, the new sea-wall, which had held. One of the pan-men found it. Mother Gannet said afterward that it was the only thing the Reeve ever built that did what it was meant to.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Salt House\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Lisbet Ormund @@Tomas Brenn @@Corin Ashe @@Wren @@Mother Gannet\n  When: +14 days\n]\n\nThe auditor left Varrow on the twelfth day of Seedmonth, in the same carriage that had come down Tallow Street and frightened the porter, and half the lower town came out to see her go.\n\nShe had stayed a fortnight longer than she meant to. She had sat in the Magister's room with the firebrick out of the stove and the Magister's true book open on the desk — a small black book, scorched at the corners, with three years of real numbers in it in a long thin careful hand — and she had copied every page. Then she had sat in the third-floor room with Aurek and Ilse for ten days more and put the real numbers into the real books, page by page, in the margins, in red.\n\n\"The Exchequer will want the Magister in the capital,\" she told Ilse, the last evening, standing at the door of the Salt House with her gloves in her hand. \"There'll be a trial. I'll speak for him. I'll say he kept a true book when he could have kept none, and gave it up when he could have burned it, and that's not nothing.\" She pulled on one glove. \"I don't know what they'll do. Something less than they might have, I hope.\"\n\n\"And the Salt House?\"\n\n\"Will need a Magister.\" Lisbet Ormund pulled on the other glove. \"I've written to say so. I've written to say who.\" She looked up at Ilse with her kindly hen's face. \"Master Stiles has been here twenty years and is a very good clerk, and he told me himself that he would rather go into the sea after the Reeve than have the stove. So I've written your name.\"\n\nIlse opened her mouth and shut it again.\n\n\"You'll say you're too young,\" said the auditor. \"And that you lied to me. You did. You also told me you'd lied, in front of the whole town, which I have never once in thirty years of this work seen anyone do. Your mother would have.\" She held out her small gloved hand, and Ilse took it. \"Count everything, Miss Varga. Write in the margins. Good night.\"\n\nThe carriage went down Tallow Street in the morning with the carters cursing it, and Ilse watched it from the third-floor window until it was gone.\n\nTomas came up the Gull Stair that afternoon on two crutches, which took him nearly an hour and which he would not let anyone help him with, and sat on the bench outside the pilots' shed at the bottom with his splinted leg stuck out in front of him and his unlit pipe in his teeth. Corin Ashe sat beside him. Neither of them said much. Corin had a pilot's oilskin on, which was too big for him, and a pilot's book in his pocket, which Tomas had made him buy, and he was learning the Gullet from the bench, channel by channel, with Tomas drawing it in the dust with the end of a crutch.\n\n\"He'll make a pilot,\" Tomas said to Ilse, when she came down to them at the end of the day. \"God knows how. He gets everything wrong and then argues with the sea about it.\"\n\n\"I'm very persuasive,\" said Corin.\n\n\"The sea isn't.\"\n\nWren was sitting on the bottom step of the Gull Stair eating a cold potato. Mother Gannet was gutting eels. The salt-ships lay over in the mud on the ebb, and the evening light came down the harbour the colour of honey, and somewhere up on the hill a bell was ringing for the end of the day.\n\nIlse climbed back up to the Salt House as the light went. The third floor was empty; Aurek had gone home. She lit the lamp and sat down at the long table, at her own place, and opened the Kestrel's book at the ninth.\n\nFour hundred, in her own clean hand. And beside it, in the margin, in red, in the auditor's tiny writing: *340. Sticks counted. I.V.*\n\nShe dipped her pen, and turned to a clean page, and began to count.","wordCount":2819,"createdAt":1794213959508},{"id":"7wT58qig0qTIgbwtqGqiq","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"2i5J2Lc7W9L8liiqtAFCG","text":"The Salt House kept its ledgers on the third floor, under the eaves, where the damp could not reach them and the clerks could not reach anything else. Ilse Varga had worked there for four years and had never once been warm.\n\nIt was the seventeenth of Brinemonth. She had already written two queries that morning in the margin of the Heron's book, one about a date and one about a sum, in the small slanting hand that Aurek called her second job.\n\nShe sat at the long table with the Kestrel's book open in front of her and the Kestrel's tally-sticks laid out beside it in a row, like the bones of a small animal. Every sack that went over the side of a salt-ship at Varrow was notched on a stick by the pan-reeve and notched again by the ship's mate, and the stick was split down its length so that each man kept half. When the halves came back to the Salt House they were matched, and counted, and the count went into the book. Nobody could cut a notch out of a stick without the other half showing it. It was the oldest honest thing in the city.\n\nShe counted the sticks again. Thirty-four sticks, ten notches to a stick. Three hundred and forty measures.\n\nThe book said four hundred.\n\n\"You're frowning at it,\" said Aurek Stiles from the far end of the table. He was the senior of the two tallyclerks and had been for twenty years, and he said it without looking up from his own work. \"Frowning at a ledger is how the ink gets into your face.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel,\" Ilse said. \"Her book says four hundred measures out on the ninth. I've got three hundred and forty on the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then you've dropped six sticks behind the stove.\"\n\n\"I haven't.\"\n\n\"Then the mate's dropped them in the harbour. It happens.\" Aurek turned a page. \"Write it as received and put a query in the margin. The Magister reads the margins.\"\n\nShe did not write it as received. She sat with the pen over the page until the ink beaded on the nib and fell, a small black star on the blotter, and then she got up and went down the narrow stair to the Magister's room.\n\nMagister Oduya Fenn had run the Salt House since before Ilse was born. He was a tall man gone thin in the way of tall men who forget to eat, and his room was the only warm one in the building, because he had the only good stove. He glanced up when she knocked and smiled at her with real pleasure, which made what she had come to say harder.\n\n\"Ilse. Sit. You look like you've been out on the Pans.\"\n\n\"Only on the third floor, Magister.\" She did not sit. \"The Kestrel's book is sixty measures over her sticks.\"\n\nThe smile stayed exactly where it was. That was the first thing she would remember afterwards — not that it went, but that it stayed, a little too long, like a man holding a door that nobody was coming through.\n\n\"Sixty,\" he said.\n\n\"Six sticks. I've counted three times.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel came in through the Gullet in a rising sea. Her mate was sick over the side twice before they'd cleared the bar — your brother will tell you; he took her in.\" Fenn spread his long hands. \"Sticks go missing. The book is written from the manifest at the pans, and the manifest is right.\"\n\n\"Then the sticks should be with the manifest.\"\n\n\"Then they should.\" He said it kindly. \"Write the book as it stands, Ilse. I'll speak to the pan-reeve myself.\"\n\nShe went back up the stairs. Aurek did not ask her what had been said, and she did not tell him. She wrote four hundred in the column in her own clean hand, and in the margin she wrote nothing at all, because the Magister read the margins.\n\nWhen the bell went for the end of the day she put the thirty-four sticks into her satchel instead of into the bin. She did not decide to do it. Her hands did it while she was thinking about something else, and by the time she noticed, she was already on the stair.","wordCount":719,"createdAt":1794230236198},{"id":"86wWjT-vTEcANfGmXe8na","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"GIXhkOMgrF_FKcO5VyMzd","text":"The auditor left Varrow on the twelfth day of Seedmonth, in the same carriage that had come down Tallow Street and frightened the porter, and the whole of Tallow Street came out to see her go.\n\nShe had stayed three weeks longer than she meant to. She had sat in the Magister's room with the firebrick out of the chimney-breast and the Magister's true book open on the desk — a small black book, black with soot at the corners, with three years of real numbers in it in a long thin careful hand — and she had copied every page. Then she had sat in the third-floor room with Aurek and Ilse for ten days more and put the real numbers into the real books, page by page, in the margins, in red.\n\n\"The Exchequer will want the Magister in the capital,\" she told Ilse, the last evening, standing at the door of the Salt House with her gloves in her hand. \"There'll be a trial. I'll speak for him. I'll say he kept a true book when he could have kept none, and gave it up when he could have burned it, and that's not nothing.\" She pulled on one glove. \"I don't know what they'll do. Something less than they might have, I hope.\"\n\n\"And the Salt House?\"\n\n\"Will need a Magister.\" Lisbet Ormund pulled on the other glove. \"I've written to say so. I've written to say who.\" She looked up at Ilse with her kindly hen's face. \"Master Stiles has been here twenty years and is a very good clerk, and he told me himself that he would rather go into the sea after the Reeve than have the stove. So I've written your name.\"\n\nIlse opened her mouth and shut it again.\n\n\"You'll say you're too young,\" said the auditor. \"And that you lied to me. You did. You also told me you'd lied, in front of the whole town, which I have never once in thirty years of this work seen anyone do. Your mother would have.\" She held out her small gloved hand, and Ilse took it. \"Count everything, Miss Varga. Write in the margins. Good night.\"\n\nThe carriage went down Tallow Street in the morning with the carters cursing it, and Ilse watched it from the third-floor window until it was gone.\n\nTomas came down the Gull Stair that afternoon on two crutches, which took him nearly an hour and which he would not let anyone help him with, and sat on the bench outside the pilots' shed at the bottom with his splinted leg stuck out in front of him and his unlit pipe in his teeth. Corin Ashe sat beside him. Neither of them said much. Corin had a pilot's oilskin on, which was too big for him, and a pilot's book in his pocket, which Tomas had made him buy, and he was learning the Gullet from the bench, channel by channel, with Tomas drawing it in the dust with the end of a crutch.\n\n\"He'll make a pilot,\" Tomas said to Ilse, when she came down to them at the end of the day. \"Gods know how. He gets everything wrong and then argues with the sea about it.\"\n\n\"I'm very persuasive,\" said Corin.\n\n\"The sea isn't.\"\n\nWren was sitting on the bottom step of the Gull Stair eating a cold potato. Mother Gannet was gutting eels. The salt-ships lay over in the mud on the ebb, and the evening light came down the harbour the colour of honey, and somewhere up on the hill a bell was ringing for the end of the day.\n\nIlse climbed back up to the Salt House as the light went. The third floor was empty; Aurek had gone home. She lit the lamp and sat down at the long table, at her own place, and opened the Kestrel's book at the ninth.\n\nFour hundred, in her own clean hand. And beside it, in the margin, in red, in the auditor's tiny writing: _340. Sticks counted by I.V. L.O._\n\nShe dipped her pen, and turned to a clean page, and began to count.","wordCount":692,"createdAt":1794230238265},{"id":"8T5jmWYlY1iHlmCSNl0H6","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"W6kDqSJm2O5zS3kC2H7Of","text":"The storm came in at four, with the turn of the tide, as the old people on the quay had said it would. It came out of the south-west in one long grey wall, and the sea came with it.\n\nIlse watched from the top of the Gull Stair. She had run there from the Reeve's Hall with Corin behind her and most of the hall behind him, because Wren had come pelting up the hill shouting that the Reeve's longboat had gone out of the harbour with the Reeve in it, and that the Black Mare was lying off the Chapel again in the teeth of the weather, waiting for him.\n\n\"He's mad,\" Corin said, beside her. He had to shout it. \"He'll never get over the bar in this.\"\n\n\"He knows the bar.\"\n\n\"Nobody knows the bar in this.\"\n\nOut past the harbour mouth the sea had gone white. The salt-ships at their moorings were pitching and snatching at their chains, and the spray was coming over the quay wall in sheets, and down below them on the quay the pilots were out in their oilskins, shouting, hauling boats up the slip. Ilse searched for Tomas among them out of habit before she remembered that Tomas was in a bed at the Pans with his leg in a splint.\n\nThen she saw the longboat.\n\nIt was a small dark thing in the white water out by the Chapel, six oars rising and falling out of time, and it was making for the black ship. She could see the Black Mare now too, beyond it — two masts, no sail, her anchors down, plunging. Someone on her deck was waving a light.\n\n\"They'll make it,\" said Corin. \"Gods damn him, they'll —\"\n\nThe sea took the longboat on the Chapel bar.\n\nShe did not see it happen. One moment there were six oars and a dark hull and the next there was a wave, enormous, grey-green, standing up across the bar the way the sea stands up only where the bottom is very close, and when it went down there was nothing on the bar but white water. A long way off, the light on the Black Mare's deck stopped waving.\n\nNobody on the Gull Stair said anything at all.\n\nThe Black Mare did not wait. They saw her get her anchors up, or cut them — it was too far to tell — and get a scrap of sail on her, and claw her way round to the south, away from the bar and the Chapel and Varrow. Within half an hour she was gone into the rain.\n\nThe Reeve's body came ashore two days later on the hard below the sea-wall at the Pans, the new sea-wall, which had held. One of the pan-men found it. Mother Gannet said afterward that it was the only thing the Reeve ever built that did what it was meant to.","wordCount":490,"createdAt":1794216655375},{"id":"8eAjJhEJnhWL_vrsgDKZ4","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"r9SBgyYpFJBk2b1o5XkXt","text":"They brought Tomas out ahead of the flood, the two of them, Ilse under one arm and Wren under the other, and it took four hours, and Ilse did not afterwards remember much of it except the cold and the weight and her brother's breath hissing in her ear at every step. Wren found them the hard every time.At the edge of the marsh, where the path came up onto the sea-wall by the Pans, they left Tomas with a pan-man Wren knew, who asked no questions and sent his own son running for a bone-setter, and then Ilse walked home alone through the waking streets with the sun coming up behind her.\n\nThe Magister had said her name. She kept hearing it. Not _the clerk_, not _one of my girls_. _The Varga girl_, in that tired voice she had loved for four years, like a man putting down something heavy.\n\nShe climbed the stair through the middle of the cooper's house. The step that creaked, creaked. The cooper's wife was not singing.\n\nHer door was open.\n\nNot broken. Open, the way you leave a door when you have a key and are in a hurry. Inside, the room looked almost as she had left it — the table, the chair, the lamp-hook — except that everything had been moved a little and put back a little wrong, the way a stranger puts things back. The chair faced the wrong wall. Her mother's comb was on the left of the shelf instead of the right.\n\nShe went back down the stair. The cooper's wife was in the kitchen with the baby on her hip and the door half shut, and when Ilse knocked on it she opened it no wider.\n\n\"Was anybody up?\" Ilse said. \"In the night. To my room.\"\n\n\"I was asleep.\" The cooper's wife did not look at her. She looked at the baby, who was chewing on a wooden spoon. \"We were all asleep. The cooper's been at the Pans all week, you know that. There's nobody here but me.\"\n\n\"My door was open.\"\n\n\"Then you left it open. You were out all night, weren't you? Out in the marsh, by the look of your boots.\" The woman's eyes went to the boots and came away quickly. \"I don't ask where my lodgers go, Miss Varga. I'd thank you not to ask me where I sleep.\"\n\n\"Somebody had a key.\"\n\n\"Then somebody had a key.\" The cooper's wife began to close the door. \"There's three keys to that room and one of them's been lost since Midwinter. Ask the cooper. When he's home.\"\n\nThe door shut. Behind it the baby began to cry, and the woman began to sing to it, the same three lines over and over, too loud.\n\nIlse stood in the passage for a while. Then she went back up.\n\nHer room was cold. Someone had let the lamp burn down to nothing and the wick had charred, and there was a smell in the air that was not hers — wet wool, and tar, and something sharper, like cloves.\n\nShe stood in the middle of the floor with her hands at her sides and tried to think the way her mother would have thought. Who had known she was going into the marsh. Who had known she would not be home. Wren had known. Mother Gannet had known. Anyone who had seen her go down Tallow Street at dusk in her mother's boots had known, and half the lower town had.\n\nIt did not narrow anything. It only made the room feel larger, and full of people.\n\nThe bolster lay on the bed with its seam cut open from end to end.\n\nShe did not need to look. She looked anyway. She put her hand into the hollow and felt the feathers and the ticking and nothing else, and then she sat down on the bed with the feathers settling around her like snow, and for the first time since the Kestrel's sticks she let herself cry.\n\nShe had not told anyone where she kept it. Not Tomas. Not Wren. Not the auditor, who she had lied to.\n\nShe had told no one, and someone had known.","wordCount":705,"createdAt":1794216715692},{"id":"8su2_MiN7A4-naN-heWw5","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Brenn had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. That was what the forty crowns in his coat pocket were for.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter in his hand saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"She's early.\"\n\n\"How early?\"\n\n\"A night. A whole night.\" He sat up. \"The letter said the spring tide. That's tomorrow.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his clever face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern, because he had taken her in through the Gullet on the ninth.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.\n# The Hard\n## Mother Gannet\n[\n  Place: #The Gull Stair\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Mother Gannet @@Wren\n  When: +2 days\n]\n\nTomas did not come by the next day, as he had said he would, and he did not come the day after.\n\nOn the second morning Ilse went down the Gull Stair before the Salt House bell and found Mother Gannet already at her place at the foot of it, gutting eels into a bucket with a short black knife. The old woman had sold eels at the bottom of the stair for as long as there had been a stair, by her own account, and possibly longer. She had one eye milky and one eye sharp, and it was generally understood in the lower town that the milky one saw more.\n\n\"He's not back,\" Mother Gannet said, before Ilse had opened her mouth. \"The Patience isn't on her mooring. I've had the boy up and down the quay since first light asking.\"\n\n\"Which boy?\"\n\n\"Mine.\" The old woman jerked her chin. Sitting on the bottom step, eating a cold potato with great concentration, was a child of nine or ten in a man's coat cut down at the sleeves. He had a face so dirty it was hard to say whether he was a boy at all. \"Wren. He runs for me. He's not mine, but he runs for me.\"\n\n\"Nobody's seen her,\" Wren said, through potato. \"Not in the harbour. Not at the Pans. Tad Mossop says he saw a sail in the channels last night past the Heron Creek, but Tad Mossop says a lot of things.\"\n\nIlse sat down on the step beside him because her legs had decided it for her.\n\n\"He's a pilot,\" Mother Gannet said, not unkindly. \"He knows every inch of that marsh. If he's out there he's out there because he means to be.\" She dropped an eel into the bucket. \"Question is who he's out there with.\"\n\n\"Corin Ashe.\"\n\n\"Ah.\" The old woman's sharp eye went to her. \"Then the question's what he's out there for.\"\n\nIlse looked at the bucket of eels, at the grey light on the mud, at the salt-ships still lying over on their sides from the ebb. She thought about the letter against her skin and about the auditor at the end of the long table in the draught, asking for the year of the big tide.\n\n\"Mother,\" she said. \"You remember the tide that took the Pans.\"\n\n\"I remember every tide since the old king died.\"\n\n\"What happened after? To the Pans?\"\n\nMother Gannet wiped the black knife on her apron and was quiet for long enough that Wren stopped chewing.\n\n\"Sea came over the wall at the spring tide in Thawmonth,\" she said at last. \"Took the wall, took the pans behind it, took the pan-reeve's house and the pan-reeve's wife with it. Half the salt-works in Varrow, gone under in one night. The Salt House wrote to the capital for money to build it back, and the capital wrote back and said it was a matter for the harbour.\" She spat, neatly, into the mud. \"So it was the Reeve's to mend, and the Reeve said there was no money for it, and for a whole year the pan-men sat on the quay with their hands in their laps. And then one day there was money. And the wall went up, and the pans went back, and everyone said what a fine man the Reeve was.\"\n\n\"Where did the money come from?\"\n\n\"Nobody asked. That's the thing about money that comes when you need it. Nobody asks.\" The old woman picked up another eel. \"Your mother would've asked.\"\n\n\"My mother's dead.\"\n\n\"So she is. And so's the pan-reeve's wife, and the Salt House is still standing, and the Reeve's still got his fine house on the hill, and his fine new wall, which I'll tell you for nothing is built of the worst stone I ever saw and won't see out another ten years of tides.\" The knife went in. \"You asked.\"\n\nIlse stood up. \"If Tomas comes back —\"\n\n\"The boy'll find you.\" Mother Gannet did not look up. \"Go to your counting, girl. Count something.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Salt House\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Lisbet Ormund\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?The books are short one part in seven:@Ilse Varga ?The books are short one part in seven:@Lisbet Ormund:before\n]\n\nThe ledger for the year of the big tide was bound in green, because the binder had run out of brown that winter, and it stood out on the shelf like a wrong note. Lisbet Ormund had it open on the long table when Ilse came up the stair, and beside it the book for the year before, bound in brown, and beside that a sheet of her own paper covered in tiny, very neat figures.\n\nAurek was not at his place. The auditor saw her looking.\n\n\"I've sent Master Stiles to the pan-reeve with a list of questions,\" she said. \"It'll take him most of the day, I'm afraid, because the pan-reeve won't know the answers and will have to send to the Reeve's office for them. Sit down, Miss Varga. Sit there, where I can see you.\"\n\nIlse sat.\n\n\"I want to show you something,\" said the auditor, \"and then I want to ask you something, and I'd like you to think before you answer the second thing, because I shall know if you don't.\" She turned the green ledger round. \"This is Thawmonth, the year of the tide. Here — the week of the flood. Nothing comes in at all, which is what you'd expect. Here, the month after. Very little. Here, the month after that. And here,\" she turned a page, \"is the first month that the books are short against the Hesk price.\"\n\nIlse looked. It was a column of numbers in Aurek's hand, tidy and upright, and beside each a ship's name and a date. There was nothing wrong with any of them.\n\n\"They're not short,\" she said.\n\n\"Not in the book. In the book they're very well. But I have here,\" the auditor tapped her own sheet, \"every cargo of Varrow salt that was sold at Hesk that month, from the Hesk customs ledgers, which I had copied before I left the capital. And the Hesk ledgers say that Varrow sent them a good deal less salt than Varrow's books say it sent. About one part in seven less.\" She sat back. \"One part in seven. Every month since. Three years.\"\n\nOne part in seven. Sixty measures out of four hundred was a little more than that. Ilse kept her face very still and felt the paper crackle under her bodice when she breathed.\n\n\"Somebody,\" said Lisbet Ormund, \"is selling one-seventh of the salt of Varrow somewhere other than Hesk, and the books of the Salt House are written as though they weren't. Now. Here's my question.\" She leaned forward. \"I don't think you're a thief, Miss Varga. I knew your mother. I don't think the Magister is a thief either, not in his heart, though I've been wrong about men's hearts before. But somebody in this building writes the numbers, and somebody tells them which numbers to write. Has anybody ever told you to write a number you knew was wrong?\"\n\nShe thought about the Kestrel's book. Four hundred in her own clean hand.\n\nShe thought about Tomas, who had not come home.\n\nShe thought: *if I say yes, she'll ask who. And if I say who, they'll know it was me. And Tomas is somewhere out in the marsh, and I don't know where, and I don't know who else knows.*\n\n\"No, madam,\" she said.\n\nThe auditor looked at her for a long moment. Then she nodded, slowly, and turned the green book back around, and picked up her pen.\n\n\"I'll be here until the spring tide,\" she said, without looking up. \"After that I go back to the capital and write my report with whatever I have. If you think of anything before then, Miss Varga, I'd be very grateful to hear it. I'm at the Anchor, on Rope Walk. Anyone can tell you where it is.\"\n\nIlse went back to her own place and opened the Heron's book and could not see a single number in it.","wordCount":2110,"createdAt":1794213828997},{"id":"9kLt_49raRAnDXsH1nvh1","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"kACrUDbXsjGSKBXJOWAnj","text":"Corin Ashe came back on the night before the spring tide, the way he had come the first time: a knock after midnight, a voice on the landing, a request not to be hit with whatever she was holding.\n\nThis time she opened the door all the way.\n\nHe looked terrible. He had a cut along his jaw that someone had stitched badly, and his clothes were not his own, and he was thinner than a man ought to get in four days. But he was smiling, and when he saw her face the smile went crooked and then went away altogether.\n\n\"Tomas,\" he said. \"Is he —\"\n\n\"He's alive. His leg's broken. He's at the Pans with a man called Hobb.\" She stood aside. \"Come in. You're letting the cold in.\"\n\nHe came in and stood in the middle of her small room and saw the bolster, which she had sewn up again, badly, and at the feathers that were still in the corners because she had not been able to bring herself to sweep them.\n\n\"I'm sorry about that,\" he said.\n\nShe had known. She had known since the Magister laughed. But she said, \"You,\" anyway, because it needed saying.\n\n\"Me.\" He reached inside his borrowed coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilcloth and held it out to her, exactly as he had on the first night. \"Everybody hides things in the bolster, Ilse. I've been in forty rooms in this city. It's always the bolster or the chimney, and you don't have a chimney.\"\n\nShe did not take it. \"Why?\"\n\n\"Because Seyle's men were coming up Tallow Street with a key the cooper's wife had been paid for, and I got there first. Because I knew where they'd look.\" He was still holding it out. \"And because I thought — when I came up out of that creek, half drowned, with the Reeve's men going past me close enough to touch — I thought, I could sell this. I could sell this back to Halvard Seyle for a great deal of money and go to Hesk and never come back. And I had it in my hand on the Hesk road for a whole day before I turned round.\"\n\n\"Why did you turn round?\"\n\nHe thought about it. She watched him think about it, and was surprised to find that she believed him when he answered.\n\n\"Because you didn't hit me with the poker,\" he said. \"The first night. You could have. You had it right there behind the door, I saw the end of it. And you opened the door a hand's width instead, and took a stranger's letter, and kept it.\" He shrugged. \"Nobody's ever done that. Usually they hit me.\"\n\nShe took the letter. The seal was still pressed down where she had warmed it with the knife. He had not opened it again.\n\n\"The auditor's at the Anchor,\" she said. \"On Rope Walk.\"\n\n\"I know where the Anchor is.\"\n\n\"Will you come with me?\"\n\nCorin Ashe looked at her for a long moment, the long clever face in the lamplight, the scar through his eyebrow.\n\n\"I'm a smuggler,\" he said. \"And a liar. Nobody will believe a word I say.\"\n\n\"They'll believe the sticks,\" said Ilse. \"Aurek has the sticks. And they'll believe the letter. And they'll believe me, because I'm going to tell them I lied, and nobody says that unless it's true.\" She put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, and reached for her coat. \"You only have to stand there.\"","wordCount":594,"createdAt":1794216652856},{"id":"9t-zb69Wnc175M13N70cz","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"GIXhkOMgrF_FKcO5VyMzd","text":"The auditor left Varrow on the twelfth day of Seedmonth, in the same carriage that had come down Tallow Street and frightened the porter, and the whole of Tallow Street came out to see her go.\n\nShe had stayed a fortnight longer than she meant to. She had sat in the Magister's room with the firebrick out of the stove and the Magister's true book open on the desk — a small black book, scorched at the corners, with three years of real numbers in it in a long thin careful hand — and she had copied every page. Then she had sat in the third-floor room with Aurek and Ilse for ten days more and put the real numbers into the real books, page by page, in the margins, in red.\n\n\"The Exchequer will want the Magister in the capital,\" she told Ilse, the last evening, standing at the door of the Salt House with her gloves in her hand. \"There'll be a trial. I'll speak for him. I'll say he kept a true book when he could have kept none, and gave it up when he could have burned it, and that's not nothing.\" She pulled on one glove. \"I don't know what they'll do. Something less than they might have, I hope.\"\n\n\"And the Salt House?\"\n\n\"Will need a Magister.\" Lisbet Ormund pulled on the other glove. \"I've written to say so. I've written to say who.\" She looked up at Ilse with her kindly hen's face. \"Master Stiles has been here twenty years and is a very good clerk, and he told me himself that he would rather go into the sea after the Reeve than have the stove. So I've written your name.\"\n\nIlse opened her mouth and shut it again.\n\n\"You'll say you're too young,\" said the auditor. \"And that you lied to me. You did. You also told me you'd lied, in front of the whole town, which I have never once in thirty years of this work seen anyone do. Your mother would have.\" She held out her small gloved hand, and Ilse took it. \"Count everything, Miss Varga. Write in the margins. Good night.\"\n\nThe carriage went down Tallow Street in the morning with the carters cursing it, and Ilse watched it from the third-floor window until it was gone.\n\nTomas came down the Gull Stair that afternoon on two crutches, which took him nearly an hour and which he would not let anyone help him with, and sat on the bench outside the pilots' shed at the bottom with his splinted leg stuck out in front of him and his unlit pipe in his teeth. Corin Ashe sat beside him. Neither of them said much. Corin had a pilot's oilskin on, which was too big for him, and a pilot's book in his pocket, which Tomas had made him buy, and he was learning the Gullet from the bench, channel by channel, with Tomas drawing it in the dust with the end of a crutch.\n\n\"He'll make a pilot,\" Tomas said to Ilse, when she came down to them at the end of the day. \"God knows how. He gets everything wrong and then argues with the sea about it.\"\n\n\"I'm very persuasive,\" said Corin.\n\n\"The sea isn't.\"\n\nWren was sitting on the bottom step of the Gull Stair eating a cold potato. Mother Gannet was gutting eels. The salt-ships lay over in the mud on the ebb, and the evening light came down the harbour the colour of honey, and somewhere up on the hill a bell was ringing for the end of the day.\n\nIlse climbed back up to the Salt House as the light went. The third floor was empty; Aurek had gone home. She lit the lamp and sat down at the long table, at her own place, and opened the Kestrel's book at the ninth.\n\nFour hundred, in her own clean hand. And beside it, in the margin, in red, in the auditor's tiny writing: _340. Sticks counted. I.V._\n\nShe dipped her pen, and turned to a clean page, and began to count.","wordCount":688,"createdAt":1794216656663},{"id":"AE5msbymz-OUpFdL3Eawj","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"kACrUDbXsjGSKBXJOWAnj","text":"Corin Ashe came back on the night before the spring tide, the way he had come the first time: a knock after midnight, a voice on the landing, a request not to be hit with whatever she was holding.\n\nThis time she opened the door all the way.\n\nHe looked terrible. He had a cut along his jaw that someone had stitched badly, and his clothes were not his own, and he was thinner than a man ought to get in four days. But he was smiling, and when he saw her face the smile went crooked and then went away altogether.\n\n\"Tomas,\" he said. \"Is he —\"\n\n\"He's alive. His leg's broken. He's at the Pans with a man called Hobb.\" She stood aside. \"Come in. You're letting the cold in.\"\n\nHe came in and stood in the middle of her small room and looked at the bolster, which she had sewn up again, badly, and at the feathers that were still in the corners because she had not been able to bring herself to sweep them.\n\n\"I'm sorry about that,\" he said.\n\nShe had known. She had known since the Magister laughed. But she said, \"You,\" anyway, because it needed saying.\n\n\"Me.\" He reached inside his borrowed coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilcloth and held it out to her, exactly as he had on the first night. \"Everybody hides things in the bolster, Ilse. I've been in forty rooms in this city. It's always the bolster or the chimney, and you don't have a chimney.\"\n\nShe did not take it. \"Why?\"\n\n\"Because Seyle's men were coming up Tallow Street with a key the cooper's wife had been paid for, and I got there first. Because I knew where they'd look.\" He was still holding it out. \"And because I thought — when I came up out of that creek, half drowned, with the Reeve's men going past me close enough to touch — I thought, I could sell this. I could sell this back to Halvard Seyle for a great deal of money and go to Hesk and never come back. And I had it in my hand on the Hesk road for a whole day before I turned round.\"\n\n\"Why did you turn round?\"\n\nHe thought about it. She watched him think about it, and was surprised to find that she believed him when he answered.\n\n\"Because you didn't hit me with the poker,\" he said. \"The first night. You could have. You had it right there behind the door, I saw the end of it. And you opened the door a hand's width instead, and took a stranger's letter, and kept it.\" He shrugged. \"Nobody's ever done that. Usually they hit me.\"\n\nShe took the letter. The seal was still pressed down where she had warmed it with the knife. He had not opened it again.\n\n\"The auditor's at the Anchor,\" she said. \"On Rope Walk.\"\n\n\"I know where the Anchor is.\"\n\n\"Will you come with me?\"\n\nCorin Ashe looked at her for a long moment, the long clever face in the lamplight, the scar through his eyebrow.\n\n\"I'm a smuggler,\" he said. \"And a liar. Nobody will believe a word I say.\"\n\n\"They'll believe the sticks,\" said Ilse. \"Aurek has the sticks. And they'll believe the letter. And they'll believe me, because I'm going to tell them I lied, and nobody says that unless it's true.\" She put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, and reached for her coat. \"You only have to stand there.\"\n# The Last Tally\n## The Reeve's Hall\n[\n  Place: #The Reeve's Hall\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Lisbet Ormund @@Halvard Seyle @@Oduya Fenn @@Aurek Stiles @@Corin Ashe @@Mother Gannet @@Wren\n  When: +1 day\n  Items: ^The Reeve's letter:@Lisbet Ormund\n  Facts: ?Fenn is in it with the Reeve:@Lisbet Ormund ?Fenn kept a true book:@Lisbet Ormund ?Fenn kept a true book:reader\n]\n\nThe hearing was called for the hour after noon on the day of the spring tide, in the long hall at the top of the town where the Reeve of Varrow had sat in judgement on fishing disputes and drunk sailors for as long as there had been a Reeve. Lisbet Ormund had called it. She had the right; she had shown the clerk of the Hall a paper with the Exchequer's seal on it, and the clerk had gone white and sent for the Reeve, and the Reeve had come, and smiled, and said that of course the auditor must have whatever she needed.\n\nHe sat now in his own chair at the head of the hall, in the harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs. The Magister sat below him on the bench where witnesses sat, very straight, his long hands on his knees. Half the lower town had come up the hill to stand at the back. Ilse could see Mother Gannet's milky eye in the crowd, and Wren's dirty face beside her, and a great many people she had passed on the Gull Stair every day of her life and never spoken to.\n\nThe auditor stood at a small table in the middle of the floor with her papers in front of her.\n\n\"I'll be brief,\" she said, \"because the tide turns at four and I'm told half of you have boats to see to. I have audited the books of the Salt House of Varrow for the last four years. I find that for the last three of them, one-seventh part of the salt shipped from the Pans has not reached Hesk, and that the books have been written to hide it. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. Miss Varga.\"\n\nIlse stood up.\n\nShe had thought her legs would shake. They did not. She walked out to the table in the middle of the floor in her clerk's grey with her hair pinned and laid the things down on it one by one, the way she would have laid out a ship's sticks to be counted.\n\nThe Kestrel's book, open at the ninth.\n\nThirty-four tally-sticks, which Aurek Stiles had brought up the hill in his coat.\n\nA letter with a red seal pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\n\"On the ninth,\" she said, \"the Kestrel came in through the Gullet with three hundred and forty measures of salt on her sticks. I wrote four hundred in her book, because I was told to. That was a lie, and it was mine.\" She heard the crowd at the back shift and murmur. She did not look at them. \"Two days later the auditor asked me if I'd ever been told to write a number I knew was wrong, and I said no. That was a lie too.\"\n\n\"Miss Varga,\" said the Reeve, pleasantly, from his chair. \"You'll understand that a clerk who admits to two lies in one breath is not —\"\n\n\"And this,\" said Ilse, \"is a letter in your hand, Reeve, to the Magister, telling him where the sixty measures from the Kestrel were to go, and when, and on what ship. It never reached him. A man called Corin Ashe took it out of your post-bag, and gave it to me, and I've kept it.\" She looked up at him. \"You can read it, if you like. Or the auditor can read it aloud. It's quite short.\"\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\nHalvard Seyle looked at the letter on the table, and at the auditor, and at the Magister on the witnesses' bench, who had not moved. Then he looked past them all to the back of the hall, where Corin Ashe was standing against the wall with his arms folded, in a clean coat, and smiled.\n\n\"A smuggler,\" the Reeve said. \"A smuggler steals a letter, and a clerk who lies admits she lies, and an old man —\"\n\n\"I'll speak to the letter,\" said Oduya Fenn.\n\nHe stood up from the bench. He was not looking at the Reeve; he was looking at Ilse, and his face was quite calm.\n\n\"I never received it,\" he said. \"But I know what it says, because I know what every letter Halvard Seyle has written me for three years has said. I'll tell the auditor about every one of them. I'll tell her about the wall. I have kept a book of my own, Madam Auditor — a true one. I kept it because I am a clerk, and I could not bear not to. It is in the stove in my room at the Salt House, behind the firebrick, and it has every measure in it that I ever wrote false.\"\n\nSomebody in the crowd said, very loudly, \"Gods,\" and somebody else laughed, high and shocked.\n\nThe Reeve was on his feet.\n\n\"This hearing is closed,\" he said. \"I am the Reeve of Varrow and this is my hall, and I'll not have —\"\n\n\"It isn't closed, Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund mildly. \"It's the Exchequer's hearing. It's only being held in your hall.\" She picked up the letter. \"Sit down, please. I'm going to read this aloud.\"\n\nHe did not sit down. He looked at the auditor, and at the Magister, and at the crowd filling the back of the hall, and something went out of his big red face all at once, like air out of a bladder. Then he turned and walked out through the door behind his chair, the one that only the Reeve used, and nobody stopped him, because nobody in Varrow had ever stopped the Reeve doing anything.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Gull Stair\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Corin Ashe @@Wren @@Halvard Seyle:dead elsewhere\n  When: same day\n]\n\nThe storm came in at four, with the turn of the tide, as the old people on the quay had said it would. It came out of the south-west in one long grey wall, and the sea came with it.\n\nIlse watched from the top of the Gull Stair. She had run there from the Reeve's Hall with Corin behind her and half the lower town behind him, because Wren had come pelting up the hill shouting that the Reeve's longboat had gone out of the harbour with the Reeve in it, and that the Black Mare was lying off the Chapel again in the teeth of the weather, waiting for him.\n\n\"He's mad,\" Corin said, beside her. He had to shout it. \"He'll never get over the bar in this.\"\n\n\"He knows the bar.\"\n\n\"Nobody knows the bar in this.\"\n\nOut past the harbour mouth the sea had gone white. The salt-ships at their moorings were pitching and snatching at their chains, and the spray was coming over the quay wall in sheets, and down below them on the quay the pilots were out in their oilskins, shouting, hauling boats up the slip. Ilse looked for Tomas among them out of habit before she remembered that Tomas was in a bed at the Pans with his leg in a splint.\n\nThen she saw the longboat.\n\nIt was a small dark thing in the white water out by the Chapel, six oars rising and falling out of time, and it was making for the black ship. She could see the Black Mare now too, beyond it — two masts, no sail, her anchors down, plunging. Someone on her deck was waving a light.\n\n\"They'll make it,\" said Corin. \"Gods damn him, they'll —\"\n\nThe sea took the longboat on the Chapel bar.\n\nShe did not see it happen. One moment there were six oars and a dark hull and the next there was a wave, enormous, grey-green, standing up across the bar the way the sea stands up only where the bottom is very close, and when it went down there was nothing on the bar but white water. A long way off, the light on the Black Mare's deck stopped waving.\n\nNobody on the Gull Stair said anything at all.\n\nThe Black Mare did not wait. They saw her get her anchors up, or cut them — it was too far to tell — and get a scrap of sail on her, and claw her way round to the south, away from the bar and the Chapel and Varrow. Within half an hour she was gone into the rain.\n\nThe Reeve's body came ashore two days later on the hard below the sea-wall at the Pans, the new sea-wall, which had held. One of the pan-men found it. Mother Gannet said afterward that it was the only thing the Reeve ever built that did what it was meant to.","wordCount":2108,"createdAt":1794213959576},{"id":"AmqKjP7GMxdN8g6i0XY07","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Brenn had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. That was what the forty crowns in his coat pocket were for.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter in his hand saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"She's early.\"\n\n\"How early?\"\n\n\"A night. A whole night.\" He sat up. \"The letter said the spring tide. That's tomorrow.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his clever face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern, because he had taken her in through the Gullet on the ninth.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.\n# The Hard\n\n[\n  Place: #The Gull Stair\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Mother Gannet @@Wren\n  When: +2 days\n]\n\nTomas did not come by the next day, as he had said he would, and he did not come the day after.\n\nOn the second morning Ilse went down the Gull Stair before the Salt House bell and found Mother Gannet already at her place at the foot of it, gutting eels into a bucket with a short black knife. The old woman had sold eels at the bottom of the stair for as long as there had been a stair, by her own account, and possibly longer. She had one eye milky and one eye sharp, and it was generally understood in the lower town that the milky one saw more.\n\n\"He's not back,\" Mother Gannet said, before Ilse had opened her mouth. \"The Patience isn't on her mooring. I've had the boy up and down the quay since first light asking.\"\n\n\"Which boy?\"\n\n\"Mine.\" The old woman jerked her chin. Sitting on the bottom step, eating a cold potato with great concentration, was a child of nine or ten in a man's coat cut down at the sleeves. He had a face so dirty it was hard to say whether he was a boy at all. \"Wren. He runs for me. He's not mine, but he runs for me.\"\n\n\"Nobody's seen her,\" Wren said, through potato. \"Not in the harbour. Not at the Pans. Tad Mossop says he saw a sail in the channels last night past the Heron Creek, but Tad Mossop says a lot of things.\"\n\nIlse sat down on the step beside him because her legs had decided it for her.\n\n\"He's a pilot,\" Mother Gannet said, not unkindly. \"He knows every inch of that marsh. If he's out there he's out there because he means to be.\" She dropped an eel into the bucket. \"Question is who he's out there with.\"\n\n\"Corin Ashe.\"\n\n\"Ah.\" The old woman's sharp eye went to her. \"Then the question's what he's out there for.\"\n\nIlse looked at the bucket of eels, at the grey light on the mud, at the salt-ships still lying over on their sides from the ebb. She thought about the letter against her skin and about the auditor at the end of the long table in the draught, asking for the year of the big tide.\n\n\"Mother,\" she said. \"You remember the tide that took the Pans.\"\n\n\"I remember every tide since the old king died.\"\n\n\"What happened after? To the Pans?\"\n\nMother Gannet wiped the black knife on her apron and was quiet for long enough that Wren stopped chewing.\n\n\"Sea came over the wall at the spring tide in Thawmonth,\" she said at last. \"Took the wall, took the pans behind it, took the pan-reeve's house and the pan-reeve's wife with it. Half the salt-works in Varrow, gone under in one night. The Salt House wrote to the capital for money to build it back, and the capital wrote back and said it was a matter for the harbour.\" She spat, neatly, into the mud. \"So it was the Reeve's to mend, and the Reeve said there was no money for it, and for a whole year the pan-men sat on the quay with their hands in their laps. And then one day there was money. And the wall went up, and the pans went back, and everyone said what a fine man the Reeve was.\"\n\n\"Where did the money come from?\"\n\n\"Nobody asked. That's the thing about money that comes when you need it. Nobody asks.\" The old woman picked up another eel. \"Your mother would've asked.\"\n\n\"My mother's dead.\"\n\n\"So she is. And so's the pan-reeve's wife, and the Salt House is still standing, and the Reeve's still got his fine house on the hill, and his fine new wall, which I'll tell you for nothing is built of the worst stone I ever saw and won't see out another ten years of tides.\" The knife went in. \"You asked.\"\n\nIlse stood up. \"If Tomas comes back —\"\n\n\"The boy'll find you.\" Mother Gannet did not look up. \"Go to your counting, girl. Count something.\"","wordCount":1416,"createdAt":1794213829098},{"id":"BeyRzQIlfU3NV8V50wNRg","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"E8x7iKzVjIVHZtYoZ0wvQ","text":"Wren found her at the end of the day, as Mother Gannet had promised he would. He was sitting on the Salt House steps when she came out, with the porter glaring at him from the door, and he stood up as soon as he saw her and said, without any greeting at all, \"The Patience is up the Heron Creek. Tad Mossop saw her. She's on her side on the bar and there's nobody in her.\"\n\nIlse stood on the step with her satchel in her hand.\n\n\"Nobody,\" she said.\n\n\"Tad didn't go close. Tad said there was men about. Reeve's men, in a longboat, going up and down.\" Wren wiped his nose on his cut-down sleeve. \"Mother says you'll want to go and look, and Mother says don't, and Mother says if you're going anyway take me, because I know the way at low water and you don't.\"\n\n\"When's low water?\"\n\n\"Tonight. Middle of the night. It's near springs, so it'll go right out — you can walk to the Chapel nearly dry-shod if you know where the hard is.\" He looked at her with an expression that was much older than the rest of his face. \"Mother said you'd ask that. Mother said you were your mother's daughter.\"\n\nShe thought about going back up to the third floor and telling the auditor everything. The letter, the sticks, the Kestrel's book in her own hand. She thought about the auditor's kindly hen's face, and about the Reeve's men going up and down the channel in a longboat looking for a boat with her brother in it.\n\nIf she told the auditor tonight, the auditor would write to the capital tomorrow, and the capital would send someone in a week. A week was a long time to lie on a bar in the marsh with nobody in your boat.\n\n\"I'll need to go home first,\" she said. \"For boots.\"\n\n\"I'll come with you.\"\n\n\"You don't need to.\"\n\n\"Mother said not to let you out of my sight.\" Wren fell in beside her. \"Mother's never wrong. It's very annoying.\"\n\nThey went up Tallow Street together in the dusk, the tall woman in her clerk's grey and the small dirty boy in his enormous coat, and nobody looked at them twice, because that was the kind of street it was. At the cooper's Ilse left Wren on the step and climbed the stair through the middle of the house to her room.\n\nShe changed into her mother's old marsh boots, which were too big and which she had kept for ten years without once wearing. She took the letter out of her bodice — it had gone soft with her warmth, the seal tacky — and stood for a moment with it in her hand.\n\nIf she went into the marsh with it and they caught her, they would have it. If she left it here —\n\nShe put it in the hollow of the bolster, where the sticks had been. Then she took the lamp and went down to Wren.","wordCount":510,"createdAt":1794217051510},{"id":"Bg5LgRLWrvBfnEJkLp20p","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"r9SBgyYpFJBk2b1o5XkXt","text":"They brought Tomas out on the last of the ebb, the two of them, Ilse under one arm and Wren under the other, and it took four hours, and Ilse did not afterward remember much of it except the cold and the weight and her brother's breath hissing in her ear at every step. Wren found them the hard every time. At the edge of the marsh, where the path came up onto the sea-wall by the Pans, they left Tomas with a pan-man Wren knew, who asked no questions and sent his own son running for a bone-setter, and then Ilse walked home alone through the waking streets with the sun coming up behind her.\n\nThe Magister had said her name. She kept hearing it. Not *the clerk*, not *one of my girls*. *The Varga girl*, in that tired voice she had loved for four years, like a man putting down something heavy.\n\nShe climbed the stair through the middle of the cooper's house. The step that creaked, creaked. The cooper's wife was not singing.\n\nHer door was open.\n\nNot broken. Open, the way you leave a door when you have a key and are in a hurry. Inside, the room looked almost as she had left it — the table, the chair, the lamp-hook — except that everything had been moved a little and put back a little wrong, the way a stranger puts things back. The chair faced the wrong wall. Her mother's comb was on the left of the shelf instead of the right.\n\nThe bolster lay on the bed with its seam cut open from end to end.\n\nShe did not need to look. She looked anyway. She put her hand into the hollow and felt the feathers and the ticking and nothing else, and then she sat down on the bed with the feathers settling around her like snow, and for the first time since the Kestrel's sticks she let herself cry.\n\nShe had not told anyone where she kept it. Not Tomas. Not Wren. Not the auditor, who she had lied to.\n\nShe had told no one, and someone had known.","wordCount":358,"createdAt":1794213937730},{"id":"BgvMdo_qrUnZyYAXwGJMF","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"GIXhkOMgrF_FKcO5VyMzd","text":"The auditor left Varrow on the twelfth day of Seedmonth, in the same carriage that had come down Tallow Street and frightened the porter, and half the lower town came out to see her go.\n\nShe had stayed a fortnight longer than she meant to. She had sat in the Magister's room with the firebrick out of the stove and the Magister's true book open on the desk — a small black book, scorched at the corners, with three years of real numbers in it in a long thin careful hand — and she had copied every page. Then she had sat in the third-floor room with Aurek and Ilse for ten days more and put the real numbers into the real books, page by page, in the margins, in red.\n\n\"The Exchequer will want the Magister in the capital,\" she told Ilse, the last evening, standing at the door of the Salt House with her gloves in her hand. \"There'll be a trial. I'll speak for him. I'll say he kept a true book when he could have kept none, and gave it up when he could have burned it, and that's not nothing.\" She pulled on one glove. \"I don't know what they'll do. Something less than they might have, I hope.\"\n\n\"And the Salt House?\"\n\n\"Will need a Magister.\" Lisbet Ormund pulled on the other glove. \"I've written to say so. I've written to say who.\" She looked up at Ilse with her kindly hen's face. \"Master Stiles has been here twenty years and is a very good clerk, and he told me himself that he would rather go into the sea after the Reeve than have the stove. So I've written your name.\"\n\nIlse opened her mouth and shut it again.\n\n\"You'll say you're too young,\" said the auditor. \"And that you lied to me. You did. You also told me you'd lied, in front of the whole town, which I have never once in thirty years of this work seen anyone do. Your mother would have.\" She held out her small gloved hand, and Ilse took it. \"Count everything, Miss Varga. Write in the margins. Good night.\"\n\nThe carriage went down Tallow Street in the morning with the carters cursing it, and Ilse watched it from the third-floor window until it was gone.\n\nTomas came up the Gull Stair that afternoon on two crutches, which took him nearly an hour and which he would not let anyone help him with, and sat on the bench outside the pilots' shed at the bottom with his splinted leg stuck out in front of him and his unlit pipe in his teeth. Corin Ashe sat beside him. Neither of them said much. Corin had a pilot's oilskin on, which was too big for him, and a pilot's book in his pocket, which Tomas had made him buy, and he was learning the Gullet from the bench, channel by channel, with Tomas drawing it in the dust with the end of a crutch.\n\n\"He'll make a pilot,\" Tomas said to Ilse, when she came down to them at the end of the day. \"God knows how. He gets everything wrong and then argues with the sea about it.\"\n\n\"I'm very persuasive,\" said Corin.\n\n\"The sea isn't.\"\n\nWren was sitting on the bottom step of the Gull Stair eating a cold potato. Mother Gannet was gutting eels. The salt-ships lay over in the mud on the ebb, and the evening light came down the harbour the colour of honey, and somewhere up on the hill a bell was ringing for the end of the day.\n\nIlse climbed back up to the Salt House as the light went. The third floor was empty; Aurek had gone home. She lit the lamp and sat down at the long table, at her own place, and opened the Kestrel's book at the ninth.\n\nFour hundred, in her own clean hand. And beside it, in the margin, in red, in the auditor's tiny writing: *340. Sticks counted. I.V.*\n\nShe dipped her pen, and turned to a clean page, and began to count.","wordCount":687,"createdAt":1794214646109},{"id":"Dlt6riRWzgx48mX1fZ6Gb","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"kACrUDbXsjGSKBXJOWAnj","text":"Corin Ashe came back on the night before the spring tide, the way he had come the first time: a knock after midnight, a voice on the landing, a request not to be hit with whatever she was holding.\n\nThis time she opened the door all the way.\n\nHe looked terrible. He had a cut along his jaw that someone had stitched badly, and his clothes were not his own, and he was thinner than a man ought to get in two days. But he was smiling, and when he saw her face the smile went crooked and then went away altogether.\n\n\"Tomas,\" he said. \"Is he —\"\n\n\"He's alive. His leg's broken. He's at the Pans with a man called Hobb.\" She stood aside. \"Come in. You're letting the cold in.\"\n\nHe came in and stood in the middle of her small room and saw the bolster, which she had sewn up again, badly, and at the feathers that were still in the corners because she had not been able to bring herself to sweep them.\n\n\"I'm sorry about that,\" he said.\n\nShe had known since the Magister laughed. If the Reeve had never sent the letter to Fenn, then only one man in Varrow had known where it had gone, and he had pushed it under her door. But she said, \"You,\" anyway, because it needed saying.\n\n\"Me.\" He reached inside his borrowed coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilcloth and held it out to her, exactly as he had on the first night. \"Everybody hides things in the bolster, Ilse. I've been in forty rooms in this city. It's always the bolster or the chimney, and you don't have a chimney.\"\n\nShe did not take it. \"Why?\"\n\n\"Because Seyle's men were coming up Tallow Street with a key the cooper's wife had been paid for, and I got there first. Because I knew where they'd look.\" He was still holding it out. \"And because I thought — when I came up out of that creek, half drowned, with the Reeve's men going past me close enough to touch — I thought, I could sell this. I could sell this back to Halvard Seyle for a great deal of money and go to Hesk and never come back. And I had it in my hand on the Hesk road for a whole day before I turned round.\"\n\n\"Why did you turn round?\"\n\nHe thought about it. She watched him think about it, and was surprised to find that she believed him when he answered.\n\n\"Because you didn't hit me with the poker,\" he said. \"The first night. You could have. You had it right there behind the door, I saw the end of it. And you opened the door a hand's width instead, and took a stranger's letter, and kept it.\" He shrugged. \"Nobody's ever done that. Usually they hit me.\"\n\nShe took the letter. The seal was still pressed down where she had warmed it with the knife. He had not opened it again.\n\n\"The auditor's at the Anchor,\" she said. \"On Rope Walk.\"\n\n\"I know where the Anchor is.\"\n\n\"Will you come with me?\"\n\nCorin Ashe was quiet for a long moment, the long clever face in the lamplight, the scar through his eyebrow.\n\n\"I'm a smuggler,\" he said. \"And a liar. Nobody will believe a word I say.\"\n\n\"They'll believe the sticks,\" said Ilse. \"Aurek has the sticks. And they'll believe the letter. And they'll believe me, because I'm going to tell them I lied, and nobody says that unless it's true.\" She put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, and reached for her coat. \"You only have to stand there.\"","wordCount":620,"createdAt":1794230232064},{"id":"E1XRKAoGO0gmtXZtckF-9","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"QkH1pzVCb9awQuyhPF6LA","text":"They had been seen. Tomas never knew how — a turn of the Patience's sail against the sky, perhaps, or the creak of her blocks across still water, or simply that a man on the Chapel steps had looked up at the wrong moment. But the light at the Chapel went out all at once, and in the dark there was a shout, and then the sound of oars.\n\n\"Go,\" said Corin. \"Go, go —\"\n\nTomas went. He put the helm over and got the sail up in one long heave and the Patience came round, slow, so slow, broad-beamed old cow that she was, and the flood was against them now, pushing them back up toward the Chapel. Behind them the oars were coming on. Four oars at least. A longboat, light and fast, and the men in her knew the channel nearly as well as he did.\n\n\"They'll have us before the Heron Creek,\" Corin said.\n\n\"They won't.\"\n\n\"They will. I can count, Tomas, it's the only thing I'm good for besides lying.\" Corin was pulling off his boots. \"There's a cut on the left — the one with the dead tree. Where does it go?\"\n\n\"Nowhere. Mud. It's a dead end.\"\n\n\"Can a man stand in it?\"\n\n\"At this tide? To his chest, maybe. What are you —\"\n\n\"They want the boat. They'll follow the boat.\" Corin was on the gunwale now, crouched, his clever face white in the starlight. \"And they want me more than they want you, because I'm the one they've been paying. So when I go over, you keep going, and you make a great deal of noise, and I'll make rather less.\" He grinned suddenly. \"Tell your sister to keep the letter. Tell her it's worth more than I am.\"\n\n\"Corin —\"\n\nHe went over the side without a splash. Tomas never heard him hit the water. One moment there was a man on the gunwale and the next there was the dark and the dead tree going past on the left and nothing else.\n\nHe kept going. He did not know what else to do. He made, as instructed, a great deal of noise — he let the mainsheet run so the block screamed, and he banged the tiller against the thwart, and he shouted Corin's name once, loudly, toward the right-hand bank, as though looking for a man who had fallen in there. Behind him the oars checked, and a voice said something sharp, and the longboat split its attention for a moment between the boat and the bank.\n\nIt was enough to get him into the Heron Creek. It was not enough to get him out of it.\n\nThe Heron was narrow and shallow and he had taken it a thousand times at the top of the tide, but this was not the top of the tide and he was not looking where he was going. The Patience hit the bar at the creek's mouth with a long grinding shudder that went up through the keel and into his teeth, and stopped, and heeled, and he was thrown against the thwart so hard that he heard something in his leg go with a sound like a green stick breaking.\n\nHe lay in the bilge-water and did not scream, because the longboat was still out there.\n\nAfter a time — he did not know how long — he heard the oars go past the mouth of the creek, slow now, searching. A lantern swung. A man's voice said, \"Nothing. She's gone up the Heron and stuck, she'll be there in the morning, we'll have her then,\" and another voice, one he thought he knew, said, \"And the other one?\" and the first voice said, \"Drowned, if he's got any sense.\" Then the oars again, going away, toward the Chapel and the black ship and the salt.\n\nHe waited until he could not hear them. Then he dragged himself up onto the thwart, and looked at his leg, and looked away again quickly. The tide was still making. In an hour the Patience would float off the bar. In an hour he would have to be somewhere else.\n\nThe tower of the Drowned Chapel was a quarter mile off across the marsh, black against the first grey in the east. It was the only dry place for a mile. It was also where the men in the longboat were going.\n\nTomas looked at it for a long time. Then he found the boat-hook, and broke the shaft across his knee to make a splint, and began to bind his leg with the sail-ties, and tried to think of something else.\n# Low Water\n\n[\n  Place: #The Salt House\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Wren\n  When: day 6\n  Items: ^The Reeve's letter:#Ilse's Room\n]\n\nWren found her at the end of the day, as Mother Gannet had promised he would. He was sitting on the Salt House steps when she came out, with the porter glaring at him from the door, and he stood up as soon as he saw her and said, without any greeting at all, \"The Patience is up the Heron Creek. Tad Mossop saw her. She's on her side on the bar and there's nobody in her.\"\n\nIlse stood on the step with her satchel in her hand.\n\n\"Nobody,\" she said.\n\n\"Tad didn't go close. Tad said there was men about. Reeve's men, in a longboat, going up and down.\" Wren wiped his nose on his cut-down sleeve. \"Mother says you'll want to go and look, and Mother says don't, and Mother says if you're going anyway take me, because I know the way at low water and you don't.\"\n\n\"When's low water?\"\n\n\"Tonight. Middle of the night. It's springs tomorrow, so it'll go right out — you can walk to the Chapel nearly dry-shod if you know where the hard is.\" He looked at her with an expression that was much older than the rest of his face. \"Mother said you'd ask that. Mother said you were your mother's daughter.\"\n\nShe thought about going back up to the third floor and telling the auditor everything. The letter, the sticks, the Kestrel's book in her own hand. She thought about the auditor's kindly hen's face, and about the Reeve's men going up and down the channel in a longboat looking for a boat with her brother in it.\n\nIf she told the auditor tonight, the auditor would write to the capital tomorrow, and the capital would send someone in a week. A week was a long time to lie on a bar in the marsh with nobody in your boat.\n\n\"I'll need to go home first,\" she said. \"For boots.\"\n\n\"I'll come with you.\"\n\n\"You don't need to.\"\n\n\"Mother said not to let you out of my sight.\" Wren fell in beside her. \"Mother's never wrong. It's very annoying.\"\n\nThey went up Tallow Street together in the dusk, the tall woman in her clerk's grey and the small dirty boy in his enormous coat, and nobody looked at them twice, because that was the kind of street it was. At the cooper's Ilse left Wren on the step and climbed the stair through the middle of the house to her room.\n\nShe changed into her mother's old marsh boots, which were too big and which she had kept for ten years without once wearing. She took the letter out of her bodice — it had gone soft with her warmth, the seal tacky — and stood for a moment with it in her hand.\n\nIf she went into the marsh with it and they caught her, they would have it. If she left it here —\n\nShe put it in the hollow of the bolster, where the sticks had been. Then she took the lamp and went down to Wren.","wordCount":1310,"createdAt":1794213918986},{"id":"E4TTk8lL7CK3Md0UuPkUy","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"QkH1pzVCb9awQuyhPF6LA","text":"They had been seen. Tomas never knew how — a turn of the Patience's sail against the sky, perhaps, or the creak of her blocks across still water, or simply that a man on the Chapel steps had looked up at the wrong moment. But the light at the Chapel went out all at once, and in the dark there was a shout, and then the sound of oars.\n\n\"Go,\" said Corin. \"Go, go —\"\n\nTomas went. He put the helm over and got the sail up in one long heave and the Patience came round, slow, so slow, broad-beamed old cow that she was, and the flood was against them now, pushing them back up towards the Chapel. Behind them the oars were coming on. Four oars at least. A longboat, light and fast, and the men in her knew the channel nearly as well as he did.\n\n\"They'll have us before the Heron Creek,\" Corin said.\n\n\"They won't.\"\n\n\"They will. I can count, Tomas, it's the only thing I'm good for besides lying.\" Corin was pulling off his boots. \"There's a cut on the left — the one with the dead tree. Where does it go?\"\n\n\"Nowhere. Mud. It's a dead end.\"\n\n\"Can a man stand in it?\"\n\n\"At this tide? To his chest, maybe. What are you —\"\n\n\"They want the boat. They'll follow the boat.\" Corin was on the gunwale now, crouched, his face white in the starlight. \"And they want me more than they want you, because I'm the one they've been paying. So when I go over, you keep going, and you make a great deal of noise, and I'll make rather less.\" He grinned suddenly. \"Tell your sister to keep the letter. Tell her it's worth more than I am.\"\n\n\"Corin —\"\n\nHe went over the side without a splash. Tomas never heard him hit the water. One moment there was a man on the gunwale and the next there was the dark and the dead tree going past on the left and nothing else.\n\nHe kept going. He did not know what else to do. He made, as instructed, a great deal of noise — he let the mainsheet run so the block screamed, and he banged the tiller against the thwart, and he shouted Corin's name once, loudly, towards the right-hand bank, as though looking for a man who had fallen in there. Behind him the oars checked, and a voice said something sharp, and the longboat split its attention for a moment between the boat and the bank.\n\nIt was enough to get him into the Heron Creek. It was not enough to get him out of it.\n\nThe Heron was narrow and shallow and he had taken it a thousand times at the top of the tide, but this was not the top of the tide and he was not looking where he was going. The Patience hit the bar at the creek's mouth with a long grinding shudder that went up through the keel and into his teeth, and stopped, and heeled, and he was thrown against the thwart so hard that he heard something in his leg go with a sound like a green stick breaking.\n\nHe lay in the bilge-water and did not scream, because the longboat was still out there.\n\nAfter a time — he did not know how long — he heard the oars go past the mouth of the creek, slow now, searching. A lantern swung. A man's voice said, \"Nothing. She's gone up the Heron and stuck, she'll be there in the morning, we'll have her then,\" and another voice, one he thought he knew, said, \"And the other one?\" and the first voice said, \"Drowned, if he's got any sense.\" Then the oars again, going away, towards the Chapel and the black ship and the salt.\n\nHe waited until he could not hear them. Then he dragged himself up onto the thwart, and looked at his leg, and looked away again quickly. The tide was still making. In an hour the Patience would float off the bar. In an hour he would have to be somewhere else.\n\nThe tower of the Drowned Chapel was a quarter mile off across the marsh, black against the first grey in the east. It was the only dry place for a mile. It was also where the men in the longboat were going.\n\nTomas looked at it for a long time. Then he found the boat-hook, and broke the shaft across his knee to make a splint, and began to bind his leg with the sail-ties, and tried to think of something else.","wordCount":778,"createdAt":1794216610048},{"id":"E5q-TEzEGaLibxNJM12-n","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"dAT2R-4HoTYOS7lortcKb","text":"Tomas did not come by the next day, as he had said he would, and he did not come the day after.\n\nOn the second morning Ilse went down the Gull Stair before the Salt House bell and found Mother Gannet already at her place at the foot of it, gutting eels into a bucket with a short black knife. The old woman had sold eels at the bottom of the stair for as long as there had been a stair, by her own account, and possibly longer. She had one eye milky and one eye sharp, and it was generally understood in the lower town that the milky one saw more.\n\n\"He's not back,\" Mother Gannet said, before Ilse had opened her mouth. \"The Patience isn't on her mooring. I've had the boy up and down the quay since first light asking.\"\n\n\"Which boy?\"\n\n\"Mine.\" The old woman jerked her chin. Sitting on the bottom step, eating a cold potato with great concentration, was a child of nine or ten in a man's coat cut down at the sleeves. He had a face so dirty it was hard to say whether he was a boy at all. \"Wren. He runs for me. He's not mine, but he runs for me.\"\n\n\"Nobody's seen her,\" Wren said, through potato. \"Not in the harbour. Not at the Pans. Tad Mossop says he saw a sail in the channels last night past the Heron Creek, but Tad Mossop says a lot of things.\"\n\nIlse sat down on the step beside him because her legs had decided it for her.\n\n\"He's a pilot,\" Mother Gannet said, not unkindly. \"He knows every inch of that marsh. If he's out there he's out there because he means to be.\" She dropped an eel into the bucket. \"Question is who he's out there with.\"\n\n\"Corin Ashe.\"\n\n\"Ah.\" The old woman's sharp eye went to her. \"Then the question's what he's out there for.\"\n\nIlse looked at the bucket of eels, at the grey light on the mud, at the salt-ships still lying over on their sides from the ebb. She thought about the letter against her skin and about the auditor at the end of the long table in the draught, asking for the year of the big tide.\n\n\"Mother,\" she said. \"You remember the tide that took the Pans.\"\n\n\"I remember every tide since the old king died.\"\n\n\"What happened after? To the Pans?\"\n\nMother Gannet wiped the black knife on her apron and was quiet for long enough that Wren stopped chewing.\n\n\"Sea came over the wall at the spring tide in Thawmonth,\" she said at last. \"Took the wall, took the pans behind it, took the pan-reeve's house and the pan-reeve's wife with it. Half the salt-works in Varrow, gone under in one night. The Salt House wrote to the capital for money to build it back, and the capital wrote back and said it was a matter for the harbour.\" She spat, neatly, into the mud. \"So it was the Reeve's to mend, and the Reeve said there was no money for it, and for a whole year the pan-men sat on the quay with their hands in their laps. And then one day there was money. And the wall went up, and the pans went back, and everyone said what a fine man the Reeve was.\"\n\n\"Where did the money come from?\"\n\n\"Nobody asked. That's the thing about money that comes when you need it. Nobody asks.\" The old woman picked up another eel. \"Your mother would've asked.\"\n\n\"My mother's dead.\"\n\n\"So she is. And so's the pan-reeve's wife, and the Salt House is still standing, and the Reeve's still got his fine house on the hill, and his fine new wall, which, I'll tell you for nothing is built of the worst stone I ever saw and won't see out another ten years of tides.\" The knife went in. \"You asked.\"\n\nIlse stood up. \"If Tomas comes back —\"\n\n\"The boy'll find you.\" Mother Gannet did not look up. \"Go to your counting, girl. Count something.\"","wordCount":685,"createdAt":1794216607481},{"id":"EdBXQzpVDa2iJq9gOAM31","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"FBthgk0AwfIy4svSwzmYI","text":"The marsh at low water was a different country. The channels that the Patience had sailed were trickles now, running between banks of mud as high as Ilse's head, and the mud shone in the moonlight like the skin of something sleeping. Wren went ahead of her along a path she could not see — the hard, he called it, a spine of shingle under the mud that the old salt-carriers had used before there were boats — and she followed him step for step and did not look down.\n\nIt took them two hours to reach the Chapel. The tower stood up out of the mud on its little island of stone, the arches of the old nave around its foot half-buried, the high windows empty. At high water the sea came in through those windows. Now, with the tide out, there were steps going down into the dark of the crypt, slick with weed, and a smell of salt and old candles.\n\n\"Here's where they load,\" Wren whispered. He pointed. Along the stone of the steps there were fresh scars, white in the moonlight, where something heavy had been dragged. \"Salt. Sacks of it. They bring it up the channel from the Pans at night and keep it up in the tower, above the tide-line, till the ship comes.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\nWren shrugged inside his coat. \"Everybody knows. Nobody says.\"\n\nThey found Tomas in the tower.\n\nHe had dragged himself up the first turn of the stair and wedged himself into a window-niche, and he was so still and so grey that Ilse thought for a moment that he was dead. Then his eyes opened and he said, quite clearly, \"You weren't supposed to come,\" and she knelt on the stair beside him and held his hand and could not say anything at all.\n\nHis leg was splinted with half a boat-hook. He had done it well. He had come across the marsh on his back, ahead of the flood, pushing with his good leg and the stump of the boat-hook, and twice the water had closed over his face. He had been sitting in the niche, he said, for a day and a night, while the Reeve's men came and went below him, loading the black ship from the crypt. He had heard everything. He had not been able to move.\n\n\"There was a voice in the longboat,\" he said. \"The first night. I thought I knew it. I didn't want to.\"\n\n\"Corin,\" Ilse said. \"Where's Corin?\"\n\n\"Went over the side. Took them off me.\" Tomas closed his eyes. \"Don't know if he got out. He said to tell you to keep the letter.\"\n\n\"I have it.\"\n\n\"Good. Ilse — listen. The ship's gone. The Black Mare. She went out on this morning's tide with all of it aboard, and she'll be back for the rest.\" His hand tightened on hers. \"And it's not just the Reeve. I heard them. They were talking about the Salt House. About the books. About someone who's going to fix the books before the auditor —\"\n\nThere was a sound below them. Wren, who had been keeping watch at the foot of the stair, came up it like a cat, silent, his eyes huge.\n\n\"Lantern,\" he breathed. \"Two men. On the hard.\"\n\nThey did not move. Ilse put her hand over Tomas's mouth, not because he would have made a sound but because she needed to hold something. Below them, in the ruined nave, a lantern came and went among the arches, and two men came with it, and stood at the head of the crypt steps where the white scars were.\n\nOne of them was big and wore a harbour officer's coat. She had seen the Reeve of Varrow at a hundred ceremonies on the quay; she knew his shoulders, his walk, the way he stood with his weight back on his heels.\n\nThe other was tall and thin, and held his lantern with a long hand that she had watched sign a thousand pages.\n\n\"She's asking for the flood year,\" the Magister was saying. His voice echoed in the stone. \"She's had it on the table for two days. She has copies of the Hesk ledgers, Halvard. Copies. She knew before she came.\"\n\n\"Then she knows nothing she can prove.\" The Reeve's voice was easy, almost bored. \"Hesk ledgers. A clerk in Hesk can't count. Everyone knows that.\"\n\n\"She'll ask for the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then give her the sticks. You told me the sticks were sorted.\"\n\nThe Magister was quiet. The lantern shook a little.\n\n\"One of my clerks,\" he said at last, \"found the Kestrel short. Before I could see to it.\"\n\n\"Which clerk?\"\n\nIlse felt Tomas's hand tighten on hers until the bones ground.\n\n\"It doesn't matter. I dealt with it. She wrote it in as I told her.\"\n\n\"Oduya.\" The Reeve sounded almost gentle. \"Everything matters. That's how we've lasted three years. Which clerk?\"\n\nThere was a long silence in the nave. Ilse could hear the water beginning to come back, very far off, a sound like breathing.\n\n\"The Varga girl,\" the Magister said.","wordCount":859,"createdAt":1794230092649},{"id":"EhU1pD2LhDL5KEMBmybue","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"3z3uj6msTp-yJfszkLU-n","text":"She went to work. She did not know what else to do with herself, and it seemed important, in a way she could not have explained, that she should be at her place at the long table when the bell went, in her clerk's grey, with her hair pinned, as if nothing had happened. As if she were still the best clerk the Magister had had in thirty years.\n\nAurek glanced up when she came in and opened his mouth to say something about bags of nails, and then saw her face and shut it again.\n\nThe auditor was not there. Her sheet of tiny figures lay on the end of the table under a glass paperweight, and the green ledger was gone from beside it.\n\n\"She's downstairs,\" Aurek said quietly. \"With him. Since first bell.\" He hesitated. \"Ilse. I took the sticks out of the bin.\"\n\nShe turned.\n\n\"The Kestrel's. The day you put them in. I'm not a fool, girl, whatever you think. Nobody finds six sticks behind a stove and then finds them gone again.\" He did not look at her; he looked at his own ledger, at his own tidy upright hand. \"I've been here twenty years. I've written what I was told for three of them. I didn't count. I didn't ask. I told myself the Magister read the margins.\" He turned a page that did not need turning. \"They're in my coat. In the press. If you want them.\"\n\nShe stood very still.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\n\"Because you didn't write anything in the margin,\" said Aurek Stiles. \"You always write in the margin. You've written in the margin every day for four years. And that day you didn't, and I thought: she's frightened. And I thought: I've been frightened for three years and I never once noticed it was the same thing.\"\n\nAt midday the auditor came up the stair and took her sheet of figures from under the paperweight and went down again without a word to either of them. A little after that, the porter came up to say that the Magister wanted Miss Varga in his room.\n\nThe stove was lit. It was always lit. Oduya Fenn sat behind his desk with the green ledger closed in front of him and his long hands folded on top of it, and he did not smile when she came in. That was how she knew he had not slept either.\n\n\"Sit down, Ilse.\"\n\nShe did not sit down.\n\n\"I was at the Chapel last night,\" she said. \"In the tower. With Tomas, who has a broken leg, and has been lying in a window for two days while you loaded salt underneath him.\"\n\nThe Magister closed his eyes.\n\n\"I heard you tell the Reeve my name.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"I imagine you did.\" He opened his eyes again and looked at her, and his face was older than she had ever seen it. \"Will you let me tell you how it happened? Not to excuse it. I don't think it can be excused. But I'd like someone to know, and I think you've earned it more than anyone.\"\n\nShe sat down.\n\nHe told her. What Mother Gannet had told her, and the letter back, _a matter for the harbour._ A whole year of the pan-men sitting on the quay, and the price of salt going up at Hesk, and the Salt House half empty, and the Exchequer writing every quarter to ask why the revenue was down. And then Halvard Seyle in this room, in that chair, with a bottle of something good, saying: _I can build you a wall, Oduya. All it needs is a little salt that nobody counts._\n\n\"One part in seven,\" the Magister said. \"For one year. To pay for the wall. That was all it was going to be. I told myself it was the Exchequer's own salt paying for the Exchequer's own wall, and if the Exchequer had done its duty there'd have been no need. I told myself a great many things.\" He looked down at his hands. \"The wall was built in a year. And at the end of the year I went to Halvard and said, that's enough, and he said — he said, Oduya, you've been writing false books for a year. Do you want to explain that to the Exchequer, or shall I?\"\n\n\"So you kept writing.\"\n\n\"So I kept writing. And the wall is bad stone, and Halvard has a new house, and the Black Mare comes in on the spring tides and takes one-seventh of the salt of Varrow to wherever it goes.\" He lifted his hands, let them fall. \"And you counted the Kestrel's sticks. I knew you would, one day. You're the only one of them who ever counted.\"\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Did you take it?\"\n\nHis face did not change. \"What letter?\"\n\n\"The Reeve's letter to you. About the Kestrel. I had it.\"\n\nFor a moment the Magister of the Salt House looked at her with an expression she could not read at all, and then, very slowly, he began to laugh. It was not a happy sound.\n\n\"He never sent it,\" he said. \"I never had it. I wondered — I wondered why he was so certain I'd been told about the Black Mare coming early, when I hadn't. He said, _I wrote to you._ And I said, _you didn't._ And we each thought the other was lying.\" He wiped his eyes. \"Oh, Ilse. Somebody stole the Reeve's post. And now somebody has stolen it from you.\"","wordCount":932,"createdAt":1794216650540},{"id":"FalNVc4rr0ifr-PGLakMr","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Brenn had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. That was what the forty crowns in his coat pocket were for.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter under his seal saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"Right on her night.\"\n\n\"You knew she'd be here.\"\n\n\"I carry the Reeve's post, Tomas. Letters, mostly, and I've a habit of reading them. I knew she'd be here because I carried the letter that said so, and then I carried it somewhere else.\" He sat up. \"The first load tonight. The rest on the spring tide, three nights on.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern: he had brought the Kestrel in through the Gullet on the ninth with that same boat towing astern.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.","wordCount":758,"createdAt":1794217041228},{"id":"FsnJbtO5Kwqmv48qlpe3v","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"QkH1pzVCb9awQuyhPF6LA","text":"They had been seen. Tomas never knew how — a turn of the Patience's sail against the sky, perhaps, or the creak of her blocks across still water, or simply that a man on the Chapel steps had looked up at the wrong moment. But the light at the Chapel went out all at once, and in the dark there was a shout, and then the sound of oars.\n\n\"Go,\" said Corin. \"Go, go —\"\n\nTomas went. He put the helm over and got the sail up in one long heave and the Patience came round, slow, so slow, broad-beamed old cow that she was, and the flood was against them now, pushing them back up towards the Chapel. Behind them the oars were coming on. Four oars at least. A longboat, light and fast, and the men in her knew the channel nearly as well as he did.\n\n\"They'll have us before the Heron Creek,\" Corin said.\n\n\"They won't.\"\n\n\"They will. I can count, Tomas, it's the only thing I'm good for besides lying.\" Corin was pulling off his boots. \"There's a cut on the left — the one with the dead tree. Where does it go?\"\n\n\"Nowhere. Mud. It's a dead end.\"\n\n\"Can a man stand in it?\"\n\n\"At this tide? To his chest, maybe. What are you —\"\n\n\"They want the boat. They'll follow the boat.\" Corin was on the gunwale now, crouched, his clever face white in the starlight. \"And they want me more than they want you, because I'm the one they've been paying. So when I go over, you keep going, and you make a great deal of noise, and I'll make rather less.\" He grinned suddenly. \"Tell your sister to keep the letter. Tell her it's worth more than I am.\"\n\n\"Corin —\"\n\nHe went over the side without a splash. Tomas never heard him hit the water. One moment there was a man on the gunwale and the next there was the dark and the dead tree going past on the left and nothing else.\n\nHe kept going. He did not know what else to do. He made, as instructed, a great deal of noise — he let the mainsheet run so the block screamed, and he banged the tiller against the thwart, and he shouted Corin's name once, loudly, toward the right-hand bank, as though looking for a man who had fallen in there. Behind him the oars checked, and a voice said something sharp, and the longboat split its attention for a moment between the boat and the bank.\n\nIt was enough to get him into the Heron Creek. It was not enough to get him out of it.\n\nThe Heron was narrow and shallow and he had taken it a thousand times at the top of the tide, but this was not the top of the tide and he was not looking where he was going. The Patience hit the bar at the creek's mouth with a long grinding shudder that went up through the keel and into his teeth, and stopped, and heeled, and he was thrown against the thwart so hard that he heard something in his leg go with a sound like a green stick breaking.\n\nHe lay in the bilge-water and did not scream, because the longboat was still out there.\n\nAfter a time — he did not know how long — he heard the oars go past the mouth of the creek, slow now, searching. A lantern swung. A man's voice said, \"Nothing. She's gone up the Heron and stuck, she'll be there in the morning, we'll have her then,\" and another voice, one he thought he knew, said, \"And the other one?\" and the first voice said, \"Drowned, if he's got any sense.\" Then the oars again, going away, toward the Chapel and the black ship and the salt.\n\nHe waited until he could not hear them. Then he dragged himself up onto the thwart, and looked at his leg, and looked away again quickly. The tide was still making. In an hour the Patience would float off the bar. In an hour he would have to be somewhere else.\n\nThe tower of the Drowned Chapel was a quarter mile off across the marsh, black against the first grey in the east. It was the only dry place for a mile. It was also where the men in the longboat were going.\n\nTomas looked at it for a long time. Then he found the boat-hook, and broke the shaft across his knee to make a splint, and began to bind his leg with the sail-ties, and tried to think of something else.","wordCount":779,"createdAt":1794216609970},{"id":"G46T7LmdU48KWzh7eZjaz","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"QkH1pzVCb9awQuyhPF6LA","text":"They had been seen. Tomas never knew how — a turn of the Patience's sail against the sky, perhaps, or the creak of her blocks across still water, or simply that a man on the Chapel steps had looked up at the wrong moment. But the light at the Chapel went out all at once, and in the dark there was a shout, and then the sound of oars.\n\n\"Go,\" said Corin. \"Go, go —\"\n\nTomas went. He put the helm over and got the sail up in one long heave and the Patience came round, slow, so slow, broad-beamed old cow that she was, and the flood was against them now, pushing them back up towards the Chapel. Behind them the oars were coming on. Four oars at least. A longboat, light and fast, and the men in her knew the channel nearly as well as he did.\n\n\"They'll have us before the Heron Creek,\" Corin said.\n\n\"They won't.\"\n\n\"They will. I can count, Tomas, it's the only thing I'm good for besides lying.\" Corin was pulling off his boots. \"There's a cut on the left — the one with the dead tree. Where does it go?\"\n\n\"Nowhere. Mud. It's a dead end.\"\n\n\"Can a man stand in it?\"\n\n\"At this tide? To his chest, maybe. What are you —\"\n\n\"They want the boat. They'll follow the boat.\" Corin was on the gunwale now, crouched, his face white in the starlight. \"And they want me more than they want you, because I'm the one they've been paying. So when I go over, you keep going, and you make a great deal of noise, and I'll make rather less.\" He grinned suddenly. \"Tell your sister to keep the letter. Tell her it's worth more than I am.\"\n\n\"Corin —\"\n\nHe went over the side without a splash. Tomas never heard him hit the water. One moment there was a man on the gunwale and the next there was the dark and the dead tree going past on the left and nothing else.\n\nHe kept going. He did not know what else to do. He made, as instructed, a great deal of noise — he let the mainsheet run so the block screamed, and he banged the tiller against the thwart, and he shouted Corin's name once, loudly, towards the right-hand bank, as though looking for a man who had fallen in there. Behind him the oars checked, and a voice said something sharp, and the longboat split its attention for a moment between the boat and the bank.\n\nIt was enough to get him into the Heron Creek. It was not enough to get him out of it.\n\nThe Heron was narrow and shallow and he had taken it a thousand times at the top of the tide, but this was not the top of the tide and he was not looking where he was going. The Patience hit the bar at the creek's mouth with a long grinding shudder that went up through the keel and into his teeth, and stopped, and heeled, and he was thrown against the thwart so hard that he heard something in his leg go with a sound like a green stick breaking.\n\nHe lay in the bilge-water and did not scream, because the longboat was still out there.\n\nAfter a time — he did not know how long — he heard the oars go past the mouth of the creek, slow now, searching. A lantern swung. A man's voice said, \"Nothing. She's gone up the Heron and stuck, she'll be there in the morning, we'll have her then,\" and another voice, one he thought he knew, said, \"And the other one?\" and the first voice said, \"Drowned, if he's got any sense.\" Then the oars again, going away, towards the Chapel and the black ship and the salt.\n\nHe waited until he could not hear them. Then he dragged himself up onto the thwart, and looked at his leg, and looked away again quickly. The tide was still making. In an hour the Patience would float off the bar. In an hour he would have to be somewhere else.\n\nThe tower of the Drowned Chapel was a quarter mile off across the marsh, black against the first grey in the east. It was the only dry place for a mile. It was also where the men in the longboat were going.\n\nTomas studied it for a long time. Then he found the boat-hook, and broke the shaft across his knee to make a splint, and began to bind his leg with the sail-ties, and tried to think of something else.","wordCount":777,"createdAt":1794216610083},{"id":"GMYgrLBxQ6UTuKrcGkBmY","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"r9SBgyYpFJBk2b1o5XkXt","text":"They brought Tomas out on the last of the ebb, the two of them, Ilse under one arm and Wren under the other, and it took four hours, and Ilse did not afterward remember much of it except the cold and the weight and her brother's breath hissing in her ear at every step. Wren found them the hard every time. At the edge of the marsh, where the path came up onto the sea-wall by the Pans, they left Tomas with a pan-man Wren knew, who asked no questions and sent his own son running for a bone-setter, and then Ilse walked home alone through the waking streets with the sun coming up behind her.\n\nThe Magister had said her name. She kept hearing it. Not *the clerk*, not *one of my girls*. *The Varga girl*, in that tired voice she had loved for four years, like a man putting down something heavy.\n\nShe climbed the stair through the middle of the cooper's house. The step that creaked, creaked. The cooper's wife was not singing.\n\nHer door was open.\n\nNot broken. Open, the way you leave a door when you have a key and are in a hurry. Inside, the room looked almost as she had left it — the table, the chair, the lamp-hook — except that everything had been moved a little and put back a little wrong, the way a stranger puts things back. The chair faced the wrong wall. Her mother's comb was on the left of the shelf instead of the right.\n\nThe bolster lay on the bed with its seam cut open from end to end.\n\nShe did not need to look. She looked anyway. She put her hand into the hollow and felt the feathers and the ticking and nothing else, and then she sat down on the bed with the feathers settling around her like snow, and for the first time since the Kestrel's sticks she let herself cry.\n\nShe had not told anyone where she kept it. Not Tomas. Not Wren. Not the auditor, who she had lied to.\n\nShe had told no one, and someone had known.\n# Weights and Measures\n## The Magister's Stove\n[\n  Place: #The Salt House\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Aurek Stiles @@Oduya Fenn @@Lisbet Ormund\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The Kestrel's tally-sticks:@Aurek Stiles\n  Facts: ?The Reeve's letter never reached Fenn:@Ilse Varga\n]\n\nShe went to work. She did not know what else to do with herself, and it seemed important, in a way she could not have explained, that she should be at her place at the long table when the bell went, in her clerk's grey, with her hair pinned, as if nothing had happened. As if she were still the best clerk the Magister had had in thirty years.\n\nAurek looked up when she came in and opened his mouth to say something about bags of nails, and then looked at her face properly and shut it again.\n\nThe auditor was not there. Her sheet of tiny figures lay on the end of the table under a glass paperweight, and the green ledger was gone from beside it.\n\n\"She's downstairs,\" Aurek said quietly. \"With him. Since first bell.\" He hesitated. \"Ilse. I took the sticks out of the bin.\"\n\nShe turned.\n\n\"The Kestrel's. The night you put them in. I'm not a fool, girl, whatever you think. Nobody finds six sticks behind a stove and then finds them gone again.\" He did not look at her; he looked at his own ledger, at his own tidy upright hand. \"I've been here twenty years. I've written what I was told for three of them. I didn't count. I didn't ask. I told myself the Magister read the margins.\" He turned a page that did not need turning. \"They're in my coat. In the press. If you want them.\"\n\nShe stood very still.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\n\"Because you didn't write anything in the margin,\" said Aurek Stiles. \"You always write in the margin. You've written in the margin every day for four years. And that day you didn't, and I thought: she's frightened. And I thought: I've been frightened for three years and I never once noticed it was the same thing.\"\n\nAt midday the auditor came up the stair and took her sheet of figures from under the paperweight and went down again without a word to either of them. A little after that, the porter came up to say that the Magister wanted Miss Varga in his room.\n\nThe stove was lit. It was always lit. Oduya Fenn sat behind his desk with the green ledger closed in front of him and his long hands folded on top of it, and he did not smile when she came in. That was how she knew he had not slept either.\n\n\"Sit down, Ilse.\"\n\nShe did not sit down.\n\n\"I was at the Chapel last night,\" she said. \"In the tower. With Tomas, who has a broken leg, and has been lying in a window for two days while you loaded salt underneath him.\"\n\nThe Magister closed his eyes.\n\n\"I heard you tell the Reeve my name.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"I imagine you did.\" He opened his eyes again and looked at her, and his face was older than she had ever seen it. \"Will you let me tell you how it happened? Not to excuse it. I don't think it can be excused. But I'd like someone to know, and I think you've earned it more than anyone.\"\n\nShe sat down.\n\nHe told her. The tide in Thawmonth that took the wall and the pans and the pan-reeve's wife. The letter to the capital, and the letter back, *a matter for the harbour.* A whole year of the pan-men sitting on the quay, and the price of salt going up at Hesk, and the Salt House half empty, and the Exchequer writing every quarter to ask why the revenue was down. And then Halvard Seyle in this room, in that chair, with a bottle of something good, saying: *I can build you a wall, Oduya. All it needs is a little salt that nobody counts.*\n\n\"One part in seven,\" the Magister said. \"For one year. To pay for the wall. That was all it was going to be. I told myself it was the Exchequer's own salt paying for the Exchequer's own wall, and if the Exchequer had done its duty there'd have been no need. I told myself a great many things.\" He looked down at his hands. \"The wall was built in a year. And at the end of the year I went to Halvard and said, that's enough, and he said — he said, Oduya, you've been writing false books for a year. Do you want to explain that to the Exchequer, or shall I?\"\n\n\"So you kept writing.\"\n\n\"So I kept writing. And the wall is bad stone, and Halvard has a new house, and the Black Mare comes in on the spring tides and takes one-seventh of the salt of Varrow to wherever it goes.\" He lifted his hands, let them fall. \"And you counted the Kestrel's sticks. I knew you would, one day. You're the only one of them who ever counted.\"\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Did you take it?\"\n\nHis face did not change. \"What letter?\"\n\n\"The Reeve's letter to you. About the Kestrel. I had it.\"\n\nFor a moment the Magister of the Salt House looked at her with an expression she could not read at all, and then, very slowly, he began to laugh. It was not a happy sound.\n\n\"He never sent it,\" he said. \"I never had it. I wondered — I wondered why he was so certain I'd been told about the Black Mare coming early, when I hadn't. He said, *I wrote to you.* And I said, *you didn't.* And we each thought the other was lying.\" He wiped his eyes. \"Oh, Ilse. Somebody stole the Reeve's post. And now somebody has stolen it from you.\"\n\n[\n  Place: #The Anchor\n  Characters: @@Lisbet Ormund @@Halvard Seyle\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?The Reeve is skimming the salt:@Lisbet Ormund\n]\n\nLisbet Ormund had taken the best room at the Anchor, which was not saying a great deal, and had spent the first evening moving the furniture so that the table caught the light from the window and the bed did not catch the draught from the door. She was at the table now, with her shoes off and her stockinged feet on a hot brick wrapped in flannel, writing her report.\n\nShe had written it three times. Each time it said the same thing — *one part in seven, three years, the Salt House books written to conceal it* — and each time it ended in the same place, which was *I have no proof of who.*\n\nWhen the landlord knocked to say the Reeve was below and wished to call on her, she said, \"Yes, of course, send him up,\" and put her shoes back on, and turned her report face down on the table, and put the hot brick under the bed.\n\nHalvard Seyle filled the doorway. He was a big man and knew it, and he had come in his harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs, and he bowed to her with great courtesy and said he hoped she was comfortable, and that Varrow was not too rough for a lady from the capital, and that if there was anything at all she needed she had only to send to the Reeve's Hall.\n\n\"How kind,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Do sit down.\"\n\nHe sat. The chair complained.\n\n\"I hear you've been at the Salt House books,\" he said.\n\n\"I have.\"\n\n\"Poor Fenn. He's not the man he was. The flood hit him very hard — he took it personally, you know, as though the sea had done it to spite him. I've had to carry a good deal of the work since.\" He smiled. \"If you've found anything amiss, I'd hope you'd come to me first. Before the capital. It would be a shame to ruin an old man's name over a few clerical errors that the harbour could put right quietly.\"\n\n\"A few clerical errors,\" the auditor repeated.\n\n\"Sticks go missing,\" said the Reeve. \"It happens all the time.\"\n\nLisbet Ormund looked at him for a long moment with her kindly hen's face. Then she turned her report over on the table and pushed it across to him.\n\n\"One part in seven,\" she said. \"Every month for three years. That is not a few clerical errors, Reeve. That is twenty-two thousand crowns of the Exchequer's revenue, near enough, and it is going somewhere, and it is not going to Hesk.\" She folded her hands. \"I'll tell you what I have, since you've been good enough to call. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. I have a tallyclerk who lied to my face and was frightened to death doing it, and a Magister who hasn't slept in three nights. What I don't have is a name.\" She smiled at him. \"I don't suppose you'd care to give me one?\"\n\nThe Reeve did not smile back.\n\n\"You've been in Varrow four days, madam,\" he said. \"You don't know the tides. People who don't know the tides get into trouble out on the marsh. Every year we lose one or two. Visitors, mostly.\" He stood up. \"I'd be careful.\"\n\n\"I always am,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Good night, Reeve.\"\n\nWhen he had gone she sat for a while without moving. Then she got the hot brick back out from under the bed, which had gone lukewarm, and put her feet on it, and turned her report face down again, and wrote at the bottom of a fresh sheet, in her tiny neat hand: *Seyle. Certain. No proof.*","wordCount":1989,"createdAt":1794213937849},{"id":"GN8FwUL_eceZOYUHWEwgT","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"JLebbu3pA-RXZKvs35ANa","text":"Lisbet Ormund had taken the best room at the Anchor, which was not saying a great deal, and had spent the first evening moving the furniture so that the table caught the light from the window and the bed did not catch the draught from the door. She was at the table now, with her shoes off and her stockinged feet on a hot brick wrapped in flannel, writing her report.\n\nShe had written it three times. Each time it said the same thing — _one part in seven, three years, the Salt House books written to conceal it_ — and each time it ended in the same place, which was _I have no proof of who._\n\nWhen the landlord knocked to say the Reeve was below and wished to call on her, she said, \"Yes, of course, send him up,\" and put her shoes back on, and turned her report face down on the table, and put the hot brick under the bed.\n\nHalvard Seyle filled the doorway. He was a big man and knew it, and he had come in his harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs, and he bowed to her with great courtesy and said he hoped she was comfortable, and that Varrow was not too rough for a lady from the capital, and that if there was anything at all she needed she had only to send to the Reeve's Hall.\n\n\"How kind,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Do sit down.\"\n\nHe sat. The chair complained.\n\n\"I hear you've been at the Salt House books,\" he said.\n\n\"I have.\"\n\n\"Poor Fenn. He's not the man he was. The flood hit him very hard — he took it personally, you know, as though the sea had done it to spite him. I've had to carry a good deal of the work since.\" He smiled. \"If you've found anything amiss, I'd hope you'd come to me first. Before the capital. It would be a shame to ruin an old man's name over a few clerical errors that the harbour could put right quietly.\"\n\n\"A few clerical errors,\" the auditor repeated.\n\n\"Sticks go missing,\" said the Reeve. \"It happens all the time.\"\n\nLisbet Ormund regarded him for a long moment. Then she turned her report over on the table and pushed it across to him.\n\n\"One part in seven,\" she said. \"Every month for three years. That is not a few clerical errors, Reeve. That is twenty-two thousand crowns of the Exchequer's revenue, near enough, and it is going somewhere, and it is not going to Hesk.\" She folded her hands. \"I'll tell you what I have, since you've been good enough to call. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. I have a tallyclerk who lied to my face and was frightened to death doing it, and a Magister who hasn't slept in three nights. What I don't have is a name.\" She smiled at him. \"I don't suppose you'd care to give me one?\"\n\nThe Reeve did not smile back.\n\n\"You've been in Varrow four days, madam,\" he said. \"You don't know the tides. People who don't know the tides get into trouble out on the marsh. Every year we lose one or two. Visitors, mostly.\" He stood up. \"I'd be careful.\"\n\n\"I always am,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Good night, Reeve.\"\n\nWhen he had gone she sat for a while without moving. Then she got the hot brick back out from under the bed — it had gone lukewarm, and put her feet on it, and turned her report face down again, and wrote at the bottom of a fresh sheet, in her tiny neat hand: _Seyle. Certain. No proof._","wordCount":618,"createdAt":1794216651673},{"id":"H7Spjxfk7-rcuULDMA70V","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"Jwi40uq_PG4-AT77dhdd6","text":"The auditor came two days early.\n\nIlse heard her before she saw her — a carriage on the cobbles of Tallow Street, which was a thing that did not happen; nobody drove a carriage into the lower town because the streets were too narrow and the carters too rude. Then voices in the yard, and the porter's voice going up high the way it did when he was frightened, and then a long silence, and then feet on the stair.\n\nLisbet Ormund was not what Ilse had expected. She had pictured someone like the Magister, tall and grey and sorrowful, or someone like the Reeve, red-faced and loud. The woman who came into the third-floor room was small and round and perhaps fifty, in a brown travelling coat with the mud of the coast road still on its hem, and she had a face like a kindly hen. She stood in the doorway and took in the shelves of ledgers, all forty years of them, with an expression of pure delight.\n\n\"Oh,\" she said. \"Oh, look at that. You keep them up here? Under the roof?\"\n\n\"It's the driest room, madam,\" said Aurek, who had got to his feet so fast his stool had gone over.\n\n\"It's the hottest room in summer,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Paper doesn't like heat any more than it likes damp. Never mind. Never mind.\" She came in and took off her gloves. \"I'm the Exchequer's auditor. I expect you've been told I'd be along. I expect you were told the twentieth.\" She smiled at both of them. \"I find it saves a great deal of everyone's time to come on the eighteenth.\"\n\nIlse found that she had stood up too, and that her hand had gone flat against the front of her bodice, where the letter was. She took it away.\n\n\"Magister Fenn —\" Aurek began.\n\n\"Is downstairs, and has given me the run of the place, and very gracious he was about it. He's sending up tea. You'll be Master Stiles. And you are —?\"\n\n\"Ilse Varga, madam. Tallyclerk.\"\n\n\"Varga.\" The auditor considered her a moment longer than was comfortable. \"There was a Varga at the Hesk customs house, years ago. A woman. Very good with figures.\"\n\n\"My mother, madam.\"\n\n\"Was she? Well.\" Lisbet Ormund turned back to the shelves. \"Then you'll know the difference between a book that's right and a book that's tidy, Miss Varga. Most people don't.\" She ran a finger along the spines, the newest first. \"I'll want this year's, and last year's, and — let me see — the year of the big tide. When was that? Three years back?\"\n\n\"Four, madam,\" said Aurek.\n\n\"Three,\" said Ilse, at the same moment.\n\nIlse and Aurek looked at each other. The auditor regarded them both, pleasantly.\n\n\"The spring tide that took the Pans,\" Ilse said. \"It was three years in Thawmonth. I'd been here a year.\"\n\n\"Then I'll have that one,\" said Lisbet Ormund, \"and the one before it, so I can see the difference. And I'll have the tally-sticks for whatever's come in this month. All of them.\" She sat down at the end of the long table, in the place where nobody ever sat because the draught came under the door there, and folded her small hands. \"I'm told sticks go missing,\" she said. \"I'm told it happens all the time.\"\n\nNobody said anything. Down in the yard the porter was shouting for someone to bring up the tea.","wordCount":580,"createdAt":1794217037110},{"id":"HJcGYyKvGj6LTtwh-9yQq","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"dAT2R-4HoTYOS7lortcKb","text":"Tomas did not come by the next day, as he had said he would, and he did not come the day after.\n\nOn the second morning Ilse went down the Gull Stair before the Salt House bell and found Mother Gannet already at her place at the foot of it, gutting eels into a bucket with a short black knife. The old woman had sold eels at the bottom of the stair for as long as there had been a stair, by her own account, and possibly longer. She had one eye milky and one eye sharp, and it was generally understood in the lower town that the milky one saw more.\n\n\"He's not back,\" Mother Gannet said, before Ilse had opened her mouth. \"The Patience isn't on her mooring. I've had the boy up and down the quay since first light asking.\"\n\n\"Which boy?\"\n\n\"Mine.\" The old woman jerked her chin. Sitting on the bottom step, eating a cold potato with great concentration, was a child of nine or ten in a man's coat cut down at the sleeves. He had a face so dirty it was hard to say whether he was a boy at all. \"Wren. He runs for me. He's not mine, but he runs for me.\"\n\n\"Nobody's seen her,\" Wren said, through potato. \"Not in the harbour. Not at the Pans. Tad Mossop says he saw a sail in the channels last night past the Heron Creek, but Tad Mossop says a lot of things.\"\n\nIlse sat down on the step beside him because her legs had decided it for her.\n\n\"He's a pilot,\" Mother Gannet said, not unkindly. \"He knows every inch of that marsh. If he's out there he's out there because he means to be.\" She dropped an eel into the bucket. \"Question is who he's out there with.\"\n\n\"Corin Ashe.\"\n\n\"Ah.\" The old woman's sharp eye went to her. \"Then the question's what he's out there for.\"\n\nIlse looked at the bucket of eels, at the grey light on the mud, at the salt-ships still lying over on their sides from the ebb. She thought about the letter against her skin and about the auditor at the end of the long table in the draught, asking for the year of the big tide.\n\n\"Mother,\" she said. \"You remember the tide that took the Pans.\"\n\n\"I remember every tide since the old king died.\"\n\n\"What happened after? To the Pans?\"\n\nMother Gannet wiped the black knife on her apron and was quiet for long enough that Wren stopped chewing.\n\n\"Sea came over the wall at the spring tide in Thawmonth,\" she said at last. \"Took the wall, took the pans behind it, took the pan-reeve's house and the pan-reeve's wife with it. Half the salt-works in Varrow, gone under in one night. The Salt House wrote to the capital for money to build it back, and the capital wrote back and said it was a matter for the harbour.\" She spat, neatly, into the mud. \"So it was the Reeve's to mend, and the Reeve said there was no money for it, and for a whole year the pan-men sat on the quay with their hands in their laps. And then one day there was money. And the wall went up, and the pans went back, and everyone said what a fine man the Reeve was.\"\n\n\"Where did the money come from?\"\n\n\"Nobody asked. That's the thing about money that comes when you need it. Nobody asks.\" The old woman picked up another eel. \"Your mother would've asked.\"\n\n\"My mother's dead.\"\n\n\"So she is. And so's the pan-reeve's wife, and the Salt House is still standing, and the Reeve's still got his fine house on the hill, and his fine new wall, which I'll tell you for nothing is built of the worst stone I ever saw and won't see out another ten years of tides.\" The knife went in. \"You asked.\"\n\nIlse stood up. \"If Tomas comes back —\"\n\n\"The boy'll find you.\" Mother Gannet did not look up. \"Go to your counting, girl. Count something.\"","wordCount":685,"createdAt":1794216607440},{"id":"HwtUaQAeMfaWld02IrcPc","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"6iI4LgbwDeFqR_Cruf7mE","text":"She broke the seal in the morning, of course. Corin Ashe had known she would; that was why he had told her not to.\n\nThe storm had blown itself out in the night and left the sky scoured and pale. She sat at her table with a heel of yesterday's bread and slid the blade of her knife under the wax, carefully, so that it lifted in one piece and she could have pressed it back if she had been a different sort of person. Inside was a single sheet, folded twice, in a secretary's hand. At the bottom, a signature she had seen on a hundred harbour warrants. Halvard Seyle, Reeve of Varrow.\n\nThe letter was to Fenn.\n\n*Magister —*\n\n*The sixty from the Kestrel will go with the rest. The Black Mare lies off the Chapel on the spring tide and will take all of it then; there is no other night. Ormund leaves the capital on the twelfth and will be with you within the week, so see that the books are clean before she comes. I need not tell you what a clean book means. The levy is paid in full this year, as every year. You have my word on the Pans.*\n\n*— H.S.*\n\nShe read it four times. The fourth time she read it aloud, very quietly, to hear whether it said something else when it was spoken. It did not.\n\n*The sixty from the Kestrel.* So it was not six sticks dropped in the harbour. *With the rest.* So it was not only the Kestrel. She thought of all the books on the third floor, the forty years of them on their shelves under the eaves, and of how many times she had written a number into a column without counting the sticks, because the sticks had been counted by somebody else, because the Magister read the margins.\n\n*You have my word on the Pans.* That one she did not understand at all.\n\nShe folded the letter again and looked at the seal, and then she did a thing she was not proud of: she warmed the knife over the lamp and pressed the wax back down, so that from a little way off it looked whole. Then she put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, where it crackled when she breathed, and she went to work.\n\nAurek Stiles was already at the long table. He looked up when she came in and said, \"You look like a woman who's slept on a bag of nails,\" and she said, \"I found the sticks,\" and he said, \"Behind the stove?\" and she said, \"Behind the stove,\" and took them out of her satchel and put them in the bin, all thirty-four.\n\nIt was the first lie she had told at the Salt House. It went down easier than she had expected, and that frightened her more than the letter did.\n\nAt midday she went down to the Magister's room on an errand about the Heron's book and stood in his doorway while he signed it. His hand did not shake. He asked after Tomas, and whether the Patience had come through the storm, and he told her she was the best clerk he had had in thirty years, which he had told her before. She watched him sign and thought: *see that the books are clean.*\n\nShe had always believed that she could tell when someone was lying to her. She went back up the stairs knowing that she had been wrong about that, and did not know for how long.","wordCount":599,"createdAt":1794214646075},{"id":"I7JSveltuNP7IG5gcGqOF","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"DS5E6n6CNj-_T8xgA9nT-","text":"Varrow at low water smelt of mud and iron and the sweet rot of weed, and the whole of the lower town came down to the quay to look at the sky.\n\nThere was a storm coming. Ilse could see it from the top of the Gull Stair, a long bruise lying along the horizon to the south-west, with the light behind it gone the colour of a boiled sweet. The harbour had drained down to its channels and the salt-ships sat over on their bilges in the mud, masts leaning together like drunk men holding each other up. Below her the stair went down in its two hundred worn steps to the quay, and the quay was full.\n\nShe found Tomas where she always found him, sitting on an upturned basket outside the pilots' shed with a pipe he never lit, looking at the water as if it owed him money.\n\n\"You took the Kestrel in,\" she said.\n\n\"Hello, Ilse. Good day at the counting? Lovely to see you, Tomas.\" He moved over on the basket to make room for her. \"I took her in on the ninth. Why?\"\n\n\"Was it rough?\"\n\n\"It was the Gullet in a southerly. It's always rough.\" Her brother was three years older than her and had their mother's face, broad and patient, and the same habit of knowing what she was going to say before she said it and waiting for her to say it anyway. \"What's wrong with the Kestrel?\"\n\n\"Was her mate sick?\"\n\nTomas laughed. \"Jory Pell? Jory's been at sea since he could walk. He's never been sick in his life. He was singing.\" He stopped laughing. \"Why?\"\n\nShe told him. She kept her voice down, though nobody on the quay was listening to anything but the wind getting up. Tomas listened with the pipe in his teeth.\n\n\"Sixty measures,\" he said at last. \"That's — what is that, in silver?\"\n\n\"At the Hesk price, nine hundred crowns. Near enough.\"\n\nHe whistled, low and long. \"And the Magister said.\"\n\n\"He said sticks go missing.\"\n\n\"They do.\" But he was frowning now, the way she must have been frowning at the ledger. \"Not six of them off one ship, they don't. A man might lose one. Six is a decision.\" He took the pipe out of his mouth and turned it over. \"What are you going to do?\"\n\n\"I don't know. I wrote it in. Four hundred.\"\n\n\"Ilse.\"\n\n\"What was I supposed to write? He's the Magister. He got me the place.\" She pressed the satchel against her side and felt the sticks shift inside it. \"I took the sticks.\"\n\nTomas looked at the satchel and then at her. \"You took the — that's the Salt House's property.\"\n\n\"I know what it is.\"\n\n\"If anybody knew they were short, they'll know you took them.\"\n\n\"Nobody knows they were short. Aurek thinks I dropped them behind the stove.\"\n\nThe first of the rain came then, a few fat drops striking the stones between their feet, each one leaving a dark coin. All along the quay people began to move, hauling covers over baskets, shouting for children. Out past the harbour mouth the sea had gone a hard, flat grey.\n\n\"Go home,\" Tomas said. \"Put them somewhere dry. Don't do anything else tonight.\" He stood up and knocked the pipe against his palm out of habit, though there was nothing in it. \"I've got to see to the Patience before this comes in. I'll come by tomorrow.\"\n\n\"Tomas —\"\n\n\"Tomorrow.\" He put a hand on her head, the way he had when she was small, and then he was gone into the crowd, a broad back in an oilskin, moving against the people coming up.\n\nShe climbed the Gull Stair in the rain. By the time she reached the top the storm had come in over the bar and the whole harbour was white.","wordCount":651,"createdAt":1794216601185},{"id":"J3yuCf9kUudQK3z3KpolP","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"6iI4LgbwDeFqR_Cruf7mE","text":"She broke the seal in the morning, of course. Corin Ashe had known she would; that was why he had told her not to.\n\nThe storm had blown itself out in the night and left the sky scoured and pale. She sat at her table with a heel of yesterday's bread and slid the blade of her knife under the wax, carefully, so that it lifted in one piece and she could have pressed it back if she had been a different sort of person. Inside was a single sheet, folded twice, in a secretary's hand. At the bottom, two initials she had seen on a hundred harbour warrants, and the seal under them: H.S. Halvard Seyle, Reeve of Varrow.\n\nThe letter was to Fenn.\n\n_Magister —_\n\n_The sixty from the Kestrel will go with the rest. The Black Mare lies off the Chapel on the spring tide and will take all of it then; there is no other night. Ormund leaves the capital on the twelfth and will be with you within the week, so see that the books are clean before she comes. I need not tell you what a clean book means. The levy is paid in full this year, as every year. You have my word on the Pans._\n\n_— H.S._\n\nShe read it four times. The fourth time she read it aloud, quietly, to hear whether it said something else when it was spoken. It did not.\n\n_The sixty from the Kestrel._ So it was not six sticks dropped in the harbour. _With the rest._ So it was not only the Kestrel. She thought of all the books on the third floor, the forty years of them on their shelves under the eaves, and of how many times she had written a number into a column without counting the sticks, because the sticks had been counted by somebody else, because the Magister read the margins.\n\n_You have my word on the Pans._ That one she did not understand at all.\n\nShe folded the letter again, and then she did a thing she was not proud of: she warmed the knife over the lamp and pressed the wax back down, so that from a little way off it looked whole. Then she put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, where it crackled when she breathed, and she went to work.\n\nAurek Stiles was already at the long table. When she came in he said, \"You look like a woman who's slept on a bag of nails,\" and she said, \"I found the sticks,\" and he said, \"Behind the stove?\" and she said, \"Behind the stove,\" and took them out of her satchel and put them in the bin, all thirty-four.\n\nIt was the first lie she had told at the Salt House. It went down easier than she had expected, and that frightened her more than the letter did.\n\nAt midday she went down to the Magister's room on an errand about the Heron's book and stood in his doorway while he signed it. His hand did not shake. He asked after Tomas, and whether the Patience had come through the storm, and he told her she was the best clerk he had had in thirty years, which he had told her before. She watched him sign and thought: _see that the books are clean._\n\nShe had always believed that she could tell when someone was lying to her. She went back up the stairs knowing that she had been wrong about that, and did not know for how long.","wordCount":596,"createdAt":1794217035063},{"id":"J57hbOPrLi7PoFAAduk-k","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"JLebbu3pA-RXZKvs35ANa","text":"Lisbet Ormund had taken the best room at the Anchor, which was not saying a great deal, and had spent the first evening moving the furniture so that the table caught the light from the window and the bed did not catch the draught from the door. She was at the table now, with her shoes off and her stockinged feet on a hot brick wrapped in flannel, writing her report.\n\nShe had written it three times. Each time it said the same thing — *one part in seven, three years, the Salt House books written to conceal it* — and each time it ended in the same place, which was *I have no proof of who.*\n\nWhen the landlord knocked to say the Reeve was below and wished to call on her, she said, \"Yes, of course, send him up,\" and put her shoes back on, and turned her report face down on the table, and put the hot brick under the bed.\n\nHalvard Seyle filled the doorway. He was a big man and knew it, and he had come in his harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs, and he bowed to her with great courtesy and said he hoped she was comfortable, and that Varrow was not too rough for a lady from the capital, and that if there was anything at all she needed she had only to send to the Reeve's Hall.\n\n\"How kind,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Do sit down.\"\n\nHe sat. The chair complained.\n\n\"I hear you've been at the Salt House books,\" he said.\n\n\"I have.\"\n\n\"Poor Fenn. He's not the man he was. The flood hit him very hard — he took it personally, you know, as though the sea had done it to spite him. I've had to carry a good deal of the work since.\" He smiled. \"If you've found anything amiss, I'd hope you'd come to me first. Before the capital. It would be a shame to ruin an old man's name over a few clerical errors that the harbour could put right quietly.\"\n\n\"A few clerical errors,\" the auditor repeated.\n\n\"Sticks go missing,\" said the Reeve. \"It happens all the time.\"\n\nLisbet Ormund looked at him for a long moment with her kindly hen's face. Then she turned her report over on the table and pushed it across to him.\n\n\"One part in seven,\" she said. \"Every month for three years. That is not a few clerical errors, Reeve. That is twenty-two thousand crowns of the Exchequer's revenue, near enough, and it is going somewhere, and it is not going to Hesk.\" She folded her hands. \"I'll tell you what I have, since you've been good enough to call. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. I have a tallyclerk who lied to my face and was frightened to death doing it, and a Magister who hasn't slept in three nights. What I don't have is a name.\" She smiled at him. \"I don't suppose you'd care to give me one?\"\n\nThe Reeve did not smile back.\n\n\"You've been in Varrow four days, madam,\" he said. \"You don't know the tides. People who don't know the tides get into trouble out on the marsh. Every year we lose one or two. Visitors, mostly.\" He stood up. \"I'd be careful.\"\n\n\"I always am,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Good night, Reeve.\"\n\nWhen he had gone she sat for a while without moving. Then she got the hot brick back out from under the bed, which had gone lukewarm, and put her feet on it, and turned her report face down again, and wrote at the bottom of a fresh sheet, in her tiny neat hand: *Seyle. Certain. No proof.*","wordCount":623,"createdAt":1794214646106},{"id":"JvqOOaKLeITk45GayjwSZ","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"kACrUDbXsjGSKBXJOWAnj","text":"Corin Ashe came back on the night before the spring tide, the way he had come the first time: a knock after midnight, a voice on the landing, a request not to be hit with whatever she was holding.\n\nThis time she opened the door all the way.\n\nHe looked terrible. He had a cut along his jaw that someone had stitched badly, and his clothes were not his own, and he was thinner than a man ought to get in four days. But he was smiling, and when he saw her face the smile went crooked and then went away altogether.\n\n\"Tomas,\" he said. \"Is he —\"\n\n\"He's alive. His leg's broken. He's at the Pans with a man called Hobb.\" She stood aside. \"Come in. You're letting the cold in.\"\n\nHe came in and stood in the middle of her small room and looked at the bolster, which she had sewn up again, badly, and at the feathers that were still in the corners because she had not been able to bring herself to sweep them.\n\n\"I'm sorry about that,\" he said.\n\nShe had known. She had known since the Magister laughed. But she said, \"You,\" anyway, because it needed saying.\n\n\"Me.\" He reached inside his borrowed coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilcloth and held it out to her, exactly as he had on the first night. \"Everybody hides things in the bolster, Ilse. I've been in forty rooms in this city. It's always the bolster or the chimney, and you don't have a chimney.\"\n\nShe did not take it. \"Why?\"\n\n\"Because Seyle's men were coming up Tallow Street with a key the cooper's wife had been paid for, and I got there first. Because I knew where they'd look.\" He was still holding it out. \"And because I thought — when I came up out of that creek, half drowned, with the Reeve's men going past me close enough to touch — I thought, I could sell this. I could sell this back to Halvard Seyle for a great deal of money and go to Hesk and never come back. And I had it in my hand on the Hesk road for a whole day before I turned round.\"\n\n\"Why did you turn round?\"\n\nHe thought about it. She watched him think about it, and was surprised to find that she believed him when he answered.\n\n\"Because you didn't hit me with the poker,\" he said. \"The first night. You could have. You had it right there behind the door, I saw the end of it. And you opened the door a hand's width instead, and took a stranger's letter, and kept it.\" He shrugged. \"Nobody's ever done that. Usually they hit me.\"\n\nShe took the letter. The seal was still pressed down where she had warmed it with the knife. He had not opened it again.\n\n\"The auditor's at the Anchor,\" she said. \"On Rope Walk.\"\n\n\"I know where the Anchor is.\"\n\n\"Will you come with me?\"\n\nCorin Ashe looked at her for a long moment, the long clever face in the lamplight, the scar through his eyebrow.\n\n\"I'm a smuggler,\" he said. \"And a liar. Nobody will believe a word I say.\"\n\n\"They'll believe the sticks,\" said Ilse. \"Aurek has the sticks. And they'll believe the letter. And they'll believe me, because I'm going to tell them I lied, and nobody says that unless it's true.\" She put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, and reached for her coat. \"You only have to stand there.\"","wordCount":595,"createdAt":1794213959495},{"id":"JwVw3DPF75MrSW4lqpIEX","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"LQxthMqMx2ix2u3pYQu7A","text":"The hearing was called for the hour after noon on the day of the spring tide, in the long hall at the top of the town where the Reeve of Varrow had sat in judgement on fishing disputes and drunk sailors for as long as there had been a Reeve. Lisbet Ormund had called it. She had the right; she had shown the clerk of the Hall a paper with the Exchequer's seal on it, and the clerk had gone white and sent for the Reeve, and the Reeve had come, and smiled, and said that of course the auditor must have whatever she needed.\n\nHe sat now in his own chair at the head of the hall, in the harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs. The Magister sat below him on the bench where witnesses sat, very straight, his long hands on his knees. Half the lower town had come up the hill to stand at the back. Ilse could see Mother Gannet's milky eye in the crowd, and Wren's dirty face beside her, and a great many people she had passed on the Gull Stair every day of her life and never spoken to. The old people on the quay had been saying since dawn that there was weather coming in with the tide.\n\nThe auditor stood at a small table in the middle of the floor with her papers in front of her.\n\n\"I'll be brief,\" she said, \"because the tide turns at four and I'm told half of you have boats to see to. I have audited the books of the Salt House of Varrow for the last four years. I find that for the last three of them, one-seventh part of the salt shipped from the Pans has not reached Hesk, and that the books have been written to hide it. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. Miss Varga.\"\n\nIlse stood up.\n\nShe had thought her legs would shake. They did not. She walked out to the table in the middle of the floor in her clerk's grey with her hair pinned and laid the things down on it one by one, the way she would have laid out a ship's sticks to be counted.\n\nThe Kestrel's book, open at the ninth.\n\nThirty-four tally-sticks, which Aurek Stiles had brought up the hill in his coat.\n\nA letter with a red seal pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\n\"On the ninth,\" she said, \"the Kestrel came in through the Gullet with three hundred and forty measures of salt on her sticks. I wrote four hundred in her book, because I was told to. That was a lie, and it was mine.\" She heard the crowd at the back shift and murmur. She did not look at them. \"Two days after I wrote it, the auditor asked me if I'd ever been told to write a number I knew was wrong, and I said no. That was a lie too.\"\n\n\"Miss Varga,\" said the Reeve, pleasantly, from his chair. \"You'll understand that a clerk who admits to two lies in one breath is not —\"\n\n\"And this,\" said Ilse, \"is a letter under your seal, Reeve, to the Magister, telling him where the sixty measures from the Kestrel were to go, and when, and on what ship. It never reached him. A man called Corin Ashe took it out of your post-bag, and gave it to me, and I've kept it.\" She looked up at him. \"You can read it, if you like. Or the auditor can read it aloud. It's quite short.\"\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\nHalvard Seyle looked at the letter on the table, and at the auditor, and at the Magister on the witnesses' bench, who had not moved. Then he looked past them all to the back of the hall, where Corin Ashe was standing against the wall with his arms folded, in a clean coat, and smiled.\n\n\"A smuggler,\" the Reeve said. \"A smuggler steals a letter, and a clerk who lies admits she lies, and an old man —\"\n\n\"I'll speak to the letter,\" said Oduya Fenn.\n\nHe stood up from the bench. He was not looking at the Reeve; he was looking at Ilse, and his face was quite calm.\n\n\"I never received it,\" he said. \"But I know what it says, because I know what every letter Halvard Seyle has written me for three years has said. I'll tell the auditor about every one of them. I'll tell her about the wall. I have kept a book of my own, Madam Auditor — a true one. I kept it because I am a clerk, and I could not bear not to. It is in the stove in my room at the Salt House, behind the firebrick, and it has every measure in it that I ever wrote false.\"\n\nSomebody in the crowd said, very loudly, \"Gods,\" and somebody else laughed, high and shocked.\n\nThe Reeve was on his feet.\n\n\"This hearing is closed,\" he said. \"I am the Reeve of Varrow and this is my hall, and I'll not have —\"\n\n\"It isn't closed, Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund mildly. \"It's the Exchequer's hearing. It's only being held in your hall.\" She picked up the letter. \"Sit down, please. I'm going to read this aloud.\"\n\nHe did not sit down. He looked at the auditor, and at the Magister, and at the crowd filling the back of the hall, and something went out of his big red face all at once, like air out of a bladder. Then he turned and walked out through the door behind his chair, the one that only the Reeve used, and nobody stopped him, because nobody in Varrow had ever stopped the Reeve doing anything.","wordCount":970,"createdAt":1794216654186},{"id":"KB0H1CZ4o2iXKwf7zuGQb","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Brenn had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. That was what the forty crowns in his coat pocket were for.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter in his hand saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"She's early.\"\n\n\"How early?\"\n\n\"A night. A whole night.\" He sat up. \"The letter said the spring tide. That's tomorrow.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his clever face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern, because he had taken her in through the Gullet on the ninth.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.\n# The Hard\n## Mother Gannet\n[\n  Place: #The Gull Stair\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Mother Gannet @@Wren\n  When: +2 days\n]\n\nTomas did not come by the next day, as he had said he would, and he did not come the day after.\n\nOn the second morning Ilse went down the Gull Stair before the Salt House bell and found Mother Gannet already at her place at the foot of it, gutting eels into a bucket with a short black knife. The old woman had sold eels at the bottom of the stair for as long as there had been a stair, by her own account, and possibly longer. She had one eye milky and one eye sharp, and it was generally understood in the lower town that the milky one saw more.\n\n\"He's not back,\" Mother Gannet said, before Ilse had opened her mouth. \"The Patience isn't on her mooring. I've had the boy up and down the quay since first light asking.\"\n\n\"Which boy?\"\n\n\"Mine.\" The old woman jerked her chin. Sitting on the bottom step, eating a cold potato with great concentration, was a child of nine or ten in a man's coat cut down at the sleeves. He had a face so dirty it was hard to say whether he was a boy at all. \"Wren. He runs for me. He's not mine, but he runs for me.\"\n\n\"Nobody's seen her,\" Wren said, through potato. \"Not in the harbour. Not at the Pans. Tad Mossop says he saw a sail in the channels last night past the Heron Creek, but Tad Mossop says a lot of things.\"\n\nIlse sat down on the step beside him because her legs had decided it for her.\n\n\"He's a pilot,\" Mother Gannet said, not unkindly. \"He knows every inch of that marsh. If he's out there he's out there because he means to be.\" She dropped an eel into the bucket. \"Question is who he's out there with.\"\n\n\"Corin Ashe.\"\n\n\"Ah.\" The old woman's sharp eye went to her. \"Then the question's what he's out there for.\"\n\nIlse looked at the bucket of eels, at the grey light on the mud, at the salt-ships still lying over on their sides from the ebb. She thought about the letter against her skin and about the auditor at the end of the long table in the draught, asking for the year of the big tide.\n\n\"Mother,\" she said. \"You remember the tide that took the Pans.\"\n\n\"I remember every tide since the old king died.\"\n\n\"What happened after? To the Pans?\"\n\nMother Gannet wiped the black knife on her apron and was quiet for long enough that Wren stopped chewing.\n\n\"Sea came over the wall at the spring tide in Thawmonth,\" she said at last. \"Took the wall, took the pans behind it, took the pan-reeve's house and the pan-reeve's wife with it. Half the salt-works in Varrow, gone under in one night. The Salt House wrote to the capital for money to build it back, and the capital wrote back and said it was a matter for the harbour.\" She spat, neatly, into the mud. \"So it was the Reeve's to mend, and the Reeve said there was no money for it, and for a whole year the pan-men sat on the quay with their hands in their laps. And then one day there was money. And the wall went up, and the pans went back, and everyone said what a fine man the Reeve was.\"\n\n\"Where did the money come from?\"\n\n\"Nobody asked. That's the thing about money that comes when you need it. Nobody asks.\" The old woman picked up another eel. \"Your mother would've asked.\"\n\n\"My mother's dead.\"\n\n\"So she is. And so's the pan-reeve's wife, and the Salt House is still standing, and the Reeve's still got his fine house on the hill, and his fine new wall, which I'll tell you for nothing is built of the worst stone I ever saw and won't see out another ten years of tides.\" The knife went in. \"You asked.\"\n\nIlse stood up. \"If Tomas comes back —\"\n\n\"The boy'll find you.\" Mother Gannet did not look up. \"Go to your counting, girl. Count something.\"\n\n## The Year of the Big Tide\n[\n  Place: #The Salt House\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Lisbet Ormund\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?The books are short one part in seven:@Ilse Varga ?The books are short one part in seven:@Lisbet Ormund:before\n]\n\nThe ledger for the year of the big tide was bound in green, because the binder had run out of brown that winter, and it stood out on the shelf like a wrong note. Lisbet Ormund had it open on the long table when Ilse came up the stair, and beside it the book for the year before, bound in brown, and beside that a sheet of her own paper covered in tiny, very neat figures.\n\nAurek was not at his place. The auditor saw her looking.\n\n\"I've sent Master Stiles to the pan-reeve with a list of questions,\" she said. \"It'll take him most of the day, I'm afraid, because the pan-reeve won't know the answers and will have to send to the Reeve's office for them. Sit down, Miss Varga. Sit there, where I can see you.\"\n\nIlse sat.\n\n\"I want to show you something,\" said the auditor, \"and then I want to ask you something, and I'd like you to think before you answer the second thing, because I shall know if you don't.\" She turned the green ledger round. \"This is Thawmonth, the year of the tide. Here — the week of the flood. Nothing comes in at all, which is what you'd expect. Here, the month after. Very little. Here, the month after that. And here,\" she turned a page, \"is the first month that the books are short against the Hesk price.\"\n\nIlse looked. It was a column of numbers in Aurek's hand, tidy and upright, and beside each a ship's name and a date. There was nothing wrong with any of them.\n\n\"They're not short,\" she said.\n\n\"Not in the book. In the book they're very well. But I have here,\" the auditor tapped her own sheet, \"every cargo of Varrow salt that was sold at Hesk that month, from the Hesk customs ledgers, which I had copied before I left the capital. And the Hesk ledgers say that Varrow sent them a good deal less salt than Varrow's books say it sent. About one part in seven less.\" She sat back. \"One part in seven. Every month since. Three years.\"\n\nOne part in seven. Sixty measures out of four hundred was a little more than that. Ilse kept her face very still and felt the paper crackle under her bodice when she breathed.\n\n\"Somebody,\" said Lisbet Ormund, \"is selling one-seventh of the salt of Varrow somewhere other than Hesk, and the books of the Salt House are written as though they weren't. Now. Here's my question.\" She leaned forward. \"I don't think you're a thief, Miss Varga. I knew your mother. I don't think the Magister is a thief either, not in his heart, though I've been wrong about men's hearts before. But somebody in this building writes the numbers, and somebody tells them which numbers to write. Has anybody ever told you to write a number you knew was wrong?\"\n\nShe thought about the Kestrel's book. Four hundred in her own clean hand.\n\nShe thought about Tomas, who had not come home.\n\nShe thought: *if I say yes, she'll ask who. And if I say who, they'll know it was me. And Tomas is somewhere out in the marsh, and I don't know where, and I don't know who else knows.*\n\n\"No, madam,\" she said.\n\nThe auditor looked at her for a long moment. Then she nodded, slowly, and turned the green book back around, and picked up her pen.\n\n\"I'll be here until the spring tide,\" she said, without looking up. \"After that I go back to the capital and write my report with whatever I have. If you think of anything before then, Miss Varga, I'd be very grateful to hear it. I'm at the Anchor, on Rope Walk. Anyone can tell you where it is.\"\n\nIlse went back to her own place and opened the Heron's book and could not see a single number in it.\n\n[\n  Place: #Heron Creek\n  Characters: @@Tomas Brenn @@Corin Ashe\n  When: day 4\n]\n\nThey had been seen. Tomas never knew how — a turn of the Patience's sail against the sky, perhaps, or the creak of her blocks across still water, or simply that a man on the Chapel steps had looked up at the wrong moment. But the light at the Chapel went out all at once, and in the dark there was a shout, and then the sound of oars.\n\n\"Go,\" said Corin. \"Go, go —\"\n\nTomas went. He put the helm over and got the sail up in one long heave and the Patience came round, slow, so slow, broad-beamed old cow that she was, and the flood was against them now, pushing them back up toward the Chapel. Behind them the oars were coming on. Four oars at least. A longboat, light and fast, and the men in her knew the channel nearly as well as he did.\n\n\"They'll have us before the Heron Creek,\" Corin said.\n\n\"They won't.\"\n\n\"They will. I can count, Tomas, it's the only thing I'm good for besides lying.\" Corin was pulling off his boots. \"There's a cut on the left — the one with the dead tree. Where does it go?\"\n\n\"Nowhere. Mud. It's a dead end.\"\n\n\"Can a man stand in it?\"\n\n\"At this tide? To his chest, maybe. What are you —\"\n\n\"They want the boat. They'll follow the boat.\" Corin was on the gunwale now, crouched, his clever face white in the starlight. \"And they want me more than they want you, because I'm the one they've been paying. So when I go over, you keep going, and you make a great deal of noise, and I'll make rather less.\" He grinned suddenly. \"Tell your sister to keep the letter. Tell her it's worth more than I am.\"\n\n\"Corin —\"\n\nHe went over the side without a splash. Tomas never heard him hit the water. One moment there was a man on the gunwale and the next there was the dark and the dead tree going past on the left and nothing else.\n\nHe kept going. He did not know what else to do. He made, as instructed, a great deal of noise — he let the mainsheet run so the block screamed, and he banged the tiller against the thwart, and he shouted Corin's name once, loudly, toward the right-hand bank, as though looking for a man who had fallen in there. Behind him the oars checked, and a voice said something sharp, and the longboat split its attention for a moment between the boat and the bank.\n\nIt was enough to get him into the Heron Creek. It was not enough to get him out of it.\n\nThe Heron was narrow and shallow and he had taken it a thousand times at the top of the tide, but this was not the top of the tide and he was not looking where he was going. The Patience hit the bar at the creek's mouth with a long grinding shudder that went up through the keel and into his teeth, and stopped, and heeled, and he was thrown against the thwart so hard that he heard something in his leg go with a sound like a green stick breaking.\n\nHe lay in the bilge-water and did not scream, because the longboat was still out there.\n\nAfter a time — he did not know how long — he heard the oars go past the mouth of the creek, slow now, searching. A lantern swung. A man's voice said, \"Nothing. She's gone up the Heron and stuck, she'll be there in the morning, we'll have her then,\" and another voice, one he thought he knew, said, \"And the other one?\" and the first voice said, \"Drowned, if he's got any sense.\" Then the oars again, going away, toward the Chapel and the black ship and the salt.\n\nHe waited until he could not hear them. Then he dragged himself up onto the thwart, and looked at his leg, and looked away again quickly. The tide was still making. In an hour the Patience would float off the bar. In an hour he would have to be somewhere else.\n\nThe tower of the Drowned Chapel was a quarter mile off across the marsh, black against the first grey in the east. It was the only dry place for a mile. It was also where the men in the longboat were going.\n\nTomas looked at it for a long time. Then he found the boat-hook, and broke the shaft across his knee to make a splint, and began to bind his leg with the sail-ties, and tried to think of something else.","wordCount":2909,"createdAt":1794213828900},{"id":"KTAdQEGvMl-UIt6vk6w0n","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"3z3uj6msTp-yJfszkLU-n","text":"She went to work. She did not know what else to do with herself, and it seemed important, in a way she could not have explained, that she should be at her place at the long table when the bell went, in her clerk's grey, with her hair pinned, as if nothing had happened. As if she were still the best clerk the Magister had had in thirty years.\n\nAurek glanced up when she came in and opened his mouth to say something about bags of nails, and then saw her face and shut it again.\n\nThe auditor was not there. Her sheet of tiny figures lay on the end of the table under a glass paperweight, and the green ledger was gone from beside it.\n\n\"She's downstairs,\" Aurek said quietly. \"With him. Since first bell.\" He hesitated. \"Ilse. I took the sticks out of the bin.\"\n\nShe turned.\n\n\"The Kestrel's. The day you put them in. I'm not a fool, girl, whatever you think. Nobody finds six sticks behind a stove and then finds them gone again.\" He did not look at her; he looked at his own ledger, at his own tidy upright hand. \"I've been here twenty years. I've written what I was told for three of them. I didn't count. I didn't ask. I told myself the Magister read the margins.\" He turned a page that did not need turning. \"They're in my coat. In the press. If you want them.\"\n\nShe stood very still.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\n\"Because you didn't write anything in the margin,\" said Aurek Stiles. \"You always write in the margin. You've written in the margin every day for four years. And that day you didn't, and I thought: she's frightened. And I thought: I've been frightened for three years and I never once noticed it was the same thing.\"\n\nAt midday the auditor came up the stair and took her sheet of figures from under the paperweight and went down again without a word to either of them. A little after that, the porter came up to say that the Magister wanted Miss Varga in his room.\n\nThe stove was lit. It was always lit. Oduya Fenn sat behind his desk with the green ledger closed in front of him and his long hands folded on top of it, and he did not smile when she came in. That was how she knew he had not slept either.\n\n\"Sit down, Ilse.\"\n\nShe did not sit down. She had decided on the stair that she would tell him, all of it. If he was going to give her name to the Reeve a second time, she wanted to watch him do it.\n\n\"I was at the Chapel last night,\" she said. \"In the tower. With Tomas, who has a broken leg, and has been lying in a window for two days while you loaded salt underneath him.\"\n\nThe Magister closed his eyes.\n\n\"I heard you tell the Reeve my name.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"I imagine you did.\" He opened his eyes again and looked at her, and his face was older than she had ever seen it. \"Will you let me tell you how it happened? Not to excuse it. I don't think it can be excused. But I'd like someone to know, and I think you've earned it more than anyone.\"\n\nShe sat down.\n\nHe told her. What Mother Gannet had told her, and what Mother Gannet could not have known: the price of salt going up at Hesk, and the Salt House half empty, and the Exchequer writing every quarter to ask why the revenue was down. And then Halvard Seyle in this room, in that chair, with a bottle of something good, saying: _I can build you a wall, Oduya. All it needs is a little salt that nobody counts._\n\n\"One part in seven,\" the Magister said. \"For one year. To pay for the wall. That was all it was going to be. I told myself it was the Exchequer's own salt paying for the Exchequer's own wall, and if the Exchequer had done its duty there'd have been no need. I told myself a great many things.\" He looked down at his hands. \"The wall was built in a year. And at the end of the year I went to Halvard and said, that's enough, and he said — he said, Oduya, you've been writing false books for a year. Do you want to explain that to the Exchequer, or shall I?\"\n\n\"So you kept writing.\"\n\n\"So I kept writing. And the wall is bad stone, and Halvard has a new house, and the Black Mare comes in on the spring tides and takes one-seventh of the salt of Varrow to wherever it goes.\" He lifted his hands, let them fall. \"And you counted the Kestrel's sticks. I knew you would, one day. You're the only one of them who ever counted.\"\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Did you take it?\"\n\nHis face did not change. \"What letter?\"\n\n\"The Reeve's letter to you. About the Kestrel. I had it.\"\n\nFor a moment the Magister of the Salt House looked at her with an expression she could not read at all, and then, very slowly, he began to laugh. It was not a happy sound.\n\n\"He never sent it,\" he said. \"I never had it. I wondered — I wondered why he was so certain I'd been told about the Black Mare coming early, when I hadn't. He said, _I wrote to you._ And I said, _you didn't._ And we each thought the other was lying.\" He wiped his eyes. \"Oh, Ilse. Somebody stole the Reeve's post. And now somebody has stolen it from you.\"","wordCount":955,"createdAt":1794217057692},{"id":"L9hWQiaxpSfgIjhtUz78P","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"FBthgk0AwfIy4svSwzmYI","text":"The marsh at low water was a different country. The channels that the Patience had sailed were trickles now, running between banks of mud as high as Ilse's head, and the mud shone in the moonlight like the skin of something sleeping. Wren went ahead of her along a path she could not see — the hard, he called it, a spine of shingle under the mud that the old salt-carriers had used before there were boats — and she followed him step for step and did not look down.\n\nIt took them two hours to reach the Chapel. The tower stood up out of the mud on its little island of stone, the arches of the old nave around its foot half-buried, the high windows empty. At high water the sea came in through those windows. Now, with the tide out, there were steps going down into the dark of the crypt, slick with weed, and a smell of salt and old candles.\n\n\"Here's where they load,\" Wren whispered. He pointed. Along the stone of the steps there were fresh scars, white in the moonlight, where something heavy had been dragged. \"Salt. Sacks of it. They bring it up the channel from the Pans at night and keep it up in the tower, above the tide-line, till the ship comes.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\nWren shrugged inside his coat. \"Everybody knows. Nobody says.\"\n\nThey found Tomas in the tower.\n\nHe had dragged himself up the first turn of the stair and wedged himself into a window-niche, and he was so still and so grey that Ilse thought for a moment that he was dead. Then his eyes opened and he said, quite clearly, \"You weren't supposed to come,\" and she knelt on the stair beside him and held his hand and could not say anything at all.\n\nHis leg was splinted with half a boat-hook. He had done it well. He had come across the marsh on his back, on the flood, with the tide still making, pushing with his good leg and the stump of the boat-hook, and twice the water had closed over his face. He had been sitting in the niche, he said, for a day and a night, while the Reeve's men came and went below him, loading the black ship from the crypt. He had heard everything. He had not been able to move.\n\n\"There was a voice in the longboat,\" he said. \"The first night. I thought I knew it. I didn't want to.\"\n\n\"Corin,\" Ilse said. \"Where's Corin?\"\n\n\"Went over the side. Took them off me.\" Tomas closed his eyes. \"Don't know if he got out. He said to tell you to keep the letter.\"\n\n\"I have it.\"\n\n\"Good. Ilse — listen. The ship's gone. The Black Mare. She went out on this morning's tide with the first load aboard, and she'll be back for the rest.\" His hand tightened on hers. \"And it's not just the Reeve. I heard them. They were talking about the Salt House. About the books. About someone who's going to fix the books before the auditor —\"\n\nThere was a sound below them. Wren, who had been keeping watch at the foot of the stair, came up it like a cat, silent, his eyes huge.\n\n\"Lantern,\" he breathed. \"Two men. On the hard.\"\n\nThey did not move. Ilse put her hand over Tomas's mouth, not because he would have made a sound but because she needed to hold something. Below them, in the ruined nave, a lantern came and went among the arches, and two men came with it, and stood at the head of the crypt steps where the white scars were.\n\nOne of them was big and wore a harbour officer's coat. She had seen the Reeve of Varrow at a hundred ceremonies on the quay; she knew his shoulders, his walk, the way he stood with his weight back on his heels.\n\nThe other was tall and thin, and held his lantern with a long hand that she had watched sign a thousand pages.\n\n\"She's asking for the flood year,\" the Magister was saying. His voice echoed in the stone. \"She's had it on the table for two days. She has copies of the Hesk ledgers, Halvard. Copies. She knew before she came.\"\n\n\"Then she knows nothing she can prove.\" The Reeve's voice was easy, almost bored. \"Hesk ledgers. A clerk in Hesk can't count. Everyone knows that.\"\n\n\"She'll ask for the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then give her the sticks. You told me the sticks were sorted.\"\n\nThe Magister was quiet. The lantern shook a little.\n\n\"One of my clerks,\" he said at last, \"found the Kestrel short. Before I could see to it.\"\n\n\"Which clerk?\"\n\nIlse felt Tomas's hand tighten on hers until the bones ground.\n\n\"It doesn't matter. I dealt with it. She wrote it in as I told her.\"\n\n\"Oduya.\" The Reeve sounded almost gentle. \"Everything matters. That's how we've lasted three years. Which clerk?\"\n\nThere was a long silence in the nave. Ilse could hear the water beginning to come back, very far off, a sound like breathing.\n\n\"The Varga girl,\" the Magister said.","wordCount":863,"createdAt":1794230225848},{"id":"LlntRBUhy9solTyfrPBus","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"JLebbu3pA-RXZKvs35ANa","text":"Lisbet Ormund had taken the best room at the Anchor, which was not saying a great deal, and had spent the first evening moving the furniture so that the table caught the light from the window and the bed did not catch the draught from the door. She was at the table now, with her shoes off and her stockinged feet on a hot brick wrapped in flannel, writing her report.\n\nShe had written it three times. Each time it said the same thing — _one part in seven, three years, the Salt House books written to conceal it_ — and each time it ended in the same place, which was _I have no proof of who._\n\nWhen the landlord knocked to say the Reeve was below and wished to call on her, she said, \"Yes, of course, send him up,\" and put her shoes back on, and turned her report face down on the table, and put the hot brick under the bed.\n\nHalvard Seyle filled the doorway. He was a big man and knew it, and he had come in his harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs, and he bowed to her with great courtesy and said he hoped she was comfortable, and that Varrow was not too rough for a lady from the capital, and that if there was anything at all she needed she had only to send to the Reeve's Hall.\n\n\"How kind,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Do sit down.\"\n\nHe sat. The chair complained.\n\n\"I hear you've been at the Salt House books,\" he said.\n\n\"I have.\"\n\n\"Poor Fenn. He's not the man he was. The flood hit him very hard — he took it personally, you know, as though the sea had done it to spite him. I've had to carry a good deal of the work since.\" He smiled. \"If you've found anything amiss, I'd hope you'd come to me first. Before the capital. It would be a shame to ruin an old man's name over a few clerical errors that the harbour could put right quietly.\"\n\n\"A few clerical errors,\" the auditor repeated.\n\n\"Sticks go missing,\" said the Reeve. \"It happens all the time.\"\n\nLisbet Ormund looked at him for a long moment with her kindly hen's face. Then she turned her report over on the table and pushed it across to him.\n\n\"One part in seven,\" she said. \"Every month for three years. That is not a few clerical errors, Reeve. That is twenty-two thousand crowns of the Exchequer's revenue, near enough, and it is going somewhere, and it is not going to Hesk.\" She folded her hands. \"I'll tell you what I have, since you've been good enough to call. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. I have a tallyclerk who lied to my face and was frightened to death doing it, and a Magister who hasn't slept in three nights. What I don't have is a name.\" She smiled at him. \"I don't suppose you'd care to give me one?\"\n\nThe Reeve did not smile back.\n\n\"You've been in Varrow four days, madam,\" he said. \"You don't know the tides. People who don't know the tides get into trouble out on the marsh. Every year we lose one or two. Visitors, mostly.\" He stood up. \"I'd be careful.\"\n\n\"I always am,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Good night, Reeve.\"\n\nWhen he had gone she sat for a while without moving. Then she got the hot brick back out from under the bed, which had gone lukewarm, and put her feet on it, and turned her report face down again, and wrote at the bottom of a fresh sheet, in her tiny neat hand: _Seyle. Certain. No proof._","wordCount":623,"createdAt":1794216651571},{"id":"LqFnzPSvgZ0dJQhhrX8K3","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"GIXhkOMgrF_FKcO5VyMzd","text":"The auditor left Varrow on the twelfth day of Seedmonth, in the same carriage that had come down Tallow Street and frightened the porter, and the whole of Tallow Street came out to see her go.\n\nShe had stayed a fortnight longer than she meant to. She had sat in the Magister's room with the firebrick out of the stove and the Magister's true book open on the desk — a small black book, scorched at the corners, with three years of real numbers in it in a long thin careful hand — and she had copied every page. Then she had sat in the third-floor room with Aurek and Ilse for ten days more and put the real numbers into the real books, page by page, in the margins, in red.\n\n\"The Exchequer will want the Magister in the capital,\" she told Ilse, the last evening, standing at the door of the Salt House with her gloves in her hand. \"There'll be a trial. I'll speak for him. I'll say he kept a true book when he could have kept none, and gave it up when he could have burned it, and that's not nothing.\" She pulled on one glove. \"I don't know what they'll do. Something less than they might have, I hope.\"\n\n\"And the Salt House?\"\n\n\"Will need a Magister.\" Lisbet Ormund pulled on the other glove. \"I've written to say so. I've written to say who.\" She looked up at Ilse with her kindly hen's face. \"Master Stiles has been here twenty years and is a very good clerk, and he told me himself that he would rather go into the sea after the Reeve than have the stove. So I've written your name.\"\n\nIlse opened her mouth and shut it again.\n\n\"You'll say you're too young,\" said the auditor. \"And that you lied to me. You did. You also told me you'd lied, in front of the whole town, which I have never once in thirty years of this work seen anyone do. Your mother would have.\" She held out her small gloved hand, and Ilse took it. \"Count everything, Miss Varga. Write in the margins. Good night.\"\n\nThe carriage went down Tallow Street in the morning with the carters cursing it, and Ilse watched it from the third-floor window until it was gone.\n\nTomas came up the Gull Stair that afternoon on two crutches, which took him nearly an hour and which he would not let anyone help him with, and sat on the bench outside the pilots' shed at the bottom with his splinted leg stuck out in front of him and his unlit pipe in his teeth. Corin Ashe sat beside him. Neither of them said much. Corin had a pilot's oilskin on, which was too big for him, and a pilot's book in his pocket, which Tomas had made him buy, and he was learning the Gullet from the bench, channel by channel, with Tomas drawing it in the dust with the end of a crutch.\n\n\"He'll make a pilot,\" Tomas said to Ilse, when she came down to them at the end of the day. \"God knows how. He gets everything wrong and then argues with the sea about it.\"\n\n\"I'm very persuasive,\" said Corin.\n\n\"The sea isn't.\"\n\nWren was sitting on the bottom step of the Gull Stair eating a cold potato. Mother Gannet was gutting eels. The salt-ships lay over in the mud on the ebb, and the evening light came down the harbour the colour of honey, and somewhere up on the hill a bell was ringing for the end of the day.\n\nIlse climbed back up to the Salt House as the light went. The third floor was empty; Aurek had gone home. She lit the lamp and sat down at the long table, at her own place, and opened the Kestrel's book at the ninth.\n\nFour hundred, in her own clean hand. And beside it, in the margin, in red, in the auditor's tiny writing: _340. Sticks counted. I.V._\n\nShe dipped her pen, and turned to a clean page, and began to count.","wordCount":688,"createdAt":1794216656626},{"id":"MLJTwdiSGclmt2EV72wF5","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"JLebbu3pA-RXZKvs35ANa","text":"Lisbet Ormund had taken the best room at the Anchor, which was not saying a great deal, and had spent the first evening moving the furniture so that the table caught the light from the window and the bed did not catch the draught from the door. She was at the table now, with her shoes off and her stockinged feet on a hot brick wrapped in flannel, writing her report.\n\nShe had written it three times. Each time it said the same thing — _one part in seven, three years, the Salt House books written to conceal it_ — and each time it ended in the same place, which was _I have no proof of who._\n\nWhen the landlord knocked to say the Reeve was below and wished to call on her, she said, \"Yes, of course, send him up,\" and put her shoes back on, and turned her report face down on the table, and put the hot brick under the bed.\n\nHalvard Seyle filled the doorway. He was a big man and knew it, and he had come in his harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs, and he bowed to her with great courtesy and said he hoped she was comfortable, and that Varrow was not too rough for a lady from the capital, and that if there was anything at all she needed she had only to send to the Reeve's Hall.\n\n\"How kind,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Do sit down.\"\n\nHe sat. The chair complained.\n\n\"I hear you've been at the Salt House books,\" he said.\n\n\"I have.\"\n\n\"Poor Fenn. He's not the man he was. The flood hit him very hard — he took it personally, you know, as though the sea had done it to spite him. I've had to carry a good deal of the work since.\" He smiled. \"If you've found anything amiss, I'd hope you'd come to me first. Before the capital. It would be a shame to ruin an old man's name over a few clerical errors that the harbour could put right quietly.\" He paused, as if a thing had only just occurred to him. \"There's a clerk of his, a Varga girl, who has something of mine that came out of my post. I'd hate her to come to harm over a piece of paper. Paper's cheap. Girls who can count are not.\"\n\n\"A few clerical errors,\" the auditor repeated.\n\n\"Sticks go missing,\" said the Reeve. \"It happens all the time.\"\n\nLisbet Ormund regarded him for a long moment. Then she turned her report over on the table and pushed it across to him.\n\n\"One part in seven,\" she said. \"Every month for three years. That is not a few clerical errors, Reeve. That is a hundred and forty thousand crowns of the Exchequer's revenue, near enough, and it is going somewhere, and it is not going to Hesk.\" She folded her hands. \"I'll tell you what I have, since you've been good enough to call. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. I have a tallyclerk who lied to my face and was frightened to death doing it, and a Magister who hasn't slept in three nights. What I don't have is a name.\" She smiled at him. \"I don't suppose you'd care to give me one?\"\n\nThe Reeve did not smile back.\n\n\"You've been in Varrow three days, madam,\" he said. \"You don't know the tides. People who don't know the tides get into trouble out on the marsh. Every year we lose one or two. Visitors, mostly.\" He stood up. \"I'd be careful.\"\n\n\"I always am,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Good night, Reeve.\"\n\nWhen he had gone she sat for a while without moving. Then she got the hot brick back out from under the bed — it had gone lukewarm — and put her feet on it, and turned her report face down again, and wrote at the bottom of a fresh sheet, in her tiny neat hand: _Seyle. Certain. No proof._","wordCount":673,"createdAt":1794230095137},{"id":"MSmtQfB36BbYwE67EzIDs","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"dAT2R-4HoTYOS7lortcKb","text":"Tomas did not come by the next day, as he had said he would, and he did not come the day after.\n\nOn the second morning Ilse went down the Gull Stair before the Salt House bell and found Mother Gannet already at her place at the foot of it, gutting eels into a bucket with a short black knife. The old woman had sold eels at the bottom of the stair for as long as there had been a stair, by her own account, and possibly longer. She had one eye milky and one eye sharp, and it was generally understood in the lower town that the milky one saw more.\n\n\"He's not back,\" Mother Gannet said, before Ilse had opened her mouth. \"The Patience isn't on her mooring. I've had the boy up and down the quay since first light asking.\"\n\n\"Which boy?\"\n\n\"Mine.\" The old woman jerked her chin. Sitting on the bottom step, eating a cold potato with great concentration, was a child of nine or ten in a man's coat cut down at the sleeves. He had a face so dirty it was hard to say whether he was a boy at all. \"Wren. He runs for me. He's not mine, but he runs for me.\"\n\n\"Nobody's seen her,\" Wren said, through potato. \"Not in the harbour. Not at the Pans. Tad Mossop says he saw a sail in the channels last night past the Heron Creek, but Tad Mossop says a lot of things.\"\n\nIlse sat down on the step beside him because her legs had decided it for her.\n\n\"He's a pilot,\" Mother Gannet said, not unkindly. \"He knows every inch of that marsh. If he's out there he's out there because he means to be.\" She dropped an eel into the bucket. \"Question is who he's out there with.\"\n\n\"Corin Ashe.\"\n\n\"Ah.\" The old woman's sharp eye went to her. \"Then the question's what he's out there for.\"\n\nIlse looked at the bucket of eels, at the grey light on the mud, at the salt-ships still lying over on their sides from the ebb. She thought about the letter against her skin and about the auditor at the end of the long table in the draught, asking for the year of the big tide.\n\n\"Mother,\" she said. \"You remember the tide that took the Pans.\"\n\n\"I remember every tide since the old king died.\"\n\n\"What happened after? To the Pans?\"\n\nMother Gannet wiped the black knife on her apron and was quiet for long enough that Wren stopped chewing.\n\n\"Sea came over the wall at the spring tide in Thawmonth,\" she said at last. \"Took the wall, took the pans behind it, took the pan-reeve's house and the pan-reeve's wife with it. Half the salt-works in Varrow, gone under in one night. The Salt House wrote to the capital for money to build it back, and the capital wrote back and said it was a matter for the harbour.\" She spat, neatly, into the mud. \"So it was the Reeve's to mend, and the Reeve said there was no money for it, and for a whole year the pan-men sat on the quay with their hands in their laps. And then one day there was money. And the wall went up, and the pans went back, and everyone said what a fine man the Reeve was.\"\n\n\"Where did the money come from?\"\n\n\"Nobody asked. That's the thing about money that comes when you need it. Nobody asks.\" The old woman picked up another eel. \"Your mother would've asked.\"\n\n\"My mother's dead.\"\n\n\"So she is. And so's the pan-reeve's wife, and the Salt House is still standing, and the Reeve's still got his fine house on the hill, and his fine new wall, which, I'll tell you for nothing, is built of the worst stone I ever saw and won't see out another ten years of tides.\" The knife went in. \"You asked.\"\n\nIlse stood up. \"If Tomas comes back —\"\n\n\"The boy'll find you.\" Mother Gannet did not look up. \"Go to your counting, girl. Count something.\"","wordCount":685,"createdAt":1794217084475},{"id":"Mn-uK8prIs-umm0Cr2AHW","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Brenn had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. That was what the forty crowns in his coat pocket were for.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter in his hand saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"She's early.\"\n\n\"How early?\"\n\n\"A night. A whole night.\" He sat up. \"The letter said the spring tide. That's tomorrow.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his clever face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern, because he had taken her in through the Gullet on the ninth.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.","wordCount":713,"createdAt":1794216530614},{"id":"NoHGuhWPSbHzw39uuYE4W","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"LQxthMqMx2ix2u3pYQu7A","text":"The hearing was called for the hour after noon on the day of the spring tide, in the long hall at the top of the town where the Reeve of Varrow had sat in judgement on fishing disputes and drunk sailors for as long as there had been a Reeve. Lisbet Ormund had called it. She had the right; she had shown the clerk of the Hall a paper with the Exchequer's seal on it, and the clerk had gone white and sent for the Reeve, and the Reeve had come, and smiled, and said that of course the auditor must have whatever she needed.\n\nHe sat now in his own chair at the head of the hall, in the harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs. The Magister sat below him on the bench where witnesses sat, very straight, his long hands on his knees. Half the lower town had come up the hill to stand at the back. Ilse could see Mother Gannet's milky eye in the crowd, and Wren's dirty face beside her, and a great many people she had passed on the Gull Stair every day of her life and never spoken to. The old people on the quay had been saying since dawn that there was weather coming in with the tide.\n\nThe auditor stood at a small table in the middle of the floor with her papers in front of her.\n\n\"I'll be brief,\" she said, \"because the tide turns at four and I'm told half of you have boats to see to. I have audited the books of the Salt House of Varrow for the last four years. I find that for the last three of them, one-seventh part of the salt shipped from the Pans has not reached Hesk, and that the books have been written to hide it. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. Miss Varga.\"\n\nIlse stood up.\n\nShe had thought her legs would shake. They did not. She walked out to the table in the middle of the floor in her clerk's grey with her hair pinned and laid the things down on it one by one, the way she would have laid out a ship's sticks to be counted.\n\nThe Kestrel's book, open at the ninth.\n\nThirty-four tally-sticks, which Aurek Stiles had brought up the hill in his coat.\n\nA letter with a red seal pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\n\"On the ninth,\" she said, \"the Kestrel came in through the Gullet with three hundred and forty measures of salt on her sticks. I wrote four hundred in her book, because I was told to. That was a lie, and it was mine.\" She heard the crowd at the back shift and murmur. She did not look at them. \"Two days after I wrote it, the auditor asked me if I'd ever been told to write a number I knew was wrong, and I said no. That was a lie too.\"\n\n\"Miss Varga,\" said the Reeve, pleasantly, from his chair. \"You'll understand that a clerk who admits to two lies in one breath is not —\"\n\n\"And this,\" said Ilse, \"is a letter under your seal, Reeve, to the Magister, telling him where the sixty measures from the Kestrel were to go, and when, and on what ship. It never reached him. A man called Corin Ashe took it out of your post-bag, and gave it to me, and I've kept it.\" She looked up at him. \"You can read it, if you like. Or the auditor can read it aloud. It's quite short.\"\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\nHalvard Seyle looked at the letter on the table, and at the auditor, and at the Magister on the witnesses' bench, who had not moved. Then he looked past them all to the back of the hall, where Corin Ashe was standing against the wall with his arms folded, in a clean coat, and smiled.\n\n\"A smuggler,\" the Reeve said. \"A smuggler steals a letter, and a clerk who lies admits she lies, and an old man —\"\n\n\"I'll speak to the letter,\" said Oduya Fenn.\n\nHe stood up from the bench. He was not looking at the Reeve; he was looking at Ilse, and his face was quite calm.\n\n\"I never received it,\" he said. \"But I know what it says, because I know what every letter Halvard Seyle has written me for three years has said. I'll tell the auditor about every one of them. I'll tell her about the wall. I have kept a book of my own, Madam Auditor — a true one. I kept it because I am a clerk, and I could not bear not to. It is in my room at the Salt House, behind the loose firebrick in the chimney-breast, where the stove's heat never reaches, and it has every measure in it that I ever wrote false.\"\n\nSomebody in the crowd said, loudly, \"Gods,\" and somebody else laughed, high and shocked.\n\nThe Reeve was on his feet.\n\n\"This hearing is closed,\" he said. \"I am the Reeve of Varrow and this is my hall, and I'll not have —\"\n\n\"It isn't closed, Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund mildly. \"It's the Exchequer's hearing. It's only being held in your hall.\" She picked up the letter. \"Sit down, please. I'm going to read this aloud.\"\n\nHe did not sit down. Something went out of his big red face all at once, like air out of a bladder. Then he turned and walked out through the door behind his chair, the one that only the Reeve used.\n\n\"Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund sharply, and then, to the clerk of the Hall: \"Stop that man.\" The clerk of the Hall looked from the Reeve's back to the auditor and did not move. Nobody stopped him, because nobody in Varrow had ever stopped the Reeve doing anything.","wordCount":988,"createdAt":1794230097836},{"id":"NtgnqXk11gJTmZx4jQS8K","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"QkH1pzVCb9awQuyhPF6LA","text":"They had been seen. Tomas never knew how — a turn of the Patience's sail against the sky, perhaps, or the creak of her blocks across still water, or simply that a man on the Chapel steps had looked up at the wrong moment. But the light at the Chapel went out all at once, and in the dark there was a shout, and then the sound of oars.\n\n\"Go,\" said Corin. \"Go, go —\"\n\nTomas went. He put the helm over and got the sail up in one long heave and the Patience came round, slow, so slow, broad-beamed old cow that she was, and the flood was against them now, pushing them back up toward the Chapel. Behind them the oars were coming on. Four oars at least. A longboat, light and fast, and the men in her knew the channel nearly as well as he did.\n\n\"They'll have us before the Heron Creek,\" Corin said.\n\n\"They won't.\"\n\n\"They will. I can count, Tomas, it's the only thing I'm good for besides lying.\" Corin was pulling off his boots. \"There's a cut on the left — the one with the dead tree. Where does it go?\"\n\n\"Nowhere. Mud. It's a dead end.\"\n\n\"Can a man stand in it?\"\n\n\"At this tide? To his chest, maybe. What are you —\"\n\n\"They want the boat. They'll follow the boat.\" Corin was on the gunwale now, crouched, his clever face white in the starlight. \"And they want me more than they want you, because I'm the one they've been paying. So when I go over, you keep going, and you make a great deal of noise, and I'll make rather less.\" He grinned suddenly. \"Tell your sister to keep the letter. Tell her it's worth more than I am.\"\n\n\"Corin —\"\n\nHe went over the side without a splash. Tomas never heard him hit the water. One moment there was a man on the gunwale and the next there was the dark and the dead tree going past on the left and nothing else.\n\nHe kept going. He did not know what else to do. He made, as instructed, a great deal of noise — he let the mainsheet run so the block screamed, and he banged the tiller against the thwart, and he shouted Corin's name once, loudly, toward the right-hand bank, as though looking for a man who had fallen in there. Behind him the oars checked, and a voice said something sharp, and the longboat split its attention for a moment between the boat and the bank.\n\nIt was enough to get him into the Heron Creek. It was not enough to get him out of it.\n\nThe Heron was narrow and shallow and he had taken it a thousand times at the top of the tide, but this was not the top of the tide and he was not looking where he was going. The Patience hit the bar at the creek's mouth with a long grinding shudder that went up through the keel and into his teeth, and stopped, and heeled, and he was thrown against the thwart so hard that he heard something in his leg go with a sound like a green stick breaking.\n\nHe lay in the bilge-water and did not scream, because the longboat was still out there.\n\nAfter a time — he did not know how long — he heard the oars go past the mouth of the creek, slow now, searching. A lantern swung. A man's voice said, \"Nothing. She's gone up the Heron and stuck, she'll be there in the morning, we'll have her then,\" and another voice, one he thought he knew, said, \"And the other one?\" and the first voice said, \"Drowned, if he's got any sense.\" Then the oars again, going away, toward the Chapel and the black ship and the salt.\n\nHe waited until he could not hear them. Then he dragged himself up onto the thwart, and looked at his leg, and looked away again quickly. The tide was still making. In an hour the Patience would float off the bar. In an hour he would have to be somewhere else.\n\nThe tower of the Drowned Chapel was a quarter mile off across the marsh, black against the first grey in the east. It was the only dry place for a mile. It was also where the men in the longboat were going.\n\nTomas looked at it for a long time. Then he found the boat-hook, and broke the shaft across his knee to make a splint, and began to bind his leg with the sail-ties, and tried to think of something else.\n# Low Water\n## The Boy on the Stair\n[\n  Place: #The Salt House\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Wren\n  When: day 6\n  Items: ^The Reeve's letter:#Ilse's Room\n]\n\nWren found her at the end of the day, as Mother Gannet had promised he would. He was sitting on the Salt House steps when she came out, with the porter glaring at him from the door, and he stood up as soon as he saw her and said, without any greeting at all, \"The Patience is up the Heron Creek. Tad Mossop saw her. She's on her side on the bar and there's nobody in her.\"\n\nIlse stood on the step with her satchel in her hand.\n\n\"Nobody,\" she said.\n\n\"Tad didn't go close. Tad said there was men about. Reeve's men, in a longboat, going up and down.\" Wren wiped his nose on his cut-down sleeve. \"Mother says you'll want to go and look, and Mother says don't, and Mother says if you're going anyway take me, because I know the way at low water and you don't.\"\n\n\"When's low water?\"\n\n\"Tonight. Middle of the night. It's springs tomorrow, so it'll go right out — you can walk to the Chapel nearly dry-shod if you know where the hard is.\" He looked at her with an expression that was much older than the rest of his face. \"Mother said you'd ask that. Mother said you were your mother's daughter.\"\n\nShe thought about going back up to the third floor and telling the auditor everything. The letter, the sticks, the Kestrel's book in her own hand. She thought about the auditor's kindly hen's face, and about the Reeve's men going up and down the channel in a longboat looking for a boat with her brother in it.\n\nIf she told the auditor tonight, the auditor would write to the capital tomorrow, and the capital would send someone in a week. A week was a long time to lie on a bar in the marsh with nobody in your boat.\n\n\"I'll need to go home first,\" she said. \"For boots.\"\n\n\"I'll come with you.\"\n\n\"You don't need to.\"\n\n\"Mother said not to let you out of my sight.\" Wren fell in beside her. \"Mother's never wrong. It's very annoying.\"\n\nThey went up Tallow Street together in the dusk, the tall woman in her clerk's grey and the small dirty boy in his enormous coat, and nobody looked at them twice, because that was the kind of street it was. At the cooper's Ilse left Wren on the step and climbed the stair through the middle of the house to her room.\n\nShe changed into her mother's old marsh boots, which were too big and which she had kept for ten years without once wearing. She took the letter out of her bodice — it had gone soft with her warmth, the seal tacky — and stood for a moment with it in her hand.\n\nIf she went into the marsh with it and they caught her, they would have it. If she left it here —\n\nShe put it in the hollow of the bolster, where the sticks had been. Then she took the lamp and went down to Wren.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Drowned Chapel\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Wren @@Tomas Brenn @@Oduya Fenn @@Halvard Seyle\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?Fenn is in it with the Reeve:@Ilse Varga ?Fenn is in it with the Reeve:@Tomas Brenn ?Fenn is in it with the Reeve:reader\n]\n\nThe marsh at low water was a different country. The channels that the Patience had sailed were trickles now, running between banks of mud as high as Ilse's head, and the mud shone in the moonlight like the skin of something sleeping. Wren went ahead of her along a path she could not see — the hard, he called it, a spine of shingle under the mud that the old salt-carriers had used before there were boats — and she followed him step for step and did not look down.\n\nIt took them two hours to reach the Chapel. The tower stood up out of the mud on its little island of stone, the arches of the old nave around its foot half-buried, the high windows empty. At high water the sea came in through those windows. Now, with the tide out, there were steps going down into the dark of the crypt, slick with weed, and a smell of salt and old candles.\n\n\"Here's where they load,\" Wren whispered. He pointed. Along the stone of the steps there were fresh scars, white in the moonlight, where something heavy had been dragged. \"Salt. Sacks of it. They bring it up the channel from the Pans at night and keep it here dry till the ship comes.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\nWren shrugged inside his coat. \"Everybody knows. Nobody says.\"\n\nThey found Tomas in the tower.\n\nHe had dragged himself up the first turn of the stair and wedged himself into a window-niche, and he was so still and so grey that Ilse thought for a long moment that he was dead. Then his eyes opened and he said, quite clearly, \"You weren't supposed to come,\" and she knelt on the stair beside him and held his hand and could not say anything at all.\n\nHis leg was splinted with half a boat-hook. He had done it well. He had been sitting in the niche, he said, for a day and a night, while the Reeve's men came and went below him, loading the black ship from the crypt. He had heard everything. He had not been able to move.\n\n\"Corin,\" Ilse said. \"Where's Corin?\"\n\n\"Went over the side. Took them off me.\" Tomas closed his eyes. \"Don't know if he got out. He said to tell you to keep the letter.\"\n\n\"I have it.\"\n\n\"Good. Ilse — listen. The ship's gone. The Black Mare. She went out on this morning's tide with all of it aboard, and she'll be back for the rest.\" His hand tightened on hers. \"And it's not just the Reeve. I heard them. They were talking about the Salt House. About the books. About someone who's going to fix the books before the auditor —\"\n\nThere was a sound below them. Wren, who had been keeping watch at the foot of the stair, came up it like a cat, silent, his eyes huge.\n\n\"Lantern,\" he breathed. \"Two men. On the hard.\"\n\nThey did not move. Ilse put her hand over Tomas's mouth, not because he would have made a sound but because she needed to hold something. Below them, in the ruined nave, a lantern came and went among the arches, and two men came with it, and stood at the head of the crypt steps where the white scars were.\n\nOne of them was big and wore a harbour officer's coat. She had seen the Reeve of Varrow at a hundred ceremonies on the quay; she knew his shoulders, his walk, the way he stood with his weight back on his heels.\n\nThe other was tall and thin, and held his lantern with a long hand that she had watched sign a thousand pages.\n\n\"She's asking for the flood year,\" the Magister was saying. His voice echoed in the stone. \"She's had it on the table for two days. She has copies of the Hesk ledgers, Halvard. Copies. She knew before she came.\"\n\n\"Then she knows nothing she can prove.\" The Reeve's voice was easy, almost bored. \"Hesk ledgers. A clerk in Hesk can't count. Everyone knows that.\"\n\n\"She'll ask for the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then give her the sticks. You told me the sticks were sorted.\"\n\nThe Magister was quiet. The lantern shook a little.\n\n\"One of my clerks,\" he said at last, \"found the Kestrel short. Before I could see to it.\"\n\n\"Which clerk?\"\n\nIlse felt Tomas's hand tighten on hers until the bones ground.\n\n\"It doesn't matter. I dealt with it. She wrote it in as I told her.\"\n\n\"Oduya.\" The Reeve sounded almost gentle. \"Everything matters. That's how we've lasted three years. Which clerk?\"\n\nThere was a long silence in the nave. Ilse could hear the water beginning to come back, very far off, a sound like breathing.\n\n\"The Varga girl,\" the Magister said.","wordCount":2160,"createdAt":1794213918912},{"id":"OFVQgBFix32t91gzEltBj","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"3z3uj6msTp-yJfszkLU-n","text":"She went to work. She did not know what else to do with herself, and it seemed important, in a way she could not have explained, that she should be at her place at the long table when the bell went, in her clerk's grey, with her hair pinned, as if nothing had happened. As if she were still the best clerk the Magister had had in thirty years.\n\nAurek looked up when she came in and opened his mouth to say something about bags of nails, and then looked at her face properly and shut it again.\n\nThe auditor was not there. Her sheet of tiny figures lay on the end of the table under a glass paperweight, and the green ledger was gone from beside it.\n\n\"She's downstairs,\" Aurek said quietly. \"With him. Since first bell.\" He hesitated. \"Ilse. I took the sticks out of the bin.\"\n\nShe turned.\n\n\"The Kestrel's. The night you put them in. I'm not a fool, girl, whatever you think. Nobody finds six sticks behind a stove and then finds them gone again.\" He did not look at her; he looked at his own ledger, at his own tidy upright hand. \"I've been here twenty years. I've written what I was told for three of them. I didn't count. I didn't ask. I told myself the Magister read the margins.\" He turned a page that did not need turning. \"They're in my coat. In the press. If you want them.\"\n\nShe stood very still.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\n\"Because you didn't write anything in the margin,\" said Aurek Stiles. \"You always write in the margin. You've written in the margin every day for four years. And that day you didn't, and I thought: she's frightened. And I thought: I've been frightened for three years and I never once noticed it was the same thing.\"\n\nAt midday the auditor came up the stair and took her sheet of figures from under the paperweight and went down again without a word to either of them. A little after that, the porter came up to say that the Magister wanted Miss Varga in his room.\n\nThe stove was lit. It was always lit. Oduya Fenn sat behind his desk with the green ledger closed in front of him and his long hands folded on top of it, and he did not smile when she came in. That was how she knew he had not slept either.\n\n\"Sit down, Ilse.\"\n\nShe did not sit down.\n\n\"I was at the Chapel last night,\" she said. \"In the tower. With Tomas, who has a broken leg, and has been lying in a window for two days while you loaded salt underneath him.\"\n\nThe Magister closed his eyes.\n\n\"I heard you tell the Reeve my name.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"I imagine you did.\" He opened his eyes again and looked at her, and his face was older than she had ever seen it. \"Will you let me tell you how it happened? Not to excuse it. I don't think it can be excused. But I'd like someone to know, and I think you've earned it more than anyone.\"\n\nShe sat down.\n\nHe told her. The tide in Thawmonth that took the wall and the pans and the pan-reeve's wife. The letter to the capital, and the letter back, *a matter for the harbour.* A whole year of the pan-men sitting on the quay, and the price of salt going up at Hesk, and the Salt House half empty, and the Exchequer writing every quarter to ask why the revenue was down. And then Halvard Seyle in this room, in that chair, with a bottle of something good, saying: *I can build you a wall, Oduya. All it needs is a little salt that nobody counts.*\n\n\"One part in seven,\" the Magister said. \"For one year. To pay for the wall. That was all it was going to be. I told myself it was the Exchequer's own salt paying for the Exchequer's own wall, and if the Exchequer had done its duty there'd have been no need. I told myself a great many things.\" He looked down at his hands. \"The wall was built in a year. And at the end of the year I went to Halvard and said, that's enough, and he said — he said, Oduya, you've been writing false books for a year. Do you want to explain that to the Exchequer, or shall I?\"\n\n\"So you kept writing.\"\n\n\"So I kept writing. And the wall is bad stone, and Halvard has a new house, and the Black Mare comes in on the spring tides and takes one-seventh of the salt of Varrow to wherever it goes.\" He lifted his hands, let them fall. \"And you counted the Kestrel's sticks. I knew you would, one day. You're the only one of them who ever counted.\"\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Did you take it?\"\n\nHis face did not change. \"What letter?\"\n\n\"The Reeve's letter to you. About the Kestrel. I had it.\"\n\nFor a moment the Magister of the Salt House looked at her with an expression she could not read at all, and then, very slowly, he began to laugh. It was not a happy sound.\n\n\"He never sent it,\" he said. \"I never had it. I wondered — I wondered why he was so certain I'd been told about the Black Mare coming early, when I hadn't. He said, *I wrote to you.* And I said, *you didn't.* And we each thought the other was lying.\" He wiped his eyes. \"Oh, Ilse. Somebody stole the Reeve's post. And now somebody has stolen it from you.\"","wordCount":948,"createdAt":1794214646101},{"id":"OQ1pMqa6X-7X57oEzncIO","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"g2q6xv5SNSaq9VyyZ2uux","text":"Her room was over the cooper's, at the top of a house so narrow that the stair went up through the middle of it like the stone in a plum. She lit the lamp and put the sticks on the table and looked at them for a while, and then she put them inside the hollow of the bolster, which was the only hiding place she had, and felt foolish.\n\nThe rain beat on the slates. Somewhere below her the cooper's wife was singing to the baby, the same three lines over and over.\n\nIt was close to midnight when someone knocked.\n\nShe did not open the door. She stood behind it with the lamp in one hand and the poker in the other and said, \"Who is it?\"\n\n\"A friend of your brother's,\" said a man's voice. It was light and pleasant and not from Varrow — there was something of the capital in the vowels, or something trying to sound like it. \"Though I doubt he'd put it that way. Please don't hit me with whatever you're holding.\"\n\n\"What do you want?\"\n\n\"To give you something. You can open the door a hand's width and I'll put it through, and then I'll go away, and you'll never have to look at me.\" A pause. \"Though I'm told I'm not bad to look at.\"\n\nShe opened the door a hand's width. The man on the landing was perhaps thirty, soaked through, his dark hair plastered to his skull. He had a long, clever face and a scar through one eyebrow and he was smiling as if this were all a great joke that she would be let in on presently.\n\nHe held out a packet wrapped in oilcloth.\n\n\"What is it?\"\n\n\"A letter. Not to you. I think you'd better have it anyway.\" He kept holding it out. \"My name's Corin Ashe. Your brother knows me. He'll tell you I'm a liar and a smuggler, and he'll be right about the second one.\"\n\n\"Why would you give me a letter that isn't to me?\"\n\n\"Because I can read, Miss Varga, and having read it I'd rather it were in the hands of someone who counts sticks than in mine.\" He pushed the packet through the gap. She took it, mostly to stop him dropping it. It was heavier than a letter ought to be. \"There's a seal on it you'll recognise. Don't break it here. Don't break it at all, if you've any sense. Just — keep it somewhere.\"\n\n\"How do you know about the sticks?\"\n\nBut he had already turned. She heard him going down the stair, light and quick, missing the step that creaked as if he knew it was there. The street door opened and shut.\n\nShe closed her own door and bolted it and set the lamp down and turned the packet over in her hands. Under the oilcloth was good paper, folded small, and a seal of red wax pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\nIt was the seal of the Harbour Reeve.\n\nIlse sat on the bed with the letter in her lap and the sticks in the bolster under her and listened to the rain until the lamp burned down.\n# Black Mare\n\n[\n  Place: #Ilse's Room\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Aurek Stiles @@Oduya Fenn\n  When: +1 day\n  Items: ^The Kestrel's tally-sticks:#The Salt House\n  Facts: ?The Reeve is skimming the salt:@Ilse Varga ?The Reeve is skimming the salt:reader\n]\n\nShe broke the seal in the morning, of course. Corin Ashe had known she would; that was why he had told her not to.\n\nThe storm had blown itself out in the night and left the sky scoured and pale. She sat at her table with a heel of yesterday's bread and slid the blade of her knife under the wax, carefully, so that it lifted in one piece and she could have pressed it back if she had been a different sort of person. Inside was a single sheet, folded twice, in a secretary's hand. At the bottom, a signature she had seen on a hundred harbour warrants. Halvard Seyle, Reeve of Varrow.\n\nThe letter was to Fenn.\n\n*Magister —*\n\n*The sixty from the Kestrel will go with the rest. The Black Mare lies off the Chapel on the spring tide and will take all of it then; there is no other night. Ormund leaves the capital on the twelfth and will be with you within the week, so see that the books are clean before she comes. I need not tell you what a clean book means. The levy is paid in full this year, as every year. You have my word on the Pans.*\n\n*— H.S.*\n\nShe read it four times. The fourth time she read it aloud, very quietly, to hear whether it said something else when it was spoken. It did not.\n\n*The sixty from the Kestrel.* So it was not six sticks dropped in the harbour. *With the rest.* So it was not only the Kestrel. She thought of all the books on the third floor, the forty years of them on their shelves under the eaves, and of how many times she had written a number into a column without counting the sticks, because the sticks had been counted by somebody else, because the Magister read the margins.\n\n*You have my word on the Pans.* That one she did not understand at all.\n\nShe folded the letter again and looked at the seal, and then she did a thing she was not proud of: she warmed the knife over the lamp and pressed the wax back down, so that from a little way off it looked whole. Then she put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, where it crackled when she breathed, and she went to work.\n\nAurek Stiles was already at the long table. He looked up when she came in and said, \"You look like a woman who's slept on a bag of nails,\" and she said, \"I found the sticks,\" and he said, \"Behind the stove?\" and she said, \"Behind the stove,\" and took them out of her satchel and put them in the bin, all thirty-four.\n\nIt was the first lie she had told at the Salt House. It went down easier than she had expected, and that frightened her more than the letter did.\n\nAt midday she went down to the Magister's room on an errand about the Heron's book and stood in his doorway while he signed it. His hand did not shake. He asked after Tomas, and whether the Patience had come through the storm, and he told her she was the best clerk he had had in thirty years, which he had told her before. She watched him sign and thought: *see that the books are clean.*\n\nShe had always believed that she could tell when someone was lying to her. She went back up the stairs knowing that she had been wrong about that, and did not know for how long.","wordCount":1176,"createdAt":1794213785789},{"id":"OgMSHw6bPH1IOIVHk_YqS","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"FBthgk0AwfIy4svSwzmYI","text":"The marsh at low water was a different country. The channels that the Patience had sailed were trickles now, running between banks of mud as high as Ilse's head, and the mud shone in the moonlight like the skin of something sleeping. Wren went ahead of her along a path she could not see — the hard, he called it, a spine of shingle under the mud that the old salt-carriers had used before there were boats — and she followed him step for step and did not look down.\n\nIt took them two hours to reach the Chapel. The tower stood up out of the mud on its little island of stone, the arches of the old nave around its foot half-buried, the high windows empty. At high water the sea came in through those windows. Now, with the tide out, there were steps going down into the dark of the crypt, slick with weed, and a smell of salt and old candles.\n\n\"Here's where they load,\" Wren whispered. He pointed. Along the stone of the steps there were fresh scars, white in the moonlight, where something heavy had been dragged. \"Salt. Sacks of it. They bring it up the channel from the Pans at night and keep it here dry till the ship comes.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\nWren shrugged inside his coat. \"Everybody knows. Nobody says.\"\n\nThey found Tomas in the tower.\n\nHe had dragged himself up the first turn of the stair and wedged himself into a window-niche, and he was so still and so grey that Ilse thought for a long moment that he was dead. Then his eyes opened and he said, quite clearly, \"You weren't supposed to come,\" and she knelt on the stair beside him and held his hand and could not say anything at all.\n\nHis leg was splinted with half a boat-hook. He had done it well. He had been sitting in the niche, he said, for a day and a night, while the Reeve's men came and went below him, loading the black ship from the crypt. He had heard everything. He had not been able to move.\n\n\"Corin,\" Ilse said. \"Where's Corin?\"\n\n\"Went over the side. Took them off me.\" Tomas closed his eyes. \"Don't know if he got out. He said to tell you to keep the letter.\"\n\n\"I have it.\"\n\n\"Good. Ilse — listen. The ship's gone. The Black Mare. She went out on this morning's tide with all of it aboard, and she'll be back for the rest.\" His hand tightened on hers. \"And it's not just the Reeve. I heard them. They were talking about the Salt House. About the books. About someone who's going to fix the books before the auditor —\"\n\nThere was a sound below them. Wren, who had been keeping watch at the foot of the stair, came up it like a cat, silent, his eyes huge.\n\n\"Lantern,\" he breathed. \"Two men. On the hard.\"\n\nThey did not move. Ilse put her hand over Tomas's mouth, not because he would have made a sound but because she needed to hold something. Below them, in the ruined nave, a lantern came and went among the arches, and two men came with it, and stood at the head of the crypt steps where the white scars were.\n\nOne of them was big and wore a harbour officer's coat. She had seen the Reeve of Varrow at a hundred ceremonies on the quay; she knew his shoulders, his walk, the way he stood with his weight back on his heels.\n\nThe other was tall and thin, and held his lantern with a long hand that she had watched sign a thousand pages.\n\n\"She's asking for the flood year,\" the Magister was saying. His voice echoed in the stone. \"She's had it on the table for two days. She has copies of the Hesk ledgers, Halvard. Copies. She knew before she came.\"\n\n\"Then she knows nothing she can prove.\" The Reeve's voice was easy, almost bored. \"Hesk ledgers. A clerk in Hesk can't count. Everyone knows that.\"\n\n\"She'll ask for the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then give her the sticks. You told me the sticks were sorted.\"\n\nThe Magister was quiet. The lantern shook a little.\n\n\"One of my clerks,\" he said at last, \"found the Kestrel short. Before I could see to it.\"\n\n\"Which clerk?\"\n\nIlse felt Tomas's hand tighten on hers until the bones ground.\n\n\"It doesn't matter. I dealt with it. She wrote it in as I told her.\"\n\n\"Oduya.\" The Reeve sounded almost gentle. \"Everything matters. That's how we've lasted three years. Which clerk?\"\n\nThere was a long silence in the nave. Ilse could hear the water beginning to come back, very far off, a sound like breathing.\n\n\"The Varga girl,\" the Magister said.","wordCount":801,"createdAt":1794216611190},{"id":"OkQzDkAMgUZP3x0UFZZ-M","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"W6kDqSJm2O5zS3kC2H7Of","text":"The storm came in at four, with the turn of the tide, as the old people on the quay had said it would. It came out of the south-west in one long grey wall, and the sea came with it.\n\nIlse watched from the top of the Gull Stair. She had run there from the Reeve's Hall with Corin behind her and half the lower town behind him, because Wren had come pelting up the hill shouting that the Reeve's longboat had gone out of the harbour with the Reeve in it, and that the Black Mare was lying off the Chapel again in the teeth of the weather, waiting for him.\n\n\"He's mad,\" Corin said, beside her. He had to shout it. \"He'll never get over the bar in this.\"\n\n\"He knows the bar.\"\n\n\"Nobody knows the bar in this.\"\n\nOut past the harbour mouth the sea had gone white. The salt-ships at their moorings were pitching and snatching at their chains, and the spray was coming over the quay wall in sheets, and down below them on the quay the pilots were out in their oilskins, shouting, hauling boats up the slip. Ilse looked for Tomas among them out of habit before she remembered that Tomas was in a bed at the Pans with his leg in a splint.\n\nThen she saw the longboat.\n\nIt was a small dark thing in the white water out by the Chapel, six oars rising and falling out of time, and it was making for the black ship. She could see the Black Mare now too, beyond it — two masts, no sail, her anchors down, plunging. Someone on her deck was waving a light.\n\n\"They'll make it,\" said Corin. \"Gods damn him, they'll —\"\n\nThe sea took the longboat on the Chapel bar.\n\nShe did not see it happen. One moment there were six oars and a dark hull and the next there was a wave, enormous, grey-green, standing up across the bar the way the sea stands up only where the bottom is very close, and when it went down there was nothing on the bar but white water. A long way off, the light on the Black Mare's deck stopped waving.\n\nNobody on the Gull Stair said anything at all.\n\nThe Black Mare did not wait. They saw her get her anchors up, or cut them — it was too far to tell — and get a scrap of sail on her, and claw her way round to the south, away from the bar and the Chapel and Varrow. Within half an hour she was gone into the rain.\n\nThe Reeve's body came ashore two days later on the hard below the sea-wall at the Pans, the new sea-wall, which had held. One of the pan-men found it. Mother Gannet said afterward that it was the only thing the Reeve ever built that did what it was meant to.","wordCount":490,"createdAt":1794216655321},{"id":"OufBDsTf2fD7Z8U7ap139","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Brenn had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. That was what the forty crowns in his coat pocket were for.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter under his hand saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"She's early.\"\n\n\"How early?\"\n\n\"A night. A whole night.\" He sat up. \"The letter said the spring tide. That's tomorrow.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his clever face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern, because he had taken her in through the Gullet on the ninth.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.","wordCount":713,"createdAt":1794216531576},{"id":"PL--WZAC-ZqeM8ZaBZ1Sf","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"r9SBgyYpFJBk2b1o5XkXt","text":"They brought Tomas out on the last of the ebb, the two of them, Ilse under one arm and Wren under the other, and it took four hours, and Ilse did not afterward remember much of it except the cold and the weight and her brother's breath hissing in her ear at every step. Wren found them the hard every time. At the edge of the marsh, where the path came up onto the sea-wall by the Pans, they left Tomas with a pan-man Wren knew, who asked no questions and sent his own son running for a bone-setter, and then Ilse walked home alone through the waking streets with the sun coming up behind her.\n\nThe Magister had said her name. She kept hearing it. Not *the clerk*, not *one of my girls*. *The Varga girl*, in that tired voice she had loved for four years, like a man putting down something heavy.\n\nShe climbed the stair through the middle of the cooper's house. The step that creaked, creaked. The cooper's wife was not singing.\n\nHer door was open.\n\nNot broken. Open, the way you leave a door when you have a key and are in a hurry. Inside, the room looked almost as she had left it — the table, the chair, the lamp-hook — except that everything had been moved a little and put back a little wrong, the way a stranger puts things back. The chair faced the wrong wall. Her mother's comb was on the left of the shelf instead of the right.\n\nShe went back down the stair. The cooper's wife was in the kitchen with the baby on her hip and the door half shut, and when Ilse knocked on it she opened it no wider.\n\n\"Was anybody up?\" Ilse said. \"In the night. To my room.\"\n\n\"I was asleep.\" The cooper's wife did not look at her. She looked at the baby, who was chewing on a wooden spoon. \"We were all asleep. The cooper's been at the Pans all week, you know that, there's nobody here but me.\"\n\n\"My door was open.\"\n\n\"Then you left it open. You were out all night, weren't you? Out in the marsh, by the look of your boots.\" The woman's eyes went to the boots and came away quickly. \"I don't ask where my lodgers go, Miss Varga. I'd thank you not to ask me where I sleep.\"\n\n\"Somebody had a key.\"\n\n\"Then somebody had a key.\" The cooper's wife began to close the door. \"There's three keys to that room and one of them's been lost since Midwinter. Ask the cooper. When he's home.\"\n\nThe door shut. Behind it the baby began to cry, and the woman began to sing to it, the same three lines over and over, too loud.\n\nIlse stood in the passage for a while. Then she went back up.\n\nHer room was cold. Someone had let the lamp burn down to nothing and the wick had charred, and there was a smell in the air that was not hers — wet wool, and tar, and something sharper, like cloves. She stood in the middle of the floor with her hands at her sides and tried to think the way her mother would have thought. Who had known she was going into the marsh. Who had known she would not be home. Wren had known. Mother Gannet had known. Anyone who had seen her go down Tallow Street at dusk in her mother's boots had known, and half the lower town had.\n\nIt did not narrow anything. It only made the room feel larger, and full of people.\n\nThe bolster lay on the bed with its seam cut open from end to end.\n\nShe did not need to look. She looked anyway. She put her hand into the hollow and felt the feathers and the ticking and nothing else, and then she sat down on the bed with the feathers settling around her like snow, and for the first time since the Kestrel's sticks she let herself cry.\n\nShe had not told anyone where she kept it. Not Tomas. Not Wren. Not the auditor, who she had lied to.\n\nShe had told no one, and someone had known.","wordCount":708,"createdAt":1794214646095},{"id":"PMCg2fmSPwHEVzRlETd9c","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"6iI4LgbwDeFqR_Cruf7mE","text":"She broke the seal in the morning, of course. Corin Ashe had known she would; that was why he had told her not to.\n\nThe storm had blown itself out in the night and left the sky scoured and pale. She sat at her table with a heel of yesterday's bread and slid the blade of her knife under the wax, carefully, so that it lifted in one piece and she could have pressed it back if she had been a different sort of person. Inside was a single sheet, folded twice, in a secretary's hand. At the bottom, two initials she had seen on a hundred harbour warrants, and the seal under them. H.S. Halvard Seyle, Reeve of Varrow.\n\nThe letter was to Fenn.\n\n_Magister —_\n\n_The sixty from the Kestrel will go with the rest. The Black Mare lies off the Chapel on the night of the eighteenth and will take the first of it then, and the rest on the spring tide; there are no other nights. Ormund leaves the capital on the twelfth and will be with you within the week, so see that the books are clean before she comes. I need not tell you what a clean book means. The levy is paid in full this year, as every year. You have my word on the Pans._\n\n_— H.S._\n\nShe read it four times. The fourth time she read it aloud, quietly, to hear whether it said something else when it was spoken. It did not.\n\n_The sixty from the Kestrel._ So it was not six sticks dropped in the harbour. _With the rest._ So it was not only the Kestrel. She thought of all the books on the third floor, the forty years of them on their shelves under the eaves, and of how many times she had written a number into a column without counting the sticks, because the sticks had been counted by somebody else, because the Magister read the margins.\n\n_You have my word on the Pans._ That one she did not understand at all.\n\nShe folded the letter again, and then she did a thing she was not proud of: she warmed the knife over the lamp and pressed the wax back down, so that from a little way off it looked whole. Then she put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, where it crackled when she breathed, and she went to work.\n\nAurek Stiles was already at the long table. When she came in he said, \"You look like a woman who's slept on a bag of nails,\" and she said, \"I found the sticks,\" and he said, \"Behind the stove?\" and she said, \"Behind the stove,\" and took them out of her satchel and put them in the bin, all thirty-four.\n\nIt was the first lie she had told at the Salt House. It went down easier than she had expected, and that frightened her more than the letter did.\n\nAt midday she went down to the Magister's room on an errand about the Heron's book and stood in his doorway while he signed it. His hand did not shake. He asked after Tomas, and whether the Patience had come through the storm, and he told her she was the best clerk he had had in thirty years, which he had told her before. She watched him sign and thought: _see that the books are clean._\n\nShe had always believed that she could tell when someone was lying to her. She went back up the stairs knowing that she had been wrong about that, and did not know for how long.","wordCount":606,"createdAt":1794230089006},{"id":"Pnlcf4p2iJF6vHtO7oNsI","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Varga had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. There were forty crowns in his coat pocket were for. He had not come for the forty crowns. He had come because he had gone to find Corin himself, the night before, to ask what a man like that knew about six missing sticks, and Corin had been quiet a while and then said: more than your sister does. Come out on the ebb tomorrow and I'll show you.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe. And I'm three hundred crowns short of a berth on anything going south, which is the kind of thing that makes a man sell what he's holding.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter under his seal saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"Right on her night.\"\n\n\"You knew she'd be here.\"\n\n\"I carry the Reeve's post, Tomas. Letters, mostly, and I've a habit of reading them. I knew she'd be here because I carried the letter that said so, and then I carried it somewhere else.\" He sat up. \"The first load tonight. The rest on the spring tide, three nights on.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern: he had brought the Kestrel in through the Gullet on the ninth with that same boat towing astern.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.","wordCount":842,"createdAt":1794230090245},{"id":"QMczVcsDj2Yiyf7p55-mk","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"2i5J2Lc7W9L8liiqtAFCG","text":"The Salt House kept its ledgers on the third floor, under the eaves, where the damp could not reach them and the clerks could not reach anything else. Ilse Varga had worked there for four years and had never once been warm.\n\nShe sat at the long table with the Kestrel's book open in front of her and the Kestrel's tally-sticks laid out beside it in a row, like the bones of a small animal. Every sack that went over the side of a salt-ship at Varrow was notched on a stick by the pan-reeve and notched again by the ship's mate, and the stick was split down its length so that each man kept half. When the halves came back to the Salt House they were matched, and counted, and the count went into the book. Nobody could cut a notch out of a stick without the other half showing it. It was the oldest honest thing in the city.\n\nShe counted the sticks again. Thirty-four sticks, ten notches to a stick. Three hundred and forty measures.\n\nThe book said four hundred.\n\n\"You're frowning at it,\" said Aurek Stiles from the far end of the table. He was the senior of the three tallyclerks and had been for twenty years, and he said it without looking up from his own work. \"Frowning at a ledger is how the ink gets into your face.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel,\" Ilse said. \"Her book says four hundred measures out on the ninth. I've got three hundred and forty on the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then you've dropped six sticks behind the stove.\"\n\n\"I haven't.\"\n\n\"Then the mate's dropped them in the harbour. It happens.\" Aurek turned a page. \"Write it as received and put a query in the margin. The Magister reads the margins.\"\n\nShe did not write it as received. She sat with the pen over the page until the ink beaded on the nib and fell, a small black star on the blotter, and then she got up and went down the narrow stair to the Magister's room.\n\nMagister Oduya Fenn had run the Salt House since before Ilse was born. He was a tall man gone thin in the way of tall men who forget to eat, and his room was the only warm one in the building, because he had the only good stove. He glanced up when she knocked and smiled at her with real pleasure, which made what she had come to say harder.\n\n\"Ilse. Sit. You look like you've been out on the Pans.\"\n\n\"Only on the third floor, Magister.\" She did not sit. \"The Kestrel's book is sixty measures over her sticks.\"\n\nThe smile stayed exactly where it was. That was the first thing she would remember afterward — not that it went, but that it stayed, a little too long, like a man holding a door that nobody was coming through.\n\n\"Sixty,\" he said.\n\n\"Six sticks. I've counted three times.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel came in through the Gullet in a rising sea. Her mate was sick over the side twice before they'd cleared the bar — your brother will tell you, he took her in.\" Fenn spread his long hands. \"Sticks go missing. The book is written from the manifest at the pans, and the manifest is right.\"\n\n\"Then the sticks should be with the manifest.\"\n\n\"Then they should.\" He said it kindly. \"Write the book as it stands, Ilse. I'll speak to the pan-reeve myself.\"\n\nShe went back up the stairs. Aurek did not ask her what had been said, and she did not tell him. She wrote four hundred in the column in her own clean hand, and in the margin she wrote nothing at all, because the Magister read the margins.\n\nWhen the bell went for the end of the day she put the thirty-four sticks into her satchel instead of into the bin. She did not decide to do it. Her hands did it while she was thinking about something else, and by the time she noticed, she was already on the stair.","wordCount":678,"createdAt":1794216599914},{"id":"RAiOYy-Avh6vMZ1ISs0_L","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"FBthgk0AwfIy4svSwzmYI","text":"The marsh at low water was a different country. The channels that the Patience had sailed were trickles now, running between banks of mud as high as Ilse's head, and the mud shone in the moonlight like the skin of something sleeping. Wren went ahead of her along a path she could not see — the hard, he called it, a spine of shingle under the mud that the old salt-carriers had used before there were boats — and she followed him step for step and did not look down.\n\nIt took them two hours to reach the Chapel. The tower stood up out of the mud on its little island of stone, the arches of the old nave around its foot half-buried, the high windows empty. At high water the sea came in through those windows. Now, with the tide out, there were steps going down into the dark of the crypt, slick with weed, and a smell of salt and old candles.\n\n\"Here's where they load,\" Wren whispered. He pointed. Along the stone of the steps there were fresh scars, white in the moonlight, where something heavy had been dragged. \"Salt. Sacks of it. They bring it up the channel from the Pans at night and keep it here dry till the ship comes.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\nWren shrugged inside his coat. \"Everybody knows. Nobody says.\"\n\nThey found Tomas in the tower.\n\nHe had dragged himself up the first turn of the stair and wedged himself into a window-niche, and he was so still and so grey that Ilse thought for a moment that he was dead. Then his eyes opened and he said, quite clearly, \"You weren't supposed to come,\" and she knelt on the stair beside him and held his hand and could not say anything at all.\n\nHis leg was splinted with half a boat-hook. He had done it well. He had been sitting in the niche, he said, for a day and a night, while the Reeve's men came and went below him, loading the black ship from the crypt. He had heard everything. He had not been able to move. He had come across the marsh on his back, ahead of the flood, pushing with his good leg and the stump of the boat-hook, and twice the water had closed over his face.\n\n\"There was a voice in the longboat,\" he said. \"The first night. I thought I knew it. I didn't want to.\"\n\n\"Corin,\" Ilse said. \"Where's Corin?\"\n\n\"Went over the side. Took them off me.\" Tomas closed his eyes. \"Don't know if he got out. He said to tell you to keep the letter.\"\n\n\"I have it.\"\n\n\"Good. Ilse — listen. The ship's gone. The Black Mare. She went out on this morning's tide with all of it aboard, and she'll be back for the rest.\" His hand tightened on hers. \"And it's not just the Reeve. I heard them. They were talking about the Salt House. About the books. About someone who's going to fix the books before the auditor —\"\n\nThere was a sound below them. Wren, who had been keeping watch at the foot of the stair, came up it like a cat, silent, his eyes huge.\n\n\"Lantern,\" he breathed. \"Two men. On the hard.\"\n\nThey did not move. Ilse put her hand over Tomas's mouth, not because he would have made a sound but because she needed to hold something. Below them, in the ruined nave, a lantern came and went among the arches, and two men came with it, and stood at the head of the crypt steps where the white scars were.\n\nOne of them was big and wore a harbour officer's coat. She had seen the Reeve of Varrow at a hundred ceremonies on the quay; she knew his shoulders, his walk, the way he stood with his weight back on his heels.\n\nThe other was tall and thin, and held his lantern with a long hand that she had watched sign a thousand pages.\n\n\"She's asking for the flood year,\" the Magister was saying. His voice echoed in the stone. \"She's had it on the table for two days. She has copies of the Hesk ledgers, Halvard. Copies. She knew before she came.\"\n\n\"Then she knows nothing she can prove.\" The Reeve's voice was easy, almost bored. \"Hesk ledgers. A clerk in Hesk can't count. Everyone knows that.\"\n\n\"She'll ask for the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then give her the sticks. You told me the sticks were sorted.\"\n\nThe Magister was quiet. The lantern shook a little.\n\n\"One of my clerks,\" he said at last, \"found the Kestrel short. Before I could see to it.\"\n\n\"Which clerk?\"\n\nIlse felt Tomas's hand tighten on hers until the bones ground.\n\n\"It doesn't matter. I dealt with it. She wrote it in as I told her.\"\n\n\"Oduya.\" The Reeve sounded almost gentle. \"Everything matters. That's how we've lasted three years. Which clerk?\"\n\nThere was a long silence in the nave. Ilse could hear the water beginning to come back, very far off, a sound like breathing.\n\n\"The Varga girl,\" the Magister said.","wordCount":854,"createdAt":1794217047409},{"id":"RZTtG_MeJHqk-BnnCktAG","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"2i5J2Lc7W9L8liiqtAFCG","text":"The Salt House kept its ledgers on the third floor, under the eaves, where the damp could not reach them and the clerks could not reach anything else. Ilse Varga had worked there for four years and had never once been warm.\n\nShe sat at the long table with the Kestrel's book open in front of her and the Kestrel's tally-sticks laid out beside it in a row, like the bones of a small animal. Every sack that went over the side of a salt-ship at Varrow was notched on a stick by the pan-reeve and notched again by the ship's mate, and the stick was split down its length so that each man kept half. When the halves came back to the Salt House they were matched, and counted, and the count went into the book. It was the oldest honest thing in the city. Nobody could cut a notch out of a stick without the other half showing it. It was the oldest honest thing in the city.\n\nShe counted the sticks again. Thirty-four sticks, ten notches to a stick. Three hundred and forty measures.\n\nThe book said four hundred.\n\n\"You're frowning at it,\" said Aurek Stiles from the far end of the table. He was the senior of the three tallyclerks and had been for twenty years, and he said it without looking up from his own work. \"Frowning at a ledger is how the ink gets into your face.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel,\" Ilse said. \"Her book says four hundred measures out on the ninth. I've got three hundred and forty on the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then you've dropped six sticks behind the stove.\"\n\n\"I haven't.\"\n\n\"Then the mate's dropped them in the harbour. It happens.\" Aurek turned a page. \"Write it as received and put a query in the margin. The Magister reads the margins.\"\n\nShe did not write it as received. She sat with the pen over the page until the ink beaded on the nib and fell, a small black star on the blotter, and then she got up and went down the narrow stair to the Magister's room.\n\nMagister Oduya Fenn had run the Salt House since before Ilse was born. He was a tall man gone thin in the way of tall men who forget to eat, and his room was the only warm one in the building, because he had the only good stove. He looked up when she knocked and smiled at her with real pleasure, which made what she had come to say harder.\n\n\"Ilse. Sit. You look like you've been out on the Pans.\"\n\n\"Only on the third floor, Magister.\" She did not sit. \"The Kestrel's book is sixty measures over her sticks.\"\n\nThe smile stayed exactly where it was. That was the first thing she would remember afterward — not that it went, but that it stayed, a little too long, like a man holding a door that nobody was coming through.\n\n\"Sixty,\" he said.\n\n\"Six sticks. I've counted three times.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel came in through the Gullet in a rising sea. Her mate was sick over the side twice before they'd cleared the bar — your brother will tell you, he took her in.\" Fenn spread his long hands. \"Sticks go missing. The book is written from the manifest at the pans, and the manifest is right.\"\n\n\"Then the sticks should be with the manifest.\"\n\n\"Then they should.\" He said it kindly. \"Write the book as it stands, Ilse. I'll speak to the pan-reeve myself.\"\n\nShe went back up the stairs. Aurek did not ask her what had been said, and she did not tell him. She wrote four hundred in the column in her own clean hand, and in the margin she wrote nothing at all, because the Magister read the margins.\n\nWhen the bell went for the end of the day she put the thirty-four sticks into her satchel instead of into the bin. She did not decide to do it. Her hands did it while she was thinking about something else, and by the time she noticed, she was already on the stair.","wordCount":687,"createdAt":1794216533144},{"id":"Ri-yvOWzTd4bNIBFhPsQL","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"r9SBgyYpFJBk2b1o5XkXt","text":"They brought Tomas out on the last of the ebb, the two of them, Ilse under one arm and Wren under the other, and it took four hours, and Ilse did not afterward remember much of it except the cold and the weight and her brother's breath hissing in her ear at every step. Wren found them the hard every time. At the edge of the marsh, where the path came up onto the sea-wall by the Pans, they left Tomas with a pan-man Wren knew, who asked no questions and sent his own son running for a bone-setter, and then Ilse walked home alone through the waking streets with the sun coming up behind her.\n\nThe Magister had said her name. She kept hearing it. Not _the clerk_, not _one of my girls_. _The Varga girl_, in that tired voice she had loved for four years, like a man putting down something heavy.\n\nShe climbed the stair through the middle of the cooper's house. The step that creaked, creaked. The cooper's wife was not singing.\n\nHer door was open.\n\nNot broken. Open, the way you leave a door when you have a key and are in a hurry. Inside, the room looked almost as she had left it — the table, the chair, the lamp-hook — except that everything had been moved a little and put back a little wrong, the way a stranger puts things back. The chair faced the wrong wall. Her mother's comb was on the left of the shelf instead of the right.\n\nShe went back down the stair. The cooper's wife was in the kitchen with the baby on her hip and the door half shut, and when Ilse knocked on it she opened it no wider.\n\n\"Was anybody up?\" Ilse said. \"In the night. To my room.\"\n\n\"I was asleep.\" The cooper's wife did not look at her. She looked at the baby, who was chewing on a wooden spoon. \"We were all asleep. The cooper's been at the Pans all week, you know that, there's nobody here but me.\"\n\n\"My door was open.\"\n\n\"Then you left it open. You were out all night, weren't you? Out in the marsh, by the look of your boots.\" The woman's eyes went to the boots and came away quickly. \"I don't ask where my lodgers go, Miss Varga. I'd thank you not to ask me where I sleep.\"\n\n\"Somebody had a key.\"\n\n\"Then somebody had a key.\" The cooper's wife began to close the door. \"There's three keys to that room and one of them's been lost since Midwinter. Ask the cooper. When he's home.\"\n\nThe door shut. Behind it the baby began to cry, and the woman began to sing to it, the same three lines over and over, too loud.\n\nIlse stood in the passage for a while. Then she went back up.\n\nHer room was cold. Someone had let the lamp burn down to nothing and the wick had charred, and there was a smell in the air that was not hers — wet wool, and tar, and something sharper, like cloves. She stood in the middle of the floor with her hands at her sides and tried to think the way her mother would have thought. Who had known she was going into the marsh. Who had known she would not be home. Wren had known. Mother Gannet had known. Anyone who had seen her go down Tallow Street at dusk in her mother's boots had known, and half the lower town had.\n\nIt did not narrow anything. It only made the room feel larger, and full of people.\n\nThe bolster lay on the bed with its seam cut open from end to end.\n\nShe did not need to look. She looked anyway. She put her hand into the hollow and felt the feathers and the ticking and nothing else, and then she sat down on the bed with the feathers settling around her like snow, and for the first time since the Kestrel's sticks she let herself cry.\n\nShe had not told anyone where she kept it. Not Tomas. Not Wren. Not the auditor, who she had lied to.\n\nShe had told no one, and someone had known.","wordCount":708,"createdAt":1794216533945},{"id":"Rj0Ckpk6XL1cZo5SJAnW0","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"g2q6xv5SNSaq9VyyZ2uux","text":"Her room was over the cooper's, at the top of a house so narrow that the stair went up through the middle of it like the stone in a plum. She lit the lamp and put the sticks on the table and looked at them for a while, and then she put them inside the hollow of the bolster, which was the only hiding place she had, and felt foolish.\n\nThe rain beat on the slates. Somewhere below her the cooper's wife was singing to the baby, the same three lines over and over.\n\nIt was close to midnight when someone knocked.\n\nShe did not open the door. She stood behind it with the lamp in one hand and the poker in the other and said, \"Who is it?\"\n\n\"A friend of your brother's,\" said a man's voice. It was light and pleasant and not from Varrow — there was something of the capital in the vowels, or something trying to sound like it. \"Though I doubt he'd put it that way. Please don't hit me with whatever you're holding.\"\n\n\"What do you want?\"\n\n\"To give you something. You can open the door a hand's width and I'll put it through, and then I'll go away, and you'll never have to look at me.\" A pause. \"Though I'm told I'm not bad to look at.\"\n\nShe opened the door a hand's width. The man on the landing was perhaps thirty, soaked through, his dark hair plastered to his skull. He had a long, clever face and a scar through one eyebrow and he was smiling as if this were all a great joke that she would be let in on presently.\n\nHe held out a packet wrapped in oilcloth.\n\n\"What is it?\"\n\n\"A letter. Not to you. I think you'd better have it anyway.\" He kept holding it out. \"My name's Corin Ashe. Your brother knows me. He'll tell you I'm a liar and a smuggler, and he'll be right about the second one.\"\n\n\"Why would you give me a letter that isn't to me?\"\n\n\"Because I can read, Miss Varga, and having read it I'd rather it were in the hands of someone who counts sticks than in mine.\" He pushed the packet through the gap. She took it, mostly to stop him dropping it. It was heavier than a letter ought to be. \"There's a seal on it you'll recognise. Don't break it here. Don't break it at all, if you've any sense. Just — keep it somewhere.\"\n\n\"How do you know about the sticks?\"\n\nBut he had already turned. She heard him going down the stair, light and quick, missing the step that creaked as if he knew it was there. The street door opened and shut.\n\nShe closed her own door and bolted it and set the lamp down and turned the packet over in her hands. Under the oilcloth was good paper, folded small, and a seal of red wax pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\nIt was the seal of the Harbour Reeve.\n\nIlse sat on the bed with the letter in her lap and the sticks in the bolster under her and listened to the rain until the lamp burned down.","wordCount":539,"createdAt":1794216602407},{"id":"Rl1Rb0EjQ4LQPK8jumX2d","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"JLebbu3pA-RXZKvs35ANa","text":"Lisbet Ormund had taken the best room at the Anchor, which was not saying a great deal, and had spent the first evening moving the furniture so that the table caught the light from the window and the bed did not catch the draught from the door. She was at the table now, with her shoes off and her stockinged feet on a hot brick wrapped in flannel, writing her report.\n\nShe had written it three times. Each time it said the same thing — _one part in seven, three years, the Salt House books written to conceal it_ — and each time it ended in the same place, which was _I have no proof of who._\n\nWhen the landlord knocked to say the Reeve was below and wished to call on her, she said, \"Yes, of course, send him up,\" and put her shoes back on, and turned her report face down on the table, and put the hot brick under the bed.\n\nHalvard Seyle filled the doorway. He was a big man and knew it, and he had come in his harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs, and he bowed to her with great courtesy and said he hoped she was comfortable, and that Varrow was not too rough for a lady from the capital, and that if there was anything at all she needed she had only to send to the Reeve's Hall.\n\n\"How kind,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Do sit down.\"\n\nHe sat. The chair complained.\n\n\"I hear you've been at the Salt House books,\" he said.\n\n\"I have.\"\n\n\"Poor Fenn. He's not the man he was. The flood hit him very hard — he took it personally, you know, as though the sea had done it to spite him. I've had to carry a good deal of the work since.\" He smiled. \"If you've found anything amiss, I'd hope you'd come to me first. Before the capital. It would be a shame to ruin an old man's name over a few clerical errors that the harbour could put right quietly.\"\n\n\"A few clerical errors,\" the auditor repeated.\n\n\"Sticks go missing,\" said the Reeve. \"It happens all the time.\"\n\nLisbet Ormund regarded him for a long moment with her kindly hen's face. Then she turned her report over on the table and pushed it across to him.\n\n\"One part in seven,\" she said. \"Every month for three years. That is not a few clerical errors, Reeve. That is twenty-two thousand crowns of the Exchequer's revenue, near enough, and it is going somewhere, and it is not going to Hesk.\" She folded her hands. \"I'll tell you what I have, since you've been good enough to call. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. I have a tallyclerk who lied to my face and was frightened to death doing it, and a Magister who hasn't slept in three nights. What I don't have is a name.\" She smiled at him. \"I don't suppose you'd care to give me one?\"\n\nThe Reeve did not smile back.\n\n\"You've been in Varrow four days, madam,\" he said. \"You don't know the tides. People who don't know the tides get into trouble out on the marsh. Every year we lose one or two. Visitors, mostly.\" He stood up. \"I'd be careful.\"\n\n\"I always am,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Good night, Reeve.\"\n\nWhen he had gone she sat for a while without moving. Then she got the hot brick back out from under the bed, which had gone lukewarm, and put her feet on it, and turned her report face down again, and wrote at the bottom of a fresh sheet, in her tiny neat hand: _Seyle. Certain. No proof._","wordCount":622,"createdAt":1794216651603},{"id":"Rz6MiRGpOALjphZepWAUZ","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"dAT2R-4HoTYOS7lortcKb","text":"Tomas did not come by that day, as he had said he would.\n\nThe next morning Ilse went down the Gull Stair before the Salt House bell and found Mother Gannet already at her place at the foot of it, gutting eels into a bucket with a short black knife. The old woman had sold eels at the bottom of the stair for as long as there had been a stair, by her own account, and possibly longer. She had one eye milky and one eye sharp, and it was generally understood in the lower town that the milky one saw more.\n\n\"He's not back,\" Mother Gannet said, before Ilse had opened her mouth. \"The Patience isn't on her mooring. I've had the boy up and down the quay since first light asking.\"\n\n\"Which boy?\"\n\n\"Mine.\" The old woman jerked her chin. Sitting on the bottom step, eating a cold potato with great concentration, was a child of nine or ten in a man's coat cut down at the sleeves. He had a face so dirty it was hard to say whether he was a boy at all. \"Wren. He runs for me. He's not mine, but he runs for me.\"\n\n\"Nobody's seen her,\" Wren said, through potato. \"Not in the harbour. Not at the Pans. Tad Mossop says he saw a sail in the channels last night past the Heron Creek, but Tad Mossop says a lot of things.\"\n\nIlse sat down on the step beside him because her legs had decided it for her.\n\n\"He's a pilot,\" Mother Gannet said, not unkindly. \"He knows every inch of that marsh. If he's out there he's out there because he means to be.\" She dropped an eel into the bucket. \"Question is who he's out there with.\"\n\n\"Corin Ashe.\"\n\n\"Ah.\" The old woman's sharp eye went to her. \"Then the question's what he's out there for.\"\n\nIlse looked at the bucket of eels, at the grey light on the mud, at the salt-ships still lying over on their sides from the ebb. She thought about the letter against her skin and about the auditor at the end of the long table in the draught, asking for the year of the big tide.\n\n\"Mother,\" she said. \"You remember the tide that took the Pans.\"\n\n\"I remember every tide since the old king died.\"\n\n\"What happened after? To the Pans?\"\n\nMother Gannet wiped the black knife on her apron and was quiet for long enough that Wren stopped chewing.\n\n\"Sea came over the wall at the spring tide in Thawmonth,\" she said at last. \"Took the wall, took the pans behind it, took the pan-reeve's house and the pan-reeve's wife with it. Half the salt-works in Varrow, gone under in one night. The Salt House wrote to the capital for money to build it back, and the capital wrote back and said it was a matter for the harbour.\" She spat, neatly, into the mud. \"So it was the Reeve's to mend, and the Reeve said there was no money for it, and for a whole year the pan-men sat on the quay with their hands in their laps. And then one day there was money. And the wall went up, and the pans went back, and everyone said what a fine man the Reeve was.\"\n\n\"Where did the money come from?\"\n\n\"Nobody asked. That's the thing about money that comes when you need it. Nobody asks.\" The old woman picked up another eel. \"Your mother would've asked.\"\n\n\"My mother's dead.\"\n\n\"So she is. And so's the pan-reeve's wife, and the Salt House is still standing, and the Reeve's still got his fine house on the hill, and his fine new wall, which, I'll tell you for nothing, is built of the worst stone I ever saw and won't see out another ten years of tides.\" The knife went in. \"You asked.\"\n\nIlse stood up. \"If Tomas comes back —\"\n\n\"The boy'll find you.\" Mother Gannet did not look up. \"Go to your counting, girl. Count something.\"","wordCount":675,"createdAt":1794230091416},{"id":"SIFVJzxhrY9p6W9248n3T","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"W6kDqSJm2O5zS3kC2H7Of","text":"The storm came in at four, with the turn of the tide, as the old people on the quay had said it would. It came out of the south-west in one long grey wall, and the sea came with it.\n\nIlse watched from the top of the Gull Stair. She had run there from the Reeve's Hall with Corin behind her and most of the hall behind him, because Wren had come pelting up the hill shouting that the Reeve's longboat had gone out of the harbour with the Reeve in it, and that the Black Mare was lying off the Chapel again in the teeth of the weather, waiting for him.\n\n\"He's mad,\" Corin said, beside her. He had to shout it. \"He'll never get over the bar in this.\"\n\n\"He knows the bar.\"\n\n\"Nobody knows the bar in this.\"\n\nOut past the harbour mouth the sea had gone white. The salt-ships at their moorings were pitching and snatching at their chains, and the spray was coming over the quay wall in sheets, and down below them on the quay the pilots were out in their oilskins, shouting, hauling boats up the slip. Ilse looked for Tomas among them out of habit before she remembered that Tomas was in a bed at the Pans with his leg in a splint.\n\nThen she saw the longboat.\n\nIt was a small dark thing in the white water out by the Chapel, six oars rising and falling out of time, and it was making for the black ship. She could see the Black Mare now too, beyond it — two masts, no sail, her anchors down, plunging. Someone on her deck was waving a light.\n\n\"They'll make it,\" said Corin. \"Gods damn him, they'll —\"\n\nThe sea took the longboat on the Chapel bar.\n\nShe did not see it happen. One moment there were six oars and a dark hull and the next there was a wave, enormous, grey-green, standing up across the bar the way the sea stands up only where the bottom is very close, and when it went down there was nothing on the bar but white water. A long way off, the light on the Black Mare's deck stopped waving.\n\nNobody on the Gull Stair said anything at all.\n\nThe Black Mare did not wait. They saw her get her anchors up, or cut them — it was too far to tell — and get a scrap of sail on her, and claw her way round to the south, away from the bar and the Chapel and Varrow. Within half an hour she was gone into the rain.\n\nThe Reeve's body came ashore two days later on the hard below the sea-wall at the Pans, the new sea-wall, which had held. One of the pan-men found it. Mother Gannet said afterward that it was the only thing the Reeve ever built that did what it was meant to.","wordCount":490,"createdAt":1794216655353},{"id":"SMijyGfDlmV1H6tb8rFrP","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"DS5E6n6CNj-_T8xgA9nT-","text":"Varrow at low water smelt of mud and iron and the sweet rot of weed, and the whole of the lower town came down to the quay to look at the sky.\n\nThere was a storm coming. Ilse could see it from the top of the Gull Stair, a long bruise lying along the horizon to the south-west, with the light behind it gone the colour of a boiled sweet. The harbour had drained down to its channels and the salt-ships sat over on their bilges in the mud, masts leaning together like drunk men holding each other up. Below her the stair went down in its two hundred worn steps to the quay, and the quay was full.\n\nShe found Tomas where she always found him, sitting on an upturned basket outside the pilots' shed with a pipe he never lit, looking at the water as if it owed him money.\n\n\"You took the Kestrel in,\" she said.\n\n\"Hello, Ilse. Good day at the counting? Lovely to see you, Tomas.\" He moved over on the basket to make room for her. \"I took her in on the ninth. Why?\"\n\n\"Was it rough?\"\n\n\"It was the Gullet in a southerly. It's always rough.\" He looked at her properly. Her brother was three years older than her and had their mother's face, broad and patient, and the same habit of knowing what she was going to say before she said it and waiting for her to say it anyway. \"What's wrong with the Kestrel?\"\n\n\"Was her mate sick?\"\n\nTomas laughed. \"Jory Pell? Jory's been at sea since he could walk. He's never been sick in his life. He was singing.\" He stopped laughing. \"Why?\"\n\nShe told him. She kept her voice down, though nobody on the quay was listening to anything but the wind getting up. Tomas listened with the pipe in his teeth.\n\n\"Sixty measures,\" he said at last. \"That's — what is that, in silver?\"\n\n\"At the Hesk price, nine hundred crowns. Near enough.\"\n\nHe whistled, low and long. \"And the Magister said.\"\n\n\"He said sticks go missing.\"\n\n\"They do.\" But he was frowning now, the way she must have been frowning at the ledger. \"Not six of them off one ship, they don't. A man might lose one. Six is a decision.\" He took the pipe out of his mouth and turned it over. \"What are you going to do?\"\n\n\"I don't know. I wrote it in. Four hundred.\"\n\n\"Ilse.\"\n\n\"What was I supposed to write? He's the Magister. He got me the place.\" She pressed the satchel against her side and felt the sticks shift inside it. \"I took the sticks.\"\n\nTomas looked at the satchel and then at her. \"You took the — that's the Salt House's property.\"\n\n\"I know what it is.\"\n\n\"If anybody knew they were short, they'll know you took them.\"\n\n\"Nobody knows they were short. Aurek thinks I dropped them behind the stove.\"\n\nThe first of the rain came then, a few fat drops striking the stones between their feet, each one leaving a dark coin. All along the quay people began to move, hauling covers over baskets, shouting for children. Out past the harbour mouth the sea had gone a hard, flat grey.\n\n\"Go home,\" Tomas said. \"Put them somewhere dry. Don't do anything else tonight.\" He stood up and knocked the pipe against his palm out of habit, though there was nothing in it. \"I've got to see to the Patience before this comes in. I'll come by tomorrow.\"\n\n\"Tomas —\"\n\n\"Tomorrow.\" He put a hand on her head, the way he had when she was small, and then he was gone into the crowd, a broad back in an oilskin, moving against the people coming up.\n\nShe climbed the Gull Stair in the rain. By the time she reached the top the storm had come in over the bar and the whole harbour was white.","wordCount":656,"createdAt":1794216601140},{"id":"Smgcn8qmcvoIOdphitYM5","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"JLebbu3pA-RXZKvs35ANa","text":"Lisbet Ormund had taken the best room at the Anchor, which was not saying a great deal, and had spent the first evening moving the furniture so that the table caught the light from the window and the bed did not catch the draught from the door. She was at the table now, with her shoes off and her stockinged feet on a hot brick wrapped in flannel, writing her report.\n\nShe had written it three times. Each time it said the same thing — _one part in seven, three years, the Salt House books written to conceal it_ — and each time it ended in the same place, which was _I have no proof of who._\n\nWhen the landlord knocked to say the Reeve was below and wished to call on her, she said, \"Yes, of course, send him up,\" and put her shoes back on, and turned her report face down on the table, and put the hot brick under the bed.\n\nHalvard Seyle filled the doorway. He was a big man and knew it, and he had come in his harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs, and he bowed to her with great courtesy and said he hoped she was comfortable, and that Varrow was not too rough for a lady from the capital, and that if there was anything at all she needed she had only to send to the Reeve's Hall.\n\n\"How kind,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Do sit down.\"\n\nHe sat. The chair complained.\n\n\"I hear you've been at the Salt House books,\" he said.\n\n\"I have.\"\n\n\"Poor Fenn. He's not the man he was. The flood hit him very hard — he took it personally, you know, as though the sea had done it to spite him. I've had to carry a good deal of the work since.\" He smiled. \"If you've found anything amiss, I'd hope you'd come to me first. Before the capital. It would be a shame to ruin an old man's name over a few clerical errors that the harbour could put right quietly.\" He paused, as if a thing had only just occurred to him. \"There's a clerk of his, a Varga girl, who has something of mine that came out of my post. I'd hate her to come to harm over a piece of paper. Paper's cheap. Girls who can count are not.\"\n\n\"A few clerical errors,\" the auditor repeated.\n\n\"Sticks go missing,\" said the Reeve. \"It happens all the time.\"\n\nLisbet Ormund regarded him for a long moment. Then she turned her report over on the table and pushed it across to him.\n\n\"One part in seven,\" she said. \"Every month for three years. That is not a few clerical errors, Reeve. That is a hundred and forty thousand crowns of the Exchequer's revenue, near enough, and it is going somewhere, and it is not going to Hesk.\" She folded her hands. \"I'll tell you what I have, since you've been good enough to call. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. I have a tallyclerk who lied to my face and was frightened to death doing it, and a Magister who hasn't slept in two nights. What I don't have is a name.\" She smiled at him. \"I don't suppose you'd care to give me one?\"\n\nThe Reeve did not smile back.\n\n\"You've been in Varrow three days, madam,\" he said. \"You don't know the tides. People who don't know the tides get into trouble out on the marsh. Every year we lose one or two. Visitors, mostly.\" He stood up. \"I'd be careful.\"\n\n\"I always am,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Good night, Reeve.\"\n\nWhen he had gone she sat for a while without moving. Then she got the hot brick back out from under the bed — it had gone lukewarm — and put her feet on it, and turned her report face down again, and wrote at the bottom of a fresh sheet, in her tiny neat hand: _Seyle. Certain. No proof._","wordCount":673,"createdAt":1794230229982},{"id":"T1n_zGJ_GSUAo65hbF-Iz","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"LQxthMqMx2ix2u3pYQu7A","text":"The hearing was called for the hour after noon on the day of the spring tide, in the long hall at the top of the town where the Reeve of Varrow had sat in judgement on fishing disputes and drunk sailors for as long as there had been a Reeve. Lisbet Ormund had called it. She had the right; she had shown the clerk of the Hall a paper with the Exchequer's seal on it, and the clerk had gone white and sent for the Reeve, and the Reeve had come, and smiled, and said that of course the auditor must have whatever she needed.\n\nHe sat now in his own chair at the head of the hall, in the harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs. The Magister sat below him on the bench where witnesses sat, very straight, his long hands on his knees. Half the lower town had come up the hill to stand at the back. Ilse could see Mother Gannet's milky eye in the crowd, and Wren's dirty face beside her, and a great many people she had passed on the Gull Stair every day of her life and never spoken to. The old people on the quay had been saying since dawn that there was weather coming in with the tide.\n\nThe auditor stood at a small table in the middle of the floor with her papers in front of her.\n\n\"I'll be brief,\" she said, \"because the tide turns at four and I'm told half of you have boats to see to. I have audited the books of the Salt House of Varrow for the last four years. I find that for the last three of them, one-seventh part of the salt shipped from the Pans has not reached Hesk, and that the books have been written to hide it. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. Miss Varga.\"\n\nIlse stood up.\n\nShe had thought her legs would shake. They did not. She walked out to the table in the middle of the floor in her clerk's grey with her hair pinned and laid the things down on it one by one, the way she would have laid out a ship's sticks to be counted.\n\nThe Kestrel's book, open at the ninth.\n\nThirty-four tally-sticks, which Aurek Stiles had brought up the hill in his coat.\n\nA letter with a red seal pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\n\"On the ninth,\" she said, \"the Kestrel came in through the Gullet with three hundred and forty measures of salt on her sticks. I wrote four hundred in her book, because I was told to. That was a lie, and it was mine.\" She heard the crowd at the back shift and murmur. She did not look at them. \"Two days later the auditor asked me if I'd ever been told to write a number I knew was wrong, and I said no. That was a lie too.\"\n\n\"Miss Varga,\" said the Reeve, pleasantly, from his chair. \"You'll understand that a clerk who admits to two lies in one breath is not —\"\n\n\"And this,\" said Ilse, \"is a letter in your hand, Reeve, to the Magister, telling him where the sixty measures from the Kestrel were to go, and when, and on what ship. It never reached him. A man called Corin Ashe took it out of your post-bag, and gave it to me, and I've kept it.\" She looked up at him. \"You can read it, if you like. Or the auditor can read it aloud. It's quite short.\"\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\nHalvard Seyle looked at the letter on the table, and at the auditor, and at the Magister on the witnesses' bench, who had not moved. Then he looked past them all to the back of the hall, where Corin Ashe was standing against the wall with his arms folded, in a clean coat, and smiled.\n\n\"A smuggler,\" the Reeve said. \"A smuggler steals a letter, and a clerk who lies admits she lies, and an old man —\"\n\n\"I'll speak to the letter,\" said Oduya Fenn.\n\nHe stood up from the bench. He was not looking at the Reeve; he was looking at Ilse, and his face was quite calm.\n\n\"I never received it,\" he said. \"But I know what it says, because I know what every letter Halvard Seyle has written me for three years has said. I'll tell the auditor about every one of them. I'll tell her about the wall. I have kept a book of my own, Madam Auditor — a true one. I kept it because I am a clerk, and I could not bear not to. It is in the stove in my room at the Salt House, behind the firebrick, and it has every measure in it that I ever wrote false.\"\n\nSomebody in the crowd said, very loudly, \"Gods,\" and somebody else laughed, high and shocked.\n\nThe Reeve was on his feet.\n\n\"This hearing is closed,\" he said. \"I am the Reeve of Varrow and this is my hall, and I'll not have —\"\n\n\"It isn't closed, Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund mildly. \"It's the Exchequer's hearing. It's only being held in your hall.\" She picked up the letter. \"Sit down, please. I'm going to read this aloud.\"\n\nHe did not sit down. He looked at the auditor, and at the Magister, and at the crowd filling the back of the hall, and something went out of his big red face all at once, like air out of a bladder. Then he turned and walked out through the door behind his chair, the one that only the Reeve used, and nobody stopped him, because nobody in Varrow had ever stopped the Reeve doing anything.","wordCount":967,"createdAt":1794216654100},{"id":"T6gEgZxNOp9NlQYoZLau2","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"2i5J2Lc7W9L8liiqtAFCG","text":"The Salt House kept its ledgers on the third floor, under the eaves, where the damp could not reach them and the clerks could not reach anything else. Ilse Varga had worked there for four years and had never once been warm.\n\nShe sat at the long table with the Kestrel's book open in front of her and the Kestrel's tally-sticks laid out beside it in a row, like the bones of a small animal. Every sack that went over the side of a salt-ship at Varrow was notched on a stick by the pan-reeve and notched again by the ship's mate, and the stick was split down its length so that each man kept half. When the halves came back to the Salt House they were matched, and counted, and the count went into the book. It was the oldest honest thing in the city. Nobody could cut a notch out of a stick without the other half showing it.\n\nShe counted the sticks again. Thirty-four sticks, ten notches to a stick. Three hundred and forty measures.\n\nThe book said four hundred.\n\n\"You're frowning at it,\" said Aurek Stiles from the far end of the table. He was the senior of the three tallyclerks and had been for twenty years, and he said it without looking up from his own work. \"Frowning at a ledger is how the ink gets into your face.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel,\" Ilse said. \"Her book says four hundred measures out on the ninth. I've got three hundred and forty on the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then you've dropped six sticks behind the stove.\"\n\n\"I haven't.\"\n\n\"Then the mate's dropped them in the harbour. It happens.\" Aurek turned a page. \"Write it as received and put a query in the margin. The Magister reads the margins.\"\n\nShe did not write it as received. She sat with the pen over the page until the ink beaded on the nib and fell, a small black star on the blotter, and then she got up and went down the narrow stair to the Magister's room.\n\nMagister Oduya Fenn had run the Salt House since before Ilse was born. He was a tall man gone thin in the way of tall men who forget to eat, and his room was the only warm one in the building, because he had the only good stove. He looked up when she knocked and smiled at her with real pleasure, which made what she had come to say harder.\n\n\"Ilse. Sit. You look like you've been out on the Pans.\"\n\n\"Only on the third floor, Magister.\" She did not sit. \"The Kestrel's book is sixty measures over her sticks.\"\n\nThe smile stayed exactly where it was. That was the first thing she would remember afterward — not that it went, but that it stayed, a little too long, like a man holding a door that nobody was coming through.\n\n\"Sixty,\" he said.\n\n\"Six sticks. I've counted three times.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel came in through the Gullet in a rising sea. Her mate was sick over the side twice before they'd cleared the bar — your brother will tell you, he took her in.\" Fenn spread his long hands. \"Sticks go missing. The book is written from the manifest at the pans, and the manifest is right.\"\n\n\"Then the sticks should be with the manifest.\"\n\n\"Then they should.\" He said it kindly. \"Write the book as it stands, Ilse. I'll speak to the pan-reeve myself.\"\n\nShe went back up the stairs. Aurek did not ask her what had been said, and she did not tell him. She wrote four hundred in the column in her own clean hand, and in the margin she wrote nothing at all, because the Magister read the margins.\n\nWhen the bell went for the end of the day she put the thirty-four sticks into her satchel instead of into the bin. She did not decide to do it. Her hands did it while she was thinking about something else, and by the time she noticed, she was already on the stair.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Quay\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Tomas Brenn\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?The Kestrel's book is sixty measures short:@Tomas Brenn\n]\n\nVarrow at low water smelt of mud and iron and the sweet rot of weed, and the whole of the lower town came down to the quay to look at the sky.\n\nThere was a storm coming. Ilse could see it from the top of the Gull Stair, a long bruise lying along the horizon to the south-west, with the light behind it gone the colour of a boiled sweet. The harbour had drained down to its channels and the salt-ships sat over on their bilges in the mud, masts leaning together like drunk men holding each other up. Below her the stair went down in its two hundred worn steps to the quay, and the quay was full.\n\nShe found Tomas where she always found him, sitting on an upturned basket outside the pilots' shed with a pipe he never lit, looking at the water as if it owed him money.\n\n\"You took the Kestrel in,\" she said.\n\n\"Hello, Ilse. Good day at the counting? Lovely to see you, Tomas.\" He moved over on the basket to make room for her. \"I took her in on the ninth. Why?\"\n\n\"Was it rough?\"\n\n\"It was the Gullet in a southerly. It's always rough.\" He looked at her properly. Her brother was three years older than her and had their mother's face, broad and patient, and the same habit of knowing what she was going to say before she said it and waiting for her to say it anyway. \"What's wrong with the Kestrel?\"\n\n\"Was her mate sick?\"\n\nTomas laughed. \"Jory Pell? Jory's been at sea since he could walk. He's never been sick in his life. He was singing.\" He stopped laughing. \"Why?\"\n\nShe told him. She kept her voice down, though nobody on the quay was listening to anything but the wind getting up. Tomas listened with the pipe in his teeth.\n\n\"Sixty measures,\" he said at last. \"That's — what is that, in silver?\"\n\n\"At the Hesk price, nine hundred crowns. Near enough.\"\n\nHe whistled, low. \"And the Magister said.\"\n\n\"He said sticks go missing.\"\n\n\"They do.\" But he was frowning now, the way she must have been frowning at the ledger. \"Not six of them off one ship, they don't. A man might lose one. Six is a decision.\" He took the pipe out of his mouth and turned it over. \"What are you going to do?\"\n\n\"I don't know. I wrote it in. Four hundred.\"\n\n\"Ilse.\"\n\n\"What was I supposed to write? He's the Magister. He got me the place.\" She pressed the satchel against her side and felt the sticks shift inside it. \"I took the sticks.\"\n\nTomas looked at the satchel and then at her. \"You took the — that's the Salt House's property.\"\n\n\"I know what it is.\"\n\n\"If anybody knew they were short, they'll know you took them.\"\n\n\"Nobody knows they were short. Aurek thinks I dropped them behind the stove.\"\n\nThe first of the rain came then, a few fat drops striking the stones between their feet, each one leaving a dark coin. All along the quay people began to move, hauling covers over baskets, shouting for children. Out past the harbour mouth the sea had gone a hard, flat grey.\n\n\"Go home,\" Tomas said. \"Put them somewhere dry. Don't do anything else tonight.\" He stood up and knocked the pipe against his palm out of habit, though there was nothing in it. \"I've got to see to the Patience before this comes in. I'll come by tomorrow.\"\n\n\"Tomas —\"\n\n\"Tomorrow.\" He put a hand on her head, the way he had when she was small, and then he was gone into the crowd, a broad back in an oilskin, moving against the people coming up.\n\nShe climbed the Gull Stair in the rain. By the time she reached the top the storm had come in over the bar and the whole harbour was white.","wordCount":1354,"createdAt":1794213586080},{"id":"T8_ekejnmVVf1gSIByLQX","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"LQxthMqMx2ix2u3pYQu7A","text":"The hearing was called for the hour after noon on the day of the spring tide, in the long hall at the top of the town where the Reeve of Varrow had sat in judgement on fishing disputes and drunk sailors for as long as there had been a Reeve. Lisbet Ormund had called it. She had the right; she had shown the clerk of the Hall a paper with the Exchequer's seal on it, and the clerk had gone white and sent for the Reeve, and the Reeve had come, and smiled, and said that of course the auditor must have whatever she needed.\n\nHe sat now in his own chair at the head of the hall, in the harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs. The Magister sat below him on the bench where witnesses sat, very straight, his long hands on his knees. Half the lower town had come up the hill to stand at the back. Ilse could see Mother Gannet's milky eye in the crowd, and Wren's dirty face beside her, and a great many people she had passed on the Gull Stair every day of her life and never spoken to. The old people on the quay had been saying since dawn that there was weather coming in with the tide.\n\nThe auditor stood at a small table in the middle of the floor with her papers in front of her.\n\n\"I'll be brief,\" she said, \"because the tide turns at four and I'm told half of you have boats to see to. I have audited the books of the Salt House of Varrow for the last four years. I find that for the last three of them, one-seventh part of the salt shipped from the Pans has not reached Hesk, and that the books have been written to hide it. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. Miss Varga.\"\n\nIlse stood up.\n\nShe had thought her legs would shake. They did not. She walked out to the table in the middle of the floor in her clerk's grey with her hair pinned and laid the things down on it one by one, the way she would have laid out a ship's sticks to be counted.\n\nThe Kestrel's book, open at the ninth.\n\nThirty-four tally-sticks, which Aurek Stiles had brought up the hill in his coat.\n\nA letter with a red seal pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\n\"On the ninth,\" she said, \"the Kestrel came in through the Gullet with three hundred and forty measures of salt on her sticks. I wrote four hundred in her book, because I was told to. That was a lie, and it was mine.\" She heard the crowd at the back shift and murmur. She did not look at them. \"Two days after I wrote it, the auditor asked me if I'd ever been told to write a number I knew was wrong, and I said no. That was a lie too.\"\n\n\"Miss Varga,\" said the Reeve, pleasantly, from his chair. \"You'll understand that a clerk who admits to two lies in one breath is not —\"\n\n\"And this,\" said Ilse, \"is a letter under your seal, Reeve, to the Magister, telling him where the sixty measures from the Kestrel were to go, and when, and on what ship. It never reached him. A man called Corin Ashe took it out of your post-bag, and gave it to me, and I've kept it.\" She looked up at him. \"You can read it, if you like. Or the auditor can read it aloud. It's quite short.\"\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\nHalvard Seyle looked at the letter on the table, and at the auditor, and at the Magister on the witnesses' bench, who had not moved. Then he looked past them all to the back of the hall, where Corin Ashe was standing against the wall with his arms folded, in a clean coat, and smiled.\n\n\"A smuggler,\" the Reeve said. \"A smuggler steals a letter, and a clerk who lies admits she lies, and an old man —\"\n\n\"I'll speak to the letter,\" said Oduya Fenn.\n\nHe stood up from the bench. He was not looking at the Reeve; he was looking at Ilse, and his face was quite calm.\n\n\"I never received it,\" he said. \"But I know what it says, because I know what every letter Halvard Seyle has written me for three years has said. I'll tell the auditor about every one of them. I'll tell her about the wall. I have kept a book of my own, Madam Auditor — a true one. I kept it because I am a clerk, and I could not bear not to. It is in my room at the Salt House, behind the loose firebrick in the chimney-breast, where the stove's heat never reaches, and it has every measure in it that I ever wrote false.\"\n\nSomebody in the crowd said, loudly, \"Gods,\" and somebody else laughed, high and shocked.\n\nThe Reeve was on his feet.\n\n\"This hearing is closed,\" he said. \"I am the Reeve of Varrow and this is my hall, and I'll not have —\"\n\n\"It isn't closed, Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund mildly. \"It's the Exchequer's hearing. It's only being held in your hall.\" She picked up the letter. \"Sit down, please. I'm going to read this aloud.\"\n\nHe did not sit down. Something went out of his big red face all at once, like air out of a bladder. Then he turned and walked out through the door behind his chair, the one that only the Reeve used.\n\n\"Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund sharply, and then, to the clerk of the Hall: \"Stop that man.\" The clerk of the Hall looked at the Reeve's back, and at the auditor, and did not move. Nobody stopped him, because nobody in Varrow had ever stopped the Reeve doing anything.","wordCount":989,"createdAt":1794230097670},{"id":"TGG-zbn33-Dgp0w9yETfJ","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"W6kDqSJm2O5zS3kC2H7Of","text":"The storm came in at four, with the turn of the tide, as the old people on the quay had said it would. It came out of the south-west in one long grey wall, and the sea came with it.\n\nIlse watched from the top of the Gull Stair. She had run there from the Reeve's Hall with Corin behind her and most of the hall behind him, because Wren had come pelting up the hill shouting that the Reeve's longboat had gone out of the harbour with the Reeve in it, and that the Black Mare was lying off the Chapel again in the teeth of the weather, waiting for him.\n\n\"He's mad,\" Corin said, beside her. He had to shout it. \"He'll never get over the bar in this.\"\n\n\"He knows the bar.\"\n\n\"Nobody knows the bar in this.\"\n\nOut past the harbour mouth the sea had gone white. The salt-ships at their moorings were pitching and snatching at their chains, and the spray was coming over the quay wall in sheets, and down below them on the quay the pilots were out in their oilskins, shouting, hauling boats up the slip. Ilse searched for Tomas among them out of habit before she remembered that Tomas was in a bed at the Pans with his leg in a splint.\n\nThen she saw the longboat.\n\nIt was a small dark thing in the white water out by the Chapel, six oars rising and falling out of time, and it was making for the black ship. She could see the Black Mare now too, beyond it — two masts, no sail, her anchors down, plunging. Someone on her deck was waving a light.\n\n\"They'll make it,\" said Corin. \"Gods damn him, they'll —\"\n\nThe sea took the longboat on the Chapel bar.\n\nShe did not see it happen. One moment there were six oars and a dark hull and the next there was a wave, enormous, grey-green, standing up across the bar the way the sea stands up only where the bottom is very close, and when it went down there was nothing on the bar but white water. A long way off, the light on the Black Mare's deck stopped waving.\n\nNobody on the Gull Stair said anything at all.\n\nThe Black Mare did not wait. They saw her get her anchors up, or cut them — it was too far to tell — and get a scrap of sail on her, and claw her way round to the south, away from the bar and the Chapel and Varrow. Within half an hour she was gone into the rain.\n\nThe Reeve's body came ashore two days later on the hard below the sea-wall at the Pans, the new sea-wall, which had held. One of the pan-men found it. Mother Gannet said afterwards that it was the only thing the Reeve ever built that did what it was meant to.","wordCount":490,"createdAt":1794230068551},{"id":"TkXNmsHhh7n2ZaeNcqj2c","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"GIXhkOMgrF_FKcO5VyMzd","text":"The auditor left Varrow on the twelfth day of Seedmonth, in the same carriage that had come down Tallow Street and frightened the porter, and the lower town came out to see her go.\n\nShe had stayed a fortnight longer than she meant to. She had sat in the Magister's room with the firebrick out of the stove and the Magister's true book open on the desk — a small black book, scorched at the corners, with three years of real numbers in it in a long thin careful hand — and she had copied every page. Then she had sat in the third-floor room with Aurek and Ilse for ten days more and put the real numbers into the real books, page by page, in the margins, in red.\n\n\"The Exchequer will want the Magister in the capital,\" she told Ilse, the last evening, standing at the door of the Salt House with her gloves in her hand. \"There'll be a trial. I'll speak for him. I'll say he kept a true book when he could have kept none, and gave it up when he could have burned it, and that's not nothing.\" She pulled on one glove. \"I don't know what they'll do. Something less than they might have, I hope.\"\n\n\"And the Salt House?\"\n\n\"Will need a Magister.\" Lisbet Ormund pulled on the other glove. \"I've written to say so. I've written to say who.\" She looked up at Ilse with her kindly hen's face. \"Master Stiles has been here twenty years and is a very good clerk, and he told me himself that he would rather go into the sea after the Reeve than have the stove. So I've written your name.\"\n\nIlse opened her mouth and shut it again.\n\n\"You'll say you're too young,\" said the auditor. \"And that you lied to me. You did. You also told me you'd lied, in front of the whole town, which I have never once in thirty years of this work seen anyone do. Your mother would have.\" She held out her small gloved hand, and Ilse took it. \"Count everything, Miss Varga. Write in the margins. Good night.\"\n\nThe carriage went down Tallow Street in the morning with the carters cursing it, and Ilse watched it from the third-floor window until it was gone.\n\nTomas came up the Gull Stair that afternoon on two crutches, which took him nearly an hour and which he would not let anyone help him with, and sat on the bench outside the pilots' shed at the bottom with his splinted leg stuck out in front of him and his unlit pipe in his teeth. Corin Ashe sat beside him. Neither of them said much. Corin had a pilot's oilskin on, which was too big for him, and a pilot's book in his pocket, which Tomas had made him buy, and he was learning the Gullet from the bench, channel by channel, with Tomas drawing it in the dust with the end of a crutch.\n\n\"He'll make a pilot,\" Tomas said to Ilse, when she came down to them at the end of the day. \"God knows how. He gets everything wrong and then argues with the sea about it.\"\n\n\"I'm very persuasive,\" said Corin.\n\n\"The sea isn't.\"\n\nWren was sitting on the bottom step of the Gull Stair eating a cold potato. Mother Gannet was gutting eels. The salt-ships lay over in the mud on the ebb, and the evening light came down the harbour the colour of honey, and somewhere up on the hill a bell was ringing for the end of the day.\n\nIlse climbed back up to the Salt House as the light went. The third floor was empty; Aurek had gone home. She lit the lamp and sat down at the long table, at her own place, and opened the Kestrel's book at the ninth.\n\nFour hundred, in her own clean hand. And beside it, in the margin, in red, in the auditor's tiny writing: _340. Sticks counted. I.V._\n\nShe dipped her pen, and turned to a clean page, and began to count.","wordCount":686,"createdAt":1794216656598},{"id":"UCHwDIPo0AdX2UMJF6xcp","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"3z3uj6msTp-yJfszkLU-n","text":"She went to work. She did not know what else to do with herself, and it seemed important, in a way she could not have explained, that she should be at her place at the long table when the bell went, in her clerk's grey, with her hair pinned, as if nothing had happened. As if she were still the best clerk the Magister had had in thirty years.\n\nAurek glanced up when she came in and opened his mouth to say something about bags of nails, and then saw her face and shut it again.\n\nThe auditor was not there. Her sheet of tiny figures lay on the end of the table under a glass paperweight, and the green ledger was gone from beside it.\n\n\"She's downstairs,\" Aurek said quietly. \"With him. Since first bell.\" He hesitated. \"Ilse. I took the sticks out of the bin.\"\n\nShe turned.\n\n\"The Kestrel's. The day you put them in. I'm not a fool, girl, whatever you think. Nobody finds six sticks behind a stove and then finds them gone again.\" He did not look at her; he looked at his own ledger, at his own tidy upright hand. \"I've been here twenty years. I've written what I was told for three of them. I didn't count. I didn't ask. I told myself the Magister read the margins.\" He turned a page that did not need turning. \"They're in my coat. In the press. If you want them.\"\n\nShe stood very still.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\n\"Because you didn't write anything in the margin,\" said Aurek Stiles. \"You always write in the margin. You've written in the margin every day for four years. And that day you didn't, and I thought: she's frightened. And I thought: I've been frightened for three years and I never once noticed it was the same thing.\"\n\nAt midday the auditor came up the stair and took her sheet of figures from under the paperweight and went down again without a word to either of them. A little after that, the porter came up to say that the Magister wanted Miss Varga in his room.\n\nThe stove was lit. It was always lit. Oduya Fenn sat behind his desk with the green ledger closed in front of him and his long hands folded on top of it, and he did not smile when she came in. That was how she knew he had not slept either.\n\n\"Sit down, Ilse.\"\n\nShe did not sit down.\n\n\"I was at the Chapel last night,\" she said. \"In the tower. With Tomas, who has a broken leg, and has been lying in a window for two days while you loaded salt underneath him.\"\n\nThe Magister closed his eyes.\n\n\"I heard you tell the Reeve my name.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"I imagine you did.\" He opened his eyes again and looked at her, and his face was older than she had ever seen it. \"Will you let me tell you how it happened? Not to excuse it. I don't think it can be excused. But I'd like someone to know, and I think you've earned it more than anyone.\"\n\nShe sat down.\n\nHe told her. What Mother Gannet had told her, and what Mother Gannet could not have known: the price of salt going up at Hesk, and the Salt House half empty, and the Exchequer writing every quarter to ask why the revenue was down. And then Halvard Seyle in this room, in that chair, with a bottle of something good, saying: _I can build you a wall, Oduya. All it needs is a little salt that nobody counts._\n\n\"One part in seven,\" the Magister said. \"For one year. To pay for the wall. That was all it was going to be. I told myself it was the Exchequer's own salt paying for the Exchequer's own wall, and if the Exchequer had done its duty there'd have been no need. I told myself a great many things.\" He looked down at his hands. \"The wall was built in a year. And at the end of the year I went to Halvard and said, that's enough, and he said — he said, Oduya, you've been writing false books for a year. Do you want to explain that to the Exchequer, or shall I?\"\n\n\"So you kept writing.\"\n\n\"So I kept writing. And the wall is bad stone, and Halvard has a new house, and the Black Mare comes in on the spring tides and takes one-seventh of the salt of Varrow to wherever it goes.\" He lifted his hands, let them fall. \"And you counted the Kestrel's sticks. I knew you would, one day. You're the only one of them who ever counted.\"\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Did you take it?\"\n\nHis face did not change. \"What letter?\"\n\n\"The Reeve's letter to you. About the Kestrel. I had it.\"\n\nFor a moment the Magister of the Salt House looked at her with an expression she could not read at all, and then, very slowly, he began to laugh. It was not a happy sound.\n\n\"He never sent it,\" he said. \"I never had it. I wondered — I wondered why he was so certain I'd been told about the Black Mare coming early, when I hadn't. He said, _I wrote to you._ And I said, _you didn't._ And we each thought the other was lying.\" He wiped his eyes. \"Oh, Ilse. Somebody stole the Reeve's post. And now somebody has stolen it from you.\"","wordCount":920,"createdAt":1794217055627},{"id":"Ucargs-uCO3gc60hxHOBX","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"QkH1pzVCb9awQuyhPF6LA","text":"They had been seen. Tomas never knew how — a turn of the Patience's sail against the sky, perhaps, or the creak of her blocks across still water, or simply that a man on the Chapel steps had looked up at the wrong moment. But the light at the Chapel went out all at once, and in the dark there was a shout, and then the sound of oars.\n\n\"Go,\" said Corin. \"Go, go —\"\n\nTomas went. He put the helm over and got the sail up in one long heave and the Patience came round, slow, so slow, broad-beamed old cow that she was, and the flood was against them now, pushing them back up towards the Chapel. Behind them the oars were coming on. Four oars at least. A longboat, light and fast, and the men in her knew the channel nearly as well as he did.\n\n\"They'll have us before the Heron Creek,\" Corin said.\n\n\"They won't.\"\n\n\"They will. I can count, Tomas, it's the only thing I'm good for besides lying.\" Corin was pulling off his boots. \"There's a cut on the left — the one with the dead tree. Where does it go?\"\n\n\"Nowhere. Mud. It's a dead end.\"\n\n\"Can a man stand in it?\"\n\n\"At this tide? To his chest, maybe. What are you —\"\n\n\"They want the boat. They'll follow the boat.\" Corin was on the gunwale now, crouched, his face white in the starlight. \"And they want me more than they want you, because I'm the one they've been paying. So when I go over, you keep going, and you make a great deal of noise, and I'll make rather less.\" He grinned suddenly. \"Tell your sister to keep the letter. Tell her it's worth more than I am.\"\n\n\"Corin —\"\n\nHe went over the side without a splash. Tomas never heard him hit the water. One moment there was a man on the gunwale and the next there was the dark and the dead tree going past on the left and nothing else.\n\nHe kept going. He did not know what else to do. He made, as instructed, a great deal of noise — he let the mainsheet run so the block screamed, and he banged the tiller against the thwart, and he shouted Corin's name once, loudly, towards the right-hand bank, as though looking for a man who had fallen in there. Behind him the oars checked, and a voice said something sharp, and the longboat split its attention for a moment between the boat and the bank.\n\nIt was enough to get him into the Heron Creek. It was not enough to get him out of it.\n\nThe Heron was narrow and shallow and he had taken it a thousand times at the top of the tide, but this was not the top of the tide and he was not looking where he was going. The Patience hit the bar at the creek's mouth with a long grinding shudder that went up through the keel and into his teeth, and stopped, and heeled, and he was thrown against the thwart so hard that he heard something in his leg go with a sound like a green stick breaking.\n\nHe lay in the bilge-water and did not scream, because the longboat was still out there.\n\nAfter a time — he did not know how long — he heard the oars go past the mouth of the creek, slow now, searching. A lantern swung. A man's voice said, \"Nothing. She's gone up the Heron and stuck, she'll be there in the morning, we'll have her then,\" and another voice, one he thought he knew, said, \"And the other one?\" and the first voice said, \"Drowned, if he's got any sense.\" Then the oars again, going away, toward the Chapel and the black ship and the salt.\n\nHe waited until he could not hear them. Then he dragged himself up onto the thwart, and looked at his leg, and looked away again quickly. The tide was still making. In an hour the Patience would float off the bar. In an hour he would have to be somewhere else.\n\nThe tower of the Drowned Chapel was a quarter mile off across the marsh, black against the first grey in the east. It was the only dry place for a mile. It was also where the men in the longboat were going.\n\nTomas looked at it for a long time. Then he found the boat-hook, and broke the shaft across his knee to make a splint, and began to bind his leg with the sail-ties, and tried to think of something else.","wordCount":778,"createdAt":1794216610027},{"id":"WJadRY840K28ASis1k1bX","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"kACrUDbXsjGSKBXJOWAnj","text":"Corin Ashe came back on the night before the spring tide, the way he had come the first time: a knock after midnight, a voice on the landing, a request not to be hit with whatever she was holding.\n\nThis time she opened the door all the way.\n\nHe looked terrible. He had a cut along his jaw that someone had stitched badly, and his clothes were not his own, and he was thinner than a man ought to get in two days. But he was smiling, and when he saw her face the smile went crooked and then went away altogether.\n\n\"Tomas,\" he said. \"Is he —\"\n\n\"He's alive. His leg's broken. He's at the Pans with a man called Hobb.\" She stood aside. \"Come in. You're letting the cold in.\"\n\nHe came in and stood in the middle of her small room and saw the bolster, which she had sewn up again, badly, and at the feathers that were still in the corners because she had not been able to bring herself to sweep them.\n\n\"I'm sorry about that,\" he said.\n\nShe had known since the Magister laughed. If the Reeve had never sent the letter to Fenn, then only one man in Varrow had known where it existed, and he had pushed it under her door. But she said, \"You,\" anyway, because it needed saying.\n\n\"Me.\" He reached inside his borrowed coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilcloth and held it out to her, exactly as he had on the first night. \"Everybody hides things in the bolster, Ilse. I've been in forty rooms in this city. It's always the bolster or the chimney, and you don't have a chimney.\"\n\nShe did not take it. \"Why?\"\n\n\"Because Seyle's men were coming up Tallow Street with a key the cooper's wife had been paid for, and I got there first. Because I knew where they'd look.\" He was still holding it out. \"And because I thought — when I came up out of that creek, half drowned, with the Reeve's men going past me close enough to touch — I thought, I could sell this. I could sell this back to Halvard Seyle for a great deal of money and go to Hesk and never come back. And I had it in my hand on the Hesk road for a whole day before I turned round.\"\n\n\"Why did you turn round?\"\n\nHe thought about it. She watched him think about it, and was surprised to find that she believed him when he answered.\n\n\"Because you didn't hit me with the poker,\" he said. \"The first night. You could have. You had it right there behind the door, I saw the end of it. And you opened the door a hand's width instead, and took a stranger's letter, and kept it.\" He shrugged. \"Nobody's ever done that. Usually they hit me.\"\n\nShe took the letter. The seal was still pressed down where she had warmed it with the knife. He had not opened it again.\n\n\"The auditor's at the Anchor,\" she said. \"On Rope Walk.\"\n\n\"I know where the Anchor is.\"\n\n\"Will you come with me?\"\n\nCorin Ashe was quiet for a long moment, the long clever face in the lamplight, the scar through his eyebrow.\n\n\"I'm a smuggler,\" he said. \"And a liar. Nobody will believe a word I say.\"\n\n\"They'll believe the sticks,\" said Ilse. \"Aurek has the sticks. And they'll believe the letter. And they'll believe me, because I'm going to tell them I lied, and nobody says that unless it's true.\" She put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, and reached for her coat. \"You only have to stand there.\"","wordCount":619,"createdAt":1794230096424},{"id":"WJju_fz9Qs_urU5fCcbJu","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"g2q6xv5SNSaq9VyyZ2uux","text":"Her room was over the cooper's, at the top of a house so narrow that the stair went up through the middle of it like the stone in a plum. She lit the lamp and put the sticks on the table and looked at them for a while, and then she put them inside the hollow of the bolster, which was the only hiding place she had, and felt foolish.\n\nThe rain beat on the slates. Somewhere below her the cooper's wife was singing to the baby, the same three lines over and over.\n\nIt was close to midnight when someone knocked.\n\nShe did not open the door. She stood behind it with the lamp in one hand and the poker in the other and said, \"Who is it?\"\n\n\"A friend of your brother's,\" said a man's voice. It was light and pleasant and not from Varrow — there was something of the capital in the vowels, or something trying to sound like it. \"Though I doubt he'd put it that way. Please don't hit me with whatever you're holding.\"\n\n\"What do you want?\"\n\n\"To give you something. You can open the door a hand's width and I'll put it through, and then I'll go away, and you'll never have to look at me.\" A pause. \"Though I'm told I'm not bad to look at.\"\n\nShe opened the door a hand's width. The man on the landing was perhaps thirty, soaked through, his dark hair plastered to his skull. He had a long, clever face and a scar through one eyebrow and he was smiling as if this were all a great joke that she would be let in on presently.\n\nHe held out a packet wrapped in oilcloth.\n\n\"What is it?\"\n\n\"A letter. Not to you. I think you'd better have it anyway.\" He kept holding it out. \"My name's Corin Ashe. Your brother knows me. He'll tell you I'm a liar and a smuggler, and he'll be right about the second one.\"\n\n\"Why would you give me a letter that isn't to me?\"\n\n\"Because I can read, Miss Varga, and having read it I'd rather it were in the hands of someone who counts sticks than in mine.\" He pushed the packet through the gap. She took it, mostly to stop him dropping it. It was heavier than a letter ought to be. \"There's a seal on it you'll recognise. Don't break it here. Don't break it at all, if you've any sense. Just — keep it somewhere.\"\n\n\"How do you know about the sticks?\"\n\nBut he had already turned. She heard him going down the stair, light and quick, missing the step that creaked as if he knew it was there. The street door opened and shut.\n\nShe closed her own door and bolted it and set the lamp down and turned the packet over in her hands. Under the oilcloth was good paper, folded small, and a seal of red wax pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\nIt was the seal of the Harbour Reeve.\n\nIlse sat on the bed with the letter in her lap and the sticks in the bolster under her and listened to the rain until the lamp burned down.\n# Black Mare\n## The Reeve's Seal\n[\n  Place: #Ilse's Room\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Aurek Stiles @@Oduya Fenn\n  When: +1 day\n  Items: ^The Kestrel's tally-sticks:#The Salt House\n  Facts: ?The Reeve is skimming the salt:@Ilse Varga ?The Reeve is skimming the salt:reader\n]\n\nShe broke the seal in the morning, of course. Corin Ashe had known she would; that was why he had told her not to.\n\nThe storm had blown itself out in the night and left the sky scoured and pale. She sat at her table with a heel of yesterday's bread and slid the blade of her knife under the wax, carefully, so that it lifted in one piece and she could have pressed it back if she had been a different sort of person. Inside was a single sheet, folded twice, in a secretary's hand. At the bottom, a signature she had seen on a hundred harbour warrants. Halvard Seyle, Reeve of Varrow.\n\nThe letter was to Fenn.\n\n*Magister —*\n\n*The sixty from the Kestrel will go with the rest. The Black Mare lies off the Chapel on the spring tide and will take all of it then; there is no other night. Ormund leaves the capital on the twelfth and will be with you within the week, so see that the books are clean before she comes. I need not tell you what a clean book means. The levy is paid in full this year, as every year. You have my word on the Pans.*\n\n*— H.S.*\n\nShe read it four times. The fourth time she read it aloud, very quietly, to hear whether it said something else when it was spoken. It did not.\n\n*The sixty from the Kestrel.* So it was not six sticks dropped in the harbour. *With the rest.* So it was not only the Kestrel. She thought of all the books on the third floor, the forty years of them on their shelves under the eaves, and of how many times she had written a number into a column without counting the sticks, because the sticks had been counted by somebody else, because the Magister read the margins.\n\n*You have my word on the Pans.* That one she did not understand at all.\n\nShe folded the letter again and looked at the seal, and then she did a thing she was not proud of: she warmed the knife over the lamp and pressed the wax back down, so that from a little way off it looked whole. Then she put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, where it crackled when she breathed, and she went to work.\n\nAurek Stiles was already at the long table. He looked up when she came in and said, \"You look like a woman who's slept on a bag of nails,\" and she said, \"I found the sticks,\" and he said, \"Behind the stove?\" and she said, \"Behind the stove,\" and took them out of her satchel and put them in the bin, all thirty-four.\n\nIt was the first lie she had told at the Salt House. It went down easier than she had expected, and that frightened her more than the letter did.\n\nAt midday she went down to the Magister's room on an errand about the Heron's book and stood in his doorway while he signed it. His hand did not shake. He asked after Tomas, and whether the Patience had come through the storm, and he told her she was the best clerk he had had in thirty years, which he had told her before. She watched him sign and thought: *see that the books are clean.*\n\nShe had always believed that she could tell when someone was lying to her. She went back up the stairs knowing that she had been wrong about that, and did not know for how long.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Salt House\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Aurek Stiles @@Lisbet Ormund @@Mother Gannet\n  When: +2 days\n]\n\nThe auditor came two days early.\n\nIlse heard her before she saw her — a carriage on the cobbles of Tallow Street, which was a thing that did not happen; nobody drove a carriage into the lower town because the streets were too narrow and the carters too rude. Then voices in the yard, and the porter's voice going up high the way it did when he was frightened, and then a long silence, and then feet on the stair.\n\nLisbet Ormund was not what Ilse had expected. She had pictured someone like the Magister, tall and grey and sorrowful, or someone like the Reeve, red-faced and loud. The woman who came into the third-floor room was small and round and perhaps fifty, in a brown travelling coat with the mud of the coast road still on its hem, and she had a face like a kindly hen. She stood in the doorway and looked at the shelves of ledgers, all forty years of them, with an expression of pure delight.\n\n\"Oh,\" she said. \"Oh, look at that. You keep them up here? Under the roof?\"\n\n\"It's the driest room, madam,\" said Aurek, who had got to his feet so fast his stool had gone over.\n\n\"It's the hottest room in summer,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Paper doesn't like heat any more than it likes damp. Never mind. Never mind.\" She came in and took off her gloves. \"I'm the Exchequer's auditor. I expect you've been told I'd be along. I expect you were told the twentieth.\" She smiled at both of them. \"I find it saves a great deal of everyone's time to come on the eighteenth.\"\n\nIlse found that she had stood up too, and that her hand had gone flat against the front of her bodice, where the letter was. She took it away.\n\n\"Magister Fenn —\" Aurek began.\n\n\"Is downstairs, and has given me the run of the place, and very gracious he was about it. He's sending up tea. You'll be Master Stiles. And you are —?\"\n\n\"Ilse Varga, madam. Tallyclerk.\"\n\n\"Varga.\" The auditor looked at her a moment longer than was comfortable. \"There was a Varga at the Hesk customs house, years ago. A woman. Very good with figures.\"\n\n\"My mother, madam.\"\n\n\"Was she? Well.\" Lisbet Ormund turned back to the shelves. \"Then you'll know the difference between a book that's right and a book that's tidy, Miss Varga. Most people don't.\" She ran a finger along the spines, the newest first. \"I'll want this year's, and last year's, and — let me see — the year of the big tide. When was that? Three years back?\"\n\n\"Four, madam,\" said Aurek.\n\n\"Three,\" said Ilse, at the same moment.\n\nThey looked at each other. The auditor looked at both of them, pleasantly.\n\n\"The spring tide that took the Pans,\" Ilse said. \"It was three years in Thawmonth. I'd been here a year.\"\n\n\"Then I'll have that one,\" said Lisbet Ormund, \"and the one before it, so I can see the difference. And I'll have the tally-sticks for whatever's come in this month. All of them.\" She sat down at the end of the long table, in the place where nobody ever sat because the draught came under the door there, and folded her small hands. \"I'm told sticks go missing,\" she said. \"I'm told it happens all the time.\"\n\nNobody said anything. Down in the yard the porter was shouting for someone to bring up the tea.\n\nThat night Ilse did not go home over the cooper's. She went down the Gull Stair to the quay, and she did not find Tomas on his basket outside the pilots' shed, and when she asked old Mother Gannet, who sold eels at the foot of the stair and saw everything, the old woman only said that the Patience had gone out on the evening ebb, and that there'd been two men in her, and that one of them was a long clever one with a scar in his eyebrow.","wordCount":1869,"createdAt":1794213785707},{"id":"X0uhadPALMZb49s3j_SJg","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"kACrUDbXsjGSKBXJOWAnj","text":"Corin Ashe came back on the night before the spring tide, the way he had come the first time: a knock after midnight, a voice on the landing, a request not to be hit with whatever she was holding.\n\nThis time she opened the door all the way.\n\nHe looked terrible. He had a cut along his jaw that someone had stitched badly, and his clothes were not his own, and he was thinner than a man ought to get in two days. But he was smiling, and when he saw her face the smile went crooked and then went away altogether.\n\n\"Tomas,\" he said. \"Is he —\"\n\n\"He's alive. His leg's broken. He's at the Pans with a man called Hobb.\" She stood aside. \"Come in. You're letting the cold in.\"\n\nHe came in and stood in the middle of her small room and saw the bolster, which she had sewn up again, badly, and at the feathers that were still in the corners because she had not been able to bring herself to sweep them.\n\n\"I'm sorry about that,\" he said.\n\nShe had known since the Magister laughed. If the Reeve had never sent the letter to Fenn, then only one man in Varrow had known it existed, and he had pushed it under her door. But she said, \"You,\" anyway, because it needed saying.\n\n\"Me.\" He reached inside his borrowed coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilcloth and held it out to her, exactly as he had on the first night. \"Everybody hides things in the bolster, Ilse. I've been in forty rooms in this city. It's always the bolster or the chimney, and you don't have a chimney.\"\n\nShe did not take it. \"Why?\"\n\n\"Because Seyle's men were coming up Tallow Street with a key the cooper's wife had been paid for, and I got there first. Because I knew where they'd look.\" He was still holding it out. \"And because I thought — when I came up out of that creek, half drowned, with the Reeve's men going past me close enough to touch — I thought, I could sell this. I could sell this back to Halvard Seyle for a great deal of money and go to Hesk and never come back. And I had it in my hand on the Hesk road for a whole day before I turned round.\"\n\n\"Why did you turn round?\"\n\nHe thought about it. She watched him think about it, and was surprised to find that she believed him when he answered.\n\n\"Because you didn't hit me with the poker,\" he said. \"The first night. You could have. You had it right there behind the door, I saw the end of it. And you opened the door a hand's width instead, and took a stranger's letter, and kept it.\" He shrugged. \"Nobody's ever done that. Usually they hit me.\"\n\nShe took the letter. The seal was still pressed down where she had warmed it with the knife. He had not opened it again.\n\n\"The auditor's at the Anchor,\" she said. \"On Rope Walk.\"\n\n\"I know where the Anchor is.\"\n\n\"Will you come with me?\"\n\nCorin Ashe was quiet for a long moment, the long clever face in the lamplight, the scar through his eyebrow.\n\n\"I'm a smuggler,\" he said. \"And a liar. Nobody will believe a word I say.\"\n\n\"They'll believe the sticks,\" said Ilse. \"Aurek has the sticks. And they'll believe the letter. And they'll believe me, because I'm going to tell them I lied, and nobody says that unless it's true.\" She put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, and reached for her coat. \"You only have to stand there.\"","wordCount":618,"createdAt":1794230096366},{"id":"XITJVCcukYxjEpTEZQrSP","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"6iI4LgbwDeFqR_Cruf7mE","text":"She broke the seal in the morning, of course. Corin Ashe had known she would; that was why he had told her not to.\n\nThe storm had blown itself out in the night and left the sky scoured and pale. She sat at her table with a heel of yesterday's bread and slid the blade of her knife under the wax, carefully, so that it lifted in one piece and she could have pressed it back if she had been a different sort of person. Inside was a single sheet, folded twice, in a secretary's hand. At the bottom, a signature she had seen on a hundred harbour warrants: Halvard Seyle, Reeve of Varrow.\n\nThe letter was to Fenn.\n\n_Magister —_\n\n_The sixty from the Kestrel will go with the rest. The Black Mare lies off the Chapel on the spring tide and will take all of it then; there is no other night. Ormund leaves the capital on the twelfth and will be with you within the week, so see that the books are clean before she comes. I need not tell you what a clean book means. The levy is paid in full this year, as every year. You have my word on the Pans._\n\n_— H.S._\n\nShe read it four times. The fourth time she read it aloud, very quietly, to hear whether it said something else when it was spoken. It did not.\n\n_The sixty from the Kestrel._ So it was not six sticks dropped in the harbour. _With the rest._ So it was not only the Kestrel. She thought of all the books on the third floor, the forty years of them on their shelves under the eaves, and of how many times she had written a number into a column without counting the sticks, because the sticks had been counted by somebody else, because the Magister read the margins.\n\n_You have my word on the Pans._ That one she did not understand at all.\n\nShe folded the letter again and looked at the seal, and then she did a thing she was not proud of: she warmed the knife over the lamp and pressed the wax back down, so that from a little way off it looked whole. Then she put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, where it crackled when she breathed, and she went to work.\n\nAurek Stiles was already at the long table. He looked up when she came in and said, \"You look like a woman who's slept on a bag of nails,\" and she said, \"I found the sticks,\" and he said, \"Behind the stove?\" and she said, \"Behind the stove,\" and took them out of her satchel and put them in the bin, all thirty-four.\n\nIt was the first lie she had told at the Salt House. It went down easier than she had expected, and that frightened her more than the letter did.\n\nAt midday she went down to the Magister's room on an errand about the Heron's book and stood in his doorway while he signed it. His hand did not shake. He asked after Tomas, and whether the Patience had come through the storm, and he told her she was the best clerk he had had in thirty years, which he had told her before. She watched him sign and thought: _see that the books are clean._\n\nShe had always believed that she could tell when someone was lying to her. She went back up the stairs knowing that she had been wrong about that, and did not know for how long.","wordCount":599,"createdAt":1794216603723},{"id":"XPOTwlOO13ZX3FGQrN5xN","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"2i5J2Lc7W9L8liiqtAFCG","text":"The Salt House kept its ledgers on the third floor, under the eaves, where the damp could not reach them and the clerks could not reach anything else. Ilse Varga had worked there for four years and had never once been warm.\n\nShe sat at the long table with the Kestrel's book open in front of her and the Kestrel's tally-sticks laid out beside it in a row, like the bones of a small animal. Every sack that went over the side of a salt-ship at Varrow was notched on a stick by the pan-reeve and notched again by the ship's mate, and the stick was split down its length so that each man kept half. When the halves came back to the Salt House they were matched, and counted, and the count went into the book. Nobody could cut a notch out of a stick without the other half showing it. It was the oldest honest thing in the city.\n\nShe counted the sticks again. Thirty-four sticks, ten notches to a stick. Three hundred and forty measures.\n\nThe book said four hundred.\n\n\"You're frowning at it,\" said Aurek Stiles from the far end of the table. He was the senior of the three tallyclerks and had been for twenty years, and he said it without looking up from his own work. \"Frowning at a ledger is how the ink gets into your face.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel,\" Ilse said. \"Her book says four hundred measures out on the ninth. I've got three hundred and forty on the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then you've dropped six sticks behind the stove.\"\n\n\"I haven't.\"\n\n\"Then the mate's dropped them in the harbour. It happens.\" Aurek turned a page. \"Write it as received and put a query in the margin. The Magister reads the margins.\"\n\nShe did not write it as received. She sat with the pen over the page until the ink beaded on the nib and fell, a small black star on the blotter, and then she got up and went down the narrow stair to the Magister's room.\n\nMagister Oduya Fenn had run the Salt House since before Ilse was born. He was a tall man gone thin in the way of tall men who forget to eat, and his room was the only warm one in the building, because he had the only good stove. He glanced up when she knocked and smiled at her with real pleasure, which made what she had come to say harder.\n\n\"Ilse. Sit. You look like you've been out on the Pans.\"\n\n\"Only on the third floor, Magister.\" She did not sit. \"The Kestrel's book is sixty measures over her sticks.\"\n\nThe smile stayed exactly where it was. That was the first thing she would remember afterwards — not that it went, but that it stayed, a little too long, like a man holding a door that nobody was coming through.\n\n\"Sixty,\" he said.\n\n\"Six sticks. I've counted three times.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel came in through the Gullet in a rising sea. Her mate was sick over the side twice before they'd cleared the bar — your brother will tell you, he took her in.\" Fenn spread his long hands. \"Sticks go missing. The book is written from the manifest at the pans, and the manifest is right.\"\n\n\"Then the sticks should be with the manifest.\"\n\n\"Then they should.\" He said it kindly. \"Write the book as it stands, Ilse. I'll speak to the pan-reeve myself.\"\n\nShe went back up the stairs. Aurek did not ask her what had been said, and she did not tell him. She wrote four hundred in the column in her own clean hand, and in the margin she wrote nothing at all, because the Magister read the margins.\n\nWhen the bell went for the end of the day she put the thirty-four sticks into her satchel instead of into the bin. She did not decide to do it. Her hands did it while she was thinking about something else, and by the time she noticed, she was already on the stair.","wordCount":678,"createdAt":1794216599958},{"id":"XtNv0ol97nsdBo58FVyh7","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"3z3uj6msTp-yJfszkLU-n","text":"She went to work. She did not know what else to do with herself, and it seemed important, in a way she could not have explained, that she should be at her place at the long table when the bell went, in her clerk's grey, with her hair pinned, as if nothing had happened. As if she were still the best clerk the Magister had had in thirty years.\n\nAurek glanced up when she came in and opened his mouth to say something about bags of nails, and then saw her face and shut it again.\n\nThe auditor was not there. Her sheet of tiny figures lay on the end of the table under a glass paperweight, and the green ledger was gone from beside it.\n\n\"She's downstairs,\" Aurek said quietly. \"With him. Since first bell.\" He hesitated. \"Ilse. I took the sticks out of the bin.\"\n\nShe turned.\n\n\"The Kestrel's. The night you put them in. I'm not a fool, girl, whatever you think. Nobody finds six sticks behind a stove and then finds them gone again.\" He did not look at her; he looked at his own ledger, at his own tidy upright hand. \"I've been here twenty years. I've written what I was told for three of them. I didn't count. I didn't ask. I told myself the Magister read the margins.\" He turned a page that did not need turning. \"They're in my coat. In the press. If you want them.\"\n\nShe stood very still.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\n\"Because you didn't write anything in the margin,\" said Aurek Stiles. \"You always write in the margin. You've written in the margin every day for four years. And that day you didn't, and I thought: she's frightened. And I thought: I've been frightened for three years and I never once noticed it was the same thing.\"\n\nAt midday the auditor came up the stair and took her sheet of figures from under the paperweight and went down again without a word to either of them. A little after that, the porter came up to say that the Magister wanted Miss Varga in his room.\n\nThe stove was lit. It was always lit. Oduya Fenn sat behind his desk with the green ledger closed in front of him and his long hands folded on top of it, and he did not smile when she came in. That was how she knew he had not slept either.\n\n\"Sit down, Ilse.\"\n\nShe did not sit down.\n\n\"I was at the Chapel last night,\" she said. \"In the tower. With Tomas, who has a broken leg, and has been lying in a window for two days while you loaded salt underneath him.\"\n\nThe Magister closed his eyes.\n\n\"I heard you tell the Reeve my name.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"I imagine you did.\" He opened his eyes again and looked at her, and his face was older than she had ever seen it. \"Will you let me tell you how it happened? Not to excuse it. I don't think it can be excused. But I'd like someone to know, and I think you've earned it more than anyone.\"\n\nShe sat down.\n\nHe told her. The tide in Thawmonth that took the wall and the pans and the pan-reeve's wife. The letter to the capital, and the letter back, _a matter for the harbour._ A whole year of the pan-men sitting on the quay, and the price of salt going up at Hesk, and the Salt House half empty, and the Exchequer writing every quarter to ask why the revenue was down. And then Halvard Seyle in this room, in that chair, with a bottle of something good, saying: _I can build you a wall, Oduya. All it needs is a little salt that nobody counts._\n\n\"One part in seven,\" the Magister said. \"For one year. To pay for the wall. That was all it was going to be. I told myself it was the Exchequer's own salt paying for the Exchequer's own wall, and if the Exchequer had done its duty there'd have been no need. I told myself a great many things.\" He looked down at his hands. \"The wall was built in a year. And at the end of the year I went to Halvard and said, that's enough, and he said — he said, Oduya, you've been writing false books for a year. Do you want to explain that to the Exchequer, or shall I?\"\n\n\"So you kept writing.\"\n\n\"So I kept writing. And the wall is bad stone, and Halvard has a new house, and the Black Mare comes in on the spring tides and takes one-seventh of the salt of Varrow to wherever it goes.\" He lifted his hands, let them fall. \"And you counted the Kestrel's sticks. I knew you would, one day. You're the only one of them who ever counted.\"\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Did you take it?\"\n\nHis face did not change. \"What letter?\"\n\n\"The Reeve's letter to you. About the Kestrel. I had it.\"\n\nFor a moment the Magister of the Salt House looked at her with an expression she could not read at all, and then, very slowly, he began to laugh. It was not a happy sound.\n\n\"He never sent it,\" he said. \"I never had it. I wondered — I wondered why he was so certain I'd been told about the Black Mare coming early, when I hadn't. He said, _I wrote to you._ And I said, _you didn't._ And we each thought the other was lying.\" He wiped his eyes. \"Oh, Ilse. Somebody stole the Reeve's post. And now somebody has stolen it from you.\"","wordCount":946,"createdAt":1794216650460},{"id":"XtdJoJX-iRdwCzzc164uD","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Brenn had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. That was what the forty crowns in his coat pocket were for.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter in his hand saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"She's early.\"\n\n\"How early?\"\n\n\"A night. A whole night.\" He sat up. \"The letter said the spring tide. That's tomorrow.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his clever face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern, because he had taken her in through the Gullet on the ninth.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.","wordCount":713,"createdAt":1794213828884},{"id":"Y5UIV_It99Ila7VTVrJZa","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"QkH1pzVCb9awQuyhPF6LA","text":"They had been seen. Tomas never knew how — a turn of the Patience's sail against the sky, perhaps, or the creak of her blocks across still water, or simply that a man on the Chapel steps had looked up at the wrong moment. But the light at the Chapel went out all at once, and in the dark there was a shout, and then the sound of oars.\n\n\"Go,\" said Corin. \"Go, go —\"\n\nTomas went. He put the helm over and got the sail up in one long heave and the Patience came round, slow, so slow, broad-beamed old cow that she was, and the flood was against them now, pushing them back up toward the Chapel. Behind them the oars were coming on. Four oars at least. A longboat, light and fast, and the men in her knew the channel nearly as well as he did.\n\n\"They'll have us before the Heron Creek,\" Corin said.\n\n\"They won't.\"\n\n\"They will. I can count, Tomas, it's the only thing I'm good for besides lying.\" Corin was pulling off his boots. \"There's a cut on the left — the one with the dead tree. Where does it go?\"\n\n\"Nowhere. Mud. It's a dead end.\"\n\n\"Can a man stand in it?\"\n\n\"At this tide? To his chest, maybe. What are you —\"\n\n\"They want the boat. They'll follow the boat.\" Corin was on the gunwale now, crouched, his clever face white in the starlight. \"And they want me more than they want you, because I'm the one they've been paying. So when I go over, you keep going, and you make a great deal of noise, and I'll make rather less.\" He grinned suddenly. \"Tell your sister to keep the letter. Tell her it's worth more than I am.\"\n\n\"Corin —\"\n\nHe went over the side without a splash. Tomas never heard him hit the water. One moment there was a man on the gunwale and the next there was the dark and the dead tree going past on the left and nothing else.\n\nHe kept going. He did not know what else to do. He made, as instructed, a great deal of noise — he let the mainsheet run so the block screamed, and he banged the tiller against the thwart, and he shouted Corin's name once, loudly, toward the right-hand bank, as though looking for a man who had fallen in there. Behind him the oars checked, and a voice said something sharp, and the longboat split its attention for a moment between the boat and the bank.\n\nIt was enough to get him into the Heron Creek. It was not enough to get him out of it.\n\nThe Heron was narrow and shallow and he had taken it a thousand times at the top of the tide, but this was not the top of the tide and he was not looking where he was going. The Patience hit the bar at the creek's mouth with a long grinding shudder that went up through the keel and into his teeth, and stopped, and heeled, and he was thrown against the thwart so hard that he heard something in his leg go with a sound like a green stick breaking.\n\nHe lay in the bilge-water and did not scream, because the longboat was still out there.\n\nAfter a time — he did not know how long — he heard the oars go past the mouth of the creek, slow now, searching. A lantern swung. A man's voice said, \"Nothing. She's gone up the Heron and stuck, she'll be there in the morning, we'll have her then,\" and another voice, one he thought he knew, said, \"And the other one?\" and the first voice said, \"Drowned, if he's got any sense.\" Then the oars again, going away, toward the Chapel and the black ship and the salt.\n\nHe waited until he could not hear them. Then he dragged himself up onto the thwart, and looked at his leg, and looked away again quickly. The tide was still making. In an hour the Patience would float off the bar. In an hour he would have to be somewhere else.\n\nThe tower of the Drowned Chapel was a quarter mile off across the marsh, black against the first grey in the east. It was the only dry place for a mile. It was also where the men in the longboat were going.\n\nTomas looked at it for a long time. Then he found the boat-hook, and broke the shaft across his knee to make a splint, and began to bind his leg with the sail-ties, and tried to think of something else.","wordCount":779,"createdAt":1794216609939},{"id":"Z7Utv-3XW6Zyw0ux0aR5K","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"GIXhkOMgrF_FKcO5VyMzd","text":"The auditor left Varrow on the twelfth day of Seedmonth, in the same carriage that had come down Tallow Street and frightened the porter, and the whole of Tallow Street came out to see her go.\n\nShe had stayed three weeks longer than she meant to. She had sat in the Magister's room with the firebrick out of the stove and the Magister's true book open on the desk — a small black book, scorched at the corners, with three years of real numbers in it in a long thin careful hand — and she had copied every page. Then she had sat in the third-floor room with Aurek and Ilse for ten days more and put the real numbers into the real books, page by page, in the margins, in red.\n\n\"The Exchequer will want the Magister in the capital,\" she told Ilse, the last evening, standing at the door of the Salt House with her gloves in her hand. \"There'll be a trial. I'll speak for him. I'll say he kept a true book when he could have kept none, and gave it up when he could have burned it, and that's not nothing.\" She pulled on one glove. \"I don't know what they'll do. Something less than they might have, I hope.\"\n\n\"And the Salt House?\"\n\n\"Will need a Magister.\" Lisbet Ormund pulled on the other glove. \"I've written to say so. I've written to say who.\" She looked up at Ilse with her kindly hen's face. \"Master Stiles has been here twenty years and is a very good clerk, and he told me himself that he would rather go into the sea after the Reeve than have the stove. So I've written your name.\"\n\nIlse opened her mouth and shut it again.\n\n\"You'll say you're too young,\" said the auditor. \"And that you lied to me. You did. You also told me you'd lied, in front of the whole town, which I have never once in thirty years of this work seen anyone do. Your mother would have.\" She held out her small gloved hand, and Ilse took it. \"Count everything, Miss Varga. Write in the margins. Good night.\"\n\nThe carriage went down Tallow Street in the morning with the carters cursing it, and Ilse watched it from the third-floor window until it was gone.\n\nTomas came down the Gull Stair that afternoon on two crutches, which took him nearly an hour and which he would not let anyone help him with, and sat on the bench outside the pilots' shed at the bottom with his splinted leg stuck out in front of him and his unlit pipe in his teeth. Corin Ashe sat beside him. Neither of them said much. Corin had a pilot's oilskin on, which was too big for him, and a pilot's book in his pocket, which Tomas had made him buy, and he was learning the Gullet from the bench, channel by channel, with Tomas drawing it in the dust with the end of a crutch.\n\n\"He'll make a pilot,\" Tomas said to Ilse, when she came down to them at the end of the day. \"Gods know how. He gets everything wrong and then argues with the sea about it.\"\n\n\"I'm very persuasive,\" said Corin.\n\n\"The sea isn't.\"\n\nWren was sitting on the bottom step of the Gull Stair eating a cold potato. Mother Gannet was gutting eels. The salt-ships lay over in the mud on the ebb, and the evening light came down the harbour the colour of honey, and somewhere up on the hill a bell was ringing for the end of the day.\n\nIlse climbed back up to the Salt House as the light went. The third floor was empty; Aurek had gone home. She lit the lamp and sat down at the long table, at her own place, and opened the Kestrel's book at the ninth.\n\nFour hundred, in her own clean hand. And beside it, in the margin, in red, in the auditor's tiny writing: _340. Sticks counted. I.V._\n\nShe dipped her pen, and turned to a clean page, and began to count.","wordCount":688,"createdAt":1794217080376},{"id":"Z9Gkc029NKBFgXKbWSy5_","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"LQxthMqMx2ix2u3pYQu7A","text":"The hearing was called for the hour after noon on the day of the spring tide, in the long hall at the top of the town where the Reeve of Varrow had sat in judgement on fishing disputes and drunk sailors for as long as there had been a Reeve. Lisbet Ormund had called it. She had the right; she had shown the clerk of the Hall a paper with the Exchequer's seal on it, and the clerk had gone white and sent for the Reeve, and the Reeve had come, and smiled, and said that of course the auditor must have whatever she needed.\n\nHe sat now in his own chair at the head of the hall, in the harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs. The Magister sat below him on the bench where witnesses sat, very straight, his long hands on his knees. Half the lower town had come up the hill to stand at the back. Ilse could see Mother Gannet's milky eye in the crowd, and Wren's dirty face beside her, and a great many people she had passed on the Gull Stair every day of her life and never spoken to. The old people on the quay had been saying since dawn that there was weather coming in with the tide.\n\nThe auditor stood at a small table in the middle of the floor with her papers in front of her.\n\n\"I'll be brief,\" she said, \"because the tide turns at four and I'm told half of you have boats to see to. I have audited the books of the Salt House of Varrow for the last four years. I find that for the last three of them, one-seventh part of the salt shipped from the Pans has not reached Hesk, and that the books have been written to hide it. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. Miss Varga.\"\n\nIlse stood up.\n\nShe had thought her legs would shake. They did not. She walked out to the table in the middle of the floor in her clerk's grey with her hair pinned and laid the things down on it one by one, the way she would have laid out a ship's sticks to be counted.\n\nThe Kestrel's book, open at the ninth.\n\nThirty-four tally-sticks, which Aurek Stiles had brought up the hill in his coat.\n\nA letter with a red seal pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\n\"On the ninth,\" she said, \"the Kestrel came in through the Gullet with three hundred and forty measures of salt on her sticks. I wrote four hundred in her book, because I was told to. That was a lie, and it was mine.\" She heard the crowd at the back shift and murmur. She did not look at them. \"Two days after I wrote it, the auditor asked me if I'd ever been told to write a number I knew was wrong, and I said no. That was a lie too.\"\n\n\"Miss Varga,\" said the Reeve, pleasantly, from his chair. \"You'll understand that a clerk who admits to two lies in one breath is not —\"\n\n\"And this,\" said Ilse, \"is a letter under your seal, Reeve, to the Magister, telling him where the sixty measures from the Kestrel were to go, and when, and on what ship. It never reached him. A man called Corin Ashe took it out of your post-bag, and gave it to me, and I've kept it.\" She looked up at him. \"You can read it, if you like. Or the auditor can read it aloud. It's quite short.\"\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\nHalvard Seyle looked at the letter on the table, and at the auditor, and at the Magister on the witnesses' bench, who had not moved. Then he looked past them all to the back of the hall, where Corin Ashe was standing against the wall with his arms folded, in a clean coat, and smiled.\n\n\"A smuggler,\" the Reeve said. \"A smuggler steals a letter, and a clerk who lies admits she lies, and an old man —\"\n\n\"I'll speak to the letter,\" said Oduya Fenn.\n\nHe stood up from the bench. He was not looking at the Reeve; he was looking at Ilse, and his face was quite calm.\n\n\"I never received it,\" he said. \"But I know what it says, because I know what every letter Halvard Seyle has written me for three years has said. I'll tell the auditor about every one of them. I'll tell her about the wall. I have kept a book of my own, Madam Auditor — a true one. I kept it because I am a clerk, and I could not bear not to. It is in my room at the Salt House, behind the loose firebrick in the chimney-breast, where the stove's heat never reaches, and it has every measure in it that I ever wrote false.\"\n\nSomebody in the crowd said, very loudly, \"Gods,\" and somebody else laughed, high and shocked.\n\nThe Reeve was on his feet.\n\n\"This hearing is closed,\" he said. \"I am the Reeve of Varrow and this is my hall, and I'll not have —\"\n\n\"It isn't closed, Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund mildly. \"It's the Exchequer's hearing. It's only being held in your hall.\" She picked up the letter. \"Sit down, please. I'm going to read this aloud.\"\n\nHe did not sit down. Something went out of his big red face all at once, like air out of a bladder. Then he turned and walked out through the door behind his chair, the one that only the Reeve used, and nobody stopped him, because nobody in Varrow had ever stopped the Reeve doing anything.","wordCount":957,"createdAt":1794217076260},{"id":"Zb4cJgtM7-VD0rfAz072o","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"kACrUDbXsjGSKBXJOWAnj","text":"Corin Ashe came back on the night before the spring tide, the way he had come the first time: a knock after midnight, a voice on the landing, a request not to be hit with whatever she was holding.\n\nThis time she opened the door all the way.\n\nHe looked terrible. He had a cut along his jaw that someone had stitched badly, and his clothes were not his own, and he was thinner than a man ought to get in four days. But he was smiling, and when he saw her face the smile went crooked and then went away altogether.\n\n\"Tomas,\" he said. \"Is he —\"\n\n\"He's alive. His leg's broken. He's at the Pans with a man called Hobb.\" She stood aside. \"Come in. You're letting the cold in.\"\n\nHe came in and stood in the middle of her small room and saw the bolster, which she had sewn up again, badly, and at the feathers that were still in the corners because she had not been able to bring herself to sweep them.\n\n\"I'm sorry about that,\" he said.\n\nShe had known. She had known since the Magister laughed. But she said, \"You,\" anyway, because it needed saying.\n\n\"Me.\" He reached inside his borrowed coat and took out a packet wrapped in oilcloth and held it out to her, exactly as he had on the first night. \"Everybody hides things in the bolster, Ilse. I've been in forty rooms in this city. It's always the bolster or the chimney, and you don't have a chimney.\"\n\nShe did not take it. \"Why?\"\n\n\"Because Seyle's men were coming up Tallow Street with a key the cooper's wife had been paid for, and I got there first. Because I knew where they'd look.\" He was still holding it out. \"And because I thought — when I came up out of that creek, half drowned, with the Reeve's men going past me close enough to touch — I thought, I could sell this. I could sell this back to Halvard Seyle for a great deal of money and go to Hesk and never come back. And I had it in my hand on the Hesk road for a whole day before I turned round.\"\n\n\"Why did you turn round?\"\n\nHe thought about it. She watched him think about it, and was surprised to find that she believed him when he answered.\n\n\"Because you didn't hit me with the poker,\" he said. \"The first night. You could have. You had it right there behind the door, I saw the end of it. And you opened the door a hand's width instead, and took a stranger's letter, and kept it.\" He shrugged. \"Nobody's ever done that. Usually they hit me.\"\n\nShe took the letter. The seal was still pressed down where she had warmed it with the knife. He had not opened it again.\n\n\"The auditor's at the Anchor,\" she said. \"On Rope Walk.\"\n\n\"I know where the Anchor is.\"\n\n\"Will you come with me?\"\n\nCorin Ashe was quiet for a long moment, the long clever face in the lamplight, the scar through his eyebrow.\n\n\"I'm a smuggler,\" he said. \"And a liar. Nobody will believe a word I say.\"\n\n\"They'll believe the sticks,\" said Ilse. \"Aurek has the sticks. And they'll believe the letter. And they'll believe me, because I'm going to tell them I lied, and nobody says that unless it's true.\" She put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, and reached for her coat. \"You only have to stand there.\"","wordCount":593,"createdAt":1794217070060},{"id":"Zf9vct2eJjjsqT_x1Ea7j","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Brenn had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. That was what the forty crowns in his coat pocket were for.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter under his seal saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"She's early.\"\n\n\"How early?\"\n\n\"A night. A whole night.\" He sat up. \"The letter said the spring tide. That's tomorrow.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern: he had brought the Kestrel in through the Gullet on the ninth with that same boat towing astern.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.","wordCount":718,"createdAt":1794217039177},{"id":"_dDz2gdtXXhVZ-cpCPSko","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"JLebbu3pA-RXZKvs35ANa","text":"Lisbet Ormund had taken the best room at the Anchor, which was not saying a great deal, and had spent the first evening moving the furniture so that the table caught the light from the window and the bed did not catch the draught from the door. She was at the table now, with her shoes off and her stockinged feet on a hot brick wrapped in flannel, writing her report.\n\nShe had written it three times. Each time it said the same thing — _one part in seven, three years, the Salt House books written to conceal it_ — and each time it ended in the same place, which was _I have no proof of who._\n\nWhen the landlord knocked to say the Reeve was below and wished to call on her, she said, \"Yes, of course, send him up,\" and put her shoes back on, and turned her report face down on the table, and put the hot brick under the bed.\n\nHalvard Seyle filled the doorway. He was a big man and knew it, and he had come in his harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs, and he bowed to her with great courtesy and said he hoped she was comfortable, and that Varrow was not too rough for a lady from the capital, and that if there was anything at all she needed she had only to send to the Reeve's Hall.\n\n\"How kind,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Do sit down.\"\n\nHe sat. The chair complained.\n\n\"I hear you've been at the Salt House books,\" he said.\n\n\"I have.\"\n\n\"Poor Fenn. He's not the man he was. The flood hit him very hard — he took it personally, you know, as though the sea had done it to spite him. I've had to carry a good deal of the work since.\" He smiled. \"If you've found anything amiss, I'd hope you'd come to me first. Before the capital. It would be a shame to ruin an old man's name over a few clerical errors that the harbour could put right quietly.\"\n\n\"A few clerical errors,\" the auditor repeated.\n\n\"Sticks go missing,\" said the Reeve. \"It happens all the time.\"\n\nLisbet Ormund regarded him for a long moment. Then she turned her report over on the table and pushed it across to him.\n\n\"One part in seven,\" she said. \"Every month for three years. That is not a few clerical errors, Reeve. That is twenty-two thousand crowns of the Exchequer's revenue, near enough, and it is going somewhere, and it is not going to Hesk.\" She folded her hands. \"I'll tell you what I have, since you've been good enough to call. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. I have a tallyclerk who lied to my face and was frightened to death doing it, and a Magister who hasn't slept in three nights. What I don't have is a name.\" She smiled at him. \"I don't suppose you'd care to give me one?\"\n\nThe Reeve did not smile back.\n\n\"You've been in Varrow four days, madam,\" he said. \"You don't know the tides. People who don't know the tides get into trouble out on the marsh. Every year we lose one or two. Visitors, mostly.\" He stood up. \"I'd be careful.\"\n\n\"I always am,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Good night, Reeve.\"\n\nWhen he had gone she sat for a while without moving. Then she got the hot brick back out from under the bed, which had gone lukewarm, and put her feet on it, and turned her report face down again, and wrote at the bottom of a fresh sheet, in her tiny neat hand: _Seyle. Certain. No proof._","wordCount":617,"createdAt":1794216651636},{"id":"aXuQg0w2X0zk6qMnKBy3J","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"g2q6xv5SNSaq9VyyZ2uux","text":"Her room was over the cooper's, at the top of a house so narrow that the stair went up through the middle of it like the stone in a plum. She lit the lamp and put the sticks on the table and sat with them for a while, and then she put them inside the hollow of the bolster, which was the only hiding place she had, and felt foolish.\n\nThe rain beat on the slates. Somewhere below her the cooper's wife was singing to the baby, the same three lines over and over.\n\nIt was close to midnight when someone knocked.\n\nShe did not open the door. She stood behind it with the lamp in one hand and the poker in the other and said, \"Who is it?\"\n\n\"A friend of your brother's,\" said a man's voice. It was light and pleasant and not from Varrow — there was something of the capital in the vowels, or something trying to sound like it. \"Though I doubt he'd put it that way. Please don't hit me with whatever you're holding.\"\n\n\"What do you want?\"\n\n\"To give you something. You can open the door a hand's width and I'll put it through, and then I'll go away, and you'll never have to look at me.\" A pause. \"Though I'm told I'm not bad to look at.\"\n\nShe opened the door a hand's width. The man on the landing was perhaps thirty, soaked through, his dark hair plastered to his skull. He had a long, clever face and a scar through one eyebrow and he was smiling as if this were all a great joke that she would be let in on presently.\n\nHe held out a packet wrapped in oilcloth.\n\n\"What is it?\"\n\n\"A letter. Not to you. I think you'd better have it anyway.\" He kept holding it out. \"My name's Corin Ashe. Your brother knows me. He'll tell you I'm a liar and a smuggler, and he'll be right about the second one.\"\n\n\"Why would you give me a letter that isn't to me?\"\n\n\"Because I can read, Miss Varga, and having read it I'd rather it were in the hands of someone who counts sticks than in mine.\" He pushed the packet through the gap. She took it, mostly to stop him dropping it. It was heavier than a letter ought to be. \"There's a seal on it you'll recognise. Don't break it here. Don't break it at all, if you've any sense. Just — keep it somewhere.\"\n\n\"How do you know about the sticks?\"\n\nBut he had already turned. She heard him going down the stair, light and quick, missing the step that creaked as if he knew it was there. The street door opened and shut.\n\nShe closed her own door and bolted it and set the lamp down and turned the packet over in her hands. Under the oilcloth was good paper, folded small, and a seal of red wax pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\nIt was the seal of the Harbour Reeve.\n\nIlse sat on the bed with the letter in her lap and the sticks in the bolster under her and listened to the rain until the lamp burned down.","wordCount":539,"createdAt":1794217028895},{"id":"b39aWsGkNR0t9sfJgonFL","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Varga had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. There were forty crowns in his coat pocket. He had not come for them. He had come because he had gone to find Corin himself, the night before, to ask what a man like that knew about six missing sticks, and Corin had been quiet a while and then said: more than your sister does. Come out on the ebb tomorrow and I'll show you.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe. And I'm three hundred crowns short of a berth on anything going south, which is the kind of thing that makes a man sell what he's holding.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter under his seal saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"Right on her night.\"\n\n\"You knew she'd be here.\"\n\n\"I carry the Reeve's post, Tomas. Letters, mostly, and I've a habit of reading them. I knew she'd be here because I carried the letter that said so, and then I carried it somewhere else.\" He sat up. \"The first load tonight. The rest on the spring tide, three nights on.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern: he had brought the Kestrel in through the Gullet on the ninth with that same boat towing astern.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.","wordCount":838,"createdAt":1794230090368},{"id":"cLqZ3CP26lRh0ZNnTrt-v","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"QkH1pzVCb9awQuyhPF6LA","text":"They had been seen. Tomas never knew how — a turn of the Patience's sail against the sky, perhaps, or the creak of her blocks across still water, or simply that a man on the Chapel steps had looked up at the wrong moment. But the light at the Chapel went out all at once, and in the dark there was a shout, and then the sound of oars.\n\n\"Go,\" said Corin. \"Go, go —\"\n\nTomas went. He put the helm over and got the sail up in one long heave and the Patience came round, slow, so slow, broad-beamed old cow that she was, and the flood was against them now, pushing them back up toward the Chapel. Behind them the oars were coming on. Four oars at least. A longboat, light and fast, and the men in her knew the channel nearly as well as he did.\n\n\"They'll have us before the Heron Creek,\" Corin said.\n\n\"They won't.\"\n\n\"They will. I can count, Tomas, it's the only thing I'm good for besides lying.\" Corin was pulling off his boots. \"There's a cut on the left — the one with the dead tree. Where does it go?\"\n\n\"Nowhere. Mud. It's a dead end.\"\n\n\"Can a man stand in it?\"\n\n\"At this tide? To his chest, maybe. What are you —\"\n\n\"They want the boat. They'll follow the boat.\" Corin was on the gunwale now, crouched, his clever face white in the starlight. \"And they want me more than they want you, because I'm the one they've been paying. So when I go over, you keep going, and you make a great deal of noise, and I'll make rather less.\" He grinned suddenly. \"Tell your sister to keep the letter. Tell her it's worth more than I am.\"\n\n\"Corin —\"\n\nHe went over the side without a splash. Tomas never heard him hit the water. One moment there was a man on the gunwale and the next there was the dark and the dead tree going past on the left and nothing else.\n\nHe kept going. He did not know what else to do. He made, as instructed, a great deal of noise — he let the mainsheet run so the block screamed, and he banged the tiller against the thwart, and he shouted Corin's name once, loudly, toward the right-hand bank, as though looking for a man who had fallen in there. Behind him the oars checked, and a voice said something sharp, and the longboat split its attention for a moment between the boat and the bank.\n\nIt was enough to get him into the Heron Creek. It was not enough to get him out of it.\n\nThe Heron was narrow and shallow and he had taken it a thousand times at the top of the tide, but this was not the top of the tide and he was not looking where he was going. The Patience hit the bar at the creek's mouth with a long grinding shudder that went up through the keel and into his teeth, and stopped, and heeled, and he was thrown against the thwart so hard that he heard something in his leg go with a sound like a green stick breaking.\n\nHe lay in the bilge-water and did not scream, because the longboat was still out there.\n\nAfter a time — he did not know how long — he heard the oars go past the mouth of the creek, slow now, searching. A lantern swung. A man's voice said, \"Nothing. She's gone up the Heron and stuck, she'll be there in the morning, we'll have her then,\" and another voice, one he thought he knew, said, \"And the other one?\" and the first voice said, \"Drowned, if he's got any sense.\" Then the oars again, going away, toward the Chapel and the black ship and the salt.\n\nHe waited until he could not hear them. Then he dragged himself up onto the thwart, and looked at his leg, and looked away again quickly. The tide was still making. In an hour the Patience would float off the bar. In an hour he would have to be somewhere else.\n\nThe tower of the Drowned Chapel was a quarter mile off across the marsh, black against the first grey in the east. It was the only dry place for a mile. It was also where the men in the longboat were going.\n\nTomas looked at it for a long time. Then he found the boat-hook, and broke the shaft across his knee to make a splint, and began to bind his leg with the sail-ties, and tried to think of something else.","wordCount":779,"createdAt":1794213918781},{"id":"dNcA6h7pJRzxtGXxjA3D-","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"LQxthMqMx2ix2u3pYQu7A","text":"The hearing was called for the hour after noon on the day of the spring tide, in the long hall at the top of the town where the Reeve of Varrow had sat in judgement on fishing disputes and drunk sailors for as long as there had been a Reeve. Lisbet Ormund had called it. She had the right; she had shown the clerk of the Hall a paper with the Exchequer's seal on it, and the clerk had gone white and sent for the Reeve, and the Reeve had come, and smiled, and said that of course the auditor must have whatever she needed.\n\nHe sat now in his own chair at the head of the hall, in the harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs. The Magister sat below him on the bench where witnesses sat, very straight, his long hands on his knees. Half the lower town had come up the hill to stand at the back. Ilse could see Mother Gannet's milky eye in the crowd, and Wren's dirty face beside her, and a great many people she had passed on the Gull Stair every day of her life and never spoken to. The old people on the quay had been saying since dawn that there was weather coming in with the tide.\n\nThe auditor stood at a small table in the middle of the floor with her papers in front of her.\n\n\"I'll be brief,\" she said, \"because the tide turns at four and I'm told half of you have boats to see to. I have audited the books of the Salt House of Varrow for the last four years. I find that for the last three of them, one-seventh part of the salt shipped from the Pans has not reached Hesk, and that the books have been written to hide it. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. Miss Varga.\"\n\nIlse stood up.\n\nShe had thought her legs would shake. They did not. She walked out to the table in the middle of the floor in her clerk's grey with her hair pinned and laid the things down on it one by one, the way she would have laid out a ship's sticks to be counted.\n\nThe Kestrel's book, open at the ninth.\n\nThirty-four tally-sticks, which Aurek Stiles had brought up the hill in his coat.\n\nA letter with a red seal pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\n\"On the ninth,\" she said, \"the Kestrel came in through the Gullet with three hundred and forty measures of salt on her sticks. I wrote four hundred in her book, because I was told to. That was a lie, and it was mine.\" She heard the crowd at the back shift and murmur. She did not look at them. \"Two days after I wrote it, the auditor asked me if I'd ever been told to write a number I knew was wrong, and I said no. That was a lie too.\"\n\n\"Miss Varga,\" said the Reeve, pleasantly, from his chair. \"You'll understand that a clerk who admits to two lies in one breath is not —\"\n\n\"And this,\" said Ilse, \"is a letter under your seal, Reeve, to the Magister, telling him where the sixty measures from the Kestrel were to go, and when, and on what ship. It never reached him. A man called Corin Ashe took it out of your post-bag, and gave it to me, and I've kept it.\" She looked up at him. \"You can read it, if you like. Or the auditor can read it aloud. It's quite short.\"\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\nHalvard Seyle looked at the letter on the table, and at the auditor, and at the Magister on the witnesses' bench, who had not moved. Then he looked past them all to the back of the hall, where Corin Ashe was standing against the wall with his arms folded, in a clean coat, and smiled.\n\n\"A smuggler,\" the Reeve said. \"A smuggler steals a letter, and a clerk who lies admits she lies, and an old man —\"\n\n\"I'll speak to the letter,\" said Oduya Fenn.\n\nHe stood up from the bench. He was not looking at the Reeve; he was looking at Ilse, and his face was quite calm.\n\n\"I never received it,\" he said. \"But I know what it says, because I know what every letter Halvard Seyle has written me for three years has said. I'll tell the auditor about every one of them. I'll tell her about the wall. I have kept a book of my own, Madam Auditor — a true one. I kept it because I am a clerk, and I could not bear not to. It is in the stove in my room at the Salt House, behind the firebrick, and it has every measure in it that I ever wrote false.\"\n\nSomebody in the crowd said, very loudly, \"Gods,\" and somebody else laughed, high and shocked.\n\nThe Reeve was on his feet.\n\n\"This hearing is closed,\" he said. \"I am the Reeve of Varrow and this is my hall, and I'll not have —\"\n\n\"It isn't closed, Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund mildly. \"It's the Exchequer's hearing. It's only being held in your hall.\" She picked up the letter. \"Sit down, please. I'm going to read this aloud.\"\n\nHe did not sit down. Something went out of his big red face all at once, like air out of a bladder. Then he turned and walked out through the door behind his chair, the one that only the Reeve used, and nobody stopped him, because nobody in Varrow had ever stopped the Reeve doing anything.","wordCount":950,"createdAt":1794217074192},{"id":"dX0uiaHQ03PHMmuQ0JiSb","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"JLebbu3pA-RXZKvs35ANa","text":"Lisbet Ormund had taken the best room at the Anchor, which was not saying a great deal, and had spent the first evening moving the furniture so that the table caught the light from the window and the bed did not catch the draught from the door. She was at the table now, with her shoes off and her stockinged feet on a hot brick wrapped in flannel, writing her report.\n\nShe had written it three times. Each time it said the same thing — _one part in seven, three years, the Salt House books written to conceal it_ — and each time it ended in the same place, which was _I have no proof of who._\n\nWhen the landlord knocked to say the Reeve was below and wished to call on her, she said, \"Yes, of course, send him up,\" and put her shoes back on, and turned her report face down on the table, and put the hot brick under the bed.\n\nHalvard Seyle filled the doorway. He was a big man and knew it, and he had come in his harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs, and he bowed to her with great courtesy and said he hoped she was comfortable, and that Varrow was not too rough for a lady from the capital, and that if there was anything at all she needed she had only to send to the Reeve's Hall.\n\n\"How kind,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Do sit down.\"\n\nHe sat. The chair complained.\n\n\"I hear you've been at the Salt House books,\" he said.\n\n\"I have.\"\n\n\"Poor Fenn. He's not the man he was. The flood hit him very hard — he took it personally, you know, as though the sea had done it to spite him. I've had to carry a good deal of the work since.\" He smiled. \"If you've found anything amiss, I'd hope you'd come to me first. Before the capital. It would be a shame to ruin an old man's name over a few clerical errors that the harbour could put right quietly.\"\n\n\"A few clerical errors,\" the auditor repeated.\n\n\"Sticks go missing,\" said the Reeve. \"It happens all the time.\"\n\nLisbet Ormund regarded him for a long moment. Then she turned her report over on the table and pushed it across to him.\n\n\"One part in seven,\" she said. \"Every month for three years. That is not a few clerical errors, Reeve. That is a hundred and forty thousand crowns of the Exchequer's revenue, near enough, and it is going somewhere, and it is not going to Hesk.\" She folded her hands. \"I'll tell you what I have, since you've been good enough to call. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. I have a tallyclerk who lied to my face and was frightened to death doing it, and a Magister who hasn't slept in three nights. What I don't have is a name.\" She smiled at him. \"I don't suppose you'd care to give me one?\"\n\nThe Reeve did not smile back.\n\n\"You've been in Varrow three days, madam,\" he said. \"You don't know the tides. People who don't know the tides get into trouble out on the marsh. Every year we lose one or two. Visitors, mostly.\" He stood up. \"I'd be careful.\"\n\n\"I always am,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Good night, Reeve.\"\n\nWhen he had gone she sat for a while without moving. Then she got the hot brick back out from under the bed — it had gone lukewarm — and put her feet on it, and turned her report face down again, and wrote at the bottom of a fresh sheet, in her tiny neat hand: _Seyle. Certain. No proof._","wordCount":622,"createdAt":1794217067991},{"id":"e-W-monL0tMkevO4UTnSE","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"r9SBgyYpFJBk2b1o5XkXt","text":"They brought Tomas out ahead of the ebb, the two of them, Ilse under one arm and Wren under the other, and it took four hours, and Ilse did not afterward remember much of it except the cold and the weight and her brother's breath hissing in her ear at every step. Wren found them the hard every time.At the edge of the marsh, where the path came up onto the sea-wall by the Pans, they left Tomas with a pan-man Wren knew, who asked no questions and sent his own son running for a bone-setter, and then Ilse walked home alone through the waking streets with the sun coming up behind her.\n\nThe Magister had said her name. She kept hearing it. Not _the clerk_, not _one of my girls_. _The Varga girl_, in that tired voice she had loved for four years, like a man putting down something heavy.\n\nShe climbed the stair through the middle of the cooper's house. The step that creaked, creaked. The cooper's wife was not singing.\n\nHer door was open.\n\nNot broken. Open, the way you leave a door when you have a key and are in a hurry. Inside, the room looked almost as she had left it — the table, the chair, the lamp-hook — except that everything had been moved a little and put back a little wrong, the way a stranger puts things back. The chair faced the wrong wall. Her mother's comb was on the left of the shelf instead of the right.\n\nShe went back down the stair. The cooper's wife was in the kitchen with the baby on her hip and the door half shut, and when Ilse knocked on it she opened it no wider.\n\n\"Was anybody up?\" Ilse said. \"In the night. To my room.\"\n\n\"I was asleep.\" The cooper's wife did not look at her. She looked at the baby, who was chewing on a wooden spoon. \"We were all asleep. The cooper's been at the Pans all week, you know that, there's nobody here but me.\"\n\n\"My door was open.\"\n\n\"Then you left it open. You were out all night, weren't you? Out in the marsh, by the look of your boots.\" The woman's eyes went to the boots and came away quickly. \"I don't ask where my lodgers go, Miss Varga. I'd thank you not to ask me where I sleep.\"\n\n\"Somebody had a key.\"\n\n\"Then somebody had a key.\" The cooper's wife began to close the door. \"There's three keys to that room and one of them's been lost since Midwinter. Ask the cooper. When he's home.\"\n\nThe door shut. Behind it the baby began to cry, and the woman began to sing to it, the same three lines over and over, too loud.\n\nIlse stood in the passage for a while. Then she went back up.\n\nHer room was cold. Someone had let the lamp burn down to nothing and the wick had charred, and there was a smell in the air that was not hers — wet wool, and tar, and something sharper, like cloves.\n\nShe stood in the middle of the floor with her hands at her sides and tried to think the way her mother would have thought. Who had known she was going into the marsh. Who had known she would not be home. Wren had known. Mother Gannet had known. Anyone who had seen her go down Tallow Street at dusk in her mother's boots had known, and half the lower town had.\n\nIt did not narrow anything. It only made the room feel larger, and full of people.\n\nThe bolster lay on the bed with its seam cut open from end to end.\n\nShe did not need to look. She looked anyway. She put her hand into the hollow and felt the feathers and the ticking and nothing else, and then she sat down on the bed with the feathers settling around her like snow, and for the first time since the Kestrel's sticks she let herself cry.\n\nShe had not told anyone where she kept it. Not Tomas. Not Wren. Not the auditor, who she had lied to.\n\nShe had told no one, and someone had known.","wordCount":705,"createdAt":1794216612484},{"id":"en7iFsuqg1t5vJX2RO6St","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"FBthgk0AwfIy4svSwzmYI","text":"The marsh at low water was a different country. The channels that the Patience had sailed were trickles now, running between banks of mud as high as Ilse's head, and the mud shone in the moonlight like the skin of something sleeping. Wren went ahead of her along a path she could not see — the hard, he called it, a spine of shingle under the mud that the old salt-carriers had used before there were boats — and she followed him step for step and did not look down.\n\nIt took them two hours to reach the Chapel. The tower stood up out of the mud on its little island of stone, the arches of the old nave around its foot half-buried, the high windows empty. At high water the sea came in through those windows. Now, with the tide out, there were steps going down into the dark of the crypt, slick with weed, and a smell of salt and old candles.\n\n\"Here's where they load,\" Wren whispered. He pointed. Along the stone of the steps there were fresh scars, white in the moonlight, where something heavy had been dragged. \"Salt. Sacks of it. They bring it up the channel from the Pans at night and keep it up in the tower, above the tide-line, till the ship comes.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\nWren shrugged inside his coat. \"Everybody knows. Nobody says.\"\n\nThey found Tomas in the tower.\n\nHe had dragged himself up the first turn of the stair and wedged himself into a window-niche, and he was so still and so grey that Ilse thought for a moment that he was dead. Then his eyes opened and he said, quite clearly, \"You weren't supposed to come,\" and she knelt on the stair beside him and held his hand and could not say anything at all.\n\nHis leg was splinted with half a boat-hook. He had done it well. He had come across the marsh on his back, ahead of the flood, pushing with his good leg and the stump of the boat-hook, and twice the water had closed over his face. He had been sitting in the niche, he said, for a day and a night, while the Reeve's men came and went below him, loading the black ship from the crypt. He had heard everything. He had not been able to move.\n\n\"There was a voice in the longboat,\" he said. \"The first night. I thought I knew it. I didn't want to.\"\n\n\"Corin,\" Ilse said. \"Where's Corin?\"\n\n\"Went over the side. Took them off me.\" Tomas closed his eyes. \"Don't know if he got out. He said to tell you to keep the letter.\"\n\n\"I have it.\"\n\n\"Good. Ilse — listen. The ship's gone. The Black Mare. She went out on this morning's tide with the first load aboard, and she'll be back for the rest.\" His hand tightened on hers. \"And it's not just the Reeve. I heard them. They were talking about the Salt House. About the books. About someone who's going to fix the books before the auditor —\"\n\nThere was a sound below them. Wren, who had been keeping watch at the foot of the stair, came up it like a cat, silent, his eyes huge.\n\n\"Lantern,\" he breathed. \"Two men. On the hard.\"\n\nThey did not move. Ilse put her hand over Tomas's mouth, not because he would have made a sound but because she needed to hold something. Below them, in the ruined nave, a lantern came and went among the arches, and two men came with it, and stood at the head of the crypt steps where the white scars were.\n\nOne of them was big and wore a harbour officer's coat. She had seen the Reeve of Varrow at a hundred ceremonies on the quay; she knew his shoulders, his walk, the way he stood with his weight back on his heels.\n\nThe other was tall and thin, and held his lantern with a long hand that she had watched sign a thousand pages.\n\n\"She's asking for the flood year,\" the Magister was saying. His voice echoed in the stone. \"She's had it on the table for two days. She has copies of the Hesk ledgers, Halvard. Copies. She knew before she came.\"\n\n\"Then she knows nothing she can prove.\" The Reeve's voice was easy, almost bored. \"Hesk ledgers. A clerk in Hesk can't count. Everyone knows that.\"\n\n\"She'll ask for the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then give her the sticks. You told me the sticks were sorted.\"\n\nThe Magister was quiet. The lantern shook a little.\n\n\"One of my clerks,\" he said at last, \"found the Kestrel short. Before I could see to it.\"\n\n\"Which clerk?\"\n\nIlse felt Tomas's hand tighten on hers until the bones ground.\n\n\"It doesn't matter. I dealt with it. She wrote it in as I told her.\"\n\n\"Oduya.\" The Reeve sounded almost gentle. \"Everything matters. That's how we've lasted three years. Which clerk?\"\n\nThere was a long silence in the nave. Ilse could hear the water beginning to come back, very far off, a sound like breathing.\n\n\"The Varga girl,\" the Magister said.","wordCount":859,"createdAt":1794230223768},{"id":"enMHwVHVQz4fzr_2ywt_l","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"51sz_3kuWZsw6JR4Y0Ko2","text":"The ledger for the year of the big tide was bound in green, because the binder had run out of brown that winter, and it stood out on the shelf like a wrong note. Lisbet Ormund had it open on the long table when Ilse came up the stair, and beside it the book for the year before, bound in brown, and beside that a sheet of her own paper covered in tiny, neat figures.\n\nAurek was not at his place. The auditor saw her looking.\n\n\"I've sent Master Stiles to the pan-reeve with a list of questions,\" she said. \"It'll take him most of the day, I'm afraid, because the pan-reeve won't know the answers and will have to send to the Reeve's office for them. Sit down, Miss Varga. Sit there, where I can see you.\"\n\nIlse sat.\n\n\"I want to show you something,\" said the auditor, \"and then I want to ask you something, and I'd like you to think before you answer the second thing, because I shall know if you don't.\" She turned the green ledger round. \"This is Thawmonth, the year of the tide. Here — the week of the flood. Nothing comes in at all, which is what you'd expect. Here, the month after. Very little. Here, the month after that. And here,\" she turned a page, \"is the first month that the books are short against the Hesk price.\"\n\nIlse looked. It was a column of numbers in Aurek's hand, tidy and upright, and beside each a ship's name and a date. There was nothing wrong with any of them.\n\n\"They're not short,\" she said.\n\n\"Not in the book. In the book they're very well. But I have here,\" the auditor tapped her own sheet, \"every cargo of Varrow salt that was sold at Hesk that month, from the Hesk customs ledgers, which I had copied before I left the capital. And the Hesk ledgers say that Varrow sent them a good deal less salt than Varrow's books say it sent. About one part in seven less.\" She sat back. \"One part in seven. Every month since. Three years.\"\n\nOne part in seven. Sixty measures out of four hundred was a little more than that. Ilse kept her face still and felt the paper crackle under her bodice when she breathed.\n\n\"Somebody,\" said Lisbet Ormund, \"is selling one-seventh of the salt of Varrow somewhere other than Hesk, and the books of the Salt House are written as though they weren't. Now. Here's my question.\" She leaned forward. \"I don't think you're a thief, Miss Varga. I knew your mother. I don't think the Magister is a thief either, not in his heart, though I've been wrong about men's hearts before. But somebody in this building writes the numbers, and somebody tells them which numbers to write. Has anybody ever told you to write a number you knew was wrong?\"\n\nShe thought about the Kestrel's book. Four hundred in her own clean hand.\n\nShe thought about Tomas, who had not come home.\n\nShe thought: _if I say yes, she'll ask who. And if I say who, they'll know it was me. And Tomas is somewhere out in the marsh, and I don't know where, and I don't know who else knows._\n\n\"No, madam,\" she said.\n\nThe auditor looked at her for a long moment. Then she nodded, slowly, and turned the green book back around, and picked up her pen.\n\n\"I'll be here until the spring tide,\" she said, without looking up. \"After that I go back to the capital and write my report with whatever I have. If you think of anything before then, Miss Varga, I'd be very grateful to hear it. I'm at the Anchor, on Rope Walk. Anyone can tell you where it is.\"\n\nIlse went back to her own place and opened the Heron's book and could not see a single number in it.","wordCount":656,"createdAt":1794216608780},{"id":"fILQ8nouxnN5HwvLEIEdb","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"r9SBgyYpFJBk2b1o5XkXt","text":"They brought Tomas out ahead of the flood, the two of them, Ilse under one arm and Wren under the other, and it took four hours, and Ilse did not afterward remember much of it except the cold and the weight and her brother's breath hissing in her ear at every step. Wren found them the hard every time.At the edge of the marsh, where the path came up onto the sea-wall by the Pans, they left Tomas with a pan-man Wren knew, who asked no questions and sent his own son running for a bone-setter, and then Ilse walked home alone through the waking streets with the sun coming up behind her.\n\nThe Magister had said her name. She kept hearing it. Not _the clerk_, not _one of my girls_. _The Varga girl_, in that tired voice she had loved for four years, like a man putting down something heavy.\n\nShe climbed the stair through the middle of the cooper's house. The step that creaked, creaked. The cooper's wife was not singing.\n\nHer door was open.\n\nNot broken. Open, the way you leave a door when you have a key and are in a hurry. Inside, the room looked almost as she had left it — the table, the chair, the lamp-hook — except that everything had been moved a little and put back a little wrong, the way a stranger puts things back. The chair faced the wrong wall. Her mother's comb was on the left of the shelf instead of the right.\n\nShe went back down the stair. The cooper's wife was in the kitchen with the baby on her hip and the door half shut, and when Ilse knocked on it she opened it no wider.\n\n\"Was anybody up?\" Ilse said. \"In the night. To my room.\"\n\n\"I was asleep.\" The cooper's wife did not look at her. She looked at the baby, who was chewing on a wooden spoon. \"We were all asleep. The cooper's been at the Pans all week, you know that, there's nobody here but me.\"\n\n\"My door was open.\"\n\n\"Then you left it open. You were out all night, weren't you? Out in the marsh, by the look of your boots.\" The woman's eyes went to the boots and came away quickly. \"I don't ask where my lodgers go, Miss Varga. I'd thank you not to ask me where I sleep.\"\n\n\"Somebody had a key.\"\n\n\"Then somebody had a key.\" The cooper's wife began to close the door. \"There's three keys to that room and one of them's been lost since Midwinter. Ask the cooper. When he's home.\"\n\nThe door shut. Behind it the baby began to cry, and the woman began to sing to it, the same three lines over and over, too loud.\n\nIlse stood in the passage for a while. Then she went back up.\n\nHer room was cold. Someone had let the lamp burn down to nothing and the wick had charred, and there was a smell in the air that was not hers — wet wool, and tar, and something sharper, like cloves.\n\nShe stood in the middle of the floor with her hands at her sides and tried to think the way her mother would have thought. Who had known she was going into the marsh. Who had known she would not be home. Wren had known. Mother Gannet had known. Anyone who had seen her go down Tallow Street at dusk in her mother's boots had known, and half the lower town had.\n\nIt did not narrow anything. It only made the room feel larger, and full of people.\n\nThe bolster lay on the bed with its seam cut open from end to end.\n\nShe did not need to look. She looked anyway. She put her hand into the hollow and felt the feathers and the ticking and nothing else, and then she sat down on the bed with the feathers settling around her like snow, and for the first time since the Kestrel's sticks she let herself cry.\n\nShe had not told anyone where she kept it. Not Tomas. Not Wren. Not the auditor, who she had lied to.\n\nShe had told no one, and someone had known.","wordCount":705,"createdAt":1794216612505},{"id":"fJ4jVN_qy2n7buz1BNKJM","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Varga had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. There were forty crowns in his coat pocket. He had not come for them. He had come because the night before he had gone to Corin himself, the night before, to ask what a man like that knew about six missing sticks, and Corin had been quiet a while and then said: more than your sister does. Come out on the ebb tomorrow and I'll show you.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe. And I'm three hundred crowns short of a berth on anything going south, which is the kind of thing that makes a man sell what he's holding.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter under his seal saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"Right on her night.\"\n\n\"You knew she'd be here.\"\n\n\"I carry the Reeve's post, Tomas. Letters, mostly, and I've a habit of reading them. I knew she'd be here because I carried the letter that said so, and then I carried it somewhere else.\" He sat up. \"The first load tonight. The rest on the spring tide, three nights on.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern: he had brought the Kestrel in through the Gullet on the ninth with that same boat towing astern.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.","wordCount":840,"createdAt":1794230090491},{"id":"fMNm7dCAcKGCRikliutI8","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"g2q6xv5SNSaq9VyyZ2uux","text":"Her room was over the cooper's, at the top of a house so narrow that the stair went up through the middle of it like the stone in a plum. She lit the lamp and put the sticks on the table and looked at them for a while, and then she put them inside the hollow of the bolster, which was the only hiding place she had, and felt foolish.\n\nThe rain beat on the slates. Somewhere below her the cooper's wife was singing to the baby, the same three lines over and over.\n\nIt was close to midnight when someone knocked.\n\nShe did not open the door. She stood behind it with the lamp in one hand and the poker in the other and said, \"Who is it?\"\n\n\"A friend of your brother's,\" said a man's voice. It was light and pleasant and not from Varrow — there was something of the capital in the vowels, or something trying to sound like it. \"Though I doubt he'd put it that way. Please don't hit me with whatever you're holding.\"\n\n\"What do you want?\"\n\n\"To give you something. You can open the door a hand's width and I'll put it through, and then I'll go away, and you'll never have to look at me.\" A pause. \"Though I'm told I'm not bad to look at.\"\n\nShe opened the door a hand's width. The man on the landing was perhaps thirty, soaked through, his dark hair plastered to his skull. He had a long, clever face and a scar through one eyebrow and he was smiling as if this were all a great joke that she would be let in on presently.\n\nHe held out a packet wrapped in oilcloth.\n\n\"What is it?\"\n\n\"A letter. Not to you. I think you'd better have it anyway.\" He kept holding it out. \"My name's Corin Ashe. Your brother knows me. He'll tell you I'm a liar and a smuggler, and he'll be right about the second one.\"\n\n\"Why would you give me a letter that isn't to me?\"\n\n\"Because I can read, Miss Varga, and having read it I'd rather it were in the hands of someone who counts sticks than in mine.\" He pushed the packet through the gap. She took it, mostly to stop him dropping it. It was heavier than a letter ought to be. \"There's a seal on it you'll recognise. Don't break it here. Don't break it at all, if you've any sense. Just — keep it somewhere.\"\n\n\"How do you know about the sticks?\"\n\nBut he had already turned. She heard him going down the stair, light and quick, missing the step that creaked as if he knew it was there. The street door opened and shut.\n\nShe closed her own door and bolted it and set the lamp down and turned the packet over in her hands. Under the oilcloth was good paper, folded small, and a seal of red wax pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\nIt was the seal of the Harbour Reeve.\n\nIlse sat on the bed with the letter in her lap and the sticks in the bolster under her and listened to the rain until the lamp burned down.\n# Black Mare\n## The Reeve's Seal\n[\n  Place: #Ilse's Room\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Aurek Stiles @@Oduya Fenn\n  When: +1 day\n  Items: ^The Kestrel's tally-sticks:#The Salt House\n  Facts: ?The Reeve is skimming the salt:@Ilse Varga ?The Reeve is skimming the salt:reader\n]\n\nShe broke the seal in the morning, of course. Corin Ashe had known she would; that was why he had told her not to.\n\nThe storm had blown itself out in the night and left the sky scoured and pale. She sat at her table with a heel of yesterday's bread and slid the blade of her knife under the wax, carefully, so that it lifted in one piece and she could have pressed it back if she had been a different sort of person. Inside was a single sheet, folded twice, in a secretary's hand. At the bottom, a signature she had seen on a hundred harbour warrants. Halvard Seyle, Reeve of Varrow.\n\nThe letter was to Fenn.\n\n*Magister —*\n\n*The sixty from the Kestrel will go with the rest. The Black Mare lies off the Chapel on the spring tide and will take all of it then; there is no other night. Ormund leaves the capital on the twelfth and will be with you within the week, so see that the books are clean before she comes. I need not tell you what a clean book means. The levy is paid in full this year, as every year. You have my word on the Pans.*\n\n*— H.S.*\n\nShe read it four times. The fourth time she read it aloud, very quietly, to hear whether it said something else when it was spoken. It did not.\n\n*The sixty from the Kestrel.* So it was not six sticks dropped in the harbour. *With the rest.* So it was not only the Kestrel. She thought of all the books on the third floor, the forty years of them on their shelves under the eaves, and of how many times she had written a number into a column without counting the sticks, because the sticks had been counted by somebody else, because the Magister read the margins.\n\n*You have my word on the Pans.* That one she did not understand at all.\n\nShe folded the letter again and looked at the seal, and then she did a thing she was not proud of: she warmed the knife over the lamp and pressed the wax back down, so that from a little way off it looked whole. Then she put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, where it crackled when she breathed, and she went to work.\n\nAurek Stiles was already at the long table. He looked up when she came in and said, \"You look like a woman who's slept on a bag of nails,\" and she said, \"I found the sticks,\" and he said, \"Behind the stove?\" and she said, \"Behind the stove,\" and took them out of her satchel and put them in the bin, all thirty-four.\n\nIt was the first lie she had told at the Salt House. It went down easier than she had expected, and that frightened her more than the letter did.\n\nAt midday she went down to the Magister's room on an errand about the Heron's book and stood in his doorway while he signed it. His hand did not shake. He asked after Tomas, and whether the Patience had come through the storm, and he told her she was the best clerk he had had in thirty years, which he had told her before. She watched him sign and thought: *see that the books are clean.*\n\nShe had always believed that she could tell when someone was lying to her. She went back up the stairs knowing that she had been wrong about that, and did not know for how long.\n\n## The Auditor\n[\n  Place: #The Salt House\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Aurek Stiles @@Lisbet Ormund @@Mother Gannet\n  When: +2 days\n]\n\nThe auditor came two days early.\n\nIlse heard her before she saw her — a carriage on the cobbles of Tallow Street, which was a thing that did not happen; nobody drove a carriage into the lower town because the streets were too narrow and the carters too rude. Then voices in the yard, and the porter's voice going up high the way it did when he was frightened, and then a long silence, and then feet on the stair.\n\nLisbet Ormund was not what Ilse had expected. She had pictured someone like the Magister, tall and grey and sorrowful, or someone like the Reeve, red-faced and loud. The woman who came into the third-floor room was small and round and perhaps fifty, in a brown travelling coat with the mud of the coast road still on its hem, and she had a face like a kindly hen. She stood in the doorway and looked at the shelves of ledgers, all forty years of them, with an expression of pure delight.\n\n\"Oh,\" she said. \"Oh, look at that. You keep them up here? Under the roof?\"\n\n\"It's the driest room, madam,\" said Aurek, who had got to his feet so fast his stool had gone over.\n\n\"It's the hottest room in summer,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Paper doesn't like heat any more than it likes damp. Never mind. Never mind.\" She came in and took off her gloves. \"I'm the Exchequer's auditor. I expect you've been told I'd be along. I expect you were told the twentieth.\" She smiled at both of them. \"I find it saves a great deal of everyone's time to come on the eighteenth.\"\n\nIlse found that she had stood up too, and that her hand had gone flat against the front of her bodice, where the letter was. She took it away.\n\n\"Magister Fenn —\" Aurek began.\n\n\"Is downstairs, and has given me the run of the place, and very gracious he was about it. He's sending up tea. You'll be Master Stiles. And you are —?\"\n\n\"Ilse Varga, madam. Tallyclerk.\"\n\n\"Varga.\" The auditor looked at her a moment longer than was comfortable. \"There was a Varga at the Hesk customs house, years ago. A woman. Very good with figures.\"\n\n\"My mother, madam.\"\n\n\"Was she? Well.\" Lisbet Ormund turned back to the shelves. \"Then you'll know the difference between a book that's right and a book that's tidy, Miss Varga. Most people don't.\" She ran a finger along the spines, the newest first. \"I'll want this year's, and last year's, and — let me see — the year of the big tide. When was that? Three years back?\"\n\n\"Four, madam,\" said Aurek.\n\n\"Three,\" said Ilse, at the same moment.\n\nThey looked at each other. The auditor looked at both of them, pleasantly.\n\n\"The spring tide that took the Pans,\" Ilse said. \"It was three years in Thawmonth. I'd been here a year.\"\n\n\"Then I'll have that one,\" said Lisbet Ormund, \"and the one before it, so I can see the difference. And I'll have the tally-sticks for whatever's come in this month. All of them.\" She sat down at the end of the long table, in the place where nobody ever sat because the draught came under the door there, and folded her small hands. \"I'm told sticks go missing,\" she said. \"I'm told it happens all the time.\"\n\nNobody said anything. Down in the yard the porter was shouting for someone to bring up the tea.\n\nThat night Ilse did not go home over the cooper's. She went down the Gull Stair to the quay, and she did not find Tomas on his basket outside the pilots' shed, and when she asked old Mother Gannet, who sold eels at the foot of the stair and saw everything, the old woman only said that the Patience had gone out on the evening ebb, and that there'd been two men in her, and that one of them was a long clever one with a scar in his eyebrow.\n\n[\n  Place: #The Drowned Chapel\n  Characters: @@Tomas Brenn @@Corin Ashe\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?The Reeve is skimming the salt:@Tomas Brenn ?The Reeve is skimming the salt:@Corin Ashe:before\n]\n\nThe Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Brenn had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. That was what the forty crowns in his coat pocket were for.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter in his hand saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"She's early.\"\n\n\"How early?\"\n\n\"A night. A whole night.\" He sat up. \"The letter said the spring tide. That's tomorrow.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his clever face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern, because he had taken her in through the Gullet on the ninth.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.","wordCount":2614,"createdAt":1794213785653},{"id":"fXWQKhzpXkDGE2gsmMcDL","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"FBthgk0AwfIy4svSwzmYI","text":"The marsh at low water was a different country. The channels that the Patience had sailed were trickles now, running between banks of mud as high as Ilse's head, and the mud shone in the moonlight like the skin of something sleeping. Wren went ahead of her along a path she could not see — the hard, he called it, a spine of shingle under the mud that the old salt-carriers had used before there were boats — and she followed him step for step and did not look down.\n\nIt took them two hours to reach the Chapel. The tower stood up out of the mud on its little island of stone, the arches of the old nave around its foot half-buried, the high windows empty. At high water the sea came in through those windows. Now, with the tide out, there were steps going down into the dark of the crypt, slick with weed, and a smell of salt and old candles.\n\n\"Here's where they load,\" Wren whispered. He pointed. Along the stone of the steps there were fresh scars, white in the moonlight, where something heavy had been dragged. \"Salt. Sacks of it. They bring it up the channel from the Pans at night and keep it up in the tower, above the tide-line, till the ship comes.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\nWren shrugged inside his coat. \"Everybody knows. Nobody says.\"\n\nThey found Tomas in the tower.\n\nHe had dragged himself up the first turn of the stair and wedged himself into a window-niche, and he was so still and so grey that Ilse thought for a moment that he was dead. Then his eyes opened and he said, quite clearly, \"You weren't supposed to come,\" and she knelt on the stair beside him and held his hand and could not say anything at all.\n\nHis leg was splinted with half a boat-hook. He had done it well. He had been sitting in the niche, he said, for a day and a night, while the Reeve's men came and went below him, loading the black ship from the crypt. He had heard everything. He had not been able to move. He had come across the marsh on his back, ahead of the flood, pushing with his good leg and the stump of the boat-hook, and twice the water had closed over his face.\n\n\"There was a voice in the longboat,\" he said. \"The first night. I thought I knew it. I didn't want to.\"\n\n\"Corin,\" Ilse said. \"Where's Corin?\"\n\n\"Went over the side. Took them off me.\" Tomas closed his eyes. \"Don't know if he got out. He said to tell you to keep the letter.\"\n\n\"I have it.\"\n\n\"Good. Ilse — listen. The ship's gone. The Black Mare. She went out on this morning's tide with all of it aboard, and she'll be back for the rest.\" His hand tightened on hers. \"And it's not just the Reeve. I heard them. They were talking about the Salt House. About the books. About someone who's going to fix the books before the auditor —\"\n\nThere was a sound below them. Wren, who had been keeping watch at the foot of the stair, came up it like a cat, silent, his eyes huge.\n\n\"Lantern,\" he breathed. \"Two men. On the hard.\"\n\nThey did not move. Ilse put her hand over Tomas's mouth, not because he would have made a sound but because she needed to hold something. Below them, in the ruined nave, a lantern came and went among the arches, and two men came with it, and stood at the head of the crypt steps where the white scars were.\n\nOne of them was big and wore a harbour officer's coat. She had seen the Reeve of Varrow at a hundred ceremonies on the quay; she knew his shoulders, his walk, the way he stood with his weight back on his heels.\n\nThe other was tall and thin, and held his lantern with a long hand that she had watched sign a thousand pages.\n\n\"She's asking for the flood year,\" the Magister was saying. His voice echoed in the stone. \"She's had it on the table for two days. She has copies of the Hesk ledgers, Halvard. Copies. She knew before she came.\"\n\n\"Then she knows nothing she can prove.\" The Reeve's voice was easy, almost bored. \"Hesk ledgers. A clerk in Hesk can't count. Everyone knows that.\"\n\n\"She'll ask for the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then give her the sticks. You told me the sticks were sorted.\"\n\nThe Magister was quiet. The lantern shook a little.\n\n\"One of my clerks,\" he said at last, \"found the Kestrel short. Before I could see to it.\"\n\n\"Which clerk?\"\n\nIlse felt Tomas's hand tighten on hers until the bones ground.\n\n\"It doesn't matter. I dealt with it. She wrote it in as I told her.\"\n\n\"Oduya.\" The Reeve sounded almost gentle. \"Everything matters. That's how we've lasted three years. Which clerk?\"\n\nThere was a long silence in the nave. Ilse could hear the water beginning to come back, very far off, a sound like breathing.\n\n\"The Varga girl,\" the Magister said.","wordCount":859,"createdAt":1794230069367},{"id":"gKnjXl9KVPKFH0BYYIx1x","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"LQxthMqMx2ix2u3pYQu7A","text":"The hearing was called for the hour after noon on the day of the spring tide, in the long hall at the top of the town where the Reeve of Varrow had sat in judgement on fishing disputes and drunk sailors for as long as there had been a Reeve. Lisbet Ormund had called it. She had the right; she had shown the clerk of the Hall a paper with the Exchequer's seal on it, and the clerk had gone white and sent for the Reeve, and the Reeve had come, and smiled, and said that of course the auditor must have whatever she needed.\n\nHe sat now in his own chair at the head of the hall, in the harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs. The Magister sat below him on the bench where witnesses sat, very straight, his long hands on his knees. Half the lower town had come up the hill to stand at the back. Ilse could see Mother Gannet's milky eye in the crowd, and Wren's dirty face beside her, and a great many people she had passed on the Gull Stair every day of her life and never spoken to. The old people on the quay had been saying since dawn that there was weather coming in with the tide.\n\nThe auditor stood at a small table in the middle of the floor with her papers in front of her.\n\n\"I'll be brief,\" she said, \"because the tide turns at four and I'm told half of you have boats to see to. I have audited the books of the Salt House of Varrow for the last four years. I find that for the last three of them, one-seventh part of the salt shipped from the Pans has not reached Hesk, and that the books have been written to hide it. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. Miss Varga.\"\n\nIlse stood up.\n\nShe had thought her legs would shake. They did not. She walked out to the table in the middle of the floor in her clerk's grey with her hair pinned and laid the things down on it one by one, the way she would have laid out a ship's sticks to be counted.\n\nThe Kestrel's book, open at the ninth.\n\nThirty-four tally-sticks, which Aurek Stiles had brought up the hill in his coat.\n\nA letter with a red seal pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\n\"On the ninth,\" she said, \"the Kestrel came in through the Gullet with three hundred and forty measures of salt on her sticks. I wrote four hundred in her book, because I was told to. That was a lie, and it was mine.\" She heard the crowd at the back shift and murmur. She did not look at them. \"Two days after I wrote it, the auditor asked me if I'd ever been told to write a number I knew was wrong, and I said no. That was a lie too.\"\n\n\"Miss Varga,\" said the Reeve, pleasantly, from his chair. \"You'll understand that a clerk who admits to two lies in one breath is not —\"\n\n\"And this,\" said Ilse, \"is a letter under your hand, Reeve, to the Magister, telling him where the sixty measures from the Kestrel were to go, and when, and on what ship. It never reached him. A man called Corin Ashe took it out of your post-bag, and gave it to me, and I've kept it.\" She looked up at him. \"You can read it, if you like. Or the auditor can read it aloud. It's quite short.\"\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\nHalvard Seyle looked at the letter on the table, and at the auditor, and at the Magister on the witnesses' bench, who had not moved. Then he looked past them all to the back of the hall, where Corin Ashe was standing against the wall with his arms folded, in a clean coat, and smiled.\n\n\"A smuggler,\" the Reeve said. \"A smuggler steals a letter, and a clerk who lies admits she lies, and an old man —\"\n\n\"I'll speak to the letter,\" said Oduya Fenn.\n\nHe stood up from the bench. He was not looking at the Reeve; he was looking at Ilse, and his face was quite calm.\n\n\"I never received it,\" he said. \"But I know what it says, because I know what every letter Halvard Seyle has written me for three years has said. I'll tell the auditor about every one of them. I'll tell her about the wall. I have kept a book of my own, Madam Auditor — a true one. I kept it because I am a clerk, and I could not bear not to. It is in the stove in my room at the Salt House, behind the firebrick, and it has every measure in it that I ever wrote false.\"\n\nSomebody in the crowd said, very loudly, \"Gods,\" and somebody else laughed, high and shocked.\n\nThe Reeve was on his feet.\n\n\"This hearing is closed,\" he said. \"I am the Reeve of Varrow and this is my hall, and I'll not have —\"\n\n\"It isn't closed, Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund mildly. \"It's the Exchequer's hearing. It's only being held in your hall.\" She picked up the letter. \"Sit down, please. I'm going to read this aloud.\"\n\nHe did not sit down. He looked at the auditor, and at the Magister, and at the crowd filling the back of the hall, and something went out of his big red face all at once, like air out of a bladder. Then he turned and walked out through the door behind his chair, the one that only the Reeve used, and nobody stopped him, because nobody in Varrow had ever stopped the Reeve doing anything.","wordCount":970,"createdAt":1794216654160},{"id":"gMKxlMncF4y6jwdTrIrNg","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"GIXhkOMgrF_FKcO5VyMzd","text":"The auditor left Varrow on the twelfth day of Seedmonth, in the same carriage that had come down Tallow Street and frightened the porter, and half the lower town came out to see her go.\n\nShe had stayed a fortnight longer than she meant to. She had sat in the Magister's room with the firebrick out of the stove and the Magister's true book open on the desk — a small black book, scorched at the corners, with three years of real numbers in it in a long thin careful hand — and she had copied every page. Then she had sat in the third-floor room with Aurek and Ilse for ten days more and put the real numbers into the real books, page by page, in the margins, in red.\n\n\"The Exchequer will want the Magister in the capital,\" she told Ilse, the last evening, standing at the door of the Salt House with her gloves in her hand. \"There'll be a trial. I'll speak for him. I'll say he kept a true book when he could have kept none, and gave it up when he could have burned it, and that's not nothing.\" She pulled on one glove. \"I don't know what they'll do. Something less than they might have, I hope.\"\n\n\"And the Salt House?\"\n\n\"Will need a Magister.\" Lisbet Ormund pulled on the other glove. \"I've written to say so. I've written to say who.\" She looked up at Ilse with her kindly hen's face. \"Master Stiles has been here twenty years and is a very good clerk, and he told me himself that he would rather go into the sea after the Reeve than have the stove. So I've written your name.\"\n\nIlse opened her mouth and shut it again.\n\n\"You'll say you're too young,\" said the auditor. \"And that you lied to me. You did. You also told me you'd lied, in front of the whole town, which I have never once in thirty years of this work seen anyone do. Your mother would have.\" She held out her small gloved hand, and Ilse took it. \"Count everything, Miss Varga. Write in the margins. Good night.\"\n\nThe carriage went down Tallow Street in the morning with the carters cursing it, and Ilse watched it from the third-floor window until it was gone.\n\nTomas came up the Gull Stair that afternoon on two crutches, which took him nearly an hour and which he would not let anyone help him with, and sat on the bench outside the pilots' shed at the bottom with his splinted leg stuck out in front of him and his unlit pipe in his teeth. Corin Ashe sat beside him. Neither of them said much. Corin had a pilot's oilskin on, which was too big for him, and a pilot's book in his pocket, which Tomas had made him buy, and he was learning the Gullet from the bench, channel by channel, with Tomas drawing it in the dust with the end of a crutch.\n\n\"He'll make a pilot,\" Tomas said to Ilse, when she came down to them at the end of the day. \"God knows how. He gets everything wrong and then argues with the sea about it.\"\n\n\"I'm very persuasive,\" said Corin.\n\n\"The sea isn't.\"\n\nWren was sitting on the bottom step of the Gull Stair eating a cold potato. Mother Gannet was gutting eels. The salt-ships lay over in the mud on the ebb, and the evening light came down the harbour the colour of honey, and somewhere up on the hill a bell was ringing for the end of the day.\n\nIlse climbed back up to the Salt House as the light went. The third floor was empty; Aurek had gone home. She lit the lamp and sat down at the long table, at her own place, and opened the Kestrel's book at the ninth.\n\nFour hundred, in her own clean hand. And beside it, in the margin, in red, in the auditor's tiny writing: _340. Sticks counted. I.V._\n\nShe dipped her pen, and turned to a clean page, and began to count.","wordCount":687,"createdAt":1794216656572},{"id":"gTBb8Bl18NuMPtXv3PM-m","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"FBthgk0AwfIy4svSwzmYI","text":"The marsh at low water was a different country. The channels that the Patience had sailed were trickles now, running between banks of mud as high as Ilse's head, and the mud shone in the moonlight like the skin of something sleeping. Wren went ahead of her along a path she could not see — the hard, he called it, a spine of shingle under the mud that the old salt-carriers had used before there were boats — and she followed him step for step and did not look down.\n\nIt took them two hours to reach the Chapel. The tower stood up out of the mud on its little island of stone, the arches of the old nave around its foot half-buried, the high windows empty. At high water the sea came in through those windows. Now, with the tide out, there were steps going down into the dark of the crypt, slick with weed, and a smell of salt and old candles.\n\n\"Here's where they load,\" Wren whispered. He pointed. Along the stone of the steps there were fresh scars, white in the moonlight, where something heavy had been dragged. \"Salt. Sacks of it. They bring it up the channel from the Pans at night and keep it up on the old gallery over the nave, above the tide-line, till the ship comes.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\nWren shrugged inside his coat. \"Everybody knows. Nobody says.\"\n\nThey found Tomas in the tower.\n\nHe had dragged himself up the first turn of the stair and wedged himself into a window-niche, and he was so still and so grey that Ilse thought for a moment that he was dead. Then his eyes opened and he said, quite clearly, \"You weren't supposed to come,\" and she knelt on the stair beside him and held his hand and could not say anything at all.\n\nHis leg was splinted with half a boat-hook. He had done it well. He had come across the marsh on his back, on the flood, with the tide still making, pushing with his good leg and the stump of the boat-hook, and twice the water had closed over his face. He had been sitting in the niche, he said, for a day and a night, while the Reeve's men came and went below him, loading the black ship from the crypt. He had heard everything. He had not been able to move.\n\n\"There was a voice in the longboat,\" he said. \"The first night. I thought I knew it. I didn't want to.\"\n\n\"Corin,\" Ilse said. \"Where's Corin?\"\n\n\"Went over the side. Took them off me.\" Tomas closed his eyes. \"Don't know if he got out. He said to tell you to keep the letter.\"\n\n\"I have it.\"\n\n\"Good. Ilse — listen. The ship's gone. The Black Mare. She went out on this morning's tide with the first load aboard, and she'll be back for the rest.\" His hand tightened on hers. \"And it's not just the Reeve. I heard them. They were talking about the Salt House. About the books. About someone who's going to fix the books before the auditor —\"\n\nThere was a sound below them. Wren, who had been keeping watch at the foot of the stair, came up it like a cat, silent, his eyes huge.\n\n\"Lantern,\" he breathed. \"Two men. On the hard.\"\n\nThey did not move. Ilse put her hand over Tomas's mouth, not because he would have made a sound but because she needed to hold something. Below them, in the ruined nave, a lantern came and went among the arches, and two men came with it, and stood at the head of the crypt steps where the white scars were.\n\nOne of them was big and wore a harbour officer's coat. She had seen the Reeve of Varrow at a hundred ceremonies on the quay; she knew his shoulders, his walk, the way he stood with his weight back on his heels.\n\nThe other was tall and thin, and held his lantern with a long hand that she had watched sign a thousand pages.\n\n\"She's asking for the flood year,\" the Magister was saying. His voice echoed in the stone. \"She's had it on the table for two days. She has copies of the Hesk ledgers, Halvard. Copies. She knew before she came.\"\n\n\"Then she knows nothing she can prove.\" The Reeve's voice was easy, almost bored. \"Hesk ledgers. A clerk in Hesk can't count. Everyone knows that.\"\n\n\"She'll ask for the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then give her the sticks. You told me the sticks were sorted.\"\n\nThe Magister was quiet. The lantern shook a little.\n\n\"One of my clerks,\" he said at last, \"found the Kestrel short. Before I could see to it.\"\n\n\"Which clerk?\"\n\nIlse felt Tomas's hand tighten on hers until the bones ground.\n\n\"It doesn't matter. I dealt with it. She wrote it in as I told her.\"\n\n\"Oduya.\" The Reeve sounded almost gentle. \"Everything matters. That's how we've lasted three years. Which clerk?\"\n\nThere was a long silence in the nave. Ilse could hear the water beginning to come back, very far off, a sound like breathing.\n\n\"The Varga girl,\" the Magister said.","wordCount":867,"createdAt":1794230227918},{"id":"gphZP8HKK51tTq9Y8O8s3","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Brenn had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. That was what the forty crowns in his coat pocket were for.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter under his seal saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"She's early.\"\n\n\"How early?\"\n\n\"A night. A whole night.\" He sat up. \"The letter said the spring tide. That's tomorrow.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his clever face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern, because he had taken her in through the Gullet on the ninth.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.","wordCount":713,"createdAt":1794216606190},{"id":"h63_J7k20pIB56cf5w3s7","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"2i5J2Lc7W9L8liiqtAFCG","text":"The Salt House kept its ledgers on the third floor, under the eaves, where the damp could not reach them and the clerks could not reach anything else. Ilse Varga had worked there for four years and had never once been warm.\n\nShe sat at the long table with the Kestrel's book open in front of her and the Kestrel's tally-sticks laid out beside it in a row, like the bones of a small animal. Every sack that went over the side of a salt-ship at Varrow was notched on a stick by the pan-reeve and notched again by the ship's mate, and the stick was split down its length so that each man kept half. When the halves came back to the Salt House they were matched, and counted, and the count went into the book. Nobody could cut a notch out of a stick without the other half showing it. It was the oldest honest thing in the city.\n\nShe counted the sticks again. Thirty-four sticks, ten notches to a stick. Three hundred and forty measures.\n\nThe book said four hundred.\n\n\"You're frowning at it,\" said Aurek Stiles from the far end of the table. He was the senior of the three tallyclerks and had been for twenty years, and he said it without looking up from his own work. \"Frowning at a ledger is how the ink gets into your face.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel,\" Ilse said. \"Her book says four hundred measures out on the ninth. I've got three hundred and forty on the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then you've dropped six sticks behind the stove.\"\n\n\"I haven't.\"\n\n\"Then the mate's dropped them in the harbour. It happens.\" Aurek turned a page. \"Write it as received and put a query in the margin. The Magister reads the margins.\"\n\nShe did not write it as received. She sat with the pen over the page until the ink beaded on the nib and fell, a small black star on the blotter, and then she got up and went down the narrow stair to the Magister's room.\n\nMagister Oduya Fenn had run the Salt House since before Ilse was born. He was a tall man gone thin in the way of tall men who forget to eat, and his room was the only warm one in the building, because he had the only good stove. He glanced up when she knocked and smiled at her with real pleasure, which made what she had come to say harder.\n\n\"Ilse. Sit. You look like you've been out on the Pans.\"\n\n\"Only on the third floor, Magister.\" She did not sit. \"The Kestrel's book is sixty measures over her sticks.\"\n\nThe smile stayed exactly where it was. That was the first thing she would remember afterwards — not that it went, but that it stayed, a little too long, like a man holding a door that nobody was coming through.\n\n\"Sixty,\" he said.\n\n\"Six sticks. I've counted three times.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel came in through the Gullet in a rising sea. Her mate was sick over the side twice before they'd cleared the bar — your brother will tell you; he took her in.\" Fenn spread his long hands. \"Sticks go missing. The book is written from the manifest at the pans, and the manifest is right.\"\n\n\"Then the sticks should be with the manifest.\"\n\n\"Then they should.\" He said it kindly. \"Write the book as it stands, Ilse. I'll speak to the pan-reeve myself.\"\n\nShe went back up the stairs. Aurek did not ask her what had been said, and she did not tell him. She wrote four hundred in the column in her own clean hand, and in the margin she wrote nothing at all, because the Magister read the margins.\n\nWhen the bell went for the end of the day she put the thirty-four sticks into her satchel instead of into the bin. She did not decide to do it. Her hands did it while she was thinking about something else, and by the time she noticed, she was already on the stair.","wordCount":678,"createdAt":1794217024748},{"id":"hWP-hU2_a-tUa7fSNrpum","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"6iI4LgbwDeFqR_Cruf7mE","text":"She broke the seal in the morning, of course. Corin Ashe had known she would; that was why he had told her not to.\n\nThe storm had blown itself out in the night and left the sky scoured and pale. She sat at her table with a heel of yesterday's bread and slid the blade of her knife under the wax, carefully, so that it lifted in one piece and she could have pressed it back if she had been a different sort of person. Inside was a single sheet, folded twice, in a secretary's hand. At the bottom, two initials she had seen on a hundred harbour warrants, and the seal under them: H.S. Halvard Seyle, Reeve of Varrow.\n\nThe letter was to Fenn.\n\n_Magister —_\n\n_The sixty from the Kestrel will go with the rest. The Black Mare lies off the Chapel on the night of the eighteenth and will take the first of it then, and the rest on the spring tide; there are no other nights. Ormund leaves the capital on the twelfth and will be with you within the week, so see that the books are clean before she comes. I need not tell you what a clean book means. The levy is paid in full this year, as every year. You have my word on the Pans._\n\n_— H.S._\n\nShe read it four times. The fourth time she read it aloud, quietly, to hear whether it said something else when it was spoken. It did not.\n\n_The sixty from the Kestrel._ So it was not six sticks dropped in the harbour. _With the rest._ So it was not only the Kestrel. She thought of all the books on the third floor, the forty years of them on their shelves under the eaves, and of how many times she had written a number into a column without counting the sticks, because the sticks had been counted by somebody else, because the Magister read the margins.\n\n_You have my word on the Pans._ That one she did not understand at all.\n\nShe folded the letter again, and then she did a thing she was not proud of: she warmed the knife over the lamp and pressed the wax back down, so that from a little way off it looked whole. Then she put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, where it crackled when she breathed, and she went to work.\n\nAurek Stiles was already at the long table. When she came in he said, \"You look like a woman who's slept on a bag of nails,\" and she said, \"I found the sticks,\" and he said, \"Behind the stove?\" and she said, \"Behind the stove,\" and took them out of her satchel and put them in the bin, all thirty-four.\n\nIt was the first lie she had told at the Salt House. It went down easier than she had expected, and that frightened her more than the letter did.\n\nAt midday she went down to the Magister's room on an errand about the Heron's book and stood in his doorway while he signed it. His hand did not shake. He asked after Tomas, and whether the Patience had come through the storm, and he told her she was the best clerk he had had in thirty years, which he had told her before. She watched him sign and thought: _see that the books are clean._\n\nShe had always believed that she could tell when someone was lying to her. She went back up the stairs knowing that she had been wrong about that, and did not know for how long.","wordCount":606,"createdAt":1794230088936},{"id":"i1fuw6OngnTjtr5Sqxmyt","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"3z3uj6msTp-yJfszkLU-n","text":"She went to work. She did not know what else to do with herself, and it seemed important, in a way she could not have explained, that she should be at her place at the long table when the bell went, in her clerk's grey, with her hair pinned, as if nothing had happened. As if she were still the best clerk the Magister had had in thirty years.\n\nAurek glanced up when she came in and opened his mouth to say something about bags of nails, and then saw her face properly and shut it again.\n\nThe auditor was not there. Her sheet of tiny figures lay on the end of the table under a glass paperweight, and the green ledger was gone from beside it.\n\n\"She's downstairs,\" Aurek said quietly. \"With him. Since first bell.\" He hesitated. \"Ilse. I took the sticks out of the bin.\"\n\nShe turned.\n\n\"The Kestrel's. The night you put them in. I'm not a fool, girl, whatever you think. Nobody finds six sticks behind a stove and then finds them gone again.\" He did not look at her; he looked at his own ledger, at his own tidy upright hand. \"I've been here twenty years. I've written what I was told for three of them. I didn't count. I didn't ask. I told myself the Magister read the margins.\" He turned a page that did not need turning. \"They're in my coat. In the press. If you want them.\"\n\nShe stood very still.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\n\"Because you didn't write anything in the margin,\" said Aurek Stiles. \"You always write in the margin. You've written in the margin every day for four years. And that day you didn't, and I thought: she's frightened. And I thought: I've been frightened for three years and I never once noticed it was the same thing.\"\n\nAt midday the auditor came up the stair and took her sheet of figures from under the paperweight and went down again without a word to either of them. A little after that, the porter came up to say that the Magister wanted Miss Varga in his room.\n\nThe stove was lit. It was always lit. Oduya Fenn sat behind his desk with the green ledger closed in front of him and his long hands folded on top of it, and he did not smile when she came in. That was how she knew he had not slept either.\n\n\"Sit down, Ilse.\"\n\nShe did not sit down.\n\n\"I was at the Chapel last night,\" she said. \"In the tower. With Tomas, who has a broken leg, and has been lying in a window for two days while you loaded salt underneath him.\"\n\nThe Magister closed his eyes.\n\n\"I heard you tell the Reeve my name.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"I imagine you did.\" He opened his eyes again and looked at her, and his face was older than she had ever seen it. \"Will you let me tell you how it happened? Not to excuse it. I don't think it can be excused. But I'd like someone to know, and I think you've earned it more than anyone.\"\n\nShe sat down.\n\nHe told her. The tide in Thawmonth that took the wall and the pans and the pan-reeve's wife. The letter to the capital, and the letter back, _a matter for the harbour._ A whole year of the pan-men sitting on the quay, and the price of salt going up at Hesk, and the Salt House half empty, and the Exchequer writing every quarter to ask why the revenue was down. And then Halvard Seyle in this room, in that chair, with a bottle of something good, saying: _I can build you a wall, Oduya. All it needs is a little salt that nobody counts._\n\n\"One part in seven,\" the Magister said. \"For one year. To pay for the wall. That was all it was going to be. I told myself it was the Exchequer's own salt paying for the Exchequer's own wall, and if the Exchequer had done its duty there'd have been no need. I told myself a great many things.\" He looked down at his hands. \"The wall was built in a year. And at the end of the year I went to Halvard and said, that's enough, and he said — he said, Oduya, you've been writing false books for a year. Do you want to explain that to the Exchequer, or shall I?\"\n\n\"So you kept writing.\"\n\n\"So I kept writing. And the wall is bad stone, and Halvard has a new house, and the Black Mare comes in on the spring tides and takes one-seventh of the salt of Varrow to wherever it goes.\" He lifted his hands, let them fall. \"And you counted the Kestrel's sticks. I knew you would, one day. You're the only one of them who ever counted.\"\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Did you take it?\"\n\nHis face did not change. \"What letter?\"\n\n\"The Reeve's letter to you. About the Kestrel. I had it.\"\n\nFor a moment the Magister of the Salt House looked at her with an expression she could not read at all, and then, very slowly, he began to laugh. It was not a happy sound.\n\n\"He never sent it,\" he said. \"I never had it. I wondered — I wondered why he was so certain I'd been told about the Black Mare coming early, when I hadn't. He said, _I wrote to you._ And I said, _you didn't._ And we each thought the other was lying.\" He wiped his eyes. \"Oh, Ilse. Somebody stole the Reeve's post. And now somebody has stolen it from you.\"","wordCount":947,"createdAt":1794216650408},{"id":"iBtzy8pmvGfNnknVtjAi-","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"r9SBgyYpFJBk2b1o5XkXt","text":"They brought Tomas out ahead of the flood, the two of them, Ilse under one arm and Wren under the other, and it took four hours, and Ilse did not afterwards remember much of it except the cold and the weight and her brother's breath hissing in her ear at every step. Wren found them the hard every time.At the edge of the marsh, where the path came up onto the sea-wall by the Pans, they left Tomas with a pan-man Wren knew, who asked no questions and sent his own son running for a bone-setter, and then Ilse walked home alone through the waking streets with the sun coming up behind her.\n\nThe Magister had said her name. She kept hearing it. Not _the clerk_, not _one of my girls_. _The Varga girl_, in that tired voice she had loved for four years, like a man putting down something heavy.\n\nShe climbed the stair through the middle of the cooper's house. The step that creaked, creaked. The cooper's wife was not singing.\n\nHer door was open.\n\nNot broken. Open, the way you leave a door when you have a key and are in a hurry. Inside, the room looked almost as she had left it — the table, the chair, the lamp-hook — except that everything had been moved a little and put back a little wrong, the way a stranger puts things back. The chair faced the wrong wall. Her mother's comb was on the left of the shelf instead of the right.\n\nShe went back down the stair. The cooper's wife was in the kitchen with the baby on her hip and the door half shut, and when Ilse knocked on it she opened it no wider.\n\n\"Was anybody up?\" Ilse said. \"In the night. To my room.\"\n\n\"I was asleep.\" The cooper's wife did not look at her. She looked at the baby, who was chewing on a wooden spoon. \"We were all asleep. The cooper's been at the Pans all week, you know that, there's nobody here but me.\"\n\n\"My door was open.\"\n\n\"Then you left it open. You were out all night, weren't you? Out in the marsh, by the look of your boots.\" The woman's eyes went to the boots and came away quickly. \"I don't ask where my lodgers go, Miss Varga. I'd thank you not to ask me where I sleep.\"\n\n\"Somebody had a key.\"\n\n\"Then somebody had a key.\" The cooper's wife began to close the door. \"There's three keys to that room and one of them's been lost since Midwinter. Ask the cooper. When he's home.\"\n\nThe door shut. Behind it the baby began to cry, and the woman began to sing to it, the same three lines over and over, too loud.\n\nIlse stood in the passage for a while. Then she went back up.\n\nHer room was cold. Someone had let the lamp burn down to nothing and the wick had charred, and there was a smell in the air that was not hers — wet wool, and tar, and something sharper, like cloves.\n\nShe stood in the middle of the floor with her hands at her sides and tried to think the way her mother would have thought. Who had known she was going into the marsh. Who had known she would not be home. Wren had known. Mother Gannet had known. Anyone who had seen her go down Tallow Street at dusk in her mother's boots had known, and half the lower town had.\n\nIt did not narrow anything. It only made the room feel larger, and full of people.\n\nThe bolster lay on the bed with its seam cut open from end to end.\n\nShe did not need to look. She looked anyway. She put her hand into the hollow and felt the feathers and the ticking and nothing else, and then she sat down on the bed with the feathers settling around her like snow, and for the first time since the Kestrel's sticks she let herself cry.\n\nShe had not told anyone where she kept it. Not Tomas. Not Wren. Not the auditor, who she had lied to.\n\nShe had told no one, and someone had known.","wordCount":705,"createdAt":1794216612539},{"id":"iIPZvKWB4QdKIoNofSyYz","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"2i5J2Lc7W9L8liiqtAFCG","text":"The Salt House kept its ledgers on the third floor, under the eaves, where the damp could not reach them and the clerks could not reach anything else. Ilse Varga had worked there for four years and had never once been warm.\n\nShe sat at the long table with the Kestrel's book open in front of her and the Kestrel's tally-sticks laid out beside it in a row, like the bones of a small animal. Every sack that went over the side of a salt-ship at Varrow was notched on a stick by the pan-reeve and notched again by the ship's mate, and the stick was split down its length so that each man kept half. When the halves came back to the Salt House they were matched, and counted, and the count went into the book. It was the oldest honest thing in the city. Nobody could cut a notch out of a stick without the other half showing it.\n\nShe counted the sticks again. Thirty-four sticks, ten notches to a stick. Three hundred and forty measures.\n\nThe book said four hundred.\n\n\"You're frowning at it,\" said Aurek Stiles from the far end of the table. He was the senior of the three tallyclerks and had been for twenty years, and he said it without looking up from his own work. \"Frowning at a ledger is how the ink gets into your face.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel,\" Ilse said. \"Her book says four hundred measures out on the ninth. I've got three hundred and forty on the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then you've dropped six sticks behind the stove.\"\n\n\"I haven't.\"\n\n\"Then the mate's dropped them in the harbour. It happens.\" Aurek turned a page. \"Write it as received and put a query in the margin. The Magister reads the margins.\"\n\nShe did not write it as received. She sat with the pen over the page until the ink beaded on the nib and fell, a small black star on the blotter, and then she got up and went down the narrow stair to the Magister's room.\n\nMagister Oduya Fenn had run the Salt House since before Ilse was born. He was a tall man gone thin in the way of tall men who forget to eat, and his room was the only warm one in the building, because he had the only good stove. He looked up when she knocked and smiled at her with real pleasure, which made what she had come to say harder.\n\n\"Ilse. Sit. You look like you've been out on the Pans.\"\n\n\"Only on the third floor, Magister.\" She did not sit. \"The Kestrel's book is sixty measures over her sticks.\"\n\nThe smile stayed exactly where it was. That was the first thing she would remember afterward — not that it went, but that it stayed, a little too long, like a man holding a door that nobody was coming through.\n\n\"Sixty,\" he said.\n\n\"Six sticks. I've counted three times.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel came in through the Gullet in a rising sea. Her mate was sick over the side twice before they'd cleared the bar — your brother will tell you, he took her in.\" Fenn spread his long hands. \"Sticks go missing. The book is written from the manifest at the pans, and the manifest is right.\"\n\n\"Then the sticks should be with the manifest.\"\n\n\"Then they should.\" He said it kindly. \"Write the book as it stands, Ilse. I'll speak to the pan-reeve myself.\"\n\nShe went back up the stairs. Aurek did not ask her what had been said, and she did not tell him. She wrote four hundred in the column in her own clean hand, and in the margin she wrote nothing at all, because the Magister read the margins.\n\nWhen the bell went for the end of the day she put the thirty-four sticks into her satchel instead of into the bin. She did not decide to do it. Her hands did it while she was thinking about something else, and by the time she noticed, she was already on the stair.","wordCount":678,"createdAt":1794216532378},{"id":"ihXIRaszvQ6ZzvrD4vLUm","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"Jwi40uq_PG4-AT77dhdd6","text":"The auditor came two days early.\n\nIlse heard her before she saw her — a carriage on the cobbles of Tallow Street, which was a thing that did not happen; nobody drove a carriage into the lower town because the streets were too narrow and the carters too rude. Then voices in the yard, and the porter's voice going up high the way it did when he was frightened, and then a long silence, and then feet on the stair.\n\nLisbet Ormund was not what Ilse had expected. She had pictured someone like the Magister, tall and grey and sorrowful, or someone like the Reeve, red-faced and loud. The woman who came into the third-floor room was small and round and perhaps fifty, in a brown travelling coat with the mud of the coast road still on its hem, and she had a face like a kindly hen. She stood in the doorway and took in the shelves of ledgers, all forty years of them, with an expression of pure delight.\n\n\"Oh,\" she said. \"Oh, look at that. You keep them up here? Under the roof?\"\n\n\"It's the driest room, madam,\" said Aurek, who had got to his feet so fast his stool had gone over.\n\n\"It's the hottest room in summer,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Paper doesn't like heat any more than it likes damp. Never mind. Never mind.\" She came in and took off her gloves. \"I'm the Exchequer's auditor. I expect you've been told I'd be along. I expect you were told the twentieth.\" She smiled at both of them. \"I find it saves a great deal of everyone's time to come on the eighteenth.\"\n\nIlse found that she had stood up too, and that her hand had gone flat against the front of her bodice, where the letter was. She took it away.\n\n\"Magister Fenn —\" Aurek began.\n\n\"Is downstairs, and has given me the run of the place, and very gracious he was about it. He's sending up tea. You'll be Master Stiles. And you are —?\"\n\n\"Ilse Varga, madam. Tallyclerk.\"\n\n\"Varga.\" The auditor considered her a moment longer than was comfortable. \"There was a Varga at the Hesk customs house, years ago. A woman. Very good with figures.\"\n\n\"My mother, madam.\"\n\n\"Was she? Well.\" Lisbet Ormund turned back to the shelves. \"Then you'll know the difference between a book that's right and a book that's tidy, Miss Varga. Most people don't.\" She ran a finger along the spines, the newest first. \"I'll want this year's, and last year's, and — let me see — the year of the big tide. When was that? Three years back?\"\n\n\"Four, madam,\" said Aurek.\n\n\"Three,\" said Ilse, at the same moment.\n\nThey looked at each other. The auditor looked at both of them, pleasantly.\n\n\"The spring tide that took the Pans,\" Ilse said. \"It was three years in Thawmonth. I'd been here a year.\"\n\n\"Then I'll have that one,\" said Lisbet Ormund, \"and the one before it, so I can see the difference. And I'll have the tally-sticks for whatever's come in this month. All of them.\" She sat down at the end of the long table, in the place where nobody ever sat because the draught came under the door there, and folded her small hands. \"I'm told sticks go missing,\" she said. \"I'm told it happens all the time.\"\n\nNobody said anything. Down in the yard the porter was shouting for someone to bring up the tea.\n\nThat night Ilse did not go home over the cooper's. She went down the Gull Stair to the quay, and she did not find Tomas on his basket outside the pilots' shed, and when she asked old Mother Gannet, who sold eels at the foot of the stair and saw everything, the old woman only said that the Patience had gone out on the evening ebb, and that there'd been two men in her, and that one of them was a long clever one with a scar in his eyebrow.","wordCount":670,"createdAt":1794216604992},{"id":"irKGJUgwTDee8PH8VbWGG","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"6iI4LgbwDeFqR_Cruf7mE","text":"She broke the seal in the morning, of course. Corin Ashe had known she would; that was why he had told her not to.\n\nThe storm had blown itself out in the night and left the sky scoured and pale. She sat at her table with a heel of yesterday's bread and slid the blade of her knife under the wax, carefully, so that it lifted in one piece and she could have pressed it back if she had been a different sort of person. Inside was a single sheet, folded twice, in a secretary's hand. At the bottom, a signature she had seen on a hundred harbour warrants: Halvard Seyle, Reeve of Varrow.\n\nThe letter was to Fenn.\n\n_Magister —_\n\n_The sixty from the Kestrel will go with the rest. The Black Mare lies off the Chapel on the spring tide and will take all of it then; there is no other night. Ormund leaves the capital on the twelfth and will be with you within the week, so see that the books are clean before she comes. I need not tell you what a clean book means. The levy is paid in full this year, as every year. You have my word on the Pans._\n\n_— H.S._\n\nShe read it four times. The fourth time she read it aloud, quietly, to hear whether it said something else when it was spoken. It did not.\n\n_The sixty from the Kestrel._ So it was not six sticks dropped in the harbour. _With the rest._ So it was not only the Kestrel. She thought of all the books on the third floor, the forty years of them on their shelves under the eaves, and of how many times she had written a number into a column without counting the sticks, because the sticks had been counted by somebody else, because the Magister read the margins.\n\n_You have my word on the Pans._ That one she did not understand at all.\n\nShe folded the letter again, and then she did a thing she was not proud of: she warmed the knife over the lamp and pressed the wax back down, so that from a little way off it looked whole. Then she put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, where it crackled when she breathed, and she went to work.\n\nAurek Stiles was already at the long table. He looked up when she came in and said, \"You look like a woman who's slept on a bag of nails,\" and she said, \"I found the sticks,\" and he said, \"Behind the stove?\" and she said, \"Behind the stove,\" and took them out of her satchel and put them in the bin, all thirty-four.\n\nIt was the first lie she had told at the Salt House. It went down easier than she had expected, and that frightened her more than the letter did.\n\nAt midday she went down to the Magister's room on an errand about the Heron's book and stood in his doorway while he signed it. His hand did not shake. He asked after Tomas, and whether the Patience had come through the storm, and he told her she was the best clerk he had had in thirty years, which he had told her before. She watched him sign and thought: _see that the books are clean._\n\nShe had always believed that she could tell when someone was lying to her. She went back up the stairs knowing that she had been wrong about that, and did not know for how long.","wordCount":593,"createdAt":1794216603831},{"id":"iwsSZKIoxv20cmQOUa0lQ","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Varga had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. There were forty crowns in his coat pocket. He had not come for the forty crowns. He had come because he had gone to find Corin himself, the night before, to ask what a man like that knew about six missing sticks, and Corin had been quiet a while and then said: more than your sister does. Come out on the ebb tomorrow and I'll show you.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe. And I'm three hundred crowns short of a berth on anything going south, which is the kind of thing that makes a man sell what he's holding.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter under his seal saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"Right on her night.\"\n\n\"You knew she'd be here.\"\n\n\"I carry the Reeve's post, Tomas. Letters, mostly, and I've a habit of reading them. I knew she'd be here because I carried the letter that said so, and then I carried it somewhere else.\" He sat up. \"The first load tonight. The rest on the spring tide, three nights on.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern: he had brought the Kestrel in through the Gullet on the ninth with that same boat towing astern.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.","wordCount":840,"createdAt":1794230090311},{"id":"j2B06N19ydLLfVR9vgtaO","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Brenn had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. That was what the forty crowns in his coat pocket were for. He had not come for the forty crowns. He had come because he had gone to find Corin himself, the night before, to ask what a man like that knew about six missing sticks, and Corin had been quiet a while and then said: more than your sister does. Come out on the ebb tomorrow and I'll show you.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe. And I'm three hundred crowns short of a berth on anything going south, which is the kind of thing that makes a man sell what he's holding.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter under his seal saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"Right on her night.\"\n\n\"You knew she'd be here.\"\n\n\"I carry the Reeve's post, Tomas. Letters, mostly, and I've a habit of reading them. I knew she'd be here because I carried the letter that said so, and then I carried it somewhere else.\" He sat up. \"The first load tonight. The rest on the spring tide, three nights on.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern: he had brought the Kestrel in through the Gullet on the ninth with that same boat towing astern.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.","wordCount":844,"createdAt":1794217111412},{"id":"j2d5T0XvyUMXP9JudyLx3","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"6iI4LgbwDeFqR_Cruf7mE","text":"She broke the seal in the morning, of course. Corin Ashe had known she would; that was why he had told her not to.\n\nThe storm had blown itself out in the night and left the sky scoured and pale. She sat at her table with a heel of yesterday's bread and slid the blade of her knife under the wax, carefully, so that it lifted in one piece and she could have pressed it back if she had been a different sort of person. Inside was a single sheet, folded twice, in a secretary's hand. At the bottom, a signature she had seen on a hundred harbour warrants: Halvard Seyle, Reeve of Varrow.\n\nThe letter was to Fenn.\n\n_Magister —_\n\n_The sixty from the Kestrel will go with the rest. The Black Mare lies off the Chapel on the spring tide and will take all of it then; there is no other night. Ormund leaves the capital on the twelfth and will be with you within the week, so see that the books are clean before she comes. I need not tell you what a clean book means. The levy is paid in full this year, as every year. You have my word on the Pans._\n\n_— H.S._\n\nShe read it four times. The fourth time she read it aloud, quietly, to hear whether it said something else when it was spoken. It did not.\n\n_The sixty from the Kestrel._ So it was not six sticks dropped in the harbour. _With the rest._ So it was not only the Kestrel. She thought of all the books on the third floor, the forty years of them on their shelves under the eaves, and of how many times she had written a number into a column without counting the sticks, because the sticks had been counted by somebody else, because the Magister read the margins.\n\n_You have my word on the Pans._ That one she did not understand at all.\n\nShe folded the letter again and looked at the seal, and then she did a thing she was not proud of: she warmed the knife over the lamp and pressed the wax back down, so that from a little way off it looked whole. Then she put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, where it crackled when she breathed, and she went to work.\n\nAurek Stiles was already at the long table. He looked up when she came in and said, \"You look like a woman who's slept on a bag of nails,\" and she said, \"I found the sticks,\" and he said, \"Behind the stove?\" and she said, \"Behind the stove,\" and took them out of her satchel and put them in the bin, all thirty-four.\n\nIt was the first lie she had told at the Salt House. It went down easier than she had expected, and that frightened her more than the letter did.\n\nAt midday she went down to the Magister's room on an errand about the Heron's book and stood in his doorway while he signed it. His hand did not shake. He asked after Tomas, and whether the Patience had come through the storm, and he told her she was the best clerk he had had in thirty years, which he had told her before. She watched him sign and thought: _see that the books are clean._\n\nShe had always believed that she could tell when someone was lying to her. She went back up the stairs knowing that she had been wrong about that, and did not know for how long.","wordCount":598,"createdAt":1794216603756},{"id":"jkLikUktIGJTacMbPi_ic","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"DS5E6n6CNj-_T8xgA9nT-","text":"Varrow at low water smelt of mud and iron and the sweet rot of weed, and the whole of the lower town came down to the quay to look at the sky.\n\nThere was a storm coming. Ilse could see it from the top of the Gull Stair, a long bruise lying along the horizon to the south-west, with the light behind it gone the colour of a boiled sweet. The harbour had drained down to its channels and the salt-ships sat over on their bilges in the mud, masts leaning together like drunk men holding each other up. Below her the stair went down in its two hundred worn steps to the quay, and the quay was full.\n\nShe found Tomas where she always found him, sitting on an upturned basket outside the pilots' shed with a pipe he never lit, looking at the water as if it owed him money.\n\n\"You took the Kestrel in,\" she said.\n\n\"Hello, Ilse. Good day at the counting? Lovely to see you, Tomas.\" He moved over on the basket to make room for her. \"I took her in on the ninth. Why?\"\n\n\"Was it rough?\"\n\n\"It was the Gullet in a southerly. It's always rough.\" He looked at her properly. Her brother was three years older than her and had their mother's face, broad and patient, and the same habit of knowing what she was going to say before she said it and waiting for her to say it anyway. \"What's wrong with the Kestrel?\"\n\n\"Was her mate sick?\"\n\nTomas laughed. \"Jory Pell? Jory's been at sea since he could walk. He's never been sick in his life. He was singing.\" He stopped laughing. \"Why?\"\n\nShe told him. She kept her voice down, though nobody on the quay was listening to anything but the wind getting up. Tomas listened with the pipe in his teeth.\n\n\"Sixty measures,\" he said at last. \"That's — what is that, in silver?\"\n\n\"At the Hesk price, nine hundred crowns. Near enough.\"\n\nHe whistled, low. \"And the Magister said.\"\n\n\"He said sticks go missing.\"\n\n\"They do.\" But he was frowning now, the way she must have been frowning at the ledger. \"Not six of them off one ship, they don't. A man might lose one. Six is a decision.\" He took the pipe out of his mouth and turned it over. \"What are you going to do?\"\n\n\"I don't know. I wrote it in. Four hundred.\"\n\n\"Ilse.\"\n\n\"What was I supposed to write? He's the Magister. He got me the place.\" She pressed the satchel against her side and felt the sticks shift inside it. \"I took the sticks.\"\n\nTomas looked at the satchel and then at her. \"You took the — that's the Salt House's property.\"\n\n\"I know what it is.\"\n\n\"If anybody knew they were short, they'll know you took them.\"\n\n\"Nobody knows they were short. Aurek thinks I dropped them behind the stove.\"\n\nThe first of the rain came then, a few fat drops striking the stones between their feet, each one leaving a dark coin. All along the quay people began to move, hauling covers over baskets, shouting for children. Out past the harbour mouth the sea had gone a hard, flat grey.\n\n\"Go home,\" Tomas said. \"Put them somewhere dry. Don't do anything else tonight.\" He stood up and knocked the pipe against his palm out of habit, though there was nothing in it. \"I've got to see to the Patience before this comes in. I'll come by tomorrow.\"\n\n\"Tomas —\"\n\n\"Tomorrow.\" He put a hand on her head, the way he had when she was small, and then he was gone into the crowd, a broad back in an oilskin, moving against the people coming up.\n\nShe climbed the Gull Stair in the rain. By the time she reached the top the storm had come in over the bar and the whole harbour was white.","wordCount":654,"createdAt":1794215010061},{"id":"jse-TKiuy94DjNQorTPUJ","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"Jwi40uq_PG4-AT77dhdd6","text":"The auditor came two days early.\n\nIlse heard her before she saw her — a carriage on the cobbles of Tallow Street, which was a thing that did not happen; nobody drove a carriage into the lower town because the streets were too narrow and the carters too rude. Then voices in the yard, and the porter's voice going up high the way it did when he was frightened, and then a long silence, and then feet on the stair.\n\nLisbet Ormund was not what Ilse had expected. She had pictured someone like the Magister, tall and grey and sorrowful, or someone like the Reeve, red-faced and loud. The woman who came into the third-floor room was small and round and perhaps fifty, in a brown travelling coat with the mud of the coast road still on its hem, and she had a face like a kindly hen. She stood in the doorway and took in the shelves of ledgers, all forty years of them, with an expression of pure delight.\n\n\"Oh,\" she said. \"Oh, look at that. You keep them up here? Under the roof?\"\n\n\"It's the driest room, madam,\" said Aurek, who had got to his feet so fast his stool had gone over.\n\n\"It's the hottest room in summer,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Paper doesn't like heat any more than it likes damp. Never mind. Never mind.\" She came in and took off her gloves. \"I'm the Exchequer's auditor. I expect you've been told I'd be along. I expect you were told the twentieth.\" She smiled at both of them. \"I find it saves a great deal of everyone's time to come on the eighteenth.\"\n\nIlse found that she had stood up too, and that her hand had gone flat against the front of her bodice, where the letter was. She took it away.\n\n\"Magister Fenn —\" Aurek began.\n\n\"Is downstairs, and has given me the run of the place, and very gracious he was about it. He's sending up tea. You'll be Master Stiles. And you are —?\"\n\n\"Ilse Varga, madam. Tallyclerk.\"\n\n\"Varga.\" The auditor considered her a moment longer than was comfortable. \"There was a Varga at the Hesk customs house, years ago. A woman. Very good with figures.\"\n\n\"My mother, madam.\"\n\n\"Was she? Well.\" Lisbet Ormund turned back to the shelves. \"Then you'll know the difference between a book that's right and a book that's tidy, Miss Varga. Most people don't.\" She ran a finger along the spines, the newest first. \"I'll want this year's, and last year's, and — let me see — the year of the big tide. When was that? Three years back?\"\n\n\"Four, madam,\" said Aurek.\n\n\"Three,\" said Ilse, at the same moment.\n\nIlse and Aurek looked at each other. The auditor looked at both of them, pleasantly.\n\n\"The spring tide that took the Pans,\" Ilse said. \"It was three years in Thawmonth. I'd been here a year.\"\n\n\"Then I'll have that one,\" said Lisbet Ormund, \"and the one before it, so I can see the difference. And I'll have the tally-sticks for whatever's come in this month. All of them.\" She sat down at the end of the long table, in the place where nobody ever sat because the draught came under the door there, and folded her small hands. \"I'm told sticks go missing,\" she said. \"I'm told it happens all the time.\"\n\nNobody said anything. Down in the yard the porter was shouting for someone to bring up the tea.\n\nThat night Ilse did not go home over the cooper's. She went down the Gull Stair to the quay, and she did not find Tomas on his basket outside the pilots' shed, and when she asked old Mother Gannet, who sold eels at the foot of the stair and saw everything, the old woman only said that the Patience had gone out on the evening ebb, and that there'd been two men in her, and that one of them was a long clever one with a scar in his eyebrow.","wordCount":672,"createdAt":1794216605043},{"id":"jw3UNBcY71EI_R4RgCOCb","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"Jwi40uq_PG4-AT77dhdd6","text":"The auditor came two days early.\n\nIlse heard her before she saw her — a carriage on the cobbles of Tallow Street, which was a thing that did not happen; nobody drove a carriage into the lower town because the streets were too narrow and the carters too rude. Then voices in the yard, and the porter's voice going up high the way it did when he was frightened, and then a long silence, and then feet on the stair.\n\nLisbet Ormund was not what Ilse had expected. She had pictured someone like the Magister, tall and grey and sorrowful, or someone like the Reeve, red-faced and loud. The woman who came into the third-floor room was small and round and perhaps fifty, in a brown travelling coat with the mud of the coast road still on its hem, and she had a face like a kindly hen. She stood in the doorway and took in the shelves of ledgers, all forty years of them, with an expression of pure delight.\n\n\"Oh,\" she said. \"Oh, look at that. You keep them up here? Under the roof?\"\n\n\"It's the driest room, madam,\" said Aurek, who had got to his feet so fast his stool had gone over.\n\n\"It's the hottest room in summer,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Paper doesn't like heat any more than it likes damp. Never mind. Never mind.\" She came in and took off her gloves. \"I'm the Exchequer's auditor. I expect you've been told I'd be along. I expect you were told the twentieth.\" She smiled at both of them. \"I find it saves a great deal of everyone's time to come on the eighteenth.\"\n\nIlse found that she had stood up too, and that her hand had gone flat against the front of her bodice, where the letter was. She took it away.\n\n\"Magister Fenn —\" Aurek began.\n\n\"Is downstairs, and has given me the run of the place, and very gracious he was about it. He's sending up tea. You'll be Master Stiles. And you are —?\"\n\n\"Ilse Varga, madam. Tallyclerk.\"\n\n\"Varga.\" The auditor considered her a moment longer than was comfortable. \"There was a Varga at the Hesk customs house, years ago. A woman. Very good with figures.\"\n\n\"My mother, madam.\"\n\n\"Was she? Well.\" Lisbet Ormund turned back to the shelves. \"Then you'll know the difference between a book that's right and a book that's tidy, Miss Varga. Most people don't.\" She ran a finger along the spines, the newest first. \"I'll want this year's, and last year's, and — let me see — the year of the big tide. When was that? Three years back?\"\n\n\"Four, madam,\" said Aurek.\n\n\"Three,\" said Ilse, at the same moment.\n\nIlse and Aurek looked at each other. The auditor regarded them both, pleasantly.\n\n\"The spring tide that took the Pans,\" Ilse said. \"It was three years in Thawmonth. I'd been here a year.\"\n\n\"Then I'll have that one,\" said Lisbet Ormund, \"and the one before it, so I can see the difference. And I'll have the tally-sticks for whatever's come in this month. All of them.\" She sat down at the end of the long table, in the place where nobody ever sat because the draught came under the door there, and folded her small hands. \"I'm told sticks go missing,\" she said. \"I'm told it happens all the time.\"\n\nNobody said anything. Down in the yard the porter was shouting for someone to bring up the tea.\n\nThat night Ilse did not go home over the cooper's. She went down the Gull Stair to the quay, and she did not find Tomas on his basket outside the pilots' shed, and when she asked old Mother Gannet, who sold eels at the foot of the stair and saw everything, the old woman only said that the Patience had gone out on the evening ebb, and that there'd been two men in her, and that one of them was a long clever one with a scar in his eyebrow.","wordCount":670,"createdAt":1794216605075},{"id":"k2Qtbv6_R6Xezeh1mQK65","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"LQxthMqMx2ix2u3pYQu7A","text":"The hearing was called for the hour after noon on the day of the spring tide, in the long hall at the top of the town where the Reeve of Varrow had sat in judgement on fishing disputes and drunk sailors for as long as there had been a Reeve. Lisbet Ormund had called it. She had the right; she had shown the clerk of the Hall a paper with the Exchequer's seal on it, and the clerk had gone white and sent for the Reeve, and the Reeve had come, and smiled, and said that of course the auditor must have whatever she needed.\n\nHe sat now in his own chair at the head of the hall, in the harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs. The Magister sat below him on the bench where witnesses sat, very straight, his long hands on his knees. Half the lower town had come up the hill to stand at the back. Ilse could see Mother Gannet's milky eye in the crowd, and Wren's dirty face beside her, and a great many people she had passed on the Gull Stair every day of her life and never spoken to. The old people on the quay had been saying since dawn that there was weather coming in with the tide.\n\nThe auditor stood at a small table in the middle of the floor with her papers in front of her.\n\n\"I'll be brief,\" she said, \"because the tide turns at four and I'm told half of you have boats to see to. I have audited the books of the Salt House of Varrow for the last four years. I find that for the last three of them, one-seventh part of the salt shipped from the Pans has not reached Hesk, and that the books have been written to hide it. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. Miss Varga.\"\n\nIlse stood up.\n\nShe had thought her legs would shake. They did not. She walked out to the table in the middle of the floor in her clerk's grey with her hair pinned and laid the things down on it one by one, the way she would have laid out a ship's sticks to be counted.\n\nThe Kestrel's book, open at the ninth.\n\nThirty-four tally-sticks, which Aurek Stiles had brought up the hill in his coat.\n\nA letter with a red seal pressed with a ship under a crown.\n\n\"On the ninth,\" she said, \"the Kestrel came in through the Gullet with three hundred and forty measures of salt on her sticks. I wrote four hundred in her book, because I was told to. That was a lie, and it was mine.\" She heard the crowd at the back shift and murmur. She did not look at them. \"Two days after I wrote it, the auditor asked me if I'd ever been told to write a number I knew was wrong, and I said no. That was a lie too.\"\n\n\"Miss Varga,\" said the Reeve, pleasantly, from his chair. \"You'll understand that a clerk who admits to two lies in one breath is not —\"\n\n\"And this,\" said Ilse, \"is a letter in your hand, Reeve, to the Magister, telling him where the sixty measures from the Kestrel were to go, and when, and on what ship. It never reached him. A man called Corin Ashe took it out of your post-bag, and gave it to me, and I've kept it.\" She looked up at him. \"You can read it, if you like. Or the auditor can read it aloud. It's quite short.\"\n\nThe hall was very quiet.\n\nHalvard Seyle looked at the letter on the table, and at the auditor, and at the Magister on the witnesses' bench, who had not moved. Then he looked past them all to the back of the hall, where Corin Ashe was standing against the wall with his arms folded, in a clean coat, and smiled.\n\n\"A smuggler,\" the Reeve said. \"A smuggler steals a letter, and a clerk who lies admits she lies, and an old man —\"\n\n\"I'll speak to the letter,\" said Oduya Fenn.\n\nHe stood up from the bench. He was not looking at the Reeve; he was looking at Ilse, and his face was quite calm.\n\n\"I never received it,\" he said. \"But I know what it says, because I know what every letter Halvard Seyle has written me for three years has said. I'll tell the auditor about every one of them. I'll tell her about the wall. I have kept a book of my own, Madam Auditor — a true one. I kept it because I am a clerk, and I could not bear not to. It is in the stove in my room at the Salt House, behind the firebrick, and it has every measure in it that I ever wrote false.\"\n\nSomebody in the crowd said, very loudly, \"Gods,\" and somebody else laughed, high and shocked.\n\nThe Reeve was on his feet.\n\n\"This hearing is closed,\" he said. \"I am the Reeve of Varrow and this is my hall, and I'll not have —\"\n\n\"It isn't closed, Reeve,\" said Lisbet Ormund mildly. \"It's the Exchequer's hearing. It's only being held in your hall.\" She picked up the letter. \"Sit down, please. I'm going to read this aloud.\"\n\nHe did not sit down. He looked at the auditor, and at the Magister, and at the crowd filling the back of the hall, and something went out of his big red face all at once, like air out of a bladder. Then he turned and walked out through the door behind his chair, the one that only the Reeve used, and nobody stopped him, because nobody in Varrow had ever stopped the Reeve doing anything.","wordCount":970,"createdAt":1794216654123},{"id":"kfcJONuqe8QNfX9Dr1YIR","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"3z3uj6msTp-yJfszkLU-n","text":"She went to work. She did not know what else to do with herself, and it seemed important, in a way she could not have explained, that she should be at her place at the long table when the bell went, in her clerk's grey, with her hair pinned, as if nothing had happened. As if she were still the best clerk the Magister had had in thirty years.\n\nAurek glanced up when she came in and opened his mouth to say something about bags of nails, and then saw her face and shut it again.\n\nThe auditor was not there. Her sheet of tiny figures lay on the end of the table under a glass paperweight, and the green ledger was gone from beside it.\n\n\"She's downstairs,\" Aurek said quietly. \"With him. Since first bell.\" He hesitated. \"Ilse. I took the sticks out of the bin.\"\n\nShe turned.\n\n\"The Kestrel's. The day you put them in. I'm not a fool, girl, whatever you think. Nobody finds six sticks behind a stove and then finds them gone again.\" He did not look at her; he looked at his own ledger, at his own tidy upright hand. \"I've been here twenty years. I've written what I was told for three of them. I didn't count. I didn't ask. I told myself the Magister read the margins.\" He turned a page that did not need turning. \"They're in my coat. In the press. If you want them.\"\n\nShe stood very still.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\n\"Because you didn't write anything in the margin,\" said Aurek Stiles. \"You always write in the margin. You've written in the margin every day for four years. And that day you didn't, and I thought: she's frightened. And I thought: I've been frightened for three years and I never once noticed it was the same thing.\"\n\nAt midday the auditor came up the stair and took her sheet of figures from under the paperweight and went down again without a word to either of them. A little after that, the porter came up to say that the Magister wanted Miss Varga in his room.\n\nThe stove was lit. It was always lit. Oduya Fenn sat behind his desk with the green ledger closed in front of him and his long hands folded on top of it, and he did not smile when she came in. That was how she knew he had not slept either.\n\n\"Sit down, Ilse.\"\n\nShe did not sit down. She had decided on the stair that she would tell him, all of it. If he was going to give her name to the Reeve a second time, she wanted to watch him do it.\n\n\"I was at the Chapel last night,\" she said. \"In the tower. With Tomas, who has a broken leg, and has been lying in a window since the night before last while you loaded salt underneath him.\"\n\nThe Magister closed his eyes.\n\n\"I heard you tell the Reeve my name.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"I imagine you did.\" He opened his eyes again and looked at her, and his face was older than she had ever seen it. \"Will you let me tell you how it happened? Not to excuse it. I don't think it can be excused. But I'd like someone to know, and I think you've earned it more than anyone.\"\n\nShe sat down.\n\nHe told her. What Mother Gannet had told her, and what Mother Gannet could not have known: the price of salt going up at Hesk, and the Salt House half empty, and the Exchequer writing every quarter to ask why the revenue was down. And then Halvard Seyle in this room, in that chair, with a bottle of something good, saying: _I can build you a wall, Oduya. All it needs is a little salt that nobody counts._\n\n\"One part in seven,\" the Magister said. \"For one year. To pay for the wall. That was all it was going to be. I told myself it was the Exchequer's own salt paying for the Exchequer's own wall, and if the Exchequer had done its duty there'd have been no need. I told myself a great many things.\" He looked down at his hands. \"The wall was built in a year. And at the end of the year I went to Halvard and said, that's enough, and he said — he said, Oduya, you've been writing false books for a year. Do you want to explain that to the Exchequer, or shall I? And then he smiled, and gave me his word on the Pans. He has written it at the bottom of every letter since. It means the wall stands as long as I keep writing.\"\n\n\"So you kept writing.\"\n\n\"So I kept writing. And the wall is bad stone, and Halvard has a new house, and the Black Mare comes in on the spring tides and takes one-seventh of the salt of Varrow to wherever it goes.\" He lifted his hands, let them fall. \"And you counted the Kestrel's sticks. I knew you would, one day. You're the only one of them who ever counted.\"\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Did you take it?\"\n\nHis face did not change. \"What letter?\"\n\n\"The Reeve's letter to you. About the Kestrel. I had it.\"\n\nFor a moment the Magister of the Salt House looked at her with an expression she could not read at all, and then, very slowly, he began to laugh. It was not a happy sound.\n\n\"He never sent it,\" he said. \"I never had it. I wondered — I wondered why he was so certain I'd been told about the Black Mare taking a first load on the eighteenth, when I hadn't. He said, _I wrote to you._ And I said, _you didn't._ And we each thought the other was lying.\" He wiped his eyes. \"Oh, Ilse. Somebody stole the Reeve's post. And now somebody has stolen it from you.\"","wordCount":996,"createdAt":1794230093899},{"id":"lKxlR1AsXCkycWAkM_zY_","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Varga had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. That was what the forty crowns in his coat pocket were for. He had not come for the forty crowns. He had come because he had gone to find Corin himself, the night before, to ask what a man like that knew about six missing sticks, and Corin had been quiet a while and then said: more than your sister does. Come out on the ebb tomorrow and I'll show you.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe. And I'm three hundred crowns short of a berth on anything going south, which is the kind of thing that makes a man sell what he's holding.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter under his seal saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"Right on her night.\"\n\n\"You knew she'd be here.\"\n\n\"I carry the Reeve's post, Tomas. Letters, mostly, and I've a habit of reading them. I knew she'd be here because I carried the letter that said so, and then I carried it somewhere else.\" He sat up. \"The first load tonight. The rest on the spring tide, three nights on.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern: he had brought the Kestrel in through the Gullet on the ninth with that same boat towing astern.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.","wordCount":844,"createdAt":1794230090183},{"id":"ng-gpmCmUSxyyCC9bYNSv","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"GIXhkOMgrF_FKcO5VyMzd","text":"The auditor left Varrow on the twelfth day of Seedmonth, in the same carriage that had come down Tallow Street and frightened the porter, and the whole of Tallow Street came out to see her go.\n\nShe had stayed a fortnight longer than she meant to. She had sat in the Magister's room with the firebrick out of the stove and the Magister's true book open on the desk — a small black book, scorched at the corners, with three years of real numbers in it in a long thin careful hand — and she had copied every page. Then she had sat in the third-floor room with Aurek and Ilse for ten days more and put the real numbers into the real books, page by page, in the margins, in red.\n\n\"The Exchequer will want the Magister in the capital,\" she told Ilse, the last evening, standing at the door of the Salt House with her gloves in her hand. \"There'll be a trial. I'll speak for him. I'll say he kept a true book when he could have kept none, and gave it up when he could have burned it, and that's not nothing.\" She pulled on one glove. \"I don't know what they'll do. Something less than they might have, I hope.\"\n\n\"And the Salt House?\"\n\n\"Will need a Magister.\" Lisbet Ormund pulled on the other glove. \"I've written to say so. I've written to say who.\" She looked up at Ilse with her kindly hen's face. \"Master Stiles has been here twenty years and is a very good clerk, and he told me himself that he would rather go into the sea after the Reeve than have the stove. So I've written your name.\"\n\nIlse opened her mouth and shut it again.\n\n\"You'll say you're too young,\" said the auditor. \"And that you lied to me. You did. You also told me you'd lied, in front of the whole town, which I have never once in thirty years of this work seen anyone do. Your mother would have.\" She held out her small gloved hand, and Ilse took it. \"Count everything, Miss Varga. Write in the margins. Good night.\"\n\nThe carriage went down Tallow Street in the morning with the carters cursing it, and Ilse watched it from the third-floor window until it was gone.\n\nTomas came down the Gull Stair that afternoon on two crutches, which took him nearly an hour and which he would not let anyone help him with, and sat on the bench outside the pilots' shed at the bottom with his splinted leg stuck out in front of him and his unlit pipe in his teeth. Corin Ashe sat beside him. Neither of them said much. Corin had a pilot's oilskin on, which was too big for him, and a pilot's book in his pocket, which Tomas had made him buy, and he was learning the Gullet from the bench, channel by channel, with Tomas drawing it in the dust with the end of a crutch.\n\n\"He'll make a pilot,\" Tomas said to Ilse, when she came down to them at the end of the day. \"Gods know how. He gets everything wrong and then argues with the sea about it.\"\n\n\"I'm very persuasive,\" said Corin.\n\n\"The sea isn't.\"\n\nWren was sitting on the bottom step of the Gull Stair eating a cold potato. Mother Gannet was gutting eels. The salt-ships lay over in the mud on the ebb, and the evening light came down the harbour the colour of honey, and somewhere up on the hill a bell was ringing for the end of the day.\n\nIlse climbed back up to the Salt House as the light went. The third floor was empty; Aurek had gone home. She lit the lamp and sat down at the long table, at her own place, and opened the Kestrel's book at the ninth.\n\nFour hundred, in her own clean hand. And beside it, in the margin, in red, in the auditor's tiny writing: _340. Sticks counted. I.V._\n\nShe dipped her pen, and turned to a clean page, and began to count.","wordCount":688,"createdAt":1794217078325},{"id":"oBFkCAChifGdn0HbFJHDK","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"r9SBgyYpFJBk2b1o5XkXt","text":"They brought Tomas out on the last of the ebb, the two of them, Ilse under one arm and Wren under the other, and it took four hours, and Ilse did not afterward remember much of it except the cold and the weight and her brother's breath hissing in her ear at every step. Wren found them the hard every time.At the edge of the marsh, where the path came up onto the sea-wall by the Pans, they left Tomas with a pan-man Wren knew, who asked no questions and sent his own son running for a bone-setter, and then Ilse walked home alone through the waking streets with the sun coming up behind her.\n\nThe Magister had said her name. She kept hearing it. Not _the clerk_, not _one of my girls_. _The Varga girl_, in that tired voice she had loved for four years, like a man putting down something heavy.\n\nShe climbed the stair through the middle of the cooper's house. The step that creaked, creaked. The cooper's wife was not singing.\n\nHer door was open.\n\nNot broken. Open, the way you leave a door when you have a key and are in a hurry. Inside, the room looked almost as she had left it — the table, the chair, the lamp-hook — except that everything had been moved a little and put back a little wrong, the way a stranger puts things back. The chair faced the wrong wall. Her mother's comb was on the left of the shelf instead of the right.\n\nShe went back down the stair. The cooper's wife was in the kitchen with the baby on her hip and the door half shut, and when Ilse knocked on it she opened it no wider.\n\n\"Was anybody up?\" Ilse said. \"In the night. To my room.\"\n\n\"I was asleep.\" The cooper's wife did not look at her. She looked at the baby, who was chewing on a wooden spoon. \"We were all asleep. The cooper's been at the Pans all week, you know that, there's nobody here but me.\"\n\n\"My door was open.\"\n\n\"Then you left it open. You were out all night, weren't you? Out in the marsh, by the look of your boots.\" The woman's eyes went to the boots and came away quickly. \"I don't ask where my lodgers go, Miss Varga. I'd thank you not to ask me where I sleep.\"\n\n\"Somebody had a key.\"\n\n\"Then somebody had a key.\" The cooper's wife began to close the door. \"There's three keys to that room and one of them's been lost since Midwinter. Ask the cooper. When he's home.\"\n\nThe door shut. Behind it the baby began to cry, and the woman began to sing to it, the same three lines over and over, too loud.\n\nIlse stood in the passage for a while. Then she went back up.\n\nHer room was cold. Someone had let the lamp burn down to nothing and the wick had charred, and there was a smell in the air that was not hers — wet wool, and tar, and something sharper, like cloves. \n\nShe stood in the middle of the floor with her hands at her sides and tried to think the way her mother would have thought. Who had known she was going into the marsh. Who had known she would not be home. Wren had known. Mother Gannet had known. Anyone who had seen her go down Tallow Street at dusk in her mother's boots had known, and half the lower town had.\n\nIt did not narrow anything. It only made the room feel larger, and full of people.\n\nThe bolster lay on the bed with its seam cut open from end to end.\n\nShe did not need to look. She looked anyway. She put her hand into the hollow and felt the feathers and the ticking and nothing else, and then she sat down on the bed with the feathers settling around her like snow, and for the first time since the Kestrel's sticks she let herself cry.\n\nShe had not told anyone where she kept it. Not Tomas. Not Wren. Not the auditor, who she had lied to.\n\nShe had told no one, and someone had known.","wordCount":707,"createdAt":1794216553161},{"id":"olz2lywBzNGC84Zur0EN5","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"6iI4LgbwDeFqR_Cruf7mE","text":"She broke the seal in the morning, of course. Corin Ashe had known she would; that was why he had told her not to.\n\nThe storm had blown itself out in the night and left the sky scoured and pale. She sat at her table with a heel of yesterday's bread and slid the blade of her knife under the wax, carefully, so that it lifted in one piece and she could have pressed it back if she had been a different sort of person. Inside was a single sheet, folded twice, in a secretary's hand. At the bottom, a signature she had seen on a hundred harbour warrants: Halvard Seyle, Reeve of Varrow.\n\nThe letter was to Fenn.\n\n_Magister —_\n\n_The sixty from the Kestrel will go with the rest. The Black Mare lies off the Chapel on the spring tide and will take all of it then; there is no other night. Ormund leaves the capital on the twelfth and will be with you within the week, so see that the books are clean before she comes. I need not tell you what a clean book means. The levy is paid in full this year, as every year. You have my word on the Pans._\n\n_— H.S._\n\nShe read it four times. The fourth time she read it aloud, quietly, to hear whether it said something else when it was spoken. It did not.\n\n_The sixty from the Kestrel._ So it was not six sticks dropped in the harbour. _With the rest._ So it was not only the Kestrel. She thought of all the books on the third floor, the forty years of them on their shelves under the eaves, and of how many times she had written a number into a column without counting the sticks, because the sticks had been counted by somebody else, because the Magister read the margins.\n\n_You have my word on the Pans._ That one she did not understand at all.\n\nShe folded the letter again, and then she did a thing she was not proud of: she warmed the knife over the lamp and pressed the wax back down, so that from a little way off it looked whole. Then she put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, where it crackled when she breathed, and she went to work.\n\nAurek Stiles was already at the long table. When she came in and said, \"You look like a woman who's slept on a bag of nails,\" and she said, \"I found the sticks,\" and he said, \"Behind the stove?\" and she said, \"Behind the stove,\" and took them out of her satchel and put them in the bin, all thirty-four.\n\nIt was the first lie she had told at the Salt House. It went down easier than she had expected, and that frightened her more than the letter did.\n\nAt midday she went down to the Magister's room on an errand about the Heron's book and stood in his doorway while he signed it. His hand did not shake. He asked after Tomas, and whether the Patience had come through the storm, and he told her she was the best clerk he had had in thirty years, which he had told her before. She watched him sign and thought: _see that the books are clean._\n\nShe had always believed that she could tell when someone was lying to her. She went back up the stairs knowing that she had been wrong about that, and did not know for how long.","wordCount":590,"createdAt":1794216603900},{"id":"pPK9B0rIb1OoyoePCS0Sx","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"2i5J2Lc7W9L8liiqtAFCG","text":"The Salt House kept its ledgers on the third floor, under the eaves, where the damp could not reach them and the clerks could not reach anything else. Ilse Varga had worked there for four years and had never once been warm.\n\nShe sat at the long table with the Kestrel's book open in front of her and the Kestrel's tally-sticks laid out beside it in a row, like the bones of a small animal. Every sack that went over the side of a salt-ship at Varrow was notched on a stick by the pan-reeve and notched again by the ship's mate, and the stick was split down its length so that each man kept half. When the halves came back to the Salt House they were matched, and counted, and the count went into the book. Nobody could cut a notch out of a stick without the other half showing it. It was the oldest honest thing in the city.\n\nShe counted the sticks again. Thirty-four sticks, ten notches to a stick. Three hundred and forty measures.\n\nThe book said four hundred.\n\n\"You're frowning at it,\" said Aurek Stiles from the far end of the table. He was the senior of the two tallyclerks and had been for twenty years, and he said it without looking up from his own work. \"Frowning at a ledger is how the ink gets into your face.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel,\" Ilse said. \"Her book says four hundred measures out on the ninth. I've got three hundred and forty on the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then you've dropped six sticks behind the stove.\"\n\n\"I haven't.\"\n\n\"Then the mate's dropped them in the harbour. It happens.\" Aurek turned a page. \"Write it as received and put a query in the margin. The Magister reads the margins.\"\n\nShe did not write it as received. She sat with the pen over the page until the ink beaded on the nib and fell, a small black star on the blotter, and then she got up and went down the narrow stair to the Magister's room.\n\nMagister Oduya Fenn had run the Salt House since before Ilse was born. He was a tall man gone thin in the way of tall men who forget to eat, and his room was the only warm one in the building, because he had the only good stove. He glanced up when she knocked and smiled at her with real pleasure, which made what she had come to say harder.\n\n\"Ilse. Sit. You look like you've been out on the Pans.\"\n\n\"Only on the third floor, Magister.\" She did not sit. \"The Kestrel's book is sixty measures over her sticks.\"\n\nThe smile stayed exactly where it was. That was the first thing she would remember afterwards — not that it went, but that it stayed, a little too long, like a man holding a door that nobody was coming through.\n\n\"Sixty,\" he said.\n\n\"Six sticks. I've counted three times.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel came in through the Gullet in a rising sea. Her mate was sick over the side twice before they'd cleared the bar — your brother will tell you; he took her in.\" Fenn spread his long hands. \"Sticks go missing. The book is written from the manifest at the pans, and the manifest is right.\"\n\n\"Then the sticks should be with the manifest.\"\n\n\"Then they should.\" He said it kindly. \"Write the book as it stands, Ilse. I'll speak to the pan-reeve myself.\"\n\nShe went back up the stairs. Aurek did not ask her what had been said, and she did not tell him. She wrote four hundred in the column in her own clean hand, and in the margin she wrote nothing at all, because the Magister read the margins.\n\nWhen the bell went for the end of the day she put the thirty-four sticks into her satchel instead of into the bin. She did not decide to do it. Her hands did it while she was thinking about something else, and by the time she noticed, she was already on the stair.","wordCount":678,"createdAt":1794217026828},{"id":"pa80jEDy4prOzRIxHsBxL","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"2i5J2Lc7W9L8liiqtAFCG","text":"The Salt House kept its ledgers on the third floor, under the eaves, where the damp could not reach them and the clerks could not reach anything else. Ilse Varga had worked there for four years and had never once been warm.\n\nShe sat at the long table with the Kestrel's book open in front of her and the Kestrel's tally-sticks laid out beside it in a row, like the bones of a small animal. Every sack that went over the side of a salt-ship at Varrow was notched on a stick by the pan-reeve and notched again by the ship's mate, and the stick was split down its length so that each man kept half. When the halves came back to the Salt House they were matched, and counted, and the count went into the book. Nobody could cut a notch out of a stick without the other half showing it. It was the oldest honest thing in the city.\n\nShe counted the sticks again. Thirty-four sticks, ten notches to a stick. Three hundred and forty measures.\n\nThe book said four hundred.\n\n\"You're frowning at it,\" said Aurek Stiles from the far end of the table. He was the senior of the three tallyclerks and had been for twenty years, and he said it without looking up from his own work. \"Frowning at a ledger is how the ink gets into your face.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel,\" Ilse said. \"Her book says four hundred measures out on the ninth. I've got three hundred and forty on the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then you've dropped six sticks behind the stove.\"\n\n\"I haven't.\"\n\n\"Then the mate's dropped them in the harbour. It happens.\" Aurek turned a page. \"Write it as received and put a query in the margin. The Magister reads the margins.\"\n\nShe did not write it as received. She sat with the pen over the page until the ink beaded on the nib and fell, a small black star on the blotter, and then she got up and went down the narrow stair to the Magister's room.\n\nMagister Oduya Fenn had run the Salt House since before Ilse was born. He was a tall man gone thin in the way of tall men who forget to eat, and his room was the only warm one in the building, because he had the only good stove. He looked up when she knocked and smiled at her with real pleasure, which made what she had come to say harder.\n\n\"Ilse. Sit. You look like you've been out on the Pans.\"\n\n\"Only on the third floor, Magister.\" She did not sit. \"The Kestrel's book is sixty measures over her sticks.\"\n\nThe smile stayed exactly where it was. That was the first thing she would remember afterward — not that it went, but that it stayed, a little too long, like a man holding a door that nobody was coming through.\n\n\"Sixty,\" he said.\n\n\"Six sticks. I've counted three times.\"\n\n\"The Kestrel came in through the Gullet in a rising sea. Her mate was sick over the side twice before they'd cleared the bar — your brother will tell you, he took her in.\" Fenn spread his long hands. \"Sticks go missing. The book is written from the manifest at the pans, and the manifest is right.\"\n\n\"Then the sticks should be with the manifest.\"\n\n\"Then they should.\" He said it kindly. \"Write the book as it stands, Ilse. I'll speak to the pan-reeve myself.\"\n\nShe went back up the stairs. Aurek did not ask her what had been said, and she did not tell him. She wrote four hundred in the column in her own clean hand, and in the margin she wrote nothing at all, because the Magister read the margins.\n\nWhen the bell went for the end of the day she put the thirty-four sticks into her satchel instead of into the bin. She did not decide to do it. Her hands did it while she was thinking about something else, and by the time she noticed, she was already on the stair.","wordCount":678,"createdAt":1794216599878},{"id":"pb0SDHsz-g_TDvdCPcfKn","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"6iI4LgbwDeFqR_Cruf7mE","text":"She broke the seal in the morning, of course. Corin Ashe had known she would; that was why he had told her not to.\n\nThe storm had blown itself out in the night and left the sky scoured and pale. She sat at her table with a heel of yesterday's bread and slid the blade of her knife under the wax, carefully, so that it lifted in one piece and she could have pressed it back if she had been a different sort of person. Inside was a single sheet, folded twice, in a secretary's hand. At the bottom, a signature she had seen on a hundred harbour warrants: Halvard Seyle, Reeve of Varrow.\n\nThe letter was to Fenn.\n\n_Magister —_\n\n_The sixty from the Kestrel will go with the rest. The Black Mare lies off the Chapel on the spring tide and will take all of it then; there is no other night. Ormund leaves the capital on the twelfth and will be with you within the week, so see that the books are clean before she comes. I need not tell you what a clean book means. The levy is paid in full this year, as every year. You have my word on the Pans._\n\n_— H.S._\n\nShe read it four times. The fourth time she read it aloud, quietly, to hear whether it said something else when it was spoken. It did not.\n\n_The sixty from the Kestrel._ So it was not six sticks dropped in the harbour. _With the rest._ So it was not only the Kestrel. She thought of all the books on the third floor, the forty years of them on their shelves under the eaves, and of how many times she had written a number into a column without counting the sticks, because the sticks had been counted by somebody else, because the Magister read the margins.\n\n_You have my word on the Pans._ That one she did not understand at all.\n\nShe folded the letter again, and then she did a thing she was not proud of: she warmed the knife over the lamp and pressed the wax back down, so that from a little way off it looked whole. Then she put the letter inside her bodice, against her skin, where it crackled when she breathed, and she went to work.\n\nAurek Stiles was already at the long table. When she came in he said, \"You look like a woman who's slept on a bag of nails,\" and she said, \"I found the sticks,\" and he said, \"Behind the stove?\" and she said, \"Behind the stove,\" and took them out of her satchel and put them in the bin, all thirty-four.\n\nIt was the first lie she had told at the Salt House. It went down easier than she had expected, and that frightened her more than the letter did.\n\nAt midday she went down to the Magister's room on an errand about the Heron's book and stood in his doorway while he signed it. His hand did not shake. He asked after Tomas, and whether the Patience had come through the storm, and he told her she was the best clerk he had had in thirty years, which he had told her before. She watched him sign and thought: _see that the books are clean._\n\nShe had always believed that she could tell when someone was lying to her. She went back up the stairs knowing that she had been wrong about that, and did not know for how long.","wordCount":590,"createdAt":1794217033011},{"id":"pj1H2v0pf9BYviLrRPwPD","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"FBthgk0AwfIy4svSwzmYI","text":"The marsh at low water was a different country. The channels that the Patience had sailed were trickles now, running between banks of mud as high as Ilse's head, and the mud shone in the moonlight like the skin of something sleeping. Wren went ahead of her along a path she could not see — the hard, he called it, a spine of shingle under the mud that the old salt-carriers had used before there were boats — and she followed him step for step and did not look down.\n\nIt took them two hours to reach the Chapel. The tower stood up out of the mud on its little island of stone, the arches of the old nave around its foot half-buried, the high windows empty. At high water the sea came in through those windows. Now, with the tide out, there were steps going down into the dark of the crypt, slick with weed, and a smell of salt and old candles.\n\n\"Here's where they load,\" Wren whispered. He pointed. Along the stone of the steps there were fresh scars, white in the moonlight, where something heavy had been dragged. \"Salt. Sacks of it. They bring it up the channel from the Pans at night and keep it here dry till the ship comes.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\nWren shrugged inside his coat. \"Everybody knows. Nobody says.\"\n\nThey found Tomas in the tower.\n\nHe had dragged himself up the first turn of the stair and wedged himself into a window-niche, and he was so still and so grey that Ilse thought for a moment that he was dead. Then his eyes opened and he said, quite clearly, \"You weren't supposed to come,\" and she knelt on the stair beside him and held his hand and could not say anything at all.\n\nHis leg was splinted with half a boat-hook. He had done it well. He had been sitting in the niche, he said, for a day and a night, while the Reeve's men came and went below him, loading the black ship from the crypt. He had heard everything. He had not been able to move.\n\n\"Corin,\" Ilse said. \"Where's Corin?\"\n\n\"Went over the side. Took them off me.\" Tomas closed his eyes. \"Don't know if he got out. He said to tell you to keep the letter.\"\n\n\"I have it.\"\n\n\"Good. Ilse — listen. The ship's gone. The Black Mare. She went out on this morning's tide with all of it aboard, and she'll be back for the rest.\" His hand tightened on hers. \"And it's not just the Reeve. I heard them. They were talking about the Salt House. About the books. About someone who's going to fix the books before the auditor —\"\n\nThere was a sound below them. Wren, who had been keeping watch at the foot of the stair, came up it like a cat, silent, his eyes huge.\n\n\"Lantern,\" he breathed. \"Two men. On the hard.\"\n\nThey did not move. Ilse put her hand over Tomas's mouth, not because he would have made a sound but because she needed to hold something. Below them, in the ruined nave, a lantern came and went among the arches, and two men came with it, and stood at the head of the crypt steps where the white scars were.\n\nOne of them was big and wore a harbour officer's coat. She had seen the Reeve of Varrow at a hundred ceremonies on the quay; she knew his shoulders, his walk, the way he stood with his weight back on his heels.\n\nThe other was tall and thin, and held his lantern with a long hand that she had watched sign a thousand pages.\n\n\"She's asking for the flood year,\" the Magister was saying. His voice echoed in the stone. \"She's had it on the table for two days. She has copies of the Hesk ledgers, Halvard. Copies. She knew before she came.\"\n\n\"Then she knows nothing she can prove.\" The Reeve's voice was easy, almost bored. \"Hesk ledgers. A clerk in Hesk can't count. Everyone knows that.\"\n\n\"She'll ask for the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then give her the sticks. You told me the sticks were sorted.\"\n\nThe Magister was quiet. The lantern shook a little.\n\n\"One of my clerks,\" he said at last, \"found the Kestrel short. Before I could see to it.\"\n\n\"Which clerk?\"\n\nIlse felt Tomas's hand tighten on hers until the bones ground.\n\n\"It doesn't matter. I dealt with it. She wrote it in as I told her.\"\n\n\"Oduya.\" The Reeve sounded almost gentle. \"Everything matters. That's how we've lasted three years. Which clerk?\"\n\nThere was a long silence in the nave. Ilse could hear the water beginning to come back, very far off, a sound like breathing.\n\n\"The Varga girl,\" the Magister said.","wordCount":800,"createdAt":1794217045344},{"id":"pl28Gd_RllHUfB5mNWGyv","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"GIXhkOMgrF_FKcO5VyMzd","text":"The auditor left Varrow on the twelfth day of Seedmonth, in the same carriage that had come down Tallow Street and frightened the porter, and the whole of Tallow Street came out to see her go.\n\nShe had stayed three weeks longer than she meant to. She had sat in the Magister's room with the firebrick out of the chimney-breast and the Magister's true book open on the desk — a small black book, scorched at the corners, with three years of real numbers in it in a long thin careful hand — and she had copied every page. Then she had sat in the third-floor room with Aurek and Ilse for ten days more and put the real numbers into the real books, page by page, in the margins, in red.\n\n\"The Exchequer will want the Magister in the capital,\" she told Ilse, the last evening, standing at the door of the Salt House with her gloves in her hand. \"There'll be a trial. I'll speak for him. I'll say he kept a true book when he could have kept none, and gave it up when he could have burned it, and that's not nothing.\" She pulled on one glove. \"I don't know what they'll do. Something less than they might have, I hope.\"\n\n\"And the Salt House?\"\n\n\"Will need a Magister.\" Lisbet Ormund pulled on the other glove. \"I've written to say so. I've written to say who.\" She looked up at Ilse with her kindly hen's face. \"Master Stiles has been here twenty years and is a very good clerk, and he told me himself that he would rather go into the sea after the Reeve than have the stove. So I've written your name.\"\n\nIlse opened her mouth and shut it again.\n\n\"You'll say you're too young,\" said the auditor. \"And that you lied to me. You did. You also told me you'd lied, in front of the whole town, which I have never once in thirty years of this work seen anyone do. Your mother would have.\" She held out her small gloved hand, and Ilse took it. \"Count everything, Miss Varga. Write in the margins. Good night.\"\n\nThe carriage went down Tallow Street in the morning with the carters cursing it, and Ilse watched it from the third-floor window until it was gone.\n\nTomas came down the Gull Stair that afternoon on two crutches, which took him nearly an hour and which he would not let anyone help him with, and sat on the bench outside the pilots' shed at the bottom with his splinted leg stuck out in front of him and his unlit pipe in his teeth. Corin Ashe sat beside him. Neither of them said much. Corin had a pilot's oilskin on, which was too big for him, and a pilot's book in his pocket, which Tomas had made him buy, and he was learning the Gullet from the bench, channel by channel, with Tomas drawing it in the dust with the end of a crutch.\n\n\"He'll make a pilot,\" Tomas said to Ilse, when she came down to them at the end of the day. \"Gods know how. He gets everything wrong and then argues with the sea about it.\"\n\n\"I'm very persuasive,\" said Corin.\n\n\"The sea isn't.\"\n\nWren was sitting on the bottom step of the Gull Stair eating a cold potato. Mother Gannet was gutting eels. The salt-ships lay over in the mud on the ebb, and the evening light came down the harbour the colour of honey, and somewhere up on the hill a bell was ringing for the end of the day.\n\nIlse climbed back up to the Salt House as the light went. The third floor was empty; Aurek had gone home. She lit the lamp and sat down at the long table, at her own place, and opened the Kestrel's book at the ninth.\n\nFour hundred, in her own clean hand. And beside it, in the margin, in red, in the auditor's tiny writing: _340. Sticks counted. I.V._\n\nShe dipped her pen, and turned to a clean page, and began to count.","wordCount":688,"createdAt":1794217082424},{"id":"qa_OqI4JjeVvl_Xw64ado","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"W6kDqSJm2O5zS3kC2H7Of","text":"The storm came in at four, with the turn of the tide, as the old people on the quay had said it would. It came out of the south-west in one long grey wall, and the sea came with it.\n\nIlse watched from the top of the Gull Stair. She had run there from the Reeve's Hall with Corin behind her and most of the hall behind him, because Wren had come pelting up the hill shouting that the Reeve's longboat had gone out of the harbour with the Reeve in it, and that the Black Mare was lying off the Chapel again in the teeth of the weather, waiting for him.\n\n\"He's mad,\" Corin said, beside her. He had to shout it. \"He'll never get over the bar in this.\"\n\n\"He knows the bar.\"\n\n\"Nobody knows the bar in this.\"\n\nOut past the harbour mouth the sea had gone white. The salt-ships at their moorings were pitching and snatching at their chains, and the spray was coming over the quay wall in sheets, and down below them on the quay the pilots were out in their oilskins, shouting, hauling boats up the slip. Ilse searched for Tomas among them out of habit before she remembered that Tomas was in a bed at the Pans with his leg in a splint.\n\nThen she saw the longboat.\n\nIt was a small dark thing in the white water out by the Chapel, six oars rising and falling out of time, and it was making for the black ship. She could see the Black Mare now too, beyond it — two masts, no sail, her anchors down, plunging. Someone on her deck was waving a light.\n\n\"They'll make it,\" said Corin. \"Gods damn him, they'll —\"\n\nThe sea took the longboat on the Chapel bar.\n\nShe did not see it happen. One moment there were six oars and a dark hull and the next there was a wave, enormous, grey-green, standing up across the bar the way the sea stands up only where the bottom is very close, and when it went down there was nothing on the bar but white water. A long way off, the light on the Black Mare's deck stopped waving.\n\nNobody on the Gull Stair said anything at all.\n\nThe Black Mare did not wait. They saw her get her anchors up, or cut them — it was too far to tell — and get a scrap of sail on her, and claw her way round to the south, away from the bar and the Chapel and Varrow. Within half an hour she was gone into the rain.\n\nThe Reeve's body came ashore two days later on the hard below the sea-wall at the Pans, the new sea-wall, which had held. One of the pan-men found it. Mother Gannet said afterwards that it was the only thing the Reeve ever built that did what it was meant to.","wordCount":490,"createdAt":1794217143105},{"id":"rQdGQMM45rft4FRgAxcsC","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Brenn had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. That was what the forty crowns in his coat pocket were for.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter under his seal saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"She's early.\"\n\n\"How early?\"\n\n\"A night. A whole night.\" He sat up. \"The letter said the spring tide. That's tomorrow.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern: he had brought the Kestrel in through the Gullet on the ninth.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.","wordCount":712,"createdAt":1794216606357},{"id":"rjnYc9lsA5ut26XMIbbv-","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"W6kDqSJm2O5zS3kC2H7Of","text":"The storm came in at four, with the turn of the tide, as the old people on the quay had said it would. It came out of the south-west in one long grey wall, and the sea came with it.\n\nIlse watched from the top of the Gull Stair. She had run there from the Reeve's Hall with Corin behind her and most of the hall behind him, because Wren had come pelting up the hill shouting that the Reeve's longboat had gone out of the harbour with the Reeve in it, and that the Black Mare was lying off the Chapel again in the teeth of the weather, waiting for him.\n\n\"He's mad,\" Corin said, beside her. He had to shout it. \"He'll never get over the bar in this.\"\n\n\"He knows the bar.\"\n\n\"Nobody knows the bar in this.\"\n\nOut past the harbour mouth the sea had gone white. The salt-ships at their moorings were pitching and snatching at their chains, and the spray was coming over the quay wall in sheets, and down below them on the quay the pilots were out in their oilskins, shouting, hauling boats up the slip. Ilse searched for Tomas among them out of habit before she remembered that Tomas was in a bed at the Pans with his leg in a splint.\n\nThen she saw the longboat.\n\nIt was a small dark thing in the white water out by the Chapel, six oars rising and falling out of time, and it was making for the black ship. She could see the Black Mare now too, beyond it — two masts, no sail, her anchors down, plunging. Someone on her deck was waving a light.\n\n\"They'll make it,\" said Corin. \"Gods damn him, they'll —\"\n\nThe sea took the longboat on the Chapel bar.\n\nShe did not see it happen. One moment there were six oars and a dark hull and the next there was a wave, enormous, grey-green, standing up across the bar the way the sea stands up only where the bottom is very close, and when it went down there was nothing on the bar but white water. A long way off, the light on the Black Mare's deck stopped waving.\n\nNobody on the Gull Stair said anything at all.\n\nIlse found that she was holding the wet rail of the stair with both hands. She had won. She had stood in the long hall and counted the Reeve out in front of the whole town, and the sea had taken the count out of her hands. There would be no trial for Halvard Seyle now, no Exchequer, no question put to him in front of anyone. Every question would go to the Magister instead, who had stood up and answered, and to the men the Reeve had paid, who were still in Varrow and had all seen her face. She had thought it would feel like the end of something. It felt like a ledger with the last page torn out.\n\nThe Black Mare did not wait. They saw her get her anchors up, or cut them — it was too far to tell — and get a scrap of sail on her, and claw her way round to the south, away from the bar and the Chapel and Varrow. Within half an hour she was gone into the rain.\n\nThe Reeve's body came ashore two days later on the hard below the sea-wall at the Pans, the new sea-wall, which had held. One of the pan-men found it. Mother Gannet said afterwards that it was the only thing the Reeve ever built that did what it was meant to.","wordCount":612,"createdAt":1794230067769},{"id":"sVzIgJUo_XCrAXR9xqve4","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"3z3uj6msTp-yJfszkLU-n","text":"She went to work. She did not know what else to do with herself, and it seemed important, in a way she could not have explained, that she should be at her place at the long table when the bell went, in her clerk's grey, with her hair pinned, as if nothing had happened. As if she were still the best clerk the Magister had had in thirty years.\n\nAurek glanced up when she came in and opened his mouth to say something about bags of nails, and then looked at her face properly and shut it again.\n\nThe auditor was not there. Her sheet of tiny figures lay on the end of the table under a glass paperweight, and the green ledger was gone from beside it.\n\n\"She's downstairs,\" Aurek said quietly. \"With him. Since first bell.\" He hesitated. \"Ilse. I took the sticks out of the bin.\"\n\nShe turned.\n\n\"The Kestrel's. The night you put them in. I'm not a fool, girl, whatever you think. Nobody finds six sticks behind a stove and then finds them gone again.\" He did not look at her; he looked at his own ledger, at his own tidy upright hand. \"I've been here twenty years. I've written what I was told for three of them. I didn't count. I didn't ask. I told myself the Magister read the margins.\" He turned a page that did not need turning. \"They're in my coat. In the press. If you want them.\"\n\nShe stood very still.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\n\"Because you didn't write anything in the margin,\" said Aurek Stiles. \"You always write in the margin. You've written in the margin every day for four years. And that day you didn't, and I thought: she's frightened. And I thought: I've been frightened for three years and I never once noticed it was the same thing.\"\n\nAt midday the auditor came up the stair and took her sheet of figures from under the paperweight and went down again without a word to either of them. A little after that, the porter came up to say that the Magister wanted Miss Varga in his room.\n\nThe stove was lit. It was always lit. Oduya Fenn sat behind his desk with the green ledger closed in front of him and his long hands folded on top of it, and he did not smile when she came in. That was how she knew he had not slept either.\n\n\"Sit down, Ilse.\"\n\nShe did not sit down.\n\n\"I was at the Chapel last night,\" she said. \"In the tower. With Tomas, who has a broken leg, and has been lying in a window for two days while you loaded salt underneath him.\"\n\nThe Magister closed his eyes.\n\n\"I heard you tell the Reeve my name.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"I imagine you did.\" He opened his eyes again and looked at her, and his face was older than she had ever seen it. \"Will you let me tell you how it happened? Not to excuse it. I don't think it can be excused. But I'd like someone to know, and I think you've earned it more than anyone.\"\n\nShe sat down.\n\nHe told her. The tide in Thawmonth that took the wall and the pans and the pan-reeve's wife. The letter to the capital, and the letter back, _a matter for the harbour._ A whole year of the pan-men sitting on the quay, and the price of salt going up at Hesk, and the Salt House half empty, and the Exchequer writing every quarter to ask why the revenue was down. And then Halvard Seyle in this room, in that chair, with a bottle of something good, saying: _I can build you a wall, Oduya. All it needs is a little salt that nobody counts._\n\n\"One part in seven,\" the Magister said. \"For one year. To pay for the wall. That was all it was going to be. I told myself it was the Exchequer's own salt paying for the Exchequer's own wall, and if the Exchequer had done its duty there'd have been no need. I told myself a great many things.\" He looked down at his hands. \"The wall was built in a year. And at the end of the year I went to Halvard and said, that's enough, and he said — he said, Oduya, you've been writing false books for a year. Do you want to explain that to the Exchequer, or shall I?\"\n\n\"So you kept writing.\"\n\n\"So I kept writing. And the wall is bad stone, and Halvard has a new house, and the Black Mare comes in on the spring tides and takes one-seventh of the salt of Varrow to wherever it goes.\" He lifted his hands, let them fall. \"And you counted the Kestrel's sticks. I knew you would, one day. You're the only one of them who ever counted.\"\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Did you take it?\"\n\nHis face did not change. \"What letter?\"\n\n\"The Reeve's letter to you. About the Kestrel. I had it.\"\n\nFor a moment the Magister of the Salt House looked at her with an expression she could not read at all, and then, very slowly, he began to laugh. It was not a happy sound.\n\n\"He never sent it,\" he said. \"I never had it. I wondered — I wondered why he was so certain I'd been told about the Black Mare coming early, when I hadn't. He said, _I wrote to you._ And I said, _you didn't._ And we each thought the other was lying.\" He wiped his eyes. \"Oh, Ilse. Somebody stole the Reeve's post. And now somebody has stolen it from you.\"","wordCount":948,"createdAt":1794216650362},{"id":"srjUJlT0G2puTJ67MfJlP","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"r9SBgyYpFJBk2b1o5XkXt","text":"They brought Tomas out on the last of the ebb, the two of them, Ilse under one arm and Wren under the other, and it took four hours, and Ilse did not afterward remember much of it except the cold and the weight and her brother's breath hissing in her ear at every step. Wren found them the hard every time.At the edge of the marsh, where the path came up onto the sea-wall by the Pans, they left Tomas with a pan-man Wren knew, who asked no questions and sent his own son running for a bone-setter, and then Ilse walked home alone through the waking streets with the sun coming up behind her.\n\nThe Magister had said her name. She kept hearing it. Not _the clerk_, not _one of my girls_. _The Varga girl_, in that tired voice she had loved for four years, like a man putting down something heavy.\n\nShe climbed the stair through the middle of the cooper's house. The step that creaked, creaked. The cooper's wife was not singing.\n\nHer door was open.\n\nNot broken. Open, the way you leave a door when you have a key and are in a hurry. Inside, the room looked almost as she had left it — the table, the chair, the lamp-hook — except that everything had been moved a little and put back a little wrong, the way a stranger puts things back. The chair faced the wrong wall. Her mother's comb was on the left of the shelf instead of the right.\n\nShe went back down the stair. The cooper's wife was in the kitchen with the baby on her hip and the door half shut, and when Ilse knocked on it she opened it no wider.\n\n\"Was anybody up?\" Ilse said. \"In the night. To my room.\"\n\n\"I was asleep.\" The cooper's wife did not look at her. She looked at the baby, who was chewing on a wooden spoon. \"We were all asleep. The cooper's been at the Pans all week, you know that, there's nobody here but me.\"\n\n\"My door was open.\"\n\n\"Then you left it open. You were out all night, weren't you? Out in the marsh, by the look of your boots.\" The woman's eyes went to the boots and came away quickly. \"I don't ask where my lodgers go, Miss Varga. I'd thank you not to ask me where I sleep.\"\n\n\"Somebody had a key.\"\n\n\"Then somebody had a key.\" The cooper's wife began to close the door. \"There's three keys to that room and one of them's been lost since Midwinter. Ask the cooper. When he's home.\"\n\nThe door shut. Behind it the baby began to cry, and the woman began to sing to it, the same three lines over and over, too loud.\n\nIlse stood in the passage for a while. Then she went back up.\n\nHer room was cold. Someone had let the lamp burn down to nothing and the wick had charred, and there was a smell in the air that was not hers — wet wool, and tar, and something sharper, like cloves. She stood in the middle of the floor with her hands at her sides and tried to think the way her mother would have thought. Who had known she was going into the marsh. Who had known she would not be home. Wren had known. Mother Gannet had known. Anyone who had seen her go down Tallow Street at dusk in her mother's boots had known, and half the lower town had.\n\nIt did not narrow anything. It only made the room feel larger, and full of people.\n\nThe bolster lay on the bed with its seam cut open from end to end.\n\nShe did not need to look. She looked anyway. She put her hand into the hollow and felt the feathers and the ticking and nothing else, and then she sat down on the bed with the feathers settling around her like snow, and for the first time since the Kestrel's sticks she let herself cry.\n\nShe had not told anyone where she kept it. Not Tomas. Not Wren. Not the auditor, who she had lied to.\n\nShe had told no one, and someone had known.","wordCount":707,"createdAt":1794216552378},{"id":"t-Ujvu6umBtqkELSCRXV9","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"FBthgk0AwfIy4svSwzmYI","text":"The marsh at low water was a different country. The channels that the Patience had sailed were trickles now, running between banks of mud as high as Ilse's head, and the mud shone in the moonlight like the skin of something sleeping. Wren went ahead of her along a path she could not see — the hard, he called it, a spine of shingle under the mud that the old salt-carriers had used before there were boats — and she followed him step for step and did not look down.\n\nIt took them two hours to reach the Chapel. The tower stood up out of the mud on its little island of stone, the arches of the old nave around its foot half-buried, the high windows empty. At high water the sea came in through those windows. Now, with the tide out, there were steps going down into the dark of the crypt, slick with weed, and a smell of salt and old candles.\n\n\"Here's where they load,\" Wren whispered. He pointed. Along the stone of the steps there were fresh scars, white in the moonlight, where something heavy had been dragged. \"Salt. Sacks of it. They bring it up the channel from the Pans at night and keep it up in the tower, above the tide-line, till the ship comes.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\nWren shrugged inside his coat. \"Everybody knows. Nobody says.\"\n\nThey found Tomas in the tower.\n\nHe had dragged himself up the first turn of the stair and wedged himself into a window-niche, and he was so still and so grey that Ilse thought for a moment that he was dead. Then his eyes opened and he said, quite clearly, \"You weren't supposed to come,\" and she knelt on the stair beside him and held his hand and could not say anything at all.\n\nHis leg was splinted with half a boat-hook. He had done it well. He had come across the marsh on his back, ahead of the flood, pushing with his good leg and the stump of the boat-hook, and twice the water had closed over his face. He had been sitting in the niche, he said, for a day and a night, while the Reeve's men came and went below him, loading the black ship from the crypt. He had heard everything. He had not been able to move. He had come across the marsh on his back, ahead of the flood, pushing with his good leg and the stump of the boat-hook, and twice the water had closed over his face.\n\n\"There was a voice in the longboat,\" he said. \"The first night. I thought I knew it. I didn't want to.\"\n\n\"Corin,\" Ilse said. \"Where's Corin?\"\n\n\"Went over the side. Took them off me.\" Tomas closed his eyes. \"Don't know if he got out. He said to tell you to keep the letter.\"\n\n\"I have it.\"\n\n\"Good. Ilse — listen. The ship's gone. The Black Mare. She went out on this morning's tide with all of it aboard, and she'll be back for the rest.\" His hand tightened on hers. \"And it's not just the Reeve. I heard them. They were talking about the Salt House. About the books. About someone who's going to fix the books before the auditor —\"\n\nThere was a sound below them. Wren, who had been keeping watch at the foot of the stair, came up it like a cat, silent, his eyes huge.\n\n\"Lantern,\" he breathed. \"Two men. On the hard.\"\n\nThey did not move. Ilse put her hand over Tomas's mouth, not because he would have made a sound but because she needed to hold something. Below them, in the ruined nave, a lantern came and went among the arches, and two men came with it, and stood at the head of the crypt steps where the white scars were.\n\nOne of them was big and wore a harbour officer's coat. She had seen the Reeve of Varrow at a hundred ceremonies on the quay; she knew his shoulders, his walk, the way he stood with his weight back on his heels.\n\nThe other was tall and thin, and held his lantern with a long hand that she had watched sign a thousand pages.\n\n\"She's asking for the flood year,\" the Magister was saying. His voice echoed in the stone. \"She's had it on the table for two days. She has copies of the Hesk ledgers, Halvard. Copies. She knew before she came.\"\n\n\"Then she knows nothing she can prove.\" The Reeve's voice was easy, almost bored. \"Hesk ledgers. A clerk in Hesk can't count. Everyone knows that.\"\n\n\"She'll ask for the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then give her the sticks. You told me the sticks were sorted.\"\n\nThe Magister was quiet. The lantern shook a little.\n\n\"One of my clerks,\" he said at last, \"found the Kestrel short. Before I could see to it.\"\n\n\"Which clerk?\"\n\nIlse felt Tomas's hand tighten on hers until the bones ground.\n\n\"It doesn't matter. I dealt with it. She wrote it in as I told her.\"\n\n\"Oduya.\" The Reeve sounded almost gentle. \"Everything matters. That's how we've lasted three years. Which clerk?\"\n\nThere was a long silence in the nave. Ilse could hear the water beginning to come back, very far off, a sound like breathing.\n\n\"The Varga girl,\" the Magister said.","wordCount":892,"createdAt":1794230070149},{"id":"t6skw80mP2ngFAjCG1zc_","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"Jwi40uq_PG4-AT77dhdd6","text":"The auditor came two days early.\n\nIlse heard her before she saw her — a carriage on the cobbles of Tallow Street, which was a thing that did not happen; nobody drove a carriage into the lower town because the streets were too narrow and the carters too rude. Then voices in the yard, and the porter's voice going up high the way it did when he was frightened, and then a long silence, and then feet on the stair.\n\nLisbet Ormund was not what Ilse had expected. She had pictured someone like the Magister, tall and grey and sorrowful, or someone like the Reeve, red-faced and loud. The woman who came into the third-floor room was small and round and perhaps fifty, in a brown travelling coat with the mud of the coast road still on its hem, and she had a face like a kindly hen. She stood in the doorway and took in the shelves of ledgers, all forty years of them, with an expression of pure delight.\n\n\"Oh,\" she said. \"Oh, look at that. You keep them up here? Under the roof?\"\n\n\"It's the driest room, madam,\" said Aurek, who had got to his feet so fast his stool had gone over.\n\n\"It's the hottest room in summer,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Paper doesn't like heat any more than it likes damp. Never mind. Never mind.\" She came in and took off her gloves. \"I'm the Exchequer's auditor. I expect you've been told I'd be along. I expect you were told the twentieth.\" She smiled at both of them. \"I find it saves a great deal of everyone's time to come on the eighteenth.\"\n\nIlse found that she had stood up too, and that her hand had gone flat against the front of her bodice, where the letter was. She took it away.\n\n\"Magister Fenn —\" Aurek began.\n\n\"Is downstairs, and has given me the run of the place, and very gracious he was about it. He's sending up tea. You'll be Master Stiles. And you are —?\"\n\n\"Ilse Varga, madam. Tallyclerk.\"\n\n\"Varga.\" The auditor looked at her a moment longer than was comfortable. \"There was a Varga at the Hesk customs house, years ago. A woman. Very good with figures.\"\n\n\"My mother, madam.\"\n\n\"Was she? Well.\" Lisbet Ormund turned back to the shelves. \"Then you'll know the difference between a book that's right and a book that's tidy, Miss Varga. Most people don't.\" She ran a finger along the spines, the newest first. \"I'll want this year's, and last year's, and — let me see — the year of the big tide. When was that? Three years back?\"\n\n\"Four, madam,\" said Aurek.\n\n\"Three,\" said Ilse, at the same moment.\n\nThey looked at each other. The auditor looked at both of them, pleasantly.\n\n\"The spring tide that took the Pans,\" Ilse said. \"It was three years in Thawmonth. I'd been here a year.\"\n\n\"Then I'll have that one,\" said Lisbet Ormund, \"and the one before it, so I can see the difference. And I'll have the tally-sticks for whatever's come in this month. All of them.\" She sat down at the end of the long table, in the place where nobody ever sat because the draught came under the door there, and folded her small hands. \"I'm told sticks go missing,\" she said. \"I'm told it happens all the time.\"\n\nNobody said anything. Down in the yard the porter was shouting for someone to bring up the tea.\n\nThat night Ilse did not go home over the cooper's. She went down the Gull Stair to the quay, and she did not find Tomas on his basket outside the pilots' shed, and when she asked old Mother Gannet, who sold eels at the foot of the stair and saw everything, the old woman only said that the Patience had gone out on the evening ebb, and that there'd been two men in her, and that one of them was a long clever one with a scar in his eyebrow.","wordCount":671,"createdAt":1794216604968},{"id":"vWVG7v_YozCO7OpqOJW7O","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"JLebbu3pA-RXZKvs35ANa","text":"Lisbet Ormund had taken the best room at the Anchor, which was not saying a great deal, and had spent the first evening moving the furniture so that the table caught the light from the window and the bed did not catch the draught from the door. She was at the table now, with her shoes off and her stockinged feet on a hot brick wrapped in flannel, writing her report.\n\nShe had written it three times. Each time it said the same thing — _one part in seven, three years, the Salt House books written to conceal it_ — and each time it ended in the same place, which was _I have no proof of who._\n\nWhen the landlord knocked to say the Reeve was below and wished to call on her, she said, \"Yes, of course, send him up,\" and put her shoes back on, and turned her report face down on the table, and put the hot brick under the bed.\n\nHalvard Seyle filled the doorway. He was a big man and knew it, and he had come in his harbour coat with the gold at the cuffs, and he bowed to her with great courtesy and said he hoped she was comfortable, and that Varrow was not too rough for a lady from the capital, and that if there was anything at all she needed she had only to send to the Reeve's Hall.\n\n\"How kind,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Do sit down.\"\n\nHe sat. The chair complained.\n\n\"I hear you've been at the Salt House books,\" he said.\n\n\"I have.\"\n\n\"Poor Fenn. He's not the man he was. The flood hit him very hard — he took it personally, you know, as though the sea had done it to spite him. I've had to carry a good deal of the work since.\" He smiled. \"If you've found anything amiss, I'd hope you'd come to me first. Before the capital. It would be a shame to ruin an old man's name over a few clerical errors that the harbour could put right quietly.\"\n\n\"A few clerical errors,\" the auditor repeated.\n\n\"Sticks go missing,\" said the Reeve. \"It happens all the time.\"\n\nLisbet Ormund regarded him for a long moment. Then she turned her report over on the table and pushed it across to him.\n\n\"One part in seven,\" she said. \"Every month for three years. That is not a few clerical errors, Reeve. That is twenty-two thousand crowns of the Exchequer's revenue, near enough, and it is going somewhere, and it is not going to Hesk.\" She folded her hands. \"I'll tell you what I have, since you've been good enough to call. I have the Hesk ledgers. I have the Salt House books. I have a tallyclerk who lied to my face and was frightened to death doing it, and a Magister who hasn't slept in three nights. What I don't have is a name.\" She smiled at him. \"I don't suppose you'd care to give me one?\"\n\nThe Reeve did not smile back.\n\n\"You've been in Varrow three days, madam,\" he said. \"You don't know the tides. People who don't know the tides get into trouble out on the marsh. Every year we lose one or two. Visitors, mostly.\" He stood up. \"I'd be careful.\"\n\n\"I always am,\" said Lisbet Ormund. \"Good night, Reeve.\"\n\nWhen he had gone she sat for a while without moving. Then she got the hot brick back out from under the bed — it had gone lukewarm — and put her feet on it, and turned her report face down again, and wrote at the bottom of a fresh sheet, in her tiny neat hand: _Seyle. Certain. No proof._","wordCount":619,"createdAt":1794217065925},{"id":"wQ52npWgZqbghnwnj11wR","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"51sz_3kuWZsw6JR4Y0Ko2","text":"The ledger for the year of the big tide was bound in green, because the binder had run out of brown that winter, and it stood out on the shelf like a wrong note. Lisbet Ormund had it open on the long table when Ilse came up the stair, and beside it the book for the year before, bound in brown, and beside that a sheet of her own paper covered in tiny, very neat figures.\n\nAurek was not at his place. The auditor saw her looking.\n\n\"I've sent Master Stiles to the pan-reeve with a list of questions,\" she said. \"It'll take him most of the day, I'm afraid, because the pan-reeve won't know the answers and will have to send to the Reeve's office for them. Sit down, Miss Varga. Sit there, where I can see you.\"\n\nIlse sat.\n\n\"I want to show you something,\" said the auditor, \"and then I want to ask you something, and I'd like you to think before you answer the second thing, because I shall know if you don't.\" She turned the green ledger round. \"This is Thawmonth, the year of the tide. Here — the week of the flood. Nothing comes in at all, which is what you'd expect. Here, the month after. Very little. Here, the month after that. And here,\" she turned a page, \"is the first month that the books are short against the Hesk price.\"\n\nIlse looked. It was a column of numbers in Aurek's hand, tidy and upright, and beside each a ship's name and a date. There was nothing wrong with any of them.\n\n\"They're not short,\" she said.\n\n\"Not in the book. In the book they're very well. But I have here,\" the auditor tapped her own sheet, \"every cargo of Varrow salt that was sold at Hesk that month, from the Hesk customs ledgers, which I had copied before I left the capital. And the Hesk ledgers say that Varrow sent them a good deal less salt than Varrow's books say it sent. About one part in seven less.\" She sat back. \"One part in seven. Every month since. Three years.\"\n\nOne part in seven. Sixty measures out of four hundred was a little more than that. Ilse kept her face very still and felt the paper crackle under her bodice when she breathed.\n\n\"Somebody,\" said Lisbet Ormund, \"is selling one-seventh of the salt of Varrow somewhere other than Hesk, and the books of the Salt House are written as though they weren't. Now. Here's my question.\" She leaned forward. \"I don't think you're a thief, Miss Varga. I knew your mother. I don't think the Magister is a thief either, not in his heart, though I've been wrong about men's hearts before. But somebody in this building writes the numbers, and somebody tells them which numbers to write. Has anybody ever told you to write a number you knew was wrong?\"\n\nShe thought about the Kestrel's book. Four hundred in her own clean hand.\n\nShe thought about Tomas, who had not come home.\n\nShe thought: *if I say yes, she'll ask who. And if I say who, they'll know it was me. And Tomas is somewhere out in the marsh, and I don't know where, and I don't know who else knows.*\n\n\"No, madam,\" she said.\n\nThe auditor looked at her for a long moment. Then she nodded, slowly, and turned the green book back around, and picked up her pen.\n\n\"I'll be here until the spring tide,\" she said, without looking up. \"After that I go back to the capital and write my report with whatever I have. If you think of anything before then, Miss Varga, I'd be very grateful to hear it. I'm at the Anchor, on Rope Walk. Anyone can tell you where it is.\"\n\nIlse went back to her own place and opened the Heron's book and could not see a single number in it.","wordCount":658,"createdAt":1794214646089},{"id":"yNN6uzCQOCqPRoBMVHnGt","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"r9SBgyYpFJBk2b1o5XkXt","text":"They brought Tomas out on the last of the ebb, the two of them, Ilse under one arm and Wren under the other, and it took four hours, and Ilse did not afterward remember much of it except the cold and the weight and her brother's breath hissing in her ear at every step. Wren found them the hard every time. At the edge of the marsh, where the path came up onto the sea-wall by the Pans, they left Tomas with a pan-man Wren knew, who asked no questions and sent his own son running for a bone-setter, and then Ilse walked home alone through the waking streets with the sun coming up behind her.\n\nThe Magister had said her name. She kept hearing it. Not *the clerk*, not *one of my girls*. *The Varga girl*, in that tired voice she had loved for four years, like a man putting down something heavy.\n\nShe climbed the stair through the middle of the cooper's house. The step that creaked, creaked. The cooper's wife was not singing.\n\nHer door was open.\n\nNot broken. Open, the way you leave a door when you have a key and are in a hurry. Inside, the room looked almost as she had left it — the table, the chair, the lamp-hook — except that everything had been moved a little and put back a little wrong, the way a stranger puts things back. The chair faced the wrong wall. Her mother's comb was on the left of the shelf instead of the right.\n\nThe bolster lay on the bed with its seam cut open from end to end.\n\nShe did not need to look. She looked anyway. She put her hand into the hollow and felt the feathers and the ticking and nothing else, and then she sat down on the bed with the feathers settling around her like snow, and for the first time since the Kestrel's sticks she let herself cry.\n\nShe had not told anyone where she kept it. Not Tomas. Not Wren. Not the auditor, who she had lied to.\n\nShe had told no one, and someone had known.\n# Weights and Measures\n\n[\n  Place: #The Salt House\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Aurek Stiles @@Oduya Fenn @@Lisbet Ormund\n  When: same day\n  Items: ^The Kestrel's tally-sticks:@Aurek Stiles\n  Facts: ?The Reeve's letter never reached Fenn:@Ilse Varga\n]\n\nShe went to work. She did not know what else to do with herself, and it seemed important, in a way she could not have explained, that she should be at her place at the long table when the bell went, in her clerk's grey, with her hair pinned, as if nothing had happened. As if she were still the best clerk the Magister had had in thirty years.\n\nAurek looked up when she came in and opened his mouth to say something about bags of nails, and then looked at her face properly and shut it again.\n\nThe auditor was not there. Her sheet of tiny figures lay on the end of the table under a glass paperweight, and the green ledger was gone from beside it.\n\n\"She's downstairs,\" Aurek said quietly. \"With him. Since first bell.\" He hesitated. \"Ilse. I took the sticks out of the bin.\"\n\nShe turned.\n\n\"The Kestrel's. The night you put them in. I'm not a fool, girl, whatever you think. Nobody finds six sticks behind a stove and then finds them gone again.\" He did not look at her; he looked at his own ledger, at his own tidy upright hand. \"I've been here twenty years. I've written what I was told for three of them. I didn't count. I didn't ask. I told myself the Magister read the margins.\" He turned a page that did not need turning. \"They're in my coat. In the press. If you want them.\"\n\nShe stood very still.\n\n\"Why?\" she said.\n\n\"Because you didn't write anything in the margin,\" said Aurek Stiles. \"You always write in the margin. You've written in the margin every day for four years. And that day you didn't, and I thought: she's frightened. And I thought: I've been frightened for three years and I never once noticed it was the same thing.\"\n\nAt midday the auditor came up the stair and took her sheet of figures from under the paperweight and went down again without a word to either of them. A little after that, the porter came up to say that the Magister wanted Miss Varga in his room.\n\nThe stove was lit. It was always lit. Oduya Fenn sat behind his desk with the green ledger closed in front of him and his long hands folded on top of it, and he did not smile when she came in. That was how she knew he had not slept either.\n\n\"Sit down, Ilse.\"\n\nShe did not sit down.\n\n\"I was at the Chapel last night,\" she said. \"In the tower. With Tomas, who has a broken leg, and has been lying in a window for two days while you loaded salt underneath him.\"\n\nThe Magister closed his eyes.\n\n\"I heard you tell the Reeve my name.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"I imagine you did.\" He opened his eyes again and looked at her, and his face was older than she had ever seen it. \"Will you let me tell you how it happened? Not to excuse it. I don't think it can be excused. But I'd like someone to know, and I think you've earned it more than anyone.\"\n\nShe sat down.\n\nHe told her. The tide in Thawmonth that took the wall and the pans and the pan-reeve's wife. The letter to the capital, and the letter back, *a matter for the harbour.* A whole year of the pan-men sitting on the quay, and the price of salt going up at Hesk, and the Salt House half empty, and the Exchequer writing every quarter to ask why the revenue was down. And then Halvard Seyle in this room, in that chair, with a bottle of something good, saying: *I can build you a wall, Oduya. All it needs is a little salt that nobody counts.*\n\n\"One part in seven,\" the Magister said. \"For one year. To pay for the wall. That was all it was going to be. I told myself it was the Exchequer's own salt paying for the Exchequer's own wall, and if the Exchequer had done its duty there'd have been no need. I told myself a great many things.\" He looked down at his hands. \"The wall was built in a year. And at the end of the year I went to Halvard and said, that's enough, and he said — he said, Oduya, you've been writing false books for a year. Do you want to explain that to the Exchequer, or shall I?\"\n\n\"So you kept writing.\"\n\n\"So I kept writing. And the wall is bad stone, and Halvard has a new house, and the Black Mare comes in on the spring tides and takes one-seventh of the salt of Varrow to wherever it goes.\" He lifted his hands, let them fall. \"And you counted the Kestrel's sticks. I knew you would, one day. You're the only one of them who ever counted.\"\n\n\"The letter,\" she said. \"Did you take it?\"\n\nHis face did not change. \"What letter?\"\n\n\"The Reeve's letter to you. About the Kestrel. I had it.\"\n\nFor a moment the Magister of the Salt House looked at her with an expression she could not read at all, and then, very slowly, he began to laugh. It was not a happy sound.\n\n\"He never sent it,\" he said. \"I never had it. I wondered — I wondered why he was so certain I'd been told about the Black Mare coming early, when I hadn't. He said, *I wrote to you.* And I said, *you didn't.* And we each thought the other was lying.\" He wiped his eyes. \"Oh, Ilse. Somebody stole the Reeve's post. And now somebody has stolen it from you.\"","wordCount":1341,"createdAt":1794213937944},{"id":"yUVndac533V0xGzUhJlXC","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"51sz_3kuWZsw6JR4Y0Ko2","text":"The ledger for the year of the big tide was bound in green, because the binder had run out of brown that winter, and it stood out on the shelf like a wrong note. Lisbet Ormund had it open on the long table when Ilse came up the stair, and beside it the book for the year before, bound in brown, and beside that a sheet of her own paper covered in tiny, neat figures.\n\nAurek was not at his place. The auditor saw her looking.\n\n\"I've sent Master Stiles to the pan-reeve with a list of questions,\" she said. \"It'll take him most of the day, I'm afraid, because the pan-reeve won't know the answers and will have to send to the Reeve's office for them. Sit down, Miss Varga. Sit there, where I can see you.\"\n\nIlse sat.\n\n\"I want to show you something,\" said the auditor, \"and then I want to ask you something, and I'd like you to think before you answer the second thing, because I shall know if you don't.\" She turned the green ledger round. \"This is Thawmonth, the year of the tide. Here — the week of the flood. Nothing comes in at all, which is what you'd expect. Here, the month after. Very little. Here, the month after that. And here,\" she turned a page, \"is the first month that the books are short against the Hesk price.\"\n\nIlse looked. It was a column of numbers in Aurek's hand, tidy and upright, and beside each a ship's name and a date. There was nothing wrong with any of them.\n\n\"They're not short,\" she said.\n\n\"Not in the book. In the book they're very well. But I have here,\" the auditor tapped her own sheet, \"every cargo of Varrow salt that was sold at Hesk that month, from the Hesk customs ledgers, which I had copied before I left the capital. And the Hesk ledgers say that Varrow sent them a good deal less salt than Varrow's books say it sent. About one part in seven less.\" She sat back. \"One part in seven. Every month since. Three years.\"\n\nOne part in seven. Sixty measures out of four hundred was a little more than that. Ilse kept her face very still and felt the paper crackle under her bodice when she breathed.\n\n\"Somebody,\" said Lisbet Ormund, \"is selling one-seventh of the salt of Varrow somewhere other than Hesk, and the books of the Salt House are written as though they weren't. Now. Here's my question.\" She leaned forward. \"I don't think you're a thief, Miss Varga. I knew your mother. I don't think the Magister is a thief either, not in his heart, though I've been wrong about men's hearts before. But somebody in this building writes the numbers, and somebody tells them which numbers to write. Has anybody ever told you to write a number you knew was wrong?\"\n\nShe thought about the Kestrel's book. Four hundred in her own clean hand.\n\nShe thought about Tomas, who had not come home.\n\nShe thought: _if I say yes, she'll ask who. And if I say who, they'll know it was me. And Tomas is somewhere out in the marsh, and I don't know where, and I don't know who else knows._\n\n\"No, madam,\" she said.\n\nThe auditor looked at her for a long moment. Then she nodded, slowly, and turned the green book back around, and picked up her pen.\n\n\"I'll be here until the spring tide,\" she said, without looking up. \"After that I go back to the capital and write my report with whatever I have. If you think of anything before then, Miss Varga, I'd be very grateful to hear it. I'm at the Anchor, on Rope Walk. Anyone can tell you where it is.\"\n\nIlse went back to her own place and opened the Heron's book and could not see a single number in it.","wordCount":657,"createdAt":1794216608736},{"id":"yogq_h2kvERumscyg1siC","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"QkH1pzVCb9awQuyhPF6LA","text":"They had been seen. Tomas never knew how — a turn of the Patience's sail against the sky, perhaps, or the creak of her blocks across still water, or simply that a man on the Chapel steps had looked up at the wrong moment. But the light at the Chapel went out all at once, and in the dark there was a shout, and then the sound of oars.\n\n\"Go,\" said Corin. \"Go, go —\"\n\nTomas went. He put the helm over and got the sail up in one long heave and the Patience came round, slow, so slow, broad-beamed old cow that she was, and the flood was against them now, pushing them back up toward the Chapel. Behind them the oars were coming on. Four oars at least. A longboat, light and fast, and the men in her knew the channel nearly as well as he did.\n\n\"They'll have us before the Heron Creek,\" Corin said.\n\n\"They won't.\"\n\n\"They will. I can count, Tomas, it's the only thing I'm good for besides lying.\" Corin was pulling off his boots. \"There's a cut on the left — the one with the dead tree. Where does it go?\"\n\n\"Nowhere. Mud. It's a dead end.\"\n\n\"Can a man stand in it?\"\n\n\"At this tide? To his chest, maybe. What are you —\"\n\n\"They want the boat. They'll follow the boat.\" Corin was on the gunwale now, crouched, his clever face white in the starlight. \"And they want me more than they want you, because I'm the one they've been paying. So when I go over, you keep going, and you make a great deal of noise, and I'll make rather less.\" He grinned suddenly. \"Tell your sister to keep the letter. Tell her it's worth more than I am.\"\n\n\"Corin —\"\n\nHe went over the side without a splash. Tomas never heard him hit the water. One moment there was a man on the gunwale and the next there was the dark and the dead tree going past on the left and nothing else.\n\nHe kept going. He did not know what else to do. He made, as instructed, a great deal of noise — he let the mainsheet run so the block screamed, and he banged the tiller against the thwart, and he shouted Corin's name once, loudly, toward the right-hand bank, as though looking for a man who had fallen in there. Behind him the oars checked, and a voice said something sharp, and the longboat split its attention for a moment between the boat and the bank.\n\nIt was enough to get him into the Heron Creek. It was not enough to get him out of it.\n\nThe Heron was narrow and shallow and he had taken it a thousand times at the top of the tide, but this was not the top of the tide and he was not looking where he was going. The Patience hit the bar at the creek's mouth with a long grinding shudder that went up through the keel and into his teeth, and stopped, and heeled, and he was thrown against the thwart so hard that he heard something in his leg go with a sound like a green stick breaking.\n\nHe lay in the bilge-water and did not scream, because the longboat was still out there.\n\nAfter a time — he did not know how long — he heard the oars go past the mouth of the creek, slow now, searching. A lantern swung. A man's voice said, \"Nothing. She's gone up the Heron and stuck, she'll be there in the morning, we'll have her then,\" and another voice, one he thought he knew, said, \"And the other one?\" and the first voice said, \"Drowned, if he's got any sense.\" Then the oars again, going away, toward the Chapel and the black ship and the salt.\n\nHe waited until he could not hear them. Then he dragged himself up onto the thwart, and looked at his leg, and looked away again quickly. The tide was still making. In an hour the Patience would float off the bar. In an hour he would have to be somewhere else.\n\nThe tower of the Drowned Chapel was a quarter mile off across the marsh, black against the first grey in the east. It was the only dry place for a mile. It was also where the men in the longboat were going.\n\nTomas looked at it for a long time. Then he found the boat-hook, and broke the shaft across his knee to make a splint, and began to bind his leg with the sail-ties, and tried to think of something else.\n# Low Water\n## The Boy on the Stair\n[\n  Place: #The Salt House\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Wren\n  When: day 6\n  Items: ^The Reeve's letter:#Ilse's Room\n]\n\nWren found her at the end of the day, as Mother Gannet had promised he would. He was sitting on the Salt House steps when she came out, with the porter glaring at him from the door, and he stood up as soon as he saw her and said, without any greeting at all, \"The Patience is up the Heron Creek. Tad Mossop saw her. She's on her side on the bar and there's nobody in her.\"\n\nIlse stood on the step with her satchel in her hand.\n\n\"Nobody,\" she said.\n\n\"Tad didn't go close. Tad said there was men about. Reeve's men, in a longboat, going up and down.\" Wren wiped his nose on his cut-down sleeve. \"Mother says you'll want to go and look, and Mother says don't, and Mother says if you're going anyway take me, because I know the way at low water and you don't.\"\n\n\"When's low water?\"\n\n\"Tonight. Middle of the night. It's springs tomorrow, so it'll go right out — you can walk to the Chapel nearly dry-shod if you know where the hard is.\" He looked at her with an expression that was much older than the rest of his face. \"Mother said you'd ask that. Mother said you were your mother's daughter.\"\n\nShe thought about going back up to the third floor and telling the auditor everything. The letter, the sticks, the Kestrel's book in her own hand. She thought about the auditor's kindly hen's face, and about the Reeve's men going up and down the channel in a longboat looking for a boat with her brother in it.\n\nIf she told the auditor tonight, the auditor would write to the capital tomorrow, and the capital would send someone in a week. A week was a long time to lie on a bar in the marsh with nobody in your boat.\n\n\"I'll need to go home first,\" she said. \"For boots.\"\n\n\"I'll come with you.\"\n\n\"You don't need to.\"\n\n\"Mother said not to let you out of my sight.\" Wren fell in beside her. \"Mother's never wrong. It's very annoying.\"\n\nThey went up Tallow Street together in the dusk, the tall woman in her clerk's grey and the small dirty boy in his enormous coat, and nobody looked at them twice, because that was the kind of street it was. At the cooper's Ilse left Wren on the step and climbed the stair through the middle of the house to her room.\n\nShe changed into her mother's old marsh boots, which were too big and which she had kept for ten years without once wearing. She took the letter out of her bodice — it had gone soft with her warmth, the seal tacky — and stood for a moment with it in her hand.\n\nIf she went into the marsh with it and they caught her, they would have it. If she left it here —\n\nShe put it in the hollow of the bolster, where the sticks had been. Then she took the lamp and went down to Wren.\n\n## The Drowned Chapel\n[\n  Place: #The Drowned Chapel\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga @@Wren @@Tomas Brenn @@Oduya Fenn @@Halvard Seyle\n  When: same day\n  Facts: ?Fenn is in it with the Reeve:@Ilse Varga ?Fenn is in it with the Reeve:@Tomas Brenn ?Fenn is in it with the Reeve:reader\n]\n\nThe marsh at low water was a different country. The channels that the Patience had sailed were trickles now, running between banks of mud as high as Ilse's head, and the mud shone in the moonlight like the skin of something sleeping. Wren went ahead of her along a path she could not see — the hard, he called it, a spine of shingle under the mud that the old salt-carriers had used before there were boats — and she followed him step for step and did not look down.\n\nIt took them two hours to reach the Chapel. The tower stood up out of the mud on its little island of stone, the arches of the old nave around its foot half-buried, the high windows empty. At high water the sea came in through those windows. Now, with the tide out, there were steps going down into the dark of the crypt, slick with weed, and a smell of salt and old candles.\n\n\"Here's where they load,\" Wren whispered. He pointed. Along the stone of the steps there were fresh scars, white in the moonlight, where something heavy had been dragged. \"Salt. Sacks of it. They bring it up the channel from the Pans at night and keep it here dry till the ship comes.\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\nWren shrugged inside his coat. \"Everybody knows. Nobody says.\"\n\nThey found Tomas in the tower.\n\nHe had dragged himself up the first turn of the stair and wedged himself into a window-niche, and he was so still and so grey that Ilse thought for a long moment that he was dead. Then his eyes opened and he said, quite clearly, \"You weren't supposed to come,\" and she knelt on the stair beside him and held his hand and could not say anything at all.\n\nHis leg was splinted with half a boat-hook. He had done it well. He had been sitting in the niche, he said, for a day and a night, while the Reeve's men came and went below him, loading the black ship from the crypt. He had heard everything. He had not been able to move.\n\n\"Corin,\" Ilse said. \"Where's Corin?\"\n\n\"Went over the side. Took them off me.\" Tomas closed his eyes. \"Don't know if he got out. He said to tell you to keep the letter.\"\n\n\"I have it.\"\n\n\"Good. Ilse — listen. The ship's gone. The Black Mare. She went out on this morning's tide with all of it aboard, and she'll be back for the rest.\" His hand tightened on hers. \"And it's not just the Reeve. I heard them. They were talking about the Salt House. About the books. About someone who's going to fix the books before the auditor —\"\n\nThere was a sound below them. Wren, who had been keeping watch at the foot of the stair, came up it like a cat, silent, his eyes huge.\n\n\"Lantern,\" he breathed. \"Two men. On the hard.\"\n\nThey did not move. Ilse put her hand over Tomas's mouth, not because he would have made a sound but because she needed to hold something. Below them, in the ruined nave, a lantern came and went among the arches, and two men came with it, and stood at the head of the crypt steps where the white scars were.\n\nOne of them was big and wore a harbour officer's coat. She had seen the Reeve of Varrow at a hundred ceremonies on the quay; she knew his shoulders, his walk, the way he stood with his weight back on his heels.\n\nThe other was tall and thin, and held his lantern with a long hand that she had watched sign a thousand pages.\n\n\"She's asking for the flood year,\" the Magister was saying. His voice echoed in the stone. \"She's had it on the table for two days. She has copies of the Hesk ledgers, Halvard. Copies. She knew before she came.\"\n\n\"Then she knows nothing she can prove.\" The Reeve's voice was easy, almost bored. \"Hesk ledgers. A clerk in Hesk can't count. Everyone knows that.\"\n\n\"She'll ask for the sticks.\"\n\n\"Then give her the sticks. You told me the sticks were sorted.\"\n\nThe Magister was quiet. The lantern shook a little.\n\n\"One of my clerks,\" he said at last, \"found the Kestrel short. Before I could see to it.\"\n\n\"Which clerk?\"\n\nIlse felt Tomas's hand tighten on hers until the bones ground.\n\n\"It doesn't matter. I dealt with it. She wrote it in as I told her.\"\n\n\"Oduya.\" The Reeve sounded almost gentle. \"Everything matters. That's how we've lasted three years. Which clerk?\"\n\nThere was a long silence in the nave. Ilse could hear the water beginning to come back, very far off, a sound like breathing.\n\n\"The Varga girl,\" the Magister said.\n\n[\n  Place: #Ilse's Room\n  Characters: @@Ilse Varga\n  When: +1 day\n  Items: ^The Reeve's letter:lost\n]\n\nThey brought Tomas out on the last of the ebb, the two of them, Ilse under one arm and Wren under the other, and it took four hours, and Ilse did not afterward remember much of it except the cold and the weight and her brother's breath hissing in her ear at every step. Wren found them the hard every time. At the edge of the marsh, where the path came up onto the sea-wall by the Pans, they left Tomas with a pan-man Wren knew, who asked no questions and sent his own son running for a bone-setter, and then Ilse walked home alone through the waking streets with the sun coming up behind her.\n\nThe Magister had said her name. She kept hearing it. Not *the clerk*, not *one of my girls*. *The Varga girl*, in that tired voice she had loved for four years, like a man putting down something heavy.\n\nShe climbed the stair through the middle of the cooper's house. The step that creaked, creaked. The cooper's wife was not singing.\n\nHer door was open.\n\nNot broken. Open, the way you leave a door when you have a key and are in a hurry. Inside, the room looked almost as she had left it — the table, the chair, the lamp-hook — except that everything had been moved a little and put back a little wrong, the way a stranger puts things back. The chair faced the wrong wall. Her mother's comb was on the left of the shelf instead of the right.\n\nThe bolster lay on the bed with its seam cut open from end to end.\n\nShe did not need to look. She looked anyway. She put her hand into the hollow and felt the feathers and the ticking and nothing else, and then she sat down on the bed with the feathers settling around her like snow, and for the first time since the Kestrel's sticks she let herself cry.\n\nShe had not told anyone where she kept it. Not Tomas. Not Wren. Not the auditor, who she had lied to.\n\nShe had told no one, and someone had known.","wordCount":2537,"createdAt":1794213918798},{"id":"zB-z6RHF1IVX_YRT0In_M","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"PxNngKaMJ02laOeegTtnG","text":"The Patience was a pilot's boat, broad in the beam and built for the Gullet, and she did not like being asked to go quietly. Every block on her creaked; the mast talked to itself; the bilge-water slopped from side to side like a fat man laughing. Tomas Varga had sailed her for eleven years and had never once wanted her to be quiet before.\n\n\"You could oil something,\" said Corin Ashe from the bow.\n\n\"You could shut up.\"\n\n\"I could.\" Corin was lying back against the anchor-warp with his hands behind his head, looking at the stars, as if he had been brought out here for the air. \"I won't, though. I talk when I'm nervous. Everyone has something.\"\n\n\"You don't look nervous.\"\n\n\"That's the trick of it.\"\n\nThey had come out on the ebb at dusk, through the harbour mouth and then hard to starboard into the marsh channels, where the Salt House had its Pans and nobody else had any reason to go. The tide was making now. Tomas could feel it through the tiller, the water beginning to push up the channels from the sea, filling the little creeks one by one. In two hours there would be enough water over the Chapel bar for something bigger than the Patience to come in. That was what Corin had told him. There were forty crowns in his coat pocket. He had not come for them. He had come because the night before he had gone to find Corin himself, the night before, to ask what a man like that knew about six missing sticks, and Corin had been quiet a while and then said: more than your sister does. Come out on the ebb tomorrow and I'll show you.\n\n\"I'm not carrying anything,\" Tomas said. \"You said that. Nothing in the boat.\"\n\n\"Nothing in the boat. We're going to look. That's all. You know the channels and I don't, and I want to see a thing with my own eyes before I say I've seen it.\" Corin turned his head. \"Your sister's got the letter.\"\n\nTomas nearly put them on the mud. \"You gave it to her.\"\n\n\"Somebody had to have it, and it couldn't be me. I'm not the sort of man people believe. And I'm three hundred crowns short of a berth on anything going south, which is the kind of thing that makes a man sell what he's holding.\"\n\n\"She's a clerk. She's — she's got a room over the cooper's and a place at the Salt House and that's all she's got. If the Reeve finds out —\"\n\n\"Then he'll find out that a clerk at the Salt House has a letter under his seal saying he's robbing the Exchequer,\" said Corin. \"And then he'll have to decide what to do about her, in front of an auditor. Which is a great deal harder than deciding what to do about me in a dark alley.\" He was quiet a moment. \"I'm sorry. I didn't think she'd be in it this long. I thought she'd take it straight to the auditor and the auditor would take it straight to the capital and that would be that.\"\n\n\"You don't know Ilse.\"\n\n\"I'm learning.\"\n\nThe channel opened out. Ahead of them, black against the stars, rose the broken tower of the Drowned Chapel, which had stood on an island in the marsh once, before the sea moved, and now stood up to its knees in the tide twice a day. Tomas let the sail down and they drifted, the Patience turning slowly on the flood.\n\nThere was a light at the Chapel.\n\nNot a lantern — a shielded thing, a slit of yellow, low down near the water. And past the Chapel, out where the channel met the sea, a shape that did not belong to the marsh: a hull, long and low, with two masts and no lights at all.\n\n\"The Black Mare,\" Corin said softly. All the laughter had gone out of his voice. \"Right on her night.\"\n\n\"You knew she'd be here.\"\n\n\"I carry the Reeve's post, Tomas. Letters, mostly, and I've a habit of reading them. I knew she'd be here because I carried the letter that said so, and then I carried it somewhere else.\" He sat up. \"The first load tonight. The rest on the spring tide, three nights on.\"\n\nOut at the Chapel the light moved. Tomas heard, very faint across the water, the knock of a boat against stone, and a man's voice, and the long dragging sound of something heavy being hauled over a gunwale.\n\n\"Salt,\" said Corin. \"They're loading.\"\n\n\"We should go.\"\n\n\"We should.\" But Corin did not move. He was leaning forward, staring, his face gone still. \"Tomas. There's a boat at the Chapel steps. Can you see whose?\"\n\nTomas could. He had known her the moment the light swung across her stern: he had brought the Kestrel in through the Gullet on the ninth with that same boat towing astern.\n\nIt was the Kestrel's longboat.","wordCount":841,"createdAt":1794230090432},{"id":"zgGw_uugHt74cGYWaZkTA","worldId":"jEoeUiqEF92tsA3Kga6E0","eventId":"E8x7iKzVjIVHZtYoZ0wvQ","text":"Wren found her at the end of the day, as Mother Gannet had promised he would. He was sitting on the Salt House steps when she came out, with the porter glaring at him from the door, and he stood up as soon as he saw her and said, without any greeting at all, \"The Patience is up the Heron Creek. Tad Mossop saw her. She's on her side on the bar and there's nobody in her.\"\n\nIlse stood on the step with her satchel in her hand.\n\n\"Nobody,\" she said.\n\n\"Tad didn't go close. Tad said there was men about. Reeve's men, in a longboat, going up and down.\" Wren wiped his nose on his cut-down sleeve. \"Mother says you'll want to go and look, and Mother says don't, and Mother says if you're going anyway take me, because I know the way at low water and you don't.\"\n\n\"When's low water?\"\n\n\"Tonight. Middle of the night. It's springs tomorrow, so it'll go right out — you can walk to the Chapel nearly dry-shod if you know where the hard is.\" He looked at her with an expression that was much older than the rest of his face. \"Mother said you'd ask that. Mother said you were your mother's daughter.\"\n\nShe thought about going back up to the third floor and telling the auditor everything. The letter, the sticks, the Kestrel's book in her own hand. She thought about the auditor's kindly hen's face, and about the Reeve's men going up and down the channel in a longboat looking for a boat with her brother in it.\n\nIf she told the auditor tonight, the auditor would write to the capital tomorrow, and the capital would send someone in a week. A week was a long time to lie on a bar in the marsh with nobody in your boat.\n\n\"I'll need to go home first,\" she said. \"For boots.\"\n\n\"I'll come with you.\"\n\n\"You don't need to.\"\n\n\"Mother said not to let you out of my sight.\" Wren fell in beside her. \"Mother's never wrong. It's very annoying.\"\n\nThey went up Tallow Street together in the dusk, the tall woman in her clerk's grey and the small dirty boy in his enormous coat, and nobody looked at them twice, because that was the kind of street it was. At the cooper's Ilse left Wren on the step and climbed the stair through the middle of the house to her room.\n\nShe changed into her mother's old marsh boots, which were too big and which she had kept for ten years without once wearing. She took the letter out of her bodice — it had gone soft with her warmth, the seal tacky — and stood for a moment with it in her hand.\n\nIf she went into the marsh with it and they caught her, they would have it. If she left it here —\n\nShe put it in the hollow of the bolster, where the sticks had been. Then she took the lamp and went down to Wren.","wordCount":510,"createdAt":1794217049463}],"tombstones":[],"blobs":[]}